	(421, 3, 6, NULL, 'Information Security Policy', '<h1>Information Security Policy</h1>
<h2>Purpose</h2>
<p>The purpose of this Information Security Policy is to establish a comprehensive framework to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information assets within our organization. This policy is critical to safeguard our company from cybersecurity threats and ensure compliance with relevant legal and regulatory requirements.</p>
<h2>Terms and Definitions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Information Assets</strong>: Data, applications, infrastructure, and processes that support the business activities of the organization.</li>
<li><strong>Confidentiality</strong>: Ensuring that information is accessible only to those authorized to have access.</li>
<li><strong>Integrity</strong>: Safeguarding the accuracy and completeness of information and processing methods.</li>
<li><strong>Availability</strong>: Ensuring that authorized users have access to information and associated assets when required.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Scope</h2>
<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, and any other individuals or entities using, accessing, or managing the company&#39;s information assets. It encompasses all systems, networks, and data, regardless of location.</p>
<h2>Information Security Objectives</h2>
<ol>
<li>Protect sensitive customer and proprietary data from unauthorized access and disclosure.</li>
<li>Maintain data integrity and ensure the reliability of information.</li>
<li>Ensure the availability of IT services and data to support business operations.</li>
<li>Comply with applicable laws, regulations, and standards, such as SOC 2, ISO/IEC 27001, and GDPR.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Roles and Responsibilities</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO)</strong>: Overall responsibility for the development, implementation, and enforcement of the Information Security Policy.</li>
<li><strong>IT Security Team</strong>: Responsible for maintaining the security infrastructure and responding to security incidents.</li>
<li><strong>Employees</strong>: Required to adhere to the security practices and report any security incidents or breaches.</li>
<li><strong>Management</strong>: Support and enforce security practices, ensuring adequate resources are available for security initiatives.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Policy Requirements/Statements</h2>
<ul>
<li>Implement and maintain an Information Security Management System (ISMS) in compliance with SOC 2 and ISO 27001 standards.</li>
<li>Conduct regular risk assessments to identify and mitigate security risks.</li>
<li>Implement access controls to ensure that information is available only to authorized personnel.</li>
<li>Encrypt sensitive data both in transit and at rest.</li>
<li>Regularly update and patch systems to protect against vulnerabilities.</li>
<li>Conduct regular security awareness training for all employees.</li>
<li>Monitor network and information systems for security breaches and respond promptly to incidents.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Key Requirements</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Access Control</strong>: Use role-based access control (RBAC) to manage permissions.</li>
<li><strong>Data Encryption</strong>: Use industry-standard encryption methods for data protection.</li>
<li><strong>Incident Response</strong>: Maintain an incident response plan to address potential security breaches effectively.</li>
<li><strong>Audit and Monitoring</strong>: Conduct regular audits of systems and processes to ensure compliance with security policies.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Compliance and Regulatory References</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>SOC 2</strong>: Service Organization Control 2 for service providers storing customer data in the cloud.</li>
<li><strong>ISO/IEC 27001</strong>: International standard for information security management.</li>
<li><strong>GDPR</strong>: General Data Protection Regulation for companies handling personal data of EU residents.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Enforcement/Violations</h2>
<p>Non-compliance with this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract. Violations may also lead to legal and financial penalties for the organization.</p>
<h2>Exceptions And Waivers</h2>
<p>Exceptions to this policy must be approved by the CISO. Waivers will be granted on a case-by-case basis and documented with a risk assessment and mitigation strategy.</p>
<h2>Review and Approval</h2>
<p>This policy will be reviewed annually and updated as necessary to ensure its effectiveness and compliance with regulatory requirements.</p>
<h2>Approval Signature</h2>
<pre><code>
</code></pre>
', 'approved', 2, '', '2026-01-28 07:38:27.682', '2026-01-23 00:33:00.697319', 'general', false, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1679, 3, 30, NULL, 'Capacity Management Policy', '# Intellfence Capacity Management Policy

## 1.0 Purpose

This policy establishes the mandatory, proactive framework for ensuring all Intellfence information technology resources possess sufficient, secure, and resilient capacity to meet current and future business and client service requirements. The policy mandates systematic planning, monitoring, analysis, and tuning to prevent service degradation, security compromise, or breach of contractual obligations due to resource exhaustion, ensuring the sustained confidentiality, integrity, and availability of our cybersecurity services and data. Adherence to this policy is critical for maintaining operational resilience, client trust, and compliance with relevant security standards.

-   The primary objective is to guarantee that IT capacity aligns with business objectives, supporting the reliable delivery of all cybersecurity services, including managed detection and response (MDR), threat intelligence platforms, and security consulting portals.
-   Capacity shall be managed proactively using data-driven forecasts and continuous monitoring to mitigate risks associated with denial-of-service conditions, performance bottlenecks, and the inability to scale during security incidents.
-   This policy enforces the integration of capacity considerations into all technology lifecycle processes, from architecture design and procurement to change management and service decommissioning.

## 2.0 Scope

This policy applies to all information technology resources owned, operated, leased, or managed by Intellfence that support business operations and client service delivery. It encompasses all environments, including those hosted by third-party cloud service providers.

-   All compute resources are in scope, including physical servers, virtual machines, container orchestration platforms (e.g., Kubernetes clusters), serverless functions, and endpoint analysis systems.
-   All storage resources are in scope, including block storage, file shares, databases (SQL/NoSQL), data lakes for threat intelligence, and backup/archive repositories.
-   All network resources are in scope, including internal bandwidth, internet ingress/egress, VPN concentrators, firewall throughput, and SD-WAN capacity.
-   All critical application platforms and cybersecurity service infrastructure are in scope, such as SIEM/SOAR platforms, vulnerability scanners, case management systems, and client-facing portals.
-   This policy applies to all personnel, including employees, contractors, and third-party service providers, involved in the design, provisioning, operation, or management of these resources.
-   The policy covers production, development, testing, staging, and disaster recovery environments.

## 3.0 Definitions

Standardized terminology is used throughout this policy to ensure clear communication and consistent implementation of capacity management activities.

-   **Business Capacity Management (BCM):** The strategic process of translating business plans, sales forecasts, and client onboarding pipelines into quantifiable, long-term IT service capacity requirements. It ensures future business needs are understood, funded, and provisioned.
-   **Service Capacity Management (SCM):** The tactical process focused on managing the performance and capacity of live, operational IT services (e.g., the Threat Intelligence Feed API), ensuring they consistently meet their agreed-upon Service Level Objectives (SLOs) and SLAs.
-   **Component Capacity Management (CCM):** The operational process focused on managing the performance, utilization, and capacity of individual technology components (e.g., CPU core utilization, memory consumption, disk I/O latency, network interface packet drops).
-   **Capacity Plan:** A formal, forward-looking document detailing current resource utilization, forecasting future demand based on business and threat landscape drivers, and specifying the initiatives, costs, and timelines required to ensure adequate, secure capacity.
-   **Proactive Warning Threshold:** A predefined utilization or performance level (e.g., 70% sustained CPU usage) which, when breached, triggers an automated alert to initiate planning and preventive action, avoiding critical impact.
-   **Critical Alert Threshold:** A predefined utilization or performance level (e.g., 90% sustained memory usage) which, when breached, triggers an urgent alert and may initiate immediate remedial or scaling actions to prevent service disruption.
-   **Baselining:** The process of establishing and documenting known performance and utilization standards under normal, peak, and attack-simulated operating conditions. These baselines serve as a benchmark for anomaly detection and forecasting.

## 4.0 Policy Statement

Intellfence shall implement and maintain a formal, documented, and proactive Capacity Management process as a cornerstone of its operational resilience and security posture. The process will be aligned with best practice frameworks including ITIL and ISO/IEC 27001:2022, specifically supporting control A.8.9 (Management of technical vulnerabilities) and A.12.1.3 (Capacity management). The organization is committed to ensuring IT service capacity aligns dynamically with business needs while incorporating threat-informed design principles to resist resource exhaustion attacks. Reactive, ad-hoc capacity management is strictly prohibited.

-   All capacity-related decisions must be data-driven, based on continuously monitored metrics, documented threat models, validated forecasts, and formally approved plans.
-   The Capacity Management process must be integrated with the Change Management, Incident Management, and Information Security Management System (ISMS) processes to ensure a holistic risk management approach.
-   Capacity for security monitoring and analytics infrastructure (e.g., log ingestion pipelines, correlation engines) will be prioritized to ensure no degradation of security visibility during peak loads or cyber incidents.

## 5.0 Policy Requirements

### 5.1 Formal Capacity Management Process

A documented Capacity Management process shall be established, maintained, and enforced, defining clear workflows, roles, and integration points with other IT Service Management (ITSM) processes.

-   The process documentation shall be maintained in the company''s ISMS repository and shall define procedures for demand forecasting, modeling, planning, implementation, and review.
-   The process must explicitly integrate BCM, SCM, and CCM activities, ensuring strategic, tactical, and operational alignment.
-   A designated Capacity Manager role is responsible for the day-to-day execution and evolution of this process.

### 5.2 Proactive Monitoring and Baselining

All in-scope IT resources shall be continuously monitored for key performance, capacity, and security-related metrics to establish intelligent baselines and detect anomalies.

-   Monitoring must capture metrics including, but not limited to: CPU utilization, memory consumption, disk I/O operations and latency, network throughput and error rates, database connection pools, application transaction volumes/rates, and log ingestion rates.
-   Historical baselines for normal business hours, peak usage periods, and simulated attack/load scenarios must be established, documented, and reviewed at least quarterly.
-   Monitoring tools must be configured to detect anomalous patterns that may indicate a security event (e.g., cryptojacking, DDoS, data exfiltration) masquerading as a capacity issue.

### 5.3 Threshold Management and Alerting

Proactive warning and critical alert thresholds shall be scientifically defined for all monitored metrics based on baselines, vendor recommendations, and service SLAs.

-   Thresholds must be documented in a central register and reviewed bi-annually or following significant architectural changes.
-   Threshold breaches must trigger automated, prioritized alerts to the IT Operations and Security Operations Center (SOC) teams via the approved centralized monitoring platform (e.g., Datadog, Splunk ITSI).
-   Escalation procedures for unacknowledged capacity alerts must be defined and integrated with the Incident Management process.

### 5.4 Regular Capacity Analysis and Reporting

Capacity and performance data shall be analyzed regularly to identify trends, forecast demand, and surface risks. Formal reporting is required to maintain governance and audit readiness.

-   Trend analysis and review of alert logs must be conducted weekly by the Operations team.
-   A formal Capacity Report must be produced and presented to IT leadership on a quarterly basis. This report must include:
    -   Current utilization trends against baselines and thresholds.
    -   Analysis of all threshold breaches and corrective actions taken.
    -   Forecasted business and threat-led demand for the next 12 months.
    -   Identification of potential capacity shortfalls, risks, and single points of failure.
    -   Recommendations for remedial actions, with preliminary cost estimates.

### 5.5 Annual Capacity Planning

A strategic Capacity Plan shall be developed annually to guide budgeting and procurement, ensuring future capacity needs are met in a timely and cost-effective manner.

-   The Capacity Manager is responsible for drafting the annual Capacity Plan, incorporating input from the quarterly reports, business forecasts, and product roadmaps.
-   The plan must forecast demand for a minimum 18-month horizon, accounting for organic growth, new client acquisitions, and planned product features.
-   The plan must specify required initiatives (e.g., hardware refresh, cloud commitment purchase, software license upgrade), including detailed technical specifications, implementation timelines, and a full cost-benefit analysis.
-   The final Capacity Plan requires formal approval by the Chief Information Officer (CIO) and alignment with the annual fiscal budget.

### 5.6 Change Integration

All proposed changes to IT services, applications, or infrastructure must undergo a capacity impact assessment to prevent unforeseen performance degradation or outages.

-   The Change Management process must require a capacity impact statement for all Standard and Major changes.
-   Changes assessed as having a "High" impact on capacity, or where the impact is "Unknown," must receive explicit approval from the Capacity Manager before proceeding to the Change Advisory Board (CAB).
-   Post-implementation reviews for major changes must include validation of capacity assumptions.

### 5.7 Performance and Load Testing

New or significantly modified business-critical applications, especially client-facing security services, must be validated for performance and scalability before production deployment.

-   Non-functional requirements (NFRs), including expected concurrent users, transaction volumes, and data processing rates, must be defined during the design phase.
-   Performance and load testing, simulating expected and peak loads (including simulated attack traffic), must be conducted in a staging environment that mirrors production specifications.
-   Test results must be documented, including breaking points and scaling characteristics, and must be reviewed and accepted by the Application Owner and Capacity Manager prior to production release.

### 5.8 Supplier and Cloud Capacity Management

For services dependent on third-party suppliers, Infrastructure-as-a-Service (IaaS), or Platform-as-a-Service (PaaS) providers, capacity obligations must be contractually defined and actively managed.

-   All relevant contracts and SLAs with cloud providers (e.g., AWS, Azure) and SaaS vendors must include explicit capacity, performance, and scalability commitments (e.g., API rate limits, throughput guarantees).
-   The Cloud/Infrastructure team is responsible for monitoring supplier performance against these commitments using provider-native tools (e.g., AWS CloudWatch, Azure Monitor).
-   Supplier performance must be a standing agenda item in quarterly business reviews (QBRs) with key technology vendors.

## 6.0 Compliance & Audit

Capacity Management activities shall provide documented evidence for demonstrating proactive control over resource availability, a fundamental aspect of security and business continuity required by multiple compliance frameworks.

-   The process must support compliance with SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Availability and Security, ISO 27001:2022 Annex A controls, and relevant data processing agreements (DPAs) that stipulate uptime and performance commitments.
-   All documentation, including the Capacity Plan, quarterly reports, threshold registers, baseline documents, and test results, must be readily available for internal and external audits.
-   The Capacity Manager is responsible for collaborating with the Compliance Officer to ensure the capacity management process meets all evidentiary requirements for targeted audits.

## 7.0 Roles and Responsibilities

Clear accountability is essential for the effective execution of this policy. The following roles are defined.

-   **Chief Information Officer (CIO):** Holds ultimate accountability for the effectiveness of the Capacity Management process. Responsible for approving the annual Capacity Plan, securing necessary budget, and ensuring organizational adherence to this policy.
-   **Capacity Manager (Designated Role):** Owns the day-to-day operation of the Capacity Management process. Duties include producing reports and plans, analyzing trends, chairing capacity review meetings, defining thresholds, and acting as the central point of contact for all capacity-related issues and impact assessments.
-   **IT/Cloud Operations Teams:** Responsible for implementing monitoring tooling, collecting and maintaining capacity data, responding to and resolving capacity alerts, and executing approved capacity-related changes (e.g., scaling cloud auto-scaling groups, applying upgrades).
-   **Application and Service Owners:** Responsible for providing accurate business demand forecasts (e.g., expected client growth), defining performance NFRs for their services, participating in capacity impact assessments, and validating that service capacity meets business and client requirements.
-   **Security Operations Center (SOC):** Responsible for monitoring capacity alerts for security anomalies, participating in threat-informed capacity planning for security tools, and declaring incidents if capacity issues are deemed to be security-related.
-   **Procurement/Finance Team:** Responsible for facilitating the procurement of hardware, software, or cloud resources as specified in the approved Capacity Plan and within the allocated budget.

## 8.0 Policy Enforcement

Compliance with this policy is mandatory. Violations undermine operational resilience and security, potentially leading to client impact and reputational damage.

-   Violations of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, in accordance with Intellfence''s HR policies.
-   The CIO and Information Security team are authorized to monitor compliance and enforce this policy.
-   Deliberate circumvention of capacity controls (e.g., disabling monitoring, ignoring procurement procedures), or willful neglect of capacity warnings that lead to a service outage or security incident, shall be treated as a serious operational and security breach.
-   All personnel are required to report suspected violations of this policy to their manager or through the company''s confidential reporting channel.

## 9.0 Review and Revision

This policy is a living document that must evolve with the business, technology landscape, and threat environment.

-   This Capacity Management Policy shall be reviewed by the CIO and Capacity Manager at least annually.
-   Ad-hoc reviews must be triggered following significant changes to the business model, technology architecture, regulatory landscape, or in response to a major capacity-related incident.
-   All revisions must be formally approved by the CIO. The approved version must be communicated to all relevant stakeholders, and archived versions maintained for audit purposes.
-   The policy document must clearly display the version number, revision date, and a summary of changes.', 'draft', 1, NULL, '2026-03-07 06:36:32.861409', '2026-03-07 06:36:32.861409', 'general', true, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1680, 3, 7, NULL, 'Access Control Policy', '# Intellfence Access Control Policy

## 1.0 Purpose

This policy establishes the mandatory framework for managing, controlling, and auditing logical and physical access to Intellfence''s information assets. It defines the principles, standards, and procedures to ensure that access is granted based on verified business need, the principle of least privilege, and strict accountability, thereby protecting the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information critical to our cybersecurity operations.

- The primary objective is to enforce a robust security perimeter and internal segmentation to protect client data, proprietary threat intelligence, source code, and critical security infrastructure from unauthorized access, misuse, or exfiltration.
- Access shall be granted strictly based on role, explicit authorization, and the need-to-know principle, with all activities logged and monitored to ensure non-repudiation and support forensic investigations.
- This policy is designed to meet the stringent requirements of our industry, ensuring compliance with relevant standards (ISO 27001, SOC 2, NIST CSF) and contractual obligations with our clients.

## 2.0 Scope

This policy applies to all information assets owned, operated, or managed by Intellfence, including all personnel and third parties who interact with these environments.

- This policy governs all logical and physical access to systems, networks, applications, databases, data repositories, source code, security tools, and corporate facilities.
- It applies to all personnel, including full-time and part-time employees, contractors, consultants, interns, temporary staff, and third-party service providers (e.g., cloud infrastructure, SaaS vendors with administrative access).
- Coverage includes on-premises infrastructure, cloud environments (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS), development and production systems, remote access solutions (VPN, VDI), and endpoints (corporate and BYOD under MDM policy).

## 3.0 Definitions

Clear definitions are essential for consistent policy interpretation and implementation across technical and business teams.

- **Access Control:** The comprehensive process of granting, denying, regulating, and revoking specific requests to: 1) obtain and use information and related information processing services; and 2) enter specific physical facilities or secure areas.
- **Least Privilege:** The foundational security principle mandating that all users, systems, and processes are granted the minimum levels of access, permissions, and capabilities necessary to perform their authorized, current job functions. No user shall have default access to sensitive data or administrative functions.
- **Privileged Access:** Any access that provides elevated capabilities beyond those of a standard user account. This includes, but is not limited to: system/network/database/cloud platform administration, security appliance management, application backend access, domain administrator rights, and access to cryptographic key material or sensitive customer data sets.
- **Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):** An authentication method that requires the user to present two or more distinct verification factors from the categories of knowledge (something you know), possession (something you have), and inherence (something you are). Time-based One-Time Passwords (TOTP) and FIDO2 security keys are the mandated standards.
- **Role-Based Access Control (RBAC):** The primary method for regulating access to resources based on the defined roles within Intellfence. Access rights are assigned to roles, and users are assigned to appropriate roles, simplifying management and ensuring consistency.

## 4.0 Policy Statement

Intellfence mandates that access to its information assets shall be strictly controlled and managed through a formal, risk-based, and auditable process. Access shall be granted solely based on the principles of least privilege, need-to-know, and explicit authorization from designated data owners.

- All access control activities, including provisioning, modification, and revocation, must be documented, monitored, and subject to periodic review to ensure ongoing appropriateness and compliance.
- The implementation of access controls must be defense-in-depth, incorporating strong authentication, network segmentation, and session monitoring, particularly for privileged and remote access.
- This policy is enforceable and violations will result in disciplinary action, as it is critical to maintaining client trust, protecting intellectual property, and fulfilling our legal and regulatory duties as a cybersecurity provider.

## 5.0 Access Control Requirements

### 5.1 Access Provisioning & Authorization

A formal, documented process must govern all access requests, ensuring authorization is obtained prior to granting any access rights. Provisioning must align with predefined roles and business justifications.

- All access requests must be submitted through the official ticketing system (Jira Service Management) and require formal approval from the relevant Data Owner or System Custodian, and the requester''s direct manager, prior to implementation by the IT/Security team.
- Access rights shall be provisioned based on predefined job roles within the RBAC model. Any request for permissions exceeding the baseline for a user''s role requires a separate, documented business justification and explicit approval from the CISO or delegated authority.
- A formal user access review for all systems handling Confidential or Restricted data (as per the Data Classification Policy), including cloud consoles, source code repositories, and customer environments, shall be conducted by data owners at least quarterly. Evidence of review and managerial attestation must be retained for a minimum of two years.

### 5.2 Authentication & Identity Management

Strong identity assurance is the cornerstone of effective access control. Each individual must have a unique, accountable identity, protected by robust authentication mechanisms.

- A unique, non-transferable user ID shall be assigned to each individual for full accountability. Shared or generic accounts for human users are strictly prohibited. Exceptions for system/service accounts require CISO approval, must have tightly controlled passwords managed via a PAM solution, and have enhanced logging.
- Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) is mandatory for: all remote network access (VPN, Citrix), all access to cloud administration consoles (AWS, Azure, GCP), all access to systems containing Confidential or Restricted data, and for all privileged account usage. Phishing-resistant MFA (FIDO2/WebAuthn) is required for administrative access to critical security infrastructure.
- Password complexity and management shall adhere to the separate Password Policy, requiring a minimum of 14 characters and use of a company-approved password manager. Password expiration is replaced with continuous monitoring for compromise, except where mandated by legacy system constraints.

### 5.3 Privileged Access Management (PAM)

Privileged accounts represent the highest risk and must be managed with extreme rigor, using specialized tools and processes to minimize standing privileges and monitor all activity.

- Privileged accounts (e.g., root, Administrator, Domain Admin, AWS Root) must be distinct from standard user accounts. Daily work must be performed using a standard user account. Privileged access is only for specific, authorized administrative tasks.
- Access to privileged accounts shall be brokered through the dedicated Privileged Access Management (PAM) solution. Credentials are vaulted, and sessions are launched through the PAM portal. All privileged sessions must be recorded (video/log), fully logged, and reviewed by the Security team on a weekly basis for anomalous activity.
- Just-in-Time (JIT) access provisioning is mandated for all elevated cloud permissions and critical on-prem systems. Privileges are granted for a pre-approved, limited time window (typically 2-8 hours) based on a ticketed request, after which they are automatically revoked.

### 5.4 Access Revocation & Modification

Access rights must be promptly modified or revoked in response to changes in employment status or job function to prevent unauthorized access by former personnel or insider threats.

- The Human Resources department must notify the IT and Security teams via automated integration (e.g., HRIS to IAM) within 4 business hours of any personnel status change: termination, resignation, role change, or extended leave. Access must be modified or revoked immediately upon notification.
- For terminations, all logical access (network, systems, applications, cloud) must be revoked simultaneously on the effective date. Physical access (keycards) must be deactivated concurrently. A checklist must be completed and signed off by HR, IT, and the departing employee''s manager.
- User accounts inactive for more than 45 days shall be automatically disabled. Accounts inactive for more than 90 days shall be permanently deleted, provided associated audit logs have been archived in accordance with the Data Retention Policy. Service accounts are exempt but require annual re-certification.

### 5.5 Logging, Monitoring & Audit

Comprehensive logging and proactive monitoring of access events are required to detect policy violations, malicious activity, and to provide an audit trail for investigations and compliance demonstrations.

- All authentication events (success/failure), privilege escalations (sudo, RunAs), access to sensitive data repositories, and configuration changes to access control systems must be logged. Logs must contain, at minimum: user ID, source IP address, timestamp (UTC), action performed, target resource, and outcome.
- Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) must centrally aggregate all access and authentication logs. Logs must be write-once, append-only, or otherwise protected from tampering and retained for a minimum of 365 days, with critical system logs retained for two years to meet potential regulatory and forensic requirements.
- The Security Operations Center (SOC) must configure automated correlation rules and alerts for anomalous access patterns, including: multiple failed logins followed by a success, access from geographically improbable locations, after-hours access by non-critical staff, bulk data download or export activities, and privileged account usage outside of approved maintenance windows.

## 6.0 Compliance & Standards Alignment

This policy is designed to provide a control framework that satisfies multiple international standards, regulatory requirements, and client security assessments.

- This policy directly maps to and satisfies control objectives from ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A controls, including A.5.15 (Access Control), A.5.16 (Identity Management), A.5.17 (Authentication Information), A.8.2 (Privileged Access Rights), A.8.3 (Information Access Restriction), and A.8.4 (Access to Source Code).
- It supports the SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security and Confidentiality, specifically the CC6 series (Logical and Physical Access Controls) by providing criteria for authorization, authentication, and monitoring.
- The policy enforces principles of data minimization and security of processing required under GDPR, and provides safeguards for any potentially applicable data under HIPAA or CCPA. Deviations from this policy require a formal risk assessment, documented business justification, and a time-bound exception approved by the CISO.

## 7.0 Roles & Responsibilities

Effective access control requires clear assignment of responsibilities across the organization to ensure accountability and proper execution of policy mandates.

- **Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):** Holds ultimate responsibility for the development, approval, dissemination, and maintenance of this policy. The CISO approves all major changes, policy exceptions, and oversees the overall effectiveness of the access control program.
- **Data Owners / System Custodians:** Business leaders responsible for classifying data and systems under their purview, authorizing access requests based on business need, and conducting quarterly access reviews. They define the appropriate access levels for their assets.
- **Information Technology & Security Teams:** Responsible for the technical implementation, configuration, and maintenance of all access control systems (IAM, PAM, SSO, MFA). They execute account provisioning/de-provisioning, generate access review reports, and manage the underlying directory services.
- **All Personnel:** Responsible for safeguarding their authentication credentials (e.g., not sharing MFA devices), using access rights only for authorized purposes, and immediately reporting any lost credentials, suspected policy violations, or signs of unauthorized access to the Security team.
- **Human Resources (HR):** Responsible for the timely and accurate communication of all personnel status changes (hire, transfer, promotion, termination) to the IT and Security teams via the established secure, automated feed to trigger access lifecycle events.

## 8.0 Policy Enforcement & Violations

Compliance with this policy is mandatory. Violations undermine the security posture of Intellfence and its obligations to clients, and will be addressed through formal disciplinary measures.

- Any employee or contractor found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, the severity of which will be commensurate with the violation. Actions may include formal warning, suspension of access privileges, mandatory re-training, or termination of employment/contract.
- Violations by third-party agents or service providers may result in contractual penalties, mandatory corrective action plans, or termination of the business relationship, and will be reported to the client if the violation impacts a client environment.
- The CISO or designated security lead is responsible for investigating suspected policy violations, in coordination with HR and Legal as necessary, to determine the root cause, impact, and appropriate corrective and disciplinary actions.

## 9.0 Review & Revision

This policy is a living document that must evolve to address emerging threats, technological changes, and evolving business and regulatory landscapes.

- This Access Control Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO, in consultation with key stakeholders, at least annually. Additional reviews will be triggered following a significant security incident, major changes in technology architecture, or updates to applicable laws, regulations, or industry standards.
- All proposed revisions must be documented with a change log, reviewed for impact and consistency, and formally approved by the CISO prior to publication.
- Upon approval, the revised policy shall be communicated to all personnel and relevant third parties. All personnel are required to acknowledge receipt and understanding of the policy upon hire and after any major update.', 'draft', 1, NULL, '2026-03-07 07:14:28.853663', '2026-03-07 07:14:28.853663', 'general', true, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1681, 3, 7, NULL, 'Access Control Policy', '# Intellfence Access Control Policy

## 1.0 Purpose

This policy establishes the mandatory framework for managing, controlling, and auditing logical and physical access to Intellfence''s information assets. It defines the principles, standards, and procedures to ensure that access is granted based on verified business need, the principle of least privilege, and strict accountability, thereby protecting the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information critical to our cybersecurity operations.

- The primary objective is to protect client data, proprietary threat intelligence, internal research, and operational systems from unauthorized access, modification, or disclosure.
- Access shall be governed by a formal, risk-based process that is documented, monitored, and auditable to ensure compliance with legal, regulatory (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), and contractual obligations (e.g., SOC 2, ISO 27001).
- This policy enforces security fundamentals essential for a cybersecurity firm, ensuring our internal controls meet or exceed the standards we advocate for our clients.

## 2.0 Scope

This policy applies to all information assets owned, operated, or managed by Intellfence, including all personnel and third parties who interact with these environments.

- All employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party service providers requiring access to Intellfence systems are bound by this policy.
- This encompasses all logical and physical assets: corporate networks, security operation centers (SOCs), development environments, cloud infrastructure (IaaS/PaaS/SaaS), endpoints, applications, databases, data repositories, and physical facilities.
