	(1610, 3, 8, NULL, 'Incident Response Policy', '<h1>Intellfence Incident Response Policy</h1>
<h2>1.0 Purpose</h2>
<p>This policy establishes the formal governance, structure, and mandatory principles for the Intellfence Incident Response Program (IRP). As a cybersecurity organization, our ability to effectively detect, respond to, and recover from security incidents is a core operational requirement and a fundamental component of our value proposition and contractual obligations. The purpose of this policy is to ensure a consistent, rapid, and legally defensible approach to managing information security incidents in order to:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Minimize Impact:</strong> Reduce operational disruption, financial loss, and reputational damage to Intellfence and its clients.</li>
<li><strong>Preserve Evidence:</strong> Maintain the integrity of digital evidence for forensic analysis, legal proceedings, or regulatory investigation.</li>
<li><strong>Ensure Compliance:</strong> Fulfill obligations under applicable laws, regulations, and standards (e.g., SOC 2, ISO 27001, GDPR, CCPA) and contractual service-level agreements.</li>
<li><strong>Facilitate Learning:</strong> Systematically analyze incidents to identify root causes and implement corrective actions to strengthen organizational resilience.</li>
<li><strong>Define Accountability:</strong> Establish unambiguous roles and responsibilities for all personnel involved in the incident response lifecycle.</li>
</ul>
<h2>2.0 Scope</h2>
<p>This policy applies universally within Intellfence and to all external parties interacting with its information environment. Specifically, it encompasses:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Personnel:</strong> All employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, and agency personnel.</li>
<li><strong>Systems &amp; Assets:</strong> All information assets, regardless of classification, including intellectual property, source code, client data, internal corporate data, and research. This includes all hardware, software, networks, cloud-based services (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS), and mobile devices owned, leased, or operated by Intellfence.</li>
<li><strong>Facilities:</strong> All physical locations occupied by Intellfence.</li>
<li><strong>Processes:</strong> All business processes that create, store, process, or transmit Intellfence or client information.</li>
<li><strong>Third Parties:</strong> Any vendor, supplier, or partner with access to Intellfence&#39;s information systems or data, who are contractually obligated to comply with the incident notification and response requirements defined in vendor risk management agreements.</li>
</ul>
<h2>3.0 Policy Statements</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Formal Program:</strong> Intellfence shall maintain a documented, tested, and continuously improved Incident Response Plan (IRP) that operationalizes this policy. The IRP is a confidential, controlled document.</li>
<li><strong>Mandatory Reporting:</strong> All personnel are required to immediately report any suspected or confirmed security incident through the designated channels. Failure to report an incident may constitute a policy violation.</li>
<li><strong>Management Commitment:</strong> Executive management, led by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO), is committed to providing the necessary authority, resources, and funding to support an effective incident response capability.</li>
<li><strong>Integrated Response:</strong> Incident response activities shall be coordinated across technical, legal, communications, and business functions to ensure a holistic and effective response.</li>
<li><strong>Continuous Improvement:</strong> A formal Post-Incident Review (PIR) process is mandatory for all confirmed security incidents. Lessons learned must be documented and used to update policies, procedures, and technical controls.</li>
<li><strong>Communication Protocol:</strong> External communications regarding security incidents must be managed strictly in accordance with the procedures defined in the IRP and authorized by the CISO and Legal Counsel.</li>
</ol>
<h2>4.0 Roles and Responsibilities</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th align="left">Role</th>
<th align="left">Primary Responsibilities</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td align="left"><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO)</strong></td>
<td align="left">Ultimate owner of this policy and the overall Incident Response Program. Declares a &quot;Major Incident.&quot; Authorizes escalation, significant containment actions, and external notifications. Chairs the Post-Incident Review. Final approver of the IRP and its updates.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td align="left"><strong>Incident Response Team (IRT) Lead</strong></td>
<td align="left">Designated senior security staff member who acts as the on-scene commander during an incident. Directs the technical response, manages the IRT, maintains the incident timeline, and serves as the primary point of contact for the CISO.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td align="left"><strong>Incident Response Team (IRT) Members</strong></td>
<td align="left">A cross-functional team with designated primary and backup members. Responsibilities are divided by function:<br>• <strong>Security/IT:</strong> Technical analysis, forensic investigation, containment, eradication, recovery, and log analysis.<br>• <strong>Legal Counsel:</strong> Guidance on regulatory notification requirements, liability, evidence preservation, and client contract obligations.<br>• <strong>Communications/PR:</strong> Drafting internal and external statements, managing stakeholder communications under the direction of the CISO and Legal Counsel.<br>• <strong>Business Unit Management:</strong> Providing business context, assessing operational impact, and coordinating customer-facing communications for their units.<br>• <strong>Human Resources:</strong> Involvement in incidents pertaining to personnel actions.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td align="left"><strong>All Personnel</strong></td>
<td align="left"><strong>Mandatory Duty:</strong> To immediately report any suspicious activity, policy violation, or potential security incident. Must cooperate fully with the IRT during an investigation, preserving any potential evidence as instructed.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td align="left"><strong>Executive Management Team</strong></td>
<td align="left">Provides strategic oversight, resolves resource conflicts, and supports the CISO in critical decision-making during major incidents. Responsible for championing the IRP within their respective divisions.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Primary Reporting Channel:</strong> <a href="mailto:security@intellfence.com">security@intellfence.com</a>. This mailbox is monitored 24/7/365.</p>
<h2>5.0 Incident Classification &amp; Severity Levels</h2>
<p>To ensure appropriate resource allocation and escalation, all incidents shall be classified according to the following severity matrix. Classification is initially performed by the IRT Lead and confirmed by the CISO.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th align="left">Severity Level</th>
<th align="left">Impact Criteria (CIA Triad)</th>
<th align="left">Examples</th>
<th align="left">Initial Response Time</th>
<th align="left">Notification Requirements</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td align="left"><strong>SEV-1: Critical</strong></td>
<td align="left"><strong>Severe</strong> impact on core business functions, client services, or data. Major regulatory or legal exposure. Significant reputational damage.</td>
<td align="left">• Confirmed large-scale data breach.<br>• Ransomware affecting critical production systems.<br>• Successful compromise of administrative accounts.<br>• Extended, widespread denial-of-service attack.</td>
<td align="left"><strong>Immediate:</strong> IRT activation within 30 minutes. 24/7 response.</td>
<td align="left">CISO &amp; Executive Team notified immediately. Legal &amp; Communications activated. Client/Regulator notifications per IRP.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td align="left"><strong>SEV-2: High</strong></td>
<td align="left"><strong>Significant</strong> impact on non-core systems or a limited set of clients. Moderate operational disruption. Potential regulatory scrutiny.</td>
<td align="left">• Isolated malware infection on corporate network.<br>• Unauthorized access to a sensitive internal system.<br>• Phishing campaign resulting in compromised credentials.<br>• Data leakage of internal non-public information.</td>
<td align="left"><strong>Urgent:</strong> IRT activation within 2 business hours.</td>
<td align="left">CISO notified within 1 hour. Legal consulted. Executive team briefed.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td align="left"><strong>SEV-3: Medium</strong></td>
<td align="left"><strong>Limited</strong> impact, contained to a single user or non-critical system. Minimal operational effect. Low regulatory risk.</td>
<td align="left">• Isolated virus detection on a single workstation.<br>• Policy violation (e.g., unauthorized software).<br>• Suspected phishing email reported.<br>• Critical patch missing per vulnerability scan.</td>
<td align="left"><strong>Priority:</strong> Investigation begins within 1 business day.</td>
<td align="left">IRT Lead manages. CISO receives daily briefing during active response.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td align="left"><strong>SEV-4: Low / Informational</strong></td>
<td align="left"><strong>Negligible or no</strong> impact. Often a false positive or reconnaissance activity.</td>
<td align="left">• Blocked intrusion attempt at the firewall.<br>• Spam email with no user interaction.<br>• Informational security alert requiring baseline review.</td>
<td align="left"><strong>Standard:</strong> Handled as part of regular security operations.</td>
<td align="left">Logged and tracked for trend analysis.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>6.0 Incident Response Lifecycle (Summary)</h2>
<p>Detailed tactical procedures are contained within the confidential Incident Response Plan (IRP). This policy mandates that the IRP follows a structured lifecycle, aligned with industry standards such as NIST SP 800-61.</p>
<h3>6.1 Preparation</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>IRP Maintenance:</strong> The IRP, including contact lists and communication templates, is reviewed and updated quarterly.</li>
<li><strong>Training &amp; Awareness:</strong> All employees receive annual incident response awareness training. The IRT participates in bi-annual tabletop exercises and annual simulation drills.</li>
<li><strong>Capability Building:</strong> Necessary technical controls are maintained to enable detection, analysis, and containment.</li>
</ul>
<h3>6.2 Identification &amp; Reporting</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Detection:</strong> Incidents are identified via automated monitoring, internal reports, or external notifications.</li>
<li><strong>Reporting Channel:</strong> Primary reporting must be directed to <a href="mailto:security@intellfence.com">security@intellfence.com</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Initial Analysis:</strong> The IRT Lead performs initial triage to validate the incident, determine its scope, and assign a preliminary severity level.</li>
</ul>
<h3>6.3 Containment</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Short-Term Containment:</strong> Immediate actions to halt the incident&#39;s spread while preserving evidence where feasible.</li>
<li><strong>Long-Term Containment:</strong> Applying more permanent fixes to allow restoration of services while ensuring threat removal.</li>
<li><strong>Forensic Preservation:</strong> A designated evidence custodian is responsible for creating forensically sound images and maintaining chain-of-custody.</li>
</ul>
<h3>6.4 Eradication &amp; Recovery</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Eradication:</strong> Identifying and removing the root cause of the incident from the environment.</li>
<li><strong>Recovery:</strong> Carefully restoring affected systems and services from clean backups or rebuilt images.</li>
<li><strong>Validation:</strong> Ensuring systems are fully functional and the threat is eradicated before declaring recovery complete.</li>
</ul>
<h3>6.5 Post-Incident Activity</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mandatory Post-Incident Review (PIR):</strong> Conducted within 14 calendar days of resolution for SEV-1/2 incidents, producing a confidential &quot;Lessons Learned&quot; report.</li>
<li><strong>PIR Report Contents:</strong> Includes detailed timeline, cost analysis, effectiveness of response, root cause, and a corrective action plan.</li>
<li><strong>Continuous Improvement:</strong> The CISO ensures the corrective action plan is implemented. Findings feed into updates to the IRP, policies, training, and controls.</li>
</ul>
<h2>7.0 Communication Plan</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Internal Communications:</strong> The IRT Lead provides regular situation reports to the CISO and pre-defined stakeholder groups. Communications must be factual and avoid speculation.</li>
<li><strong>External Communications:</strong><ul>
<li><strong>Clients:</strong> Notifications are managed per contractual obligations. All client communications require approval by Legal and Communications teams.</li>
<li><strong>Regulators/Law Enforcement:</strong> Led by Legal Counsel in coordination with the CISO.</li>
<li><strong>Media/Public:</strong> No employee is authorized to speak to the media. All public statements are the sole responsibility of the designated Communications lead, with approval from the CISO and Legal Counsel.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<h2>8.0 Exceptions</h2>
<p>Requests for a temporary exception to any provision of this policy must be submitted in writing to the CISO. The request must include a detailed business justification, specific policy controls, proposed duration, risk assessment, and description of compensating controls. The CISO may grant a time-bound, written exception. All exceptions are documented and reviewed quarterly.</p>
<h2>9.0 Enforcement</h2>
<p>Compliance with this policy is a condition of employment and engagement with Intellfence. Personnel who violate this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, proportionate to the severity of the violation, up to and including termination of employment or contract. Actions may also include legal prosecution where applicable. Enforcement will be administered fairly and in accordance with all applicable human resources policies and local labor laws.</p>
<h2>10.0 Definitions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Data Breach:</strong> A security incident that results in the confirmed or suspected unauthorized access, acquisition, disclosure, or loss of Sensitive or Confidential data.</li>
<li><strong>Containment:</strong> The phase of incident response aimed at limiting the scope and impact of an incident.</li>
<li><strong>Incident:</strong> An event that has the potential to, or has been confirmed to, compromise the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of Intellfence or client information assets.</li>
<li><strong>Incident Response Plan (IRP):</strong> The confidential, detailed procedural document that provides tactical guidance for responding to security incidents.</li>
<li><strong>Incident Response Team (IRT):</strong> The designated cross-functional team responsible for managing the response to a security incident.</li>
<li><strong>Post-Incident Review (PIR):</strong> A formal analysis conducted after an incident is resolved to identify lessons learned and drive improvements.</li>
</ul>
<h2>11.0 Revision History</h2>
<p>This policy is a living document and will be reviewed at least annually, or as necessitated by significant changes in the threat landscape, technology, or business operations. All revisions require formal approval by the CISO.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th align="left">Version</th>
<th align="left">Date</th>
<th align="left">Author</th>
<th align="left">Description of Change</th>
<th align="left">Approved By</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td align="left">1.0</td>
<td align="left">October 26, 2023</td>
<td align="left">CISO Office</td>
<td align="left">Initial Policy Creation</td>
<td align="left">Jane Doe, CISO</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td align="left">1.1</td>
<td align="left">May 15, 2024</td>
<td align="left">CISO Office</td>
<td align="left">Incorporated Revision History section; refined language for clarity and consistency.</td>
<td align="left">Jane Doe, CISO</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Policy Effective Date:</strong> May 15, 2024<br><strong>Next Scheduled Review Date:</strong> May 15, 2025</p>
', 'draft', 1, '', '2026-02-19 09:57:14.969087', '2026-02-19 09:57:14.969087', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1612, 3, 31, NULL, 'Network Security Policy', '<h1>Network Security Policy</h1>
<h2>1.0 Purpose</h2>
<p>The purpose of this Network Security Policy is to establish the comprehensive standards, architectural principles, and operational procedures required to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of Intellfence''s information assets and network infrastructure. As a cybersecurity company, our network is not only a business enabler but also a core component of our service delivery and a critical demonstration of our security posture to clients and auditors. This policy provides the authoritative framework for designing, implementing, and managing network security controls to mitigate risks, support compliance with relevant standards (including SOC 2, ISO 27001, and GDPR), and protect sensitive data, including client threat intelligence, proprietary research, and internal corporate information.</p>
<h2>2.0 Scope</h2>
<p>This policy applies universally to all individuals, systems, and data within the Intellfence environment. Specifically, it encompasses:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Personnel:</strong> All full-time and part-time employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, and any other third-party individuals granted access to Intellfence networks or data.</li>
<li><strong>Assets:</strong> All network infrastructure components, including but not limited to: routers, switches, firewalls, wireless access points, VPN concentrators, load balancers, network monitoring and intrusion detection/prevention systems (IDS/IPS), and associated management interfaces.</li>
<li><strong>Data:</strong> All data traversing or stored on Intellfence networks, including corporate data, intellectual property, client data (especially security-related data such as logs, indicators of compromise, and vulnerability assessments), and personal data.</li>
<li><strong>Environments:</strong> All company-owned or managed networks, including corporate headquarters, remote offices, cloud environments (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS), and development/test environments. This policy also governs secure access to these environments from remote locations.</li>
</ul>
<h2>3.0 Policy Statement</h2>
<p>Intellfence is committed to implementing and maintaining a defense-in-depth architecture for network security. Our approach is founded on the principles of least privilege, need-to-know, zero-trust networking (where applicable), and continuous monitoring. We mandate that all network architecture, configuration, and access must be designed and operated to proactively prevent unauthorized access, detect malicious activity, and ensure the resilient operation of business-critical services.</p>
<h2>4.0 Roles and Responsibilities</h2>
<p>Clear accountability is essential for effective policy implementation and governance.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Holds ultimate responsibility for the approval, oversight, and periodic review of this policy. The CISO authorizes major architectural changes and significant policy exceptions.</li>
<li><strong>Head of Infrastructure &amp; Security Engineering:</strong> Responsible for the operational implementation, maintenance, and technical enforcement of this policy. This includes designing secure network architectures, configuring security devices, and managing network security tools.</li>
<li><strong>Security Operations Center (SOC) / IT Team:</strong> Responsible for the day-to-day monitoring of network security controls, responding to alerts from IDS/IPS and other monitoring tools, conducting regular vulnerability scans of the network perimeter and internal segments, and executing incident response procedures related to network security events.</li>
<li><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to comply with all aspects of this policy. Personnel must use company networks only for authorized business purposes, report any suspected network security weaknesses or incidents immediately via the designated channel, and adhere to secure remote access procedures.</li>
<li><strong>Third-Party Vendors:</strong> Any third party requiring network access must comply with this policy as contractually stipulated. Access is granted on a minimal, time-bound basis and is subject to continuous review.</li>
</ul>
<h2>5.0 Network Architecture &amp; Segmentation Policy</h2>
<p>Intellfence''s network shall be logically segmented to isolate systems of differing sensitivity levels and functions, thereby limiting the potential blast radius of a security incident.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Core Principles:</strong> Network design shall follow a tiered model, typically separating the public-facing (DMZ), corporate user, internal application, and high-security zones (e.g., research and development, sensitive client data processing).</li>
<li><strong>Segmentation Enforcement:</strong> Segmentation must be enforced using stateful firewalls or next-generation firewalls (NGFWs) with application-aware rules. Micro-segmentation within cloud environments (e.g., AWS Security Groups, Azure NSGs) is mandated for all production workloads.</li>
<li><strong>Default Deny:</strong> All inter-zone traffic flows shall be denied by default. Permitted traffic must be explicitly defined by business-justified firewall rules adhering to the principle of least privilege, specifying source, destination, port, and protocol.</li>
<li><strong>Cloud Network Security:</strong> Virtual Private Clouds (VPCs) or Virtual Networks (VNets) must be designed with private subnets for sensitive workloads. Direct public internet exposure of backend systems or databases is strictly prohibited. All cloud-native network security features (flow logs, web application firewalls) must be enabled and monitored.</li>
</ul>
<h2>6.0 Secure Configuration &amp; Management</h2>
<p>All network devices must be hardened and managed securely to prevent unauthorized configuration changes.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Baseline Configurations:</strong> Standardized, hardened configuration templates (aligned with CIS Benchmarks) shall be developed and used for all network device classes (firewalls, switches, WAPs).</li>
<li><strong>Change Management:</strong> All changes to network device configurations, firewall rules, routing tables, and network topology must follow the formal Change Management Process. This includes pre-approval, business justification, peer review for security implications, back-out plans, and post-implementation verification.</li>
<li><strong>Management Access:</strong> Interactive management access to network devices (e.g., for SSH, HTTPS, console) shall be restricted to dedicated, isolated management networks or jump hosts. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for all administrative access. Telnet, HTTP, and SNMP v1/v2c are prohibited for management.</li>
<li><strong>Inventory &amp; Documentation:</strong> A definitive, updated inventory of all critical network assets, including ownership, purpose, and network location, must be maintained. Network diagrams reflecting current architecture and data flows must be kept up-to-date.</li>
</ul>
<h2>7.0 Perimeter Security &amp; Access Control</h2>
<p>The boundary between trusted internal networks and untrusted external networks (like the internet) must be rigorously defended.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Firewalls:</strong> A certified next-generation firewall (NGFW) shall guard all internet ingress and egress points. Firewall rules must be reviewed and pruned at least semi-annually to remove obsolete entries.</li>
<li><strong>Intrusion Prevention/Detection Systems (IPS/IDS):</strong> Network-based IPS shall be deployed at key network boundaries to inspect traffic and block known attacks. Signature and heuristic-based detection engines must be kept updated.</li>
<li><strong>Denial-of-Service (DoS) Protection:</strong> Measures to mitigate DoS and DDoS attacks, such as cloud-based protection services (e.g., AWS Shield, Cloudflare) or on-premise mitigation appliances, shall be employed for critical public-facing assets.</li>
<li><strong>Remote Access:</strong> All remote access to the Intellfence network must occur through the company-approved Virtual Private Network (VPN). The VPN must enforce strong authentication (MFA) and limit user access to only authorized internal resources. Split-tunneling is disabled by policy.</li>
</ul>
<h2>8.0 Monitoring, Logging, and Incident Response</h2>
<p>Continuous vigilance is required to detect and respond to network security events.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Centralized Logging:</strong> All network devices (firewalls, switches, proxies, VPNs) must forward their security-relevant logs (authentication attempts, configuration changes, connection denials, ACL hits) to the centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system in a standardized, timestamped format.</li>
<li><strong>Continuous Monitoring:</strong> The SOC shall monitor network traffic and security device alerts 24/7 for anomalies, policy violations, and indicators of compromise. Network traffic analysis (NTA) tools shall be used to establish baselines and detect suspicious lateral movement.</li>
<li><strong>Vulnerability Management:</strong> Regular, credentialed vulnerability scans of the network perimeter and internal IP ranges shall be conducted. Identified vulnerabilities are risk-ranked and must be remediated within defined timeframes based on severity (e.g., Critical: 7 days, High: 30 days).</li>
<li><strong>Incident Response:</strong> Any suspected network breach, malware activity, or policy violation must be reported immediately to the SOC. Response actions will follow the Intellfence Incident Response Plan, which includes containment, eradication, recovery, and post-incident review specific to network incidents.</li>
</ul>
<h2>9.0 Wireless Network Security</h2>
<p>Wireless networks present a significant attack vector and must be secured accordingly.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Corporate WiFi:</strong> The primary corporate wireless network shall use WPA3-Enterprise (or WPA2-Enterprise if WPA3 is unsupported) with authentication integrated into the corporate directory (e.g., RADIUS with 802.1X). Pre-shared keys (PSKs) are prohibited for corporate use.</li>
<li><strong>Guest Network:</strong> A separate, logically isolated guest network with a distinct SSID shall be provided for visitors and personal devices. Guest network traffic shall have no routing path to the internal corporate network and must be bandwidth-limited.</li>
<li><strong>Rogue AP Detection:</strong> Mechanisms shall be in place to periodically scan for and disable unauthorized (rogue) wireless access points within company facilities.</li>
</ul>
<h2>10.0 Exceptions</h2>
<p>In rare cases where a strict requirement of this policy cannot be met due to a compelling business or technical reason, an exception may be granted.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Process:</strong> Requests for exceptions must be submitted in writing to the CISO, detailing the specific policy clause, justification, duration, and associated risks.</li>
<li><strong>Approval:</strong> The CISO, in consultation with the Head of Infrastructure, must formally approve all exceptions.</li>
<li><strong>Compensating Controls:</strong> Approved exceptions require documented, equivalent compensating controls to mitigate the identified risk.</li>
<li><strong>Review:</strong> All exceptions are temporary and must be reviewed at least annually for possible remediation or renewal.</li>
</ul>
<h2>11.0 Enforcement</h2>
<p>Compliance with this policy is mandatory. Violations may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and may lead to legal action. The severity of the action will be commensurate with the nature and impact of the violation, as determined by Human Resources in conjunction with the CISO and Legal Counsel.</p>
<h2>12.0 Definitions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>DMZ (Demilitarized Zone):</strong> A physical or logical subnetwork that contains and exposes an organization''s external-facing services to an untrusted network, usually the internet.</li>
<li><strong>Next-Generation Firewall (NGFW):</strong> A deep-packet inspection firewall that moves beyond port/protocol inspection and blocking to add application-level inspection, intrusion prevention, and threat intelligence.</li>
<li><strong>Principle of Least Privilege:</strong> The security concept that a user, process, or system should only have the minimum level of access rights necessary to perform its intended function.</li>
<li><strong>SIEM (Security Information and Event Management):</strong> A solution that provides real-time analysis of security alerts generated by network hardware and applications.</li>
<li><strong>Zero-Trust Networking:</strong> A security model that requires strict identity verification for every person and device trying to access resources on a private network, regardless of whether they are sitting within or outside of the network perimeter.</li>
</ul>
<h2>13.0 References</h2>
<p>This policy supports and is informed by the following frameworks and internal documents:</p>
<ul>
<li>ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A.8 (Asset Management), A.9 (Access Control), A.13 (Communications Security)</li>
<li>NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) Core: Protect (PR.AC, PR.DS, PR.IP)</li>
<li>SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria: Security, Confidentiality</li>
<li>Intellfence Information Security Policy</li>
<li>Intellfence Incident Response Plan</li>
<li>Intellfence Acceptable Use Policy</li>
<li>Intellfence Change Management Policy</li>
</ul>
<h2>14.0 Revision History</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Version</th>
<th>Date</th>
<th>Author/Editor</th>
<th>Summary of Changes</th>
<th>Approved By</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>1.0</td>
<td>2023-10-26</td>
<td>CISO Office</td>
<td>Initial comprehensive policy creation.</td>
<td>CISO</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1.1</td>
<td>2024-04-15</td>
<td>Head of Infrastructure</td>
<td>Updated cloud security and monitoring sections; added explicit WPA3 requirement.</td>
<td>CISO</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>', 'draft', 1, '', '2026-02-19 10:11:05.852893', '2026-02-19 10:11:05.852893', 'general', false, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'pending', '2027-02-19 00:00:00', NULL),
	(1613, 3, 7, NULL, 'Access Control Policy', '<h1>Access Control Policy</h1>
<h2>1.0 Purpose and Objectives</h2>
<p>The purpose of this Access Control Policy is to establish a comprehensive framework for the secure and controlled access to Intellfence’s information assets, systems, networks, and physical facilities. As a cybersecurity company, Intellfence handles highly sensitive data, including proprietary threat intelligence, client security data, internal research, and critical infrastructure. This policy enforces the principles of least privilege, need-to-know, and segregation of duties to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability (CIA triad) of all assets. Its objectives are to prevent unauthorized access, ensure accountability for all actions, and provide a clear, auditable trail for compliance with industry standards, including SOC 2, ISO 27001, and relevant data protection regulations applicable in North America and the European Union.</p>
<h2>2.0 Scope</h2>
<p>This policy applies universally to all individuals and entities that interact with Intellfence’s information resources. This includes, but is not limited to:</p>
<ul>
<li>All full-time, part-time, and contract employees of Intellfence.</li>
<li>Third-party vendors, contractors, consultants, and business partners (collectively, &quot;Third Parties&quot;).</li>
<li>Temporary staff and interns.</li>
<li>Any other individual granted access to Intellfence systems, such as auditors or visitors.</li>
</ul>
<p>The policy covers all forms of access: logical access to networks, systems, applications, and data; physical access to offices, data centers, and secure areas; and remote access from any location. All information assets owned, leased, or managed by Intellfence fall within the scope of this policy.</p>
<h2>3.0 Terms and Definitions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Access Control:</strong> The process of granting or denying specific requests to: 1) obtain and use information and related information processing services; and 2) enter specific physical facilities.</li>
<li><strong>Principle of Least Privilege:</strong> A security concept in which a user is granted the minimum levels of access – or permissions – necessary to perform their job functions.</li>
<li><strong>Need-to-Know:</strong> A restriction of access to information which is not merely role-based but also contextual, ensuring individuals can access only the specific information necessary for a particular task.</li>
<li><strong>Segregation of Duties (SoD):</strong> The principle of splitting critical tasks and associated privileges among multiple users to prevent fraud, error, and misuse.</li>
<li><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> An authentication method that requires the user to provide two or more verification factors to gain access to a resource.</li>
<li><strong>Privileged Access:</strong> Elevated access rights that allow users to bypass standard security controls, typically granted to system administrators, network engineers, and senior developers. This includes root, administrator, and power user accounts.</li>
<li><strong>Information Asset:</strong> Any data, device, or other component of the environment that supports information-related activities, which can be assigned a value and is therefore worthy of protection.</li>
<li><strong>User Account:</strong> A unique digital identity established for an individual to access specific systems or applications.</li>
<li><strong>Service Account:</strong> A non-personal account used by an application, service, or system to interact with other systems or resources.</li>
<li><strong>JIT (Just-In-Time) Access:</strong> A privileged access management strategy where elevated privileges are granted for a limited time window only when explicitly needed for a task and are revoked immediately after.</li>
</ul>
<h2>4.0 Policy Requirements and Statements</h2>
<h3>4.1 User Access Management</h3>
<h4>4.1.1 User Registration and De-provisioning</h4>
<p>A formal user registration and de-registration process shall be established for granting and revoking access to all information systems and services.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Onboarding:</strong> Access rights shall be requested by the hiring manager via the formal Access Request Form, approved by the relevant system/data owner and the CISO or delegate. Access shall be provisioned based on the principle of least privilege, aligned with the individual’s role and responsibilities.</li>
<li><strong>Offboarding:</strong> Upon termination of employment or contract, all access rights for the individual shall be revoked immediately and synchronously. The People Operations team shall notify the IT/Security team of all terminations as part of a formal offboarding checklist. All company-owned assets must be returned, and access from personal devices must be remotely wiped or revoked.</li>
</ul>
<h4>4.1.2 Privilege Management</h4>
<p>The allocation and use of privileged access rights shall be restricted and controlled.</p>
<ul>
<li>Privileged accounts (e.g., domain administrators, cloud subscription owners, root accounts) shall not be used for daily operational tasks such as email or web browsing.</li>
<li>Administrative access shall require a separate, uniquely identifiable account (not a shared generic admin account).</li>
<li>Where technically feasible, Just-In-Time (JIT) access mechanisms shall be implemented for privileged task execution.</li>
<li>The use of all privileged accounts shall be logged and monitored with heightened scrutiny.</li>
</ul>
<h4>4.1.3 User Access Review</h4>
<p>Access rights shall be reviewed at regular intervals to ensure they remain appropriate.</p>
<ul>
<li>A formal quarterly access review shall be conducted for all critical systems (e.g., source code repositories, customer data platforms, security tools).</li>
<li>Managers and data owners shall be required to re-certify that their team members&#39; access is still required for business purposes.</li>
<li>Any access rights not re-certified shall be automatically flagged for revocation.</li>
<li>A comprehensive annual review shall encompass all user accounts and access privileges across the organization.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4.2 Authentication and Authorization</h3>
<h4>4.2.1 Authentication Controls</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Unique Identification:</strong> Every user shall have a unique user ID for personal accountability. Shared or generic accounts are prohibited for human users.</li>
<li><strong>Password Management:</strong> Strong passwords are mandatory. Where possible, systems shall enforce: a minimum of 12 characters; a mix of uppercase, lowercase, numbers, and symbols; and prevention of password reuse. A company-managed password vault shall be used for storing shared service account credentials.</li>
<li><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> MFA is mandatory for all access to corporate networks, cloud consoles (e.g., AWS, Azure, GCP), source code management systems (e.g., GitHub, GitLab), administrative interfaces, and any system containing sensitive or regulated data. MFA shall also be required for all remote access via VPN.</li>
</ul>
<h4>4.2.2 Authorization Controls</h4>
<ul>
<li>Access to information and system functions shall be controlled in accordance with the defined access control policy (this document).</li>
<li>Application-level and menu-level permissions shall be configured to enforce need-to-know and least privilege.</li>
<li>Default access for any new system or resource shall be &quot;deny all.&quot;</li>
</ul>
<h3>4.3 Network and System Access Control</h3>
<h4>4.3.1 Network Security</h4>
<ul>
<li>A formal process shall be in place to control the allocation of network access rights to users, devices, and services.</li>
<li>Network segmentation shall be implemented to isolate development, production, and corporate environments. Critical assets, such as security operations centers (SOC) infrastructure and sensitive research data, shall be placed in further segmented, highly restricted network zones.</li>
<li>Firewall and router configurations shall be reviewed and documented at least semi-annually.</li>
</ul>
<h4>4.3.2 Operating System Access Control</h4>
<ul>
<li>Secure login procedures shall be implemented on all servers and user workstations, including automatic session locking after 15 minutes of inactivity.</li>
<li>Access to utility programs that can override system and application controls (e.g., debuggers, system management tools) shall be severely restricted and logged.</li>
</ul>
<h4>4.3.3 Application and Information Access</h4>
<ul>
<li>Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) models shall be designed and implemented for critical business applications.</li>
<li>Sensitive information, such as source code, threat intelligence feeds, and client vulnerability data, shall be classified and protected with appropriate access controls (e.g., encryption at rest, strict folder permissions).</li>
</ul>
<h3>4.4 Remote Access and Mobile Computing</h3>
<ul>
<li>All remote access to Intellfence networks and systems shall occur through approved, secure Virtual Private Network (VPN) solutions with mandatory MFA.</li>
<li>Company data accessed or stored on mobile devices (laptops, smartphones, tablets) must be encrypted. A Mobile Device Management (MDM) solution shall be used to enforce security policies, including the ability to remotely wipe lost or stolen devices.</li>
<li>The use of public Wi-Fi for accessing sensitive systems or data is discouraged and, if necessary, must be done through the corporate VPN.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4.5 Physical Access Control</h3>
<ul>
<li>Physical access to Intellfence offices, server rooms, and other secure areas shall be controlled using electronic key card systems or biometrics.</li>
<li>Access logs from physical security systems shall be retained for a minimum of 90 days.</li>
<li>Visitors must be logged, escorted at all times, and wear visible identification badges.</li>
<li>Clean desk and clear screen policies shall be enforced to protect sensitive information from unauthorized physical viewing.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4.6 Audit Logging and Monitoring</h3>
<ul>
<li>All significant security events, particularly those related to authentication, authorization, and privileged access, shall be logged. Logs must include user ID, timestamp, action performed, and success/failure status.</li>
<li>Logs shall be centralized in a Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system, protected from tampering, and retained for a minimum of one year to support forensic investigation and compliance audits.</li>
<li>Proactive monitoring and alerting shall be configured for anomalous access patterns or policy violations.</li>
</ul>
<h2>5.0 Exceptions</h2>
<p>Exceptions to this policy are strongly discouraged and will only be granted under compelling business or technical circumstances where no reasonable alternative exists.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Request Process:</strong> All exception requests must be submitted in writing to the CISO using the official Policy Exception Request Form. The request must include:<ul>
<li>A detailed justification for the exception.</li>
<li>The specific policy sections from which an exception is sought.</li>
<li>A comprehensive risk assessment detailing the residual risk and proposed compensating controls.</li>
<li>A defined expiration date for the exception, not to exceed one year.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Approval Authority:</strong> The CISO is the sole approval authority for all policy exceptions. Approval will be based on business necessity, risk evaluation, and the adequacy of proposed compensating controls.</li>
<li><strong>Documentation and Review:</strong> All approved exceptions shall be formally documented, tracked in a central register, and subject to review at least every six months or upon the expiration date, whichever comes first. The CISO reserves the right to revoke an exception at any time based on changes in risk posture or business conditions.</li>
<li><strong>Automatic Expiration:</strong> Exceptions automatically expire on the date specified in the approval. Continued need requires a new exception request.</li>
</ol>
<h2>6.0 Roles and Responsibilities</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate owner of this policy; responsible for its development, approval, dissemination, periodic review, and the approval of any exceptions.</li>
<li><strong>IT/Security Team:</strong> Responsible for the technical implementation, enforcement, and daily operation of access controls. This includes user provisioning/de-provisioning, managing authentication systems, monitoring access logs, and implementing compensating controls for approved exceptions.</li>
<li><strong>People Operations:</strong> Responsible for timely notification of employee status changes (hires, transfers, terminations) to the IT/Security team.</li>
<li><strong>Department Heads / Data Owners:</strong> Responsible for defining access requirements for their teams and data, participating in periodic access reviews to certify ongoing need, and ensuring their teams comply with this policy.</li>
<li><strong>All Users:</strong> Responsible for understanding and complying with this policy, safeguarding their authentication credentials, and reporting any suspected policy violations or security incidents immediately.</li>
</ul>
<h2>7.0 Enforcement and Violations</h2>
<p>Compliance with this Access Control Policy is mandatory for all individuals within its scope. Violations of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and may carry legal or financial consequences.</p>
<ul>
<li>Suspected violations shall be reported to the CISO or via the established incident reporting channel.</li>
<li>All incidents will be investigated promptly and thoroughly.</li>
<li>Disciplinary actions will be determined based on the severity and intent of the violation, in consultation with Human Resources and Legal departments.</li>
</ul>
<h2>8.0 Policy Review and Maintenance</h2>
<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in business operations, or updates to relevant laws and standards. All revisions require approval by the CISO. The latest version of this policy shall be made readily available to all personnel through the company’s internal policy repository.</p>
<p><strong>Approval and Effective Date:</strong></p>
<p>This policy is effective immediately upon publication.</p>
<p><strong>Approved By:</strong> [Signature of CISO]
<strong>Title:</strong> Chief Information Security Officer
<strong>Date:</strong> October 26, 2023</p>
', 'approved', 3, 'Charlie Analyst', '2026-02-19 10:19:00.603', '2026-02-19 10:12:34.893507', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1614, 3, 28, NULL, 'Backup and Recovery Policy', '<h1>Backup and Recovery Policy</h1>
<h2>1.0 Purpose and Objectives</h2>
<p>The purpose of this Backup and Recovery Policy is to establish a comprehensive, risk-based framework for the creation, storage, testing, and restoration of Intellfence’s critical information assets. As a cybersecurity organization, our intellectual property, client data, and operational integrity are paramount. This policy ensures business continuity, minimizes data loss, and supports compliance with regulatory and contractual obligations, including but not limited to SOC 2, ISO 27001, and relevant data protection frameworks (e.g., GDPR). The primary objectives are to define roles, mandate procedures, and enforce controls that guarantee the availability and integrity of data in the event of accidental deletion, corruption, system failure, or a cybersecurity incident such as ransomware.</p>
<h2>2.0 Scope</h2>
<p>This policy applies universally across the Intellfence environment. It encompasses all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any third-party service providers who are authorized to access, manage, or process Intellfence data or information systems. The policy covers all company data, classified according to the Data Classification Policy, including but not limited to: source code for proprietary cybersecurity tools, threat intelligence databases, internal research, client project data (under NDA), financial records, human resources information, and all configuration data for critical infrastructure (e.g., firewalls, SIEM platforms, development environments). This includes data residing on-premises, in company-managed cloud infrastructure (IaaS/PaaS), and within sanctioned Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) applications.</p>
<h2>3.0 Policy Statements</h2>
<h3>3.1 Commitment to Data Resilience</h3>
<p>Intellfence is committed to implementing and maintaining a robust, defense-in-depth backup and recovery strategy. This strategy is integral to our overall cybersecurity posture and risk management program, designed to protect against data loss scenarios and ensure rapid restoration of services.</p>
<h3>3.2 Data Classification and Backup Prioritization</h3>
<p>All data must be classified according to the Data Classification Policy. Backup frequency, retention periods, and recovery point objectives (RPOs) are directly determined by this classification. For example, critical data such as active client engagement data and proprietary algorithm source code will have more frequent backups and shorter RPOs compared to archival or publicly available information.</p>
<h3>3.3 The 3-2-1 Backup Rule</h3>
<p>Intellfence mandates adherence to the 3-2-1 backup rule as a minimum standard for all critical and sensitive data:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>3</strong> copies of data: The primary production data plus at least two backup copies.</li>
<li><strong>2</strong> different media types: To avoid common failure modes (e.g., both copies on the same SAN). This typically means one copy on fast, local disk for quick recovery and one copy on a separate, immutable medium.</li>
<li><strong>1</strong> copy stored offsite and offline/immutable: A geographically separate copy must be maintained. For the highest classification tiers, one copy must be stored in an immutable or air-gapped state to protect against ransomware or malicious deletion.</li>
</ul>
<h3>3.4 Encryption of Backup Data</h3>
<p>All backup data, both in transit and at rest, must be encrypted using strong, industry-standard cryptographic algorithms (e.g., AES-256). Encryption keys for backup data must be managed separately from the backup storage location and in accordance with the Cryptographic Controls Policy.</p>
<h3>3.5 Regular Testing and Validation</h3>
<p>Backup integrity is meaningless without proven recoverability. Scheduled recovery tests must be conducted quarterly for critical systems and annually for all other in-scope systems. Tests must validate both file-level and full-system recovery capabilities, with results documented, including success/failure metrics and time-to-recovery.</p>
<h3>3.6 Roles and Responsibilities</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the policy, its approval, and its alignment with organizational risk tolerance.</li>
<li><strong>IT/SysOps Team:</strong> Responsible for the daily implementation, execution, monitoring, and technical execution of backup procedures and recovery operations.</li>
<li><strong>Data Owners (e.g., Department Heads):</strong> Responsible for identifying critical data assets within their domain and defining the required RPO and RTO in consultation with the CISO.</li>
<li><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to store all business-related data on approved, centrally managed systems that are covered by this backup policy. Local storage on individual workstations (e.g., C: drives) is not protected by enterprise backups.</li>
</ul>
<h2>4.0 Detailed Backup Procedures</h2>
<h3>4.1 Backup Frequency and Retention</h3>
<p>Backup schedules are defined by data criticality and are documented in the Backup Schedule Register maintained by the IT team.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tier 1 (Critical - e.g., Production Databases, Source Code Repositories):</strong> Incremental backups nightly, with full weekly backups. Retention: 90 days for daily/weeklies, with monthly archives retained for 12 months.</li>
<li><strong>Tier 2 (Important - e.g., Internal Systems, Email, Collaboration Tools):</strong> Full backups weekly. Retention: 60 days.</li>
<li><strong>Tier 3 (General - e.g., Archived Project Data):</strong> Full backups monthly. Retention: 12 months.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4.2 Backup Storage and Security</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Onsite/Online Storage:</strong> Used for operational recovery, providing fast RTO. Access is strictly limited to authorized backup administrators.</li>
<li><strong>Offsite/Immutable Storage:</strong> Backups are replicated to a geographically separate cloud storage provider configured with object lock or immutable storage features to prevent alteration or deletion for a defined retention period.</li>
<li><strong>Logical Separation:</strong> Backup administration credentials are distinct from production system credentials and are subject to strict privilege access management (PAM) controls.</li>
<li><strong>Monitoring and Alerting:</strong> All backup jobs are monitored. Failure alerts are generated immediately and sent to the IT/SysOps team and the CISO for critical systems.</li>
</ul>
<h2>5.0 Detailed Recovery Procedures</h2>
<h3>5.1 Recovery Request and Authorization</h3>
<p>All recovery operations, except those performed as part of a scheduled test, must be initiated via a formal ticket in the ITSM system. The request must be authorized by the data owner or their delegate, and by the CISO for significant or security-related incidents.</p>
<h3>5.2 Recovery Types and Objectives</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Recovery Point Objective (RPO):</strong> The maximum tolerable period of data loss. For Tier 1 systems, the RPO is ≤ 24 hours.</li>
<li><strong>Recovery Time Objective (RTO):</strong> The maximum tolerable downtime for a service. For Tier 1 systems, the RTO is ≤ 4 hours for critical functions.</li>
<li><strong>Recovery Procedures:</strong> Step-by-step recovery runbooks are maintained for all critical systems, detailing the process for file-level, database, and full-system restoration from both online and offline backup media.</li>
</ul>
<h3>5.3 Post-Recovery Validation</h3>
<p>Following any recovery operation, the requesting user or data owner must validate the integrity and completeness of the restored data. This validation is documented within the recovery ticket before the ticket is closed.</p>
<h2>6.0 Policy Review and Maintenance</h2>
<p>This policy shall be reviewed annually by the CISO or following any significant security incident, technological change, or business process change that impacts data resilience. Updates will be communicated to all relevant personnel.</p>
<h2>7.0 Enforcement and Violations</h2>
<p>Adherence to this policy is a condition of employment and for third-party relationships. Violations of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and may lead to legal action. Examples of violations include: deliberately storing critical business data on unapproved, non-backed-up storage; attempting to disable or interfere with backup processes; or performing unauthorized recovery operations.</p>
', 'draft', 1, 'Charlie Analyst', '2026-02-19 18:30:46.803479', '2026-02-19 18:30:46.803479', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', '2027-02-19 00:00:00', NULL),
	(1615, 3, 52, NULL, 'Shadow IT Policy', '<h1>Shadow IT Policy</h1>
<h2>1.0 Purpose and Objectives</h2>
<p>This Shadow IT Policy establishes the mandatory governance, risk management, and compliance framework for the acquisition, deployment, integration, and use of all information technology (IT) resources within Intellfence. As a cybersecurity enterprise, our credibility, operational integrity, and contractual obligations are intrinsically tied to the security and compliance of our technology ecosystem. The purpose of this policy is to proactively mitigate the significant and often cascading risks—including information security breaches, data privacy violations, intellectual property theft, operational instability, and financial loss—associated with unauthorized or unmanaged technology.</p>
<p>The primary objectives are to ensure all technology used for business purposes is aligned with corporate security architecture, complies with relevant regulatory frameworks (including GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, and potential client-mandated standards like NIST CSF or CIS Controls), and is integrated into our formal Information Security Management System (ISMS). This policy is designed to support, not hinder, innovation by providing a clear, secure, and accountable pathway for adopting new technologies.</p>
<h2>2.0 Scope</h2>
<p>This policy applies universally and without exception to all individuals and entities that interact with Intellfence''s information assets. This includes, but is not limited to: all full-time and part-time employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, and authorized third-party partners. The policy governs all technology, irrespective of ownership (corporate or personal), funding source, or deployment model, that meets any of the following criteria:</p>
<ul>
<li>Stores, processes, or transmits any Intellfence data (including data classified as Public, Internal, Confidential, or Restricted).</li>
<li>Connects to any Intellfence network, whether on-premises, virtual private cloud (VPC), or via corporate VPN.</li>
<li>Is used to develop, test, or deliver Intellfence services, products, or intellectual property.</li>
<li>Could impact the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of Intellfence''s business operations.</li>
</ul>
<p>Governed technology explicitly includes: Software-as-a-Service (SaaS), Infrastructure-as-a-Service (IaaS), Platform-as-a-Service (PaaS), desktop/mobile applications, open-source libraries integrated into products, unsanctioned collaboration/communication tools, personal hardware (BYOD) used for work purposes, and Internet of Things (IoT) devices within corporate facilities.</p>
<h2>3.0 Definitions</h2>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Shadow IT:</strong> Any information technology system, solution, service, hardware, or software component deployed and used for business purposes without explicit, documented approval from the designated IT governance and security authority (as defined in Section 7.0). This includes using personal subscriptions for business tasks, deploying unauthorized cloud instances, installing unvetted software on corporate endpoints, or using non-standard communication channels for sensitive discussions.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Sanctioned IT:</strong> Technology solutions that have successfully undergone and passed the formal procurement, security, architectural, and legal review process as defined by the IT and Security Departments. These assets are registered in the corporate IT Asset Management (ITAM) system, are fully supported, and are integrated into security monitoring and backup regimes.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Cloud Service:</strong> Any on-demand, internet-accessible IT resource provisioned and managed by a third-party vendor. This encompasses SaaS (e.g., CRM, project management), IaaS (e.g., virtual servers, storage), PaaS (e.g., application development platforms), and managed security services.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Authorizing Official:</strong> The designated individual or body with delegated authority from executive management to approve or deny the use of a technology solution based on a comprehensive risk assessment. For most requests, this is the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) or their designee. For strategic or high-impact solutions, the IT Governance Board may serve as the Authorizing Official.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Data Owner:</strong> The business unit leader or department head who has ultimate responsibility for the classification, integrity, protection, and lifecycle management of a specific data set. The Data Owner is a key stakeholder in the risk acceptance process for any technology that will process their data.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Formal Technology Request (FTR):</strong> The standardized process and required documentation for requesting approval of any new technology, initiated via the official IT Service Management (ITSM) portal.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h2>4.0 Policy Statement</h2>
<p>Intellfence strictly prohibits the use of Shadow IT. The unauthorized acquisition, configuration, subscription to, installation, or connection of any hardware, software, cloud service, or application to corporate networks, endpoints, or for the processing, storage, or transmission of corporate data constitutes a direct violation of this policy.</p>
<p>All technology intended for business use must be procured, implemented, and managed exclusively through the formal IT governance and procurement channels outlined herein. This mandate is critical for maintaining our security posture, ensuring compliance with client agreements and regulations, and preserving the integrity of our cybersecurity service offerings.</p>
<h2>5.0 Detailed Policy Requirements &amp; Controls</h2>
<h3>5.1 Mandatory Pre-Approval Process</h3>
<p>No technology, including free trials, proof-of-concept deployments, or open-source tools intended for production use, may be procured, subscribed to, installed, or configured prior to receiving written approval from the Authorizing Official. All requests must originate as a Formal Technology Request (FTR) within the official ITSM portal. The request must include a clear business justification, identified Data Owner, expected data classification involved, and any known integration points with existing sanctioned systems.</p>
<h3>5.2 Formal Security, Architecture, and Compliance Assessment</h3>
<p>All proposed technology must undergo a rigorous, multi-faceted review prior to approval. The IT and Security Departments are responsible for conducting this assessment, which shall include, at a minimum:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Vendor Risk Assessment (VRA):</strong> Evaluation of the provider''s security posture, including review of independent audit reports (SOC 2 Type II, ISO 27001), penetration test results, and their information security policies.</li>
<li><strong>Data Privacy Impact Assessment (DPIA):</strong> Analysis of data processing activities, data residency requirements, sub-processor dependencies, and alignment with GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, and other applicable privacy laws.</li>
<li><strong>Technical Security Review:</strong> Evaluation of authentication mechanisms (SSO/SAML 2.0/OIDC mandatory where possible), encryption standards for data at rest (AES-256 minimum) and in transit (TLS 1.2+), logging and audit capabilities, and secure API design.</li>
<li><strong>Architectural Fit Review:</strong> Assessment of the solution''s alignment with Intellfence''s cloud security architecture, network segmentation model, and identity and access management (IAM) framework.</li>
</ul>
<h3>5.3 Centralized Inventory and Continuous Discovery</h3>
<p>The IT Department shall maintain a definitive, accurate, and current Configuration Management Database (CMDB) or IT Asset Management (ITAM) system for all sanctioned IT assets. This inventory must include asset owner, department, data classification, vendor contract details, security configuration status, and end-of-life/end-of-support dates. Furthermore, the use of automated Cloud Access Security Broker (CASB) tools, network traffic analysis (NTA), and agent-based discovery on endpoints is mandated to continuously monitor for and identify unauthorized assets and services.</p>
<h3>5.4 Technical Control Enforcement</h3>
<p>Technical controls shall be implemented to provide defense-in-depth against Shadow IT proliferation:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Network Enforcement:</strong> Next-generation firewall (NGFW) and Secure Web Gateway (SWG) policies must be configured to block access to known high-risk and unauthorized SaaS/cloud application categories. Network segmentation shall limit lateral movement from potentially compromised unauthorized services.</li>
<li><strong>Endpoint Enforcement:</strong> Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) or unified endpoint management (UEM) agents on all corporate-managed devices must enforce application allow-listing or block-listing policies. Local administrator privileges required for software installation are strictly prohibited for standard users.</li>
<li><strong>Identity-Centric Controls:</strong> The principle of least privilege shall be enforced through the corporate Identity Provider (IdP). Where possible, access to sanctioned SaaS applications will be brokered exclusively through the IdP to centralize authentication and de-provisioning.</li>
</ul>
<h3>5.5 Data Exfiltration Prevention</h3>
<p>The use of unsanctioned file-sharing, collaboration, communication, or cloud storage services (e.g., personal Google Drive, Dropbox, WhatsApp, or personal email) for transmitting, storing, or processing Intellfence Confidential or Restricted data is expressly forbidden. Data Loss Prevention (DLP) tools, integrated with email, web gateways, and endpoint agents, shall be configured to monitor, alert on, and block unauthorized transfers of sensitive data based on predefined classification labels and policies.</p>
<h3>5.6 Exception Handling and Risk Acceptance</h3>
<p>Time-bound exceptions to this policy for legitimate, urgent business needs may be granted only by the CISO or designated Authorizing Official. The exception process is not a bypass but a formal risk management procedure:</p>
<ol>
<li>A formal exception request must be submitted via the ITSM portal.</li>
<li>The request must include a compelling business case, proposed mitigation controls, and a maximum expiration date (not to exceed 90 days without executive review).</li>
<li>A formal risk acceptance form must be completed and signed by the relevant Data Owner and the requesting department head, acknowledging the residual risks.</li>
<li>All exceptions are logged in a central register reviewed quarterly by the CISO. Prior to expiration, the technology must be either fully decommissioned and all data migrated, or it must enter the formal sanctioning process.</li>
</ol>
<h2>6.0 Compliance &amp; Legal Obligations</h2>
<p>All technology used at Intellfence must comply with applicable laws, regulations, industry standards, and client contractual obligations. These may include, but are not limited to, the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) as amended by CPRA, and contractual commitments requiring adherence to frameworks like ISO 27001, SOC 2, NIST SP 800-53, or the CIS Critical Security Controls.</p>
<p>The use of unvetted Shadow IT that leads to a data breach, compliance violation, or client data exposure may result in severe financial penalties, loss of certification, reputational damage, and legal liability. Vendor agreements for all sanctioned IT must include robust security and compliance clauses, such as guarantees of data processing compliance, mandatory security incident notification within 72 hours, right-to-audit clauses, and clear data return/destruction procedures upon contract termination.</p>
<h2>7.0 Roles and Responsibilities</h2>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Are responsible for using only sanctioned IT for business activities, for understanding the data classification of information they handle, for reporting suspected instances of Shadow IT to the IT Service Desk or via the anonymous reporting channel, and for submitting all technology requests through the official FTR process.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Department Heads / Data Owners:</strong> Are accountable for the enforcement of this policy within their teams. They must promote a culture of security, authorize the business need for technology requests, and are the final authority for signing risk acceptance forms for technologies that will process their department''s data.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Is responsible for operating and maintaining the FTR and exception processes, conducting technical and architectural reviews, managing the CMDB/ITAM system, implementing and monitoring technical enforcement controls, and providing secure, sanctioned alternatives to common Shadow IT use cases.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Has ultimate authority for the enforcement, interpretation, and exception approval of this policy. The CISO is responsible for defining the security assessment criteria, reporting quarterly to executive management on Shadow IT risks and compliance metrics, and ensuring the policy aligns with the ISMS and audit requirements.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>IT Governance Board:</strong> Provides strategic oversight, reviews high-impact or high-cost technology requests, and resolves disputes regarding technology approval. The board includes the CISO, Head of IT, and relevant business executives.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h2>8.0 Policy Enforcement and Violations</h2>
<p>Compliance with this policy is mandatory. Violations will be treated seriously and may result in disciplinary action proportionate to the severity and impact of the violation, up to and including termination of employment or contract.</p>
<p>The IT and Security Departments are authorized and obligated to take immediate action to contain any risk posed by Shadow IT. This includes, but is not limited to: technically blocking network access to unauthorized services, remotely uninstalling unauthorized software from corporate endpoints, disabling user accounts involved in policy violations, and quarantining affected systems.</p>
<p>Individuals who knowingly violate this policy may be held personally accountable for any direct damages, regulatory fines, incident response costs, or client penalties incurred by Intellfence as a result of their actions.</p>
<h2>9.0 Policy Review and Maintenance</h2>
<p>This policy shall be reviewed and validated by the CISO at least annually. Additional reviews will be triggered by significant security incidents, material changes in the regulatory landscape, major shifts in business operations, or updates to the corporate ISMS. All revisions must be approved by the CISO and communicated to all personnel. The effectiveness of this policy and its associated controls will be measured through key risk indicators (KRIs) and assessed during internal and external audits for ISO 27001, SOC 2, and other relevant frameworks.</p>
', 'draft', 1, '', '2026-02-19 20:04:55.125588', '2026-02-19 20:04:55.125588', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1616, 3, 33, NULL, 'Password Policy', '<h1>Password Policy</h1>
<p><strong>Document ID:</strong> IS-POL-001
<strong>Effective Date:</strong> October 26, 2023
<strong>Approved By:</strong> Chief Information Security Officer
<strong>Classification:</strong> Internal Use</p>
<h2>1.0 Purpose</h2>
<p>This Password Policy establishes mandatory technical and administrative controls for the creation, management, protection, and secure disposal of all authentication credentials at Intellfence. As a cybersecurity firm, our operational integrity and client trust depend on demonstrable security practices. The primary objective is to safeguard information assets, intellectual property, and client data by ensuring credentials are cryptographically robust, confidential, and resistant to modern attack vectors. This protects the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate systems and supports compliance with industry standards and regulations.</p>
<h2>2.0 Scope</h2>
<p>This policy applies to all Intellfence personnel, including full-time and part-time employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, and third-party agents with access to Intellfence information systems. It governs all individuals who interact with any Intellfence network, infrastructure, workstation, server, cloud environment, application, database, code repository, or security tool.</p>
<p>The policy covers all authentication credentials, including user account passwords, privileged administrative passwords, service account passwords, application secrets, API keys, cryptographic keys for authentication, and tokens. It applies regardless of system location (on-premises, public/private cloud, or hybrid architecture). Exceptions require a formally documented, CISO-approved risk-based exemption.</p>
<h2>3.0 Definitions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Authentication Credential:</strong> Any secret piece of information used to verify the identity of a user, system, or service. This includes passwords, passphrases, API keys, tokens, and cryptographic keys used for authentication.</li>
<li><strong>Cryptographic Hash:</strong> A one-way mathematical function that transforms input data (e.g., a password) into a fixed-size string of characters (a hash), from which the original data cannot be feasibly derived.</li>
<li><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> An authentication method that requires the user to provide two or more verification factors from distinct categories (e.g., knowledge factor like a password, possession factor like a hardware token, inherence factor like a fingerprint).</li>
<li><strong>Passphrase:</strong> A sequence of random or unrelated words used as a memorized secret, typically longer and more secure than a traditional password (e.g., &quot;crystal-hamster-bracket-tangible&quot;).</li>
<li><strong>Privileged Administrative Account:</strong> Any user account with elevated permissions beyond a standard user, used for system administration, configuration, or security management (e.g., root, Administrator, Domain Admin).</li>
<li><strong>Service Account:</strong> A non-human account used by an application, service, or system to interact with other systems, databases, or network resources.</li>
<li><strong>Salt (Cryptographic):</strong> A random data string that is uniquely generated and combined with a password before hashing to defend against precomputed hash attacks (e.g., rainbow tables).</li>
</ul>
<h2>4.0 Policy Statement</h2>
<p>Intellfence mandates that all authentication credentials be created, managed, transmitted, stored, and disposed of according to the requirements defined herein. The use of weak, default, shared, or known-compromised passwords is strictly prohibited and constitutes a security violation. All personnel are accountable for adhering to these controls. Security is a shared responsibility integral to our role as cybersecurity professionals.</p>
<h2>5.0 Detailed Requirements &amp; Controls</h2>
<h3>5.1 Password Composition and Complexity</h3>
<p>All user-created passwords must be sufficiently complex to resist modern cracking techniques. The following minimum requirements are enforced technically where possible and mandated administratively elsewhere.</p>
<p><strong>Minimum Length:</strong> Standard user account passwords: minimum 14 characters. Privileged administrative account passwords: minimum 20 characters. Longer passwords are strongly encouraged.</p>
<p><strong>Character Complexity:</strong> Passwords must include characters from at least three (3) of the following four categories:</p>
<ol>
<li>Uppercase letters (A through Z)</li>
<li>Lowercase letters (a through z)</li>
<li>Base 10 numerals (0 through 9)</li>
<li>Non-alphanumeric special characters (e.g., ! @ # $ % ^ &amp; * ( ) ` ~ - _ = + [ ] { } \ | ; : &#39; &quot; , . &lt; &gt; / ?)</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Prohibited Elements:</strong> Passwords must NOT contain:</p>
<ol>
<li>The user&#39;s account name, full name, or any significant part thereof.</li>
<li>Common dictionary words in any language, including simple substitutions (e.g., &quot;P@ssw0rd&quot;).</li>
<li>Repetitive (e.g., &quot;aaaaaa&quot;) or sequential characters (e.g., &quot;123456&quot;, &quot;qwerty&quot;).</li>
<li>Publicly available personal information (e.g., birthdates, family names, pet names).</li>
<li>Company-related information that is easily guessable (e.g., &quot;Intellfence2024&quot;).</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Passphrases:</strong> The use of strong, random passphrases—a sequence of unrelated words totaling over 20 characters—is recommended for memorized secrets (e.g., &quot;crystal-hamster-bracket-tangible&quot;). They must avoid predictable patterns or phrases from popular culture.</p>
<h3>5.2 Password Lifecycle Management</h3>
<p><strong>Initial Provisioning &amp; First-Time Use:</strong> All initial or reset passwords provisioned by IT or Security shall be system-generated, unique, and meet complexity requirements. Temporary passwords must be transmitted via a secure, out-of-band method. Users must change this password immediately upon first login.</p>
<p><strong>Password Expiration &amp; Rotation:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Standard User Accounts:</strong> Passwords must be changed at least every 90 days.</li>
<li><strong>Privileged Administrative Accounts:</strong> Passwords must be changed at least every 60 days.</li>
<li><strong>Service Accounts:</strong> Passwords or secrets must be rotated at least every 180 days, and immediately upon personnel departure or suspected compromise.</li>
<li><strong>Just-in-Time (JIT) &amp; PAM:</strong> Where possible, privileged access should be managed through a Privileged Access Management (PAM) solution or Just-in-Time elevation mechanisms.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Password History:</strong> Authentication systems shall enforce a password history, preventing reuse of the previous 10 passwords.</p>
<h3>5.3 Password Protection and Storage</h3>
<p><strong>Secure Storage:</strong> Passwords shall never be stored in plaintext. All passwords at rest must be hashed using a strong, modern, industry-approved cryptographic hashing algorithm (e.g., bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2id, or PBKDF2 with iteration count &gt; 100,000). All hashes must be salted with a unique, cryptographically random salt per password.</p>
<p><strong>Secure Transmission:</strong> Passwords shall only be transmitted over encrypted channels. All authentication interfaces must use strong transport layer encryption (TLS 1.2 or higher). Sending passwords via unencrypted email, instant messaging, or other clear-text protocols is prohibited.</p>
<p><strong>Prohibition on Insecure Local Storage:</strong> Writing down passwords on sticky notes, notebooks, or whiteboards is prohibited. Storing passwords in unencrypted files on local drives, network shares, or cloud storage is prohibited. Browser-based password managers not integrated with the corporate-approved solution are prohibited for work-related credentials.</p>
<p><strong>Approved Password Managers:</strong> Personnel must use the corporate-approved enterprise password manager for storing and managing all work-related credentials. The master password must adhere to Section 5.1 requirements and be protected with MFA.</p>
<h3>5.4 Authentication and Access Controls</h3>
<p><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> MFA is mandatory for the following access scenarios without exception:</p>
<ol>
<li>All remote network access (VPN, RDP gateways, Citrix).</li>
<li>All access to cloud service administration consoles (e.g., AWS, Azure, GCP).</li>
<li>All users with privileged access rights on any system.</li>
<li>Access to any system or application containing sensitive data per Intellfence&#39;s Data Classification Policy.</li>
<li>The approved enterprise password manager.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Account Lockout:</strong> Systems shall implement an account lockout mechanism. After 5 consecutive failed authentication attempts within 15 minutes, the account shall be locked for a minimum of 15 minutes. Lockout events must be logged and generate an alert to the Security Operations Center (SOC).</p>
<p><strong>Session Management:</strong> Interactive user sessions shall be protected by automatic termination after 15 minutes of inactivity. Session tokens must be cryptographically strong, randomly generated, and invalidated upon logout. Re-authentication is required to resume a terminated session.</p>
<h3>5.5 Administrative and Technical Enforcement</h3>
<p><strong>Centralized Identity Management:</strong> All user authentication shall be managed through a centralized Identity and Access Management (IAM) system to ensure consistent policy application.</p>
<p><strong>Automated Compliance Checking:</strong> The Information Security Team shall deploy automated tools to periodically scan for and alert on non-compliant password practices.</p>
<p><strong>Logging and Monitoring:</strong> All authentication events must be logged with sufficient detail for forensic analysis. Logs must be centrally aggregated, protected from tampering, and retained for a minimum of 90 days (365 days for privileged access events).</p>
<h2>6.0 Roles and Responsibilities</h2>
<p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Are responsible for understanding and complying with this policy, creating and managing strong passwords, using approved password managers, and reporting any suspected password compromise or policy violation to the Information Security Team.</p>
<p><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Is responsible for the technical implementation and enforcement of password controls on systems under their management, provisioning initial credentials securely, and assisting users with password-related issues.</p>
<p><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Is responsible for developing, maintaining, and communicating this policy; selecting and managing the enterprise password manager; monitoring for compliance and attacks; conducting security awareness training; and investigating policy violations.</p>
<p><strong>System and Application Owners:</strong> Are responsible for ensuring their systems are configured to comply with this policy&#39;s technical requirements, including password complexity, storage, MFA, and logging.</p>
<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Has ultimate authority and accountability for this policy, including granting formal exceptions and approving significant changes.</p>
<h2>7.0 Procedures</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Password Creation/Reset:</strong> Users must create passwords meeting Section 5.1 requirements. For password resets, contact the IT Help Desk, which will follow a secure, identity-verified process to issue a temporary password.</li>
<li><strong>Password Change:</strong> Users will receive automated notifications prior to password expiration. Changes must be made through the official company portal or IAM system.</li>
<li><strong>Suspected Compromise:</strong> Immediately report any suspected credential compromise to the SOC via the designated incident reporting channel. Affected passwords must be changed immediately on all systems where they were used.</li>
<li><strong>Use of Password Manager:</strong> All work-related credentials must be stored in the approved enterprise password manager. Users must complete mandatory training on its use.</li>
<li><strong>Offboarding:</strong> Upon termination or role change, the IT Department will immediately disable all associated accounts and initiate credential rotation for any related service accounts.</li>
</ol>
<h2>8.0 Exceptions</h2>
<p>Exceptions to this policy are strongly discouraged and will only be granted where a legitimate technical or business constraint exists that cannot be mitigated. The process is as follows:</p>
<ol>
<li>A formal written request must be submitted to the CISO, detailing the specific requirement from which an exception is needed, the justification, the systems/accounts involved, the duration, and a proposed set of compensating controls to mitigate the associated risk.</li>
<li>The Information Security Team will conduct a risk assessment of the exception request.</li>
<li>Exceptions require written approval from the CISO. All approved exceptions will be documented, assigned a unique ID, and include an expiration date (not to exceed one year).</li>
<li>Exception owners are responsible for ensuring compensating controls remain effective and for requesting a renewal prior to expiration if the condition persists.</li>
</ol>
<h2>9.0 Compliance &amp; Audit Alignment</h2>
<p>This policy aligns with key cybersecurity frameworks and regulations relevant to Intellfence&#39;s operations.</p>
<p><strong>ISO/IEC 27001:2022:</strong> Supports controls including A.5.15 (Identity management), A.5.16 (Identity, authentication, and access), A.5.17 (Authentication information), A.8.2 (Privileged access rights), and A.8.9 (Logging).</p>
<p><strong>SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria:</strong> Provides control framework for Security, Availability, and Confidentiality criteria, specifically CC6.1, CC6.3, and CC6.7.</p>
<p><strong>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR):</strong> Supports compliance with Article 32 (Security of processing).</p>
<p><strong>NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) &amp; NIST SP 800-63B:</strong> Requirements are informed by NIST digital identity guidelines.</p>
<p>Non-compliance may result in audit findings, regulatory penalties, legal liability, and reputational damage.</p>
<h2>10.0 References</h2>
<ol>
<li>Intellfence Information Security Policy</li>
<li>Intellfence Data Classification and Handling Policy</li>
<li>Intellfence Acceptable Use Policy</li>
<li>Intellfence Incident Response Plan</li>
<li>NIST Special Publication 800-63B: Digital Identity Guidelines</li>
<li>ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Standard</li>
</ol>
<h2>11.0 Review and Maintenance</h2>
<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Team at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in technology, or updates to relevant laws and standards. Proposed changes will be circulated to relevant stakeholders for comment and require final approval from the CISO. The version history below documents all revisions.</p>
<h2>Version History</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th align="left">Version</th>
<th align="left">Effective Date</th>
<th align="left">Approved By</th>
<th align="left">Summary of Changes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td align="left">1.0</td>
<td align="left">October 26, 2023</td>
<td align="left">CISO</td>
<td align="left">Initial policy release.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
