	(905, 7, 60, '3.3.1', 'Verify that logout and expiration invalidate the session token, such that the back button or a downstream relying party does not resume an authenticated session, including across relying parties. ()', 'Verify that logout and expiration invalidate the session token, such that the back button or a downstream relying party does not resume an authenticated session, including across relying parties. ()', '**V3.3 Session Termination**

Session timeouts have been aligned with NIST 800-63, which permits much longer session timeouts than traditionally permitted by security standards. Organizations should review the table below, and if a longer time out is desirable based around the application''s risk, the NIST value should be the upper bounds of session idle timeouts.

L1 in this context is IAL1/AAL1, L2 is IAL2/AAL3, L3 is IAL3/AAL3. For IAL2/AAL2 and IAL3/AAL3, the shorter idle timeout is, the lower bound of idle times for being logged out or re-authenticated to resume the session.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\",\"L3\",\"CWE-613\"]"', '2026-02-04 23:59:38.988533', '2026-02-04 23:59:38.988533'),
	(847, 7, 59, '2.2.1', 'Verify that anti-automation controls are effective at mitigating breached credential testing, brute force, and account lockout attacks. Such controls include blocking the most common breached passwords, soft lockouts, rate limiting, CAPTCHA, ever increasing delays between attempts, IP address restrictions, or risk-based restrictions such as location, first login on a device, recent attempts to unlock the account, or similar. Verify that no more than 100 failed attempts per hour is poss', 'Verify that anti-automation controls are effective at mitigating breached credential testing, brute force, and account lockout attacks. Such controls include blocking the most common breached passwords, soft lockouts, rate limiting, CAPTCHA, ever increasing delays between attempts, IP address restrictions, or risk-based restrictions such as location, first login on a device, recent attempts to unlock the account, or similar. Verify that no more than 100 failed attempts per hour is possible on a single account.', '**V2.2 General Authenticator Security**

Authenticator agility is essential to future-proof applications. Refactor application verifiers to allow additional authenticators as per user preferences, as well as allowing retiring deprecated or unsafe authenticators in an orderly fashion.

NIST considers email and SMS as ["restricted" authenticator types](https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-FAQ/#q-b1), and they are likely to be removed from NIST 800-63 and thus the ASVS at some point the future. Applications should plan a roadmap that does not require the use of email or SMS.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\",\"L3\",\"CWE-307\"]"', '2026-02-04 23:54:54.991034', '2026-02-04 23:54:54.991034'),
	(877, 7, 59, '2.7.6', 'Verify that the initial authentication code is generated by a secure random number generator, containing at least 20 bits of entropy (typically a six digital random number is sufficient).', 'Verify that the initial authentication code is generated by a secure random number generator, containing at least 20 bits of entropy (typically a six digital random number is sufficient).', '**V2.7 Out of Band Verifier**

In the past, a common out of band verifier would have been an email or SMS containing a password reset link. Attackers use this weak mechanism to reset accounts they don''t yet control, such as taking over a person''s email account and re-using any discovered reset links. There are better ways to handle out of band verification.

Secure out of band authenticators are physical devices that can communicate with the verifier over a secure secondary channel. Examples include push notifications to mobile devices. This type of authenticator is considered "something you have". When a user wishes to authenticate, the verifying application sends a message to the out of band authenticator via a connection to the authenticator directly or indirectly through a third party service. The message contains an authentication code (typically a random six digit number or a modal approval dialog). The verifying application waits to receive the authentication code through the primary channel and compares the hash of the received value to the hash of the original authentication code. If they match, the out of band verifier can assume that the user has authenticated.

The ASVS assumes that only a few developers will be developing new out of band authenticators, such as push notifications, and thus the following ASVS controls apply to verifiers, such as authentication API, applications, and single sign-on implementations. If developing a new out of band authenticator, please refer to NIST 800-63B &sect; 5.1.3.1.

Unsafe out of band authenticators such as e-mail and VOIP are not permitted. PSTN and SMS authentication are currently "restricted" by NIST and should be deprecated in favor of push notifications or similar. If you need to use telephone or SMS out of band authentication, please see &sect; 5.1.3.3.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\",\"CWE-5.1.3.2\"]"', '2026-02-04 23:55:01.236591', '2026-02-04 23:55:01.236591'),
	(879, 7, 59, '2.8.2', 'Verify that symmetric keys used to verify submitted OTPs are highly protected, such as by using a hardware security module or secure operating system based key storage.', 'Verify that symmetric keys used to verify submitted OTPs are highly protected, such as by using a hardware security module or secure operating system based key storage.', '**V2.8 One Time Verifier**

Single-factor One-time Passwords (OTPs) are physical or soft tokens that display a continually changing pseudo-random one-time challenge. These devices make phishing (impersonation) difficult, but not impossible. This type of authenticator is considered "something you have". Multi-factor tokens are similar to single-factor OTPs, but require a valid PIN code, biometric unlocking, USB insertion or NFC pairing or some additional value (such as transaction signing calculators) to be entered to create the final OTP.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\",\"CWE-5.1.4.2 / 5.1.5.2\"]"', '2026-02-04 23:55:01.655111', '2026-02-04 23:55:01.655111'),
	(884, 7, 59, '2.8.7', 'Verify that biometric authenticators are limited to use only as secondary factors in conjunction with either something you have and something you know.', 'Verify that biometric authenticators are limited to use only as secondary factors in conjunction with either something you have and something you know.', '**V2.8 One Time Verifier**

Single-factor One-time Passwords (OTPs) are physical or soft tokens that display a continually changing pseudo-random one-time challenge. These devices make phishing (impersonation) difficult, but not impossible. This type of authenticator is considered "something you have". Multi-factor tokens are similar to single-factor OTPs, but require a valid PIN code, biometric unlocking, USB insertion or NFC pairing or some additional value (such as transaction signing calculators) to be entered to create the final OTP.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L2\",\"CWE-5.2.3\"]"', '2026-02-04 23:55:02.704204', '2026-02-04 23:55:02.704204');

Cryptographic security keys are smart cards or FIDO keys, where the user has to plug in or pair the cryptographic device to the computer to complete authentication. Verifiers send a challenge nonce to the cryptographic devices or software, and the device or software calculates a response based upon a securely stored cryptographic key.

The requirements for single-factor cryptographic devices and software, and multi-factor cryptographic devices and software are the same, as verification of the cryptographic authenticator proves possession of the authentication factor.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\",\"CWE-5.1.7.2\"]"', '2026-02-04 23:55:02.90979', '2026-02-04 23:55:02.90979'),
	(891, 7, 59, '2.10.4', 'Verify passwords, integrations with databases and third-party systems, seeds and internal secrets, and API keys are managed securely and not included in the source code or stored within source code repositories. Such storage SHOULD resist offline attacks. The use of a secure software key store (L1), hardware TPM, or an HSM (L3) is recommended for password storage.', 'Verify passwords, integrations with databases and third-party systems, seeds and internal secrets, and API keys are managed securely and not included in the source code or stored within source code repositories. Such storage SHOULD resist offline attacks. The use of a secure software key store (L1), hardware TPM, or an HSM (L3) is recommended for password storage.', '**V2.10 Service Authentication**

This section is not penetration testable, so does not have any L1 requirements. However, if used in an architecture, coding or secure code review, please assume that software (just as Java Key Store) is the minimum requirement at L1. Clear text storage of secrets is not acceptable under any circumstances.', '"[\"ASVS\"]"', '2026-02-04 23:55:04.151849', '2026-02-04 23:55:04.151849'),
	(919, 7, 60, '3.7.1', 'Verify the application ensures a full, valid login session or requires re-authentication or secondary verification before allowing any sensitive transactions or account modifications.', 'Verify the application ensures a full, valid login session or requires re-authentication or secondary verification before allowing any sensitive transactions or account modifications.', '**V3.7 Defenses Against Session Management Exploits**

There are a small number of session management attacks, some related to the user experience (UX) of sessions. Previously, based on ISO 27002 requirements, the ASVS has required blocking multiple simultaneous sessions. Blocking simultaneous sessions is no longer appropriate, not only as modern users have many devices or the app is an API without a browser session, but in most of these implementations, the last authenticator wins, which is often the attacker. This section provides leading guidance on deterring, delaying and detecting session management attacks using code.

### Description of the half-open Attack

In early 2018, several financial institutions were compromised using what the attackers called "half-open attacks". This term has stuck in the industry. The attackers struck multiple institutions with different proprietary code bases, and indeed it seems different code bases within the same institutions. The half-open attack is exploiting a design pattern flaw commonly found in many existing authentication, session management and access control systems.

Attackers start a half-open attack by attempting to lock, reset, or recover a credential. A popular session management design pattern re-uses user profile session objects/models between unauthenticated, half-authenticated (password resets, forgot username), and fully authenticated code. This design pattern populates a valid session object or token containing the victim''s profile, including password hashes and roles. If access control checks in controllers or routers does not correctly verify that the user is fully logged in, the attacker will be able to act as the user. Attacks could include changing the user''s password to a known value, update the email address to perform a valid password reset, disable multi-factor authentication or enroll a new MFA device, reveal or change API keys, and so on.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\",\"L3\",\"CWE-306\"]"', '2026-02-04 23:59:42.4194', '2026-02-04 23:59:42.4194'),
	(930, 7, 62, '5.1.1', 'Verify that the application has defenses against HTTP parameter pollution attacks, particularly if the application framework makes no distinction about the source of request parameters (GET, POST, cookies, headers, or environment variables).', 'Verify that the application has defenses against HTTP parameter pollution attacks, particularly if the application framework makes no distinction about the source of request parameters (GET, POST, cookies, headers, or environment variables).', '**V5.1 Input Validation**

Properly implemented input validation controls, using positive allow lists and strong data typing, can eliminate more than 90% of all injection attacks. Length and range checks can reduce this further. Building in secure input validation is required during application architecture, design sprints, coding, and unit and integration testing. Although many of these items cannot be found in penetration tests, the results of not implementing them are usually found in V5.3 - Output encoding and Injection Prevention Requirements. Developers and secure code reviewers are recommended to treat this section as if L1 is required for all items to prevent injections.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\",\"L3\",\"CWE-235\"]"', '2026-02-04 23:59:45.696932', '2026-02-04 23:59:45.696932'),
	(933, 7, 62, '5.1.4', 'Verify that structured data is strongly typed and validated against a defined schema including allowed characters, length and pattern (e.g. credit card numbers, e-mail addresses, telephone numbers, or validating that two related fields are reasonable, such as checking that suburb and zip/postcode match). ()', 'Verify that structured data is strongly typed and validated against a defined schema including allowed characters, length and pattern (e.g. credit card numbers, e-mail addresses, telephone numbers, or validating that two related fields are reasonable, such as checking that suburb and zip/postcode match). ()', '**V5.1 Input Validation**

Properly implemented input validation controls, using positive allow lists and strong data typing, can eliminate more than 90% of all injection attacks. Length and range checks can reduce this further. Building in secure input validation is required during application architecture, design sprints, coding, and unit and integration testing. Although many of these items cannot be found in penetration tests, the results of not implementing them are usually found in V5.3 - Output encoding and Injection Prevention Requirements. Developers and secure code reviewers are recommended to treat this section as if L1 is required for all items to prevent injections.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\",\"L3\",\"CWE-20\"]"', '2026-02-04 23:59:46.434451', '2026-02-04 23:59:46.434451'),
	(949, 7, 62, '5.3.7', 'Verify that the application protects against LDAP injection vulnerabilities, or that specific security controls to prevent LDAP injection have been implemented. ()', 'Verify that the application protects against LDAP injection vulnerabilities, or that specific security controls to prevent LDAP injection have been implemented. ()', '**V5.3 Output Encoding and Injection Prevention**

Output encoding close or adjacent to the interpreter in use is critical to the security of any application. Typically, output encoding is not persisted, but used to render the output safe in the appropriate output context for immediate use. Failing to output encode will result in an insecure, injectable, and unsafe application.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\",\"L3\",\"CWE-90\"]"', '2026-02-04 23:59:50.378018', '2026-02-04 23:59:50.378018'),
	(962, 7, 63, '6.1.3', 'Verify that regulated financial data is stored encrypted while at rest, such as financial accounts, defaults or credit history, tax records, pay history, beneficiaries, or de-anonymized market or research records.', 'Verify that regulated financial data is stored encrypted while at rest, such as financial accounts, defaults or credit history, tax records, pay history, beneficiaries, or de-anonymized market or research records.', '**V6.1 Data Classification**

The most important asset is the data processed, stored or transmitted by an application. Always perform a privacy impact assessment to classify the data protection needs of any stored data correctly.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\"]"', '2026-02-04 23:59:53.899066', '2026-02-04 23:59:53.899066'),
	(966, 7, 63, '6.2.4', 'Verify that random number, encryption or hashing algorithms, key lengths, rounds, ciphers or modes, can be reconfigured, upgraded, or swapped at any time, to protect against cryptographic breaks. ()', 'Verify that random number, encryption or hashing algorithms, key lengths, rounds, ciphers or modes, can be reconfigured, upgraded, or swapped at any time, to protect against cryptographic breaks. ()', '**V6.2 Algorithms**

Recent advances in cryptography mean that previously safe algorithms and key lengths are no longer safe or sufficient to protect data. Therefore, it should be possible to change algorithms.

Although this section is not easily penetration tested, developers should consider this entire section as mandatory even though L1 is missing from most of the items.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\"]"', '2026-02-04 23:59:54.8772', '2026-02-04 23:59:54.8772'),
	(971, 7, 63, '6.3.1', 'Verify that all random numbers, random file names, random GUIDs, and random strings are generated using the cryptographic module''s approved cryptographically secure random number generator when these random values are intended to be not guessable by an attacker.', 'Verify that all random numbers, random file names, random GUIDs, and random strings are generated using the cryptographic module''s approved cryptographically secure random number generator when these random values are intended to be not guessable by an attacker.', '**V6.3 Random Values**

True Pseudo-random Number Generation (PRNG) is incredibly difficult to get right. Generally, good sources of entropy within a system will be quickly depleted if over-used, but sources with less randomness can lead to predictable keys and secrets.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\"]"', '2026-02-04 23:59:56.099269', '2026-02-04 23:59:56.099269'),
	(976, 7, 64, '7.1.1', 'Verify that the application does not log credentials or payment details. Session tokens should only be stored in logs in an irreversible, hashed form. ([C9, C10](https://owasp.org/www-project-proactive-controls/#div-numbering))', 'Verify that the application does not log credentials or payment details. Session tokens should only be stored in logs in an irreversible, hashed form. ([C9, C10](https://owasp.org/www-project-proactive-controls/#div-numbering))', '**V7.1 Log Content**

Logging sensitive information is dangerous - the logs become classified themselves, which means they need to be encrypted, become subject to retention policies, and must be disclosed in security audits. Ensure only necessary information is kept in logs, and certainly no payment, credentials (including session tokens), sensitive or personally identifiable information.

V7.1 covers OWASP Top 10 2017:A10. As 2017:A10 and this section are not penetration testable, it''s important for:

* Developers to ensure full compliance with this section, as if all items were marked as L1
* Penetration testers to validate full compliance of all items in V7.1 via interview, screenshots, or assertion', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\",\"L3\",\"CWE-532\"]"', '2026-02-04 23:59:57.627099', '2026-02-04 23:59:57.627099'),
	(1058, 7, 69, '12.3.1', 'Verify that user-submitted filename metadata is not used directly by system or framework filesystems and that a URL API is used to protect against path traversal.', 'Verify that user-submitted filename metadata is not used directly by system or framework filesystems and that a URL API is used to protect against path traversal.', '**V12.3 File Execution**

', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\",\"L3\",\"CWE-22\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:01:57.336062', '2026-02-05 00:01:57.336062'),
	(978, 7, 64, '7.1.3', 'Verify that the application logs security relevant events including successful and failed authentication events, access control failures, deserialization failures and input validation failures. ([C5, C7](https://owasp.org/www-project-proactive-controls/#div-numbering))', 'Verify that the application logs security relevant events including successful and failed authentication events, access control failures, deserialization failures and input validation failures. ([C5, C7](https://owasp.org/www-project-proactive-controls/#div-numbering))', '**V7.1 Log Content**

Logging sensitive information is dangerous - the logs become classified themselves, which means they need to be encrypted, become subject to retention policies, and must be disclosed in security audits. Ensure only necessary information is kept in logs, and certainly no payment, credentials (including session tokens), sensitive or personally identifiable information.

V7.1 covers OWASP Top 10 2017:A10. As 2017:A10 and this section are not penetration testable, it''s important for:

* Developers to ensure full compliance with this section, as if all items were marked as L1
* Penetration testers to validate full compliance of all items in V7.1 via interview, screenshots, or assertion', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\"]"', '2026-02-04 23:59:58.118163', '2026-02-04 23:59:58.118163'),
	(979, 7, 64, '7.1.4', 'Verify that each log event includes necessary information that would allow for a detailed investigation of the timeline when an event happens. ()', 'Verify that each log event includes necessary information that would allow for a detailed investigation of the timeline when an event happens. ()', '**V7.1 Log Content**

Logging sensitive information is dangerous - the logs become classified themselves, which means they need to be encrypted, become subject to retention policies, and must be disclosed in security audits. Ensure only necessary information is kept in logs, and certainly no payment, credentials (including session tokens), sensitive or personally identifiable information.

V7.1 covers OWASP Top 10 2017:A10. As 2017:A10 and this section are not penetration testable, it''s important for:

* Developers to ensure full compliance with this section, as if all items were marked as L1
* Penetration testers to validate full compliance of all items in V7.1 via interview, screenshots, or assertion', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\"]"', '2026-02-04 23:59:58.36065', '2026-02-04 23:59:58.36065'),
	(998, 7, 65, '8.3.1', 'Verify that sensitive data is sent to the server in the HTTP message body or headers, and that query string parameters from any HTTP verb do not contain sensitive data.', 'Verify that sensitive data is sent to the server in the HTTP message body or headers, and that query string parameters from any HTTP verb do not contain sensitive data.', '**V8.3 Sensitive Private Data**

This section helps protect sensitive data from being created, read, updated, or deleted without authorization, particularly in bulk quantities.

Compliance with this section implies compliance with V4 Access Control, and in particular V4.2. For example, to protect against unauthorized updates or disclosure of sensitive personal information requires adherence to V4.2.1. Please comply with this section and V4 for full coverage.

Note: Privacy regulations and laws, such as the Australian Privacy Principles APP-11 or GDPR, directly affect how applications must approach the implementation of storage, use, and transmission of sensitive personal information. This ranges from severe penalties to simple advice. Please consult your local laws and regulations, and consult a qualified privacy specialist or lawyer as required.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\",\"L3\",\"CWE-319\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:00:03.274708', '2026-02-05 00:00:03.274708'),
	(1004, 7, 65, '8.3.7', 'Verify that sensitive or private information that is required to be encrypted, is encrypted using approved algorithms that provide both confidentiality and integrity. ()', 'Verify that sensitive or private information that is required to be encrypted, is encrypted using approved algorithms that provide both confidentiality and integrity. ()', '**V8.3 Sensitive Private Data**

This section helps protect sensitive data from being created, read, updated, or deleted without authorization, particularly in bulk quantities.

Compliance with this section implies compliance with V4 Access Control, and in particular V4.2. For example, to protect against unauthorized updates or disclosure of sensitive personal information requires adherence to V4.2.1. Please comply with this section and V4 for full coverage.

Note: Privacy regulations and laws, such as the Australian Privacy Principles APP-11 or GDPR, directly affect how applications must approach the implementation of storage, use, and transmission of sensitive personal information. This ranges from severe penalties to simple advice. Please consult your local laws and regulations, and consult a qualified privacy specialist or lawyer as required.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:00:04.746052', '2026-02-05 00:00:04.746052'),
	(1054, 7, 69, '12.1.1', 'Verify that the application will not accept large files that could fill up storage or cause a denial of service.', 'Verify that the application will not accept large files that could fill up storage or cause a denial of service.', '**V12.1 File Upload**

Although zip bombs are eminently testable using penetration testing techniques, they are considered L2 and above to encourage design and development consideration with careful manual testing, and to avoid automated or unskilled manual penetration testing of a denial of service condition.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\",\"L3\",\"CWE-400\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:01:56.359037', '2026-02-05 00:01:56.359037'),
	(1055, 7, 69, '12.1.2', 'Verify that the application checks compressed files (e.g. zip, gz, docx, odt) against maximum allowed uncompressed size and against maximum number of files before uncompressing the file.', 'Verify that the application checks compressed files (e.g. zip, gz, docx, odt) against maximum allowed uncompressed size and against maximum number of files before uncompressing the file.', '**V12.1 File Upload**

Although zip bombs are eminently testable using penetration testing techniques, they are considered L2 and above to encourage design and development consideration with careful manual testing, and to avoid automated or unskilled manual penetration testing of a denial of service condition.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:01:56.600028', '2026-02-05 00:01:56.600028'),
	(1056, 7, 69, '12.1.3', 'Verify that a file size quota and maximum number of files per user is enforced to ensure that a single user cannot fill up the storage with too many files, or excessively large files.', 'Verify that a file size quota and maximum number of files per user is enforced to ensure that a single user cannot fill up the storage with too many files, or excessively large files.', '**V12.1 File Upload**

Although zip bombs are eminently testable using penetration testing techniques, they are considered L2 and above to encourage design and development consideration with careful manual testing, and to avoid automated or unskilled manual penetration testing of a denial of service condition.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:01:56.845629', '2026-02-05 00:01:56.845629'),
	(1057, 7, 69, '12.2.1', 'Verify that files obtained from untrusted sources are validated to be of expected type based on the file''s content.', 'Verify that files obtained from untrusted sources are validated to be of expected type based on the file''s content.', '**V12.2 File Integrity**

', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:01:57.09322', '2026-02-05 00:01:57.09322'),
	(1059, 7, 69, '12.3.2', 'Verify that user-submitted filename metadata is validated or ignored to prevent the disclosure, creation, updating or removal of local files (LFI).', 'Verify that user-submitted filename metadata is validated or ignored to prevent the disclosure, creation, updating or removal of local files (LFI).', '**V12.3 File Execution**

', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\",\"L3\",\"CWE-73\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:01:57.577289', '2026-02-05 00:01:57.577289'),
	(1019, 7, 67, '13.2.1', 'Verify that enabled RESTful HTTP methods are a valid choice for the user or action, such as preventing normal users using DELETE or PUT on protected API or resources.', 'Verify that enabled RESTful HTTP methods are a valid choice for the user or action, such as preventing normal users using DELETE or PUT on protected API or resources.', '**V13.2 RESTful Web Service**

JSON schema validation is in a draft stage of standardization (see references). When considering using JSON schema validation, which is best practice for RESTful web services, consider using these additional data validation strategies in combination with JSON schema validation:

* Parsing validation of the JSON object, such as if there are missing or extra elements.
* Validation of the JSON object values using standard input validation methods, such as data type, data format, length, etc.
* and formal JSON schema validation.

Once the JSON schema validation standard is formalized, ASVS will update its advice in this area. Carefully monitor any JSON schema validation libraries in use, as they will need to be updated regularly until the standard is formalized and bugs are ironed out of reference implementations.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\",\"L3\",\"CWE-650\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:00:09.499297', '2026-02-05 00:00:09.499297'),
	(1024, 7, 67, '13.2.6', 'Verify that the message headers and payload are trustworthy and not modified in transit. Requiring strong encryption for transport (TLS only) may be sufficient in many cases as it provides both confidentiality and integrity protection. Per-message digital signatures can provide additional assurance on top of the transport protections for high-security applications but bring with them additional complexity and risks to weigh against the benefits.', 'Verify that the message headers and payload are trustworthy and not modified in transit. Requiring strong encryption for transport (TLS only) may be sufficient in many cases as it provides both confidentiality and integrity protection. Per-message digital signatures can provide additional assurance on top of the transport protections for high-security applications but bring with them additional complexity and risks to weigh against the benefits.', '**V13.2 RESTful Web Service**

JSON schema validation is in a draft stage of standardization (see references). When considering using JSON schema validation, which is best practice for RESTful web services, consider using these additional data validation strategies in combination with JSON schema validation:

* Parsing validation of the JSON object, such as if there are missing or extra elements.
* Validation of the JSON object values using standard input validation methods, such as data type, data format, length, etc.
* and formal JSON schema validation.

Once the JSON schema validation standard is formalized, ASVS will update its advice in this area. Carefully monitor any JSON schema validation libraries in use, as they will need to be updated regularly until the standard is formalized and bugs are ironed out of reference implementations.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:00:10.721296', '2026-02-05 00:00:10.721296'),
	(1025, 7, 67, '13.3.1', 'Verify that XSD schema validation takes place to ensure a properly formed XML document, followed by validation of each input field before any processing of that data takes place.', 'Verify that XSD schema validation takes place to ensure a properly formed XML document, followed by validation of each input field before any processing of that data takes place.', '**V13.3 SOAP Web Service**

', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\",\"L3\",\"CWE-20\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:00:10.967769', '2026-02-05 00:00:10.967769'),
	(1029, 7, 68, '14.1.1', 'Verify that the application build and deployment processes are performed in a secure and repeatable way, such as CI / CD automation, automated configuration management, and automated deployment scripts.', 'Verify that the application build and deployment processes are performed in a secure and repeatable way, such as CI / CD automation, automated configuration management, and automated deployment scripts.', '**V14.1 Build and Deploy**

Build pipelines are the basis for repeatable security - every time something insecure is discovered, it can be resolved in the source code, build or deployment scripts, and tested automatically. We are strongly encouraging the use of build pipelines with automatic security and dependency checks that warn or break the build to prevent known security issues being deployed into production. Manual steps performed irregularly directly leads to avoidable security mistakes.

As the industry moves to a DevSecOps model, it is important to ensure the continued availability and integrity of deployment and configuration to achieve a "known good" state. In the past, if a system was hacked, it would take days to months to prove that no further intrusions had taken place. Today, with the advent of software defined infrastructure, rapid A/B deployments with zero downtime, and automated containerized builds, it is possible to automatically and continuously build, harden, and deploy a "known good" replacement for any compromised system.

If traditional models are still in place, then manual steps must be taken to harden and back up that configuration to allow the compromised systems to be quickly replaced with high integrity, uncompromised systems in a timely fashion.

Compliance with this section requires an automated build system, and access to build and deployment scripts.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:00:12.25882', '2026-02-05 00:00:12.25882'),
	(1031, 7, 68, '14.1.3', 'Verify that server configuration is hardened as per the recommendations of the application server and frameworks in use.', 'Verify that server configuration is hardened as per the recommendations of the application server and frameworks in use.', '**V14.1 Build and Deploy**

Build pipelines are the basis for repeatable security - every time something insecure is discovered, it can be resolved in the source code, build or deployment scripts, and tested automatically. We are strongly encouraging the use of build pipelines with automatic security and dependency checks that warn or break the build to prevent known security issues being deployed into production. Manual steps performed irregularly directly leads to avoidable security mistakes.

As the industry moves to a DevSecOps model, it is important to ensure the continued availability and integrity of deployment and configuration to achieve a "known good" state. In the past, if a system was hacked, it would take days to months to prove that no further intrusions had taken place. Today, with the advent of software defined infrastructure, rapid A/B deployments with zero downtime, and automated containerized builds, it is possible to automatically and continuously build, harden, and deploy a "known good" replacement for any compromised system.

If traditional models are still in place, then manual steps must be taken to harden and back up that configuration to allow the compromised systems to be quickly replaced with high integrity, uncompromised systems in a timely fashion.

Compliance with this section requires an automated build system, and access to build and deployment scripts.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:00:12.744909', '2026-02-05 00:00:12.744909'),
	(1114, 8, 83, 'MASVS-PLATFORM-2', 'The app uses WebViews securely.', 'WebViews are typically used by apps that have a need for increased control over the UI. This control ensures that WebViews are configured securely to prevent sensitive data leakage as well as sensitive functionality exposure (e.g. via JavaScript bridges to native code).', NULL, '"[\"MASVS\",\"Platform Interaction\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:28:54.304174', '2026-02-05 00:28:54.304174'),
	(1033, 7, 68, '14.1.5', 'Verify that authorized administrators can verify the integrity of all security-relevant configurations to detect tampering.', 'Verify that authorized administrators can verify the integrity of all security-relevant configurations to detect tampering.', '**V14.1 Build and Deploy**

Build pipelines are the basis for repeatable security - every time something insecure is discovered, it can be resolved in the source code, build or deployment scripts, and tested automatically. We are strongly encouraging the use of build pipelines with automatic security and dependency checks that warn or break the build to prevent known security issues being deployed into production. Manual steps performed irregularly directly leads to avoidable security mistakes.

As the industry moves to a DevSecOps model, it is important to ensure the continued availability and integrity of deployment and configuration to achieve a "known good" state. In the past, if a system was hacked, it would take days to months to prove that no further intrusions had taken place. Today, with the advent of software defined infrastructure, rapid A/B deployments with zero downtime, and automated containerized builds, it is possible to automatically and continuously build, harden, and deploy a "known good" replacement for any compromised system.

If traditional models are still in place, then manual steps must be taken to harden and back up that configuration to allow the compromised systems to be quickly replaced with high integrity, uncompromised systems in a timely fashion.

Compliance with this section requires an automated build system, and access to build and deployment scripts.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:00:13.232414', '2026-02-05 00:00:13.232414'),
	(1041, 7, 68, '14.3.2', 'Verify that web or application server and application framework debug modes are disabled in production to eliminate debug features, developer consoles, and unintended security disclosures.', 'Verify that web or application server and application framework debug modes are disabled in production to eliminate debug features, developer consoles, and unintended security disclosures.', '**V14.3 Unintended Security Disclosure**

Configurations for production should be hardened to protect against common attacks, such as debug consoles, raise the bar for Cross-site Scripting (XSS) and Remote File Inclusion (RFI) attacks, and to eliminate trivial information discovery "vulnerabilities" that are the unwelcome hallmark of many penetration testing reports. Many of these issues are rarely rated as a significant risk, but they are chained together with other vulnerabilities. If these issues are not present by default, it raises the bar before most attacks can succeed.', '"[\"ASVS\",\"L1\",\"L2\",\"L3\",\"CWE-497\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:00:15.201873', '2026-02-05 00:00:15.201873'),
	(1115, 8, 83, 'MASVS-PLATFORM-3', 'The app uses the user interface securely.', 'Sensitive data has to be displayed in the UI in many situations (e.g. passwords, credit card details, OTP codes in notifications). This control ensures that this data doesn''t end up being unintentionally leaked due to platform mechanisms such as auto-generated screenshots or accidentally disclosed via e.g. shoulder surfing or sharing the device with another person.', NULL, '"[\"MASVS\",\"Platform Interaction\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:28:54.508269', '2026-02-05 00:28:54.508269'),
	(1116, 8, 87, 'MASVS-CODE-1', 'The app requires an up-to-date platform version.', 'Every release of the mobile OS includes security patches and new security features. By supporting older versions, apps stay vulnerable to well-known threats. This control ensures that the app is running on an up-to-date platform version so that users have the latest security protections.', NULL, '"[\"MASVS\",\"Code Quality\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:28:54.822168', '2026-02-05 00:28:54.822168'),
	(1117, 8, 87, 'MASVS-CODE-2', 'The app has a mechanism for enforcing app updates.', 'Sometimes critical vulnerabilities are discovered in the app when it is already in production. This control ensures that there is a mechanism to force the users to update the app before they can continue using it.', NULL, '"[\"MASVS\",\"Code Quality\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:28:55.027741', '2026-02-05 00:28:55.027741'),
	(1118, 8, 87, 'MASVS-CODE-3', 'The app only uses software components without known vulnerabilities.', 'To be truly secure, a full whitebox assessment should have been performed on all app components. However, as it usually happens with e.g. for third-party components this is not always feasible and not typically part of a penetration test. This control covers "low-hanging fruit" cases, such as those that can be detected just by scanning libraries for known vulnerabilities.', NULL, '"[\"MASVS\",\"Code Quality\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:28:55.232739', '2026-02-05 00:28:55.232739'),
	(1119, 8, 87, 'MASVS-CODE-4', 'The app validates and sanitizes all untrusted inputs.', 'Apps have many data entry points including the UI, IPC, the network, the file system, etc. This incoming data might have been inadvertently modified by untrusted actors and may lead to bypass of critical security checks as well as classical injection attacks such as SQL injection, XSS or insecure deserialization. This control ensures that this data is treated as untrusted input and is properly verified and sanitized before it''s used.', NULL, '"[\"MASVS\",\"Code Quality\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:28:55.43965', '2026-02-05 00:28:55.43965'),
	(1120, 8, 92, 'MASVS-RESILIENCE-1', 'The app validates the integrity of the platform.', 'Running on a platform that has been tampered with can be very dangerous for apps, as this may disable certain security features, putting the data of the app at risk. Trusting the platform is essential for many of the MASVS controls relying on the platform being secure (e.g. secure storage, biometrics, sandboxing, etc.). This control tries to validate that the OS has not been compromised and its security features can thus be trusted.', NULL, '"[\"MASVS\",\"Resilience Against Reverse Engineering and Tampering\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:28:55.747927', '2026-02-05 00:28:55.747927'),
	(1121, 8, 92, 'MASVS-RESILIENCE-2', 'The app implements anti-tampering mechanisms.', 'Apps run on a user-controlled device, and without proper protections it''s relatively easy to run a modified version locally (e.g. to cheat in a game, or enable premium features without paying), or upload a backdoored version of it to third-party app stores. This control tries to ensure the integrity of the app''s intended functionality by preventing modifications to the original code and resources.', NULL, '"[\"MASVS\",\"Resilience Against Reverse Engineering and Tampering\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:28:55.953633', '2026-02-05 00:28:55.953633'),
	(1122, 8, 92, 'MASVS-RESILIENCE-3', 'The app implements anti-static analysis mechanisms.', 'Understanding the internals of an app is typically the first step towards tampering with it (either dynamically, or statically). This control tries to impede comprehension by making it as difficult as possible to figure out how an app works using static analysis.', NULL, '"[\"MASVS\",\"Resilience Against Reverse Engineering and Tampering\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:28:56.158189', '2026-02-05 00:28:56.158189'),
	(1123, 8, 92, 'MASVS-RESILIENCE-4', 'The app implements anti-dynamic analysis techniques.', 'Sometimes pure static analysis is very difficult and time consuming so it typically goes hand in hand with dynamic analysis. Observing and manipulating an app during runtime makes it much easier to decipher its behavior. This control aims to make it as difficult as possible to perform dynamic analysis, as well as prevent dynamic instrumentation which could allow an attacker to modify the code at runtime.', NULL, '"[\"MASVS\",\"Resilience Against Reverse Engineering and Tampering\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:28:56.363178', '2026-02-05 00:28:56.363178'),
	(1124, 8, 97, 'MASVS-PRIVACY-1', 'The app minimizes access to sensitive data and resources.', '''Apps should only request access to the data they absolutely need for their functionality and always with informed consent from the user. This control ensures that apps practice data minimization and restricts access control, reducing the potential impact of data breaches or leaks.


      Furthermore, apps should share data with third parties only when necessary, and this should include enforcing that third-party SDKs operate based on user consent, not by default or without it. Apps should prevent third-party SDKs from ignoring consent signals or from collecting data before consent is confirmed.


      Additionally, apps should be aware of the ''''supply chain'''' of SDKs they incorporate, ensuring that no data is unnecessarily passed down their chain of dependencies. This end-to-end responsibility for data aligns with recent SBOM regulatory requirements, making apps more accountable for their data practices.''', NULL, '"[\"MASVS\",\"Privacy\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:28:56.670123', '2026-02-05 00:28:56.670123'),
	(1125, 8, 97, 'MASVS-PRIVACY-2', 'The app prevents identification of the user.', '''Protecting user identity is crucial. This control emphasizes the use of unlinkability techniques like data abstraction, anonymization and pseudonymization to prevent user identification and tracking.


      Another key aspect addressed by this control is to establish technical barriers when employing complex ''''fingerprint-like'''' signals (e.g. device IDs, IP addresses, behavioral patterns) for specific purposes. For instance, a fingerprint used for fraud detection should be isolated and not repurposed for audience measurement in an analytics SDK. This ensures that each data stream serves its intended function without risking user privacy.''', NULL, '"[\"MASVS\",\"Privacy\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:28:56.874622', '2026-02-05 00:28:56.874622'),
	(1126, 8, 97, 'MASVS-PRIVACY-3', 'The app is transparent about data collection and usage.', 'Users have the right to know how their data is being used. This control ensures that apps provide clear information about data collection, storage, and sharing practices, including any behavior a user wouldn''t reasonably expect, such as background data collection. Apps should also adhere to platform guidelines on data declarations.', NULL, '"[\"MASVS\",\"Privacy\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:28:57.080165', '2026-02-05 00:28:57.080165'),
	(1127, 8, 97, 'MASVS-PRIVACY-4', 'The app offers user control over their data.', 'Users should have control over their data. This control ensures that apps provide mechanisms for users to manage, delete, and modify their data, and change privacy settings as needed (e.g. to revoke consent). Additionally, apps should re-prompt for consent and update their transparency disclosures when they require more data than initially specified.', NULL, '"[\"MASVS\",\"Privacy\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:28:57.318892', '2026-02-05 00:28:57.318892'),
	(1084, 10, 107, 'API1:2023', 'Broken Object Level Authorization', 'Broken Object Level Authorization', NULL, '"[\"OWASP\",\"API\",\"2023\",\"Tampering\",\"Elevation of Privilege\",\"BOLA\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:27:00.641621', '2026-02-05 00:27:00.641621'),
	(1132, 12, 109, 'A05:2025', 'Injection', '### Background
Includes Cross-site Scripting (XSS) and SQL Injection. 100% of apps were tested for this. 37 CWEs. Notable CWEs: CWE-79 (XSS), CWE-89 (SQLi).

### Description
An injection vulnerability allows untrusted user input to be sent to an interpreter and executed as commands. Common in SQL, NoSQL, LDAP, and OS commands.', '### How to Prevent
- Use safe APIs with parameterized interfaces (ORMs).
- Implement positive server-side input validation.
- Escape special characters using context-aware syntax for the specific interpreter.
- Implement SAST/DAST in the CI/CD pipeline.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: Injecting `'' OR ''1''=''1` into an ID parameter to return all records from a database table.
- **Scenario #2**: Concatenating user input into an OS command (e.g., `nslookup domain.com; cat /etc/passwd`) to execute arbitrary code.
- **Scenario #3**: Prompt Injection in LLMs where a user tricks an AI agent into bypassing its safety filters.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"WEB\",\"2025\",\"Tampering\",\"Security Misconfiguration\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:05:08.72264', '2026-02-05 07:05:08.72264'),
	(1133, 12, 109, 'A06:2025', 'Insecure Design', '### Background
Focuses on design and architectural flaws. Introduced in 2021. Notable CWEs: CWE-269 (Improper Privilege Management), CWE-434 (Unrestricted File Upload).

### Description
Insecure design is ''missing or ineffective control design''. An insecure design cannot be fixed by a perfect implementation because the necessary controls were never conceived.', '### How to Prevent
- Use threat modeling for critical parts of the application.
- Integrate security language into user stories.
- Establish a library of secure design patterns or ''paved roads''.
- Segregate tenants robustly by design throughout all tiers.
- Write unit and integration tests to validate security requirements.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: A cinema booking system has a 15-ticket limit for discounts but lacks protection against an attacker booking 600 seats across 40 theaters in seconds to disrupt revenue.
- **Scenario #2**: A web app uses ''secret questions'' for password recovery, which are easily guessable or found on social media (NIST 800-63b prohibited).
- **Scenario #3**: An e-commerce site lacks anti-bot design, allowing scalpers to buy high-end video cards instantly on release.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"WEB\",\"2025\",\"Information Disclosure\",\"Vulnerable Components\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:05:09.188156', '2026-02-05 07:05:09.188156'),
	(1134, 12, 109, 'A07:2025', 'Authentication Failures', '### Background
Maintains #7 rank. Notable CWEs: CWE-259 (Hard-coded Password), CWE-287 (Improper Authentication), CWE-384 (Session Fixation).

### Description
Weaknesses in how a system identifies users. This allows attackers to bypass login, reuse stolen credentials, or exploit weak password recovery processes (credential stuffing, brute force).', '### How to Prevent
- Enforce Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA).
- Validate passwords against lists of known breached credentials.
- Align password policies with NIST 800-63b.
- Use secure, built-in session managers; rotate session IDs after login.
- Harden API pathways against account enumeration by using generic error messages.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: Credential stuffing attack where an attacker uses a leaked database from another site to gain access to 5% of your users who reused the same password.
- **Scenario #2**: Session ID is visible in the URL, allowing an attacker to steal and hijack an active session by monitoring network logs.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"WEB\",\"2025\",\"Spoofing\",\"Authentication\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:05:09.649644', '2026-02-05 07:05:09.649644'),
	(1136, 12, 109, 'A09:2025', 'Security Logging and Alerting Failures', '### Background
Ranked #9. Focused on visibility and forensics. Notable CWEs: CWE-117 (Log Injection), CWE-532 (Sensitive Data in Logs), CWE-778.

### Description
Insufficient logging, monitoring, and alerting that prevents the detection and timely response to security incidents.', '### How to Prevent
- Log all login, access control, and server-side validation failures.
- Ensure audit logs are protected from tampering (append-only).
- Establish incident response playbooks for the SOC team.
- Use ''honeytokens'' to detect early reconnaissance.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: A health plan provider couldn''t detect a 7-year breach because they didn''t log failed access attempts to their records database.
- **Scenario #2**: An attacker uses Log Injection to insert fake ''Success'' entries into a log file, hiding their true malicious actions from an automated monitor.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"WEB\",\"2025\",\"Information Disclosure\",\"Logging\",\"Monitoring\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:05:10.588705', '2026-02-05 07:05:10.588705'),
	(1086, 10, 107, 'API3:2023', 'Broken Object Property Level Authorization', 'Broken Object Property Level Authorization', NULL, '"[\"OWASP\",\"API\",\"2023\",\"Information Disclosure\",\"BOPLA\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:27:01.08117', '2026-02-05 00:27:01.08117'),
	(1090, 10, 107, 'API7:2023', 'Server Side Request Forgery', 'Server Side Request Forgery', NULL, '"[\"OWASP\",\"API\",\"2023\",\"Tampering\",\"Server-Side Request Forgery\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:27:01.972675', '2026-02-05 00:27:01.972675'),
	(1091, 10, 107, 'API8:2023', 'Security Misconfiguration', 'Security Misconfiguration', NULL, '"[\"OWASP\",\"API\",\"2023\",\"Security Misconfiguration\"]"', '2026-02-05 00:27:02.193223', '2026-02-05 00:27:02.193223'),
	(1130, 12, 109, 'A03:2025', 'Software Supply Chain Failures', '### Background
Ranked #1 by 50% of community survey respondents. It includes all supply chain failures, not just ones involving known vulnerabilities. Notable CWEs: CWE-1104, CWE-1395.

### Description
Breakdowns in the process of building, distributing, or updating software. Often caused by malicious changes in third-party code, tools, or dependencies.', '### How to Prevent
- Centrally manage a Software Bill of Materials (SBOM).
- Track direct and transitive dependencies continuously.
- Monitor sources like CVE, NVD, and OSV for vulnerabilities.
- Only obtain components from official, trusted sources over secure links.
- Harden code repositories and build servers (MFA, signed builds).

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: SolarWinds (2019) compromise where a trusted vendor''s update was injected with malware.
- **Scenario #2**: Bybit hack (2025) involving wallet software that only behaved maliciously under specific target conditions.
- **Scenario #3**: NPM worm (Shai-Hulud, 2025) which used post-install scripts to exfiltrate developer tokens and spread to other packages.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"WEB\",\"2025\",\"Tampering\",\"Injection\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:05:07.801658', '2026-02-05 07:05:07.801658'),
	(1166, 15, 117, 'SCVS-3.3', 'CI Pipeline', 'Verify that application uses a continuous integration build pipeline.', 'Implementation guidance for Vulnerability Disclosure Ops based on OWASP Software Component Verification Standard v1.0.', NULL, '2026-02-06 09:50:18.919251', '2026-02-06 09:50:18.919251'),
	(1206, 19, 138, 'OSPS-AC-01', 'Two-Factor Authentication', 'Verify that 2FA is required for all project contributors and maintainers.', 'Implementation guidance for Two-Factor Authentication based on OpenSSF OSPS Baseline.', NULL, '2026-02-06 13:40:42.914528', '2026-02-06 13:40:42.914528'),
	(1165, 15, 117, 'SCVS-3.2', 'Build Documentation', 'Verify that documentation exists on how the application is built and instructions for repeating the build.', 'Implementation guidance for Component Vetting based on OWASP Software Component Verification Standard v1.0.', NULL, '2026-02-06 09:50:18.711091', '2026-02-06 09:50:18.711091'),
	(1207, 19, 138, 'OSPS-AC-02', 'Least Privilege Access', 'Verify that contributors are granted only the minimum permissions necessary.', 'Implementation guidance for Least Privilege Access based on OpenSSF OSPS Baseline.', NULL, '2026-02-06 13:40:43.112423', '2026-02-06 13:40:43.112423'),
	(1140, 13, 110, 'API3:2023', 'Broken Object Property Level Authorization', '### Description
Combines ''Excessive Data Exposure'' and ''Mass Assignment''. Occurs when an API returns full objects (relying on the client to filter) or allows users to modify internal properties they shouldn''t.', '### How to Prevent
- Use whitelists for modifiable fields.
- Validate inputs against role-based schemas.
- Return only the fields the user is authorized to see using specific Data Transfer Objects (DTOs).

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: A user updates their own profile but includes `"role": "admin"` in the JSON body. Because the API uses mass assignment, the user becomes an admin.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"API\",\"2023\",\"Information Disclosure\",\"BOPLA\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:05:12.984719', '2026-02-05 07:05:12.984719'),
	(1141, 13, 110, 'API4:2023', 'Unrestricted Resource Consumption', '### Description
APIs that don''t limit resource usage (CPU, memory, storage) are vulnerable to Denial of Service (DoS) or extreme cost increases.', '### How to Prevent
- Enforce rate limiting per user/IP/token.
- Set maximum payload sizes and execution timeouts.
- Use pagination with strict limits for large datasets.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: An attacker requests 100,000 records in a single query (e.g., `size=100000`), causing the database to hang and the server to run out of memory.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"API\",\"2023\",\"Denial of Service\",\"Resource Consumption\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:05:13.454645', '2026-02-05 07:05:13.454645'),
	(1142, 13, 110, 'API5:2023', 'Broken Function Level Authorization', '### Description
Complex access policies with different roles and unclear separation between admin/regular functions lead to authorization flaws.', '### How to Prevent
- Deny all access by default.
- Validate authorization on every request, especially administrative ones.
- Separate administrative endpoints from regular ones.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: A regular user finds that they can call `/api/admin/users/delete` because the server only checked for a valid session, not a ''manager'' role.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"API\",\"2023\",\"Tampering\",\"BFLA\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:05:13.924768', '2026-02-05 07:05:13.924768'),
	(1144, 13, 110, 'API7:2023', 'Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)', '### Description
When an API fetches a remote resource without validating the user-supplied URI, potentially scanning internal networks.', '### How to Prevent
- Filter and validate all client-supplied URLs.
- Use allowlists for permitted destinations.
- Disable unnecessary URL schemes (file://).

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: An attacker provides `http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/` as a profile picture URL, tricking the server into leaking AWS EC2 credentials.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"API\",\"2023\",\"Tampering\",\"Server-Side Request Forgery\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:05:14.852166', '2026-02-05 07:05:14.852166'),
	(1145, 13, 110, 'API8:2023', 'Security Misconfiguration', '### Description
Complex configurations, default credentials, verbose error messages, or insecure TLS settings in the API infrastructure.', '### How to Prevent
- Automate security configuration verification.
- Disable debug modes in production.
- Implement strict Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) policies.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: An API gateway is left with the default admin password, allowing an attacker to reroute traffic to their own malicious server.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"API\",\"2023\",\"Security Misconfiguration\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:05:15.31716', '2026-02-05 07:05:15.31716'),
	(1146, 13, 110, 'API9:2023', 'Improper Inventory Management', '### Description
Lack of documentation or inventory of hosts and API versions leads to exposure of deprecated or shadow endpoints.', '### How to Prevent
- Maintain an up-to-date API inventory.
- Implement clear API versioning and retirement timelines.
- Scan for ''shadow'' or unauthenticated dev/staging endpoints.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: An attacker finds that `v1/users` is still active and lacks the MFA required in `v2/users`, allowing them to bypass security.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"API\",\"2023\",\"Information Disclosure\",\"Inventory\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:05:15.781363', '2026-02-05 07:05:15.781363'),
	(1147, 13, 110, 'API10:2023', 'Unsafe Consumption of APIs', '### Description
Placing more trust in data from third-party APIs than in user input, assuming external APIs are ''inherently safe''.', '### How to Prevent
- Treat data from 3rd-party APIs as untrusted input.
- Use schema validation for all incoming 3rd-party payloads.
- Implement timeouts and circuit breakers for external calls.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: A compromised weather API sends malicious JS in its forecast string, which is then rendered unsanitized on your dashboard, leading to an XSS.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"API\",\"2023\",\"Tampering\",\"Unsafe Consumption\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:05:16.249679', '2026-02-05 07:05:16.249679'),
	(1148, 14, 111, 'ML01:2023', 'Input Manipulation Attack', '### Description
Also known as Adversarial Attacks. Attackers craft inputs with subtle modifications to deceive ML models into making incorrect predictions. These manipulations are often invisible to humans but highly effective against the model''s decision boundaries.', '### How to Prevent
- Implement adversarial training using known attack vectors.
- Use input validation and anomaly detection to filter out-of-distribution data.
- Monitor for drift in prediction confidence scores.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: A self-driving car''s vision system is tricked into seeing a ''Stop'' sign as a ''Speed Limit 45'' by placing small stickers on the sign in a specific pattern.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"ML\",\"2023\",\"AI\",\"Tampering\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:28:08.779557', '2026-02-05 07:28:08.779557'),
	(1150, 14, 111, 'ML03:2023', 'Model Inversion Attack', '### Description
Reverse-engineering the model to extract sensitive information from the training data. For example, recovering personal identities from a face recognition model.', '### How to Prevent
- Implement differential privacy during training.
- Limit access to raw confidence scores in model predictions.
- Use noise injection or output obfuscation to hide precise distributions.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: An attacker queries a medical diagnostic model repeatedly to reconstruct the genetic markers of a specific patient used in the training set.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"ML\",\"2023\",\"AI\",\"Tampering\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:28:09.646205', '2026-02-05 07:28:09.646205'),
	(1139, 13, 110, 'API2:2023', 'Broken Authentication', '### Description
Authentication mechanisms are often incorrectly implemented, allowing attackers to compromise tokens or exploit flaws to assume other identities.', '### How to Prevent
- Implement rate limiting and account lockouts.
- Use secure password hashing and enforce MFA.
- Avoid disclosing the specific reason for login failure (generic ''invalid username or password'').

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: A brute-force login attack succeeds because the API endpoint `/api/login` lacks rate limiting, allowing 10,000 attempts per minute.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"API\",\"2023\",\"Spoofing\",\"Authentication\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:05:12.519164', '2026-02-05 07:05:12.519164'),
	(1159, 15, 115, 'SCVS-1.2', 'Package Manager Usage', 'Verify that package managers are used to manage all third-party binary components.', 'Implementation guidance for Supplier Selection based on OWASP Software Component Verification Standard v1.0.', NULL, '2026-02-06 09:50:17.034069', '2026-02-06 09:50:17.034069'),
	(1160, 15, 115, 'SCVS-1.3', 'Machine-Readable Inventory', 'Verify that an accurate inventory of all third-party components is available in a machine-readable format.', 'Implementation guidance for Internal Component Repository based on OWASP Software Component Verification Standard v1.0.', NULL, '2026-02-06 09:50:17.238113', '2026-02-06 09:50:17.238113'),
	(1154, 14, 111, 'ML07:2023', 'Transfer Learning Attack', '### Description
Exploiting the use of public pre-trained models by embedding malicious ''triggers'' into the base model that remain even after the user fine-tunes it.', '### How to Prevent
- Rigorously test foundation models before use.
- Fine-tune all layers of the model, not just the head.
- Use models only from reputable, signed sources.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: An attacker uploads a performant face recognition base model that has a ''backdoor''—it always authenticates anyone wearing a specific pair of purple glasses.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"ML\",\"2023\",\"AI\",\"Tampering\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:28:11.303344', '2026-02-05 07:28:11.303344'),
	(1156, 14, 111, 'ML09:2023', 'Output Integrity Attack', '### Description
Intercepting and modifying the model''s output before it reaches the end user or a downstream action, without changing the model itself.', '### How to Prevent
- Use digital signatures for model outputs.
- Encrypt output in transit using mTLS.
- Log raw predictions for independent audit.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: An attacker intercepts the output of a medical diagnostic AI and changes a ''Benign'' result to ''Malignant'' to cause secondary harm or distress.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"ML\",\"2023\",\"AI\",\"Tampering\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:28:12.130178', '2026-02-05 07:28:12.130178'),
	(1157, 14, 111, 'ML10:2023', 'Model Poisoning', '### Description
Directly manipulating the model''s parameters (weights/biases) in storage or memory to embed malicious logic.', '### How to Prevent
- Protect model artifacts in encrypted, access-controlled storage.
- Use hash-based integrity checks when loading models from disk.
- Implement reproducible training pipelines.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: An attacker gains access to the production server and swaps the weights file of a character recognition model, causing it to misidentify dollar amounts on checks.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"ML\",\"2023\",\"AI\",\"Tampering\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:28:12.541149', '2026-02-05 07:28:12.541149'),
	(1129, 12, 109, 'A02:2025', 'Security Misconfiguration', '### Background
100% of applications tested were found to have some form of misconfiguration. This category moved up from #5. Notable CWEs: CWE-16 (Configuration), CWE-611 (XXE).

### Description
Security misconfiguration is when a system, application, or cloud service is set up incorrectly from a security perspective, creating vulnerabilities. This includes missing hardening, unnecessary features enabled, default accounts, and informative error messages.', '### How to Prevent
- Implement a repeatable, automated hardening process for all environments.
- Use a minimal platform; remove unused features, components, and samples.
- Review and update configurations as part of the patch management process.
- Use segmented application architecture (containers, security groups).
- Send security headers like HSTS, CSP.
- Use identity federation or short-lived credentials instead of static keys.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: An attacker logs into an unremoved sample admin console using the default ''admin/admin'' credentials.
- **Scenario #2**: Directory listing is enabled, allowing an attacker to download compiled classes, decompile them, and find severe logic flaws.
- **Scenario #3**: Detailed stack traces are returned to the user, exposing component versions and database schema details.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"WEB\",\"2025\",\"Information Disclosure\",\"Cryptography\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:05:07.328091', '2026-02-05 07:05:07.328091'),
	(1131, 12, 109, 'A04:2025', 'Cryptographic Failures', '### Background
Focuses on failures related to the lack of cryptography, weak algorithms, or leaking of cryptographic keys. Notable CWEs: CWE-327, CWE-338 (Weak PRNG).

### Description
Failures to protect data at rest and in transit. This includes using old algorithms (MD5), insecure modes (ECB), or failing to validate server certificates.', '### How to Prevent
- Classify data and encrypt sensitive assets at rest.
- Use authenticated encryption and strong standard algorithms (AES-GCM, Argon2).
- Disable unencrypted protocols (FTP, Telnet).
- Encrypt all data in transit with TLS 1.2+ and forward secrecy.
- Store passwords using strong adaptive hashing functions (Argon2, scrypt).
- Prepare for post-quantum cryptography (PQC).

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: An attacker on an insecure wireless network downgrades an HTTPS connection to HTTP and steals session cookies.
- **Scenario #2**: A password database using unsalted MD5 hashes is stolen; the attacker uses rainbow tables to recover all passwords in minutes.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"WEB\",\"2025\",\"Insecure Design\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:05:08.262165', '2026-02-05 07:05:08.262165'),
	(1153, 14, 111, 'ML06:2023', 'AI Supply Chain Attacks', '### Description
Targeting dependencies like libraries (PyTorch, TensorFlow), pre-trained base models (Hugging Face), or datasets. Malicious code is inserted into the pipeline.', '### How to Prevent
- Maintain an SBOM for all ML dependencies.
- Use hash-based integrity checks for model artifacts.
- Verify signatures for all third-party models and libraries.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: A popular open-source ML utility library is compromised; a post-install script downloads a backdoor that exfiltrates the company''s proprietary model weights.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"ML\",\"2023\",\"AI\",\"Tampering\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:28:10.889282', '2026-02-05 07:28:10.889282'),
	(1155, 14, 111, 'ML08:2023', 'Model Skewing', '### Description
Injecting biased or mislabeled data during online learning to subtly ''nudge'' the model toward an outcome that favors the attacker.', '### How to Prevent
- Continuously monitor for bias and performance drift.
- Implement a ''rollback'' mechanism for model updates.
- Validate and filter feedback loops to exclude malicious data.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: An attacker provides fake positive feedback to a loan-approving AI for applications with specific fraudulent characteristics, eventually ''teaching'' the AI to approve them.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"ML\",\"2023\",\"AI\",\"Tampering\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:28:11.715623', '2026-02-05 07:28:11.715623'),
	(1135, 12, 109, 'A08:2025', 'Software or Data Integrity Failures', '### Background
Focuses on verifying software and data integrity at trust boundaries. Notable CWEs: CWE-502 (Insecure Deserialization), CWE-829 (Untrusted Control Sphere).

### Description
Code and infrastructure that does not protect against invalid or untrusted code/data. Includes unsigned updates and insecure deserialization (e.g., trusting serialized objects from the client).', '### How to Prevent
- Use digital signatures to verify software and data sources.
- Host internal, vetted package repositories.
- Secure CI/CD pipelines with proper separation of duties.
- Do not use unsigned/unencrypted serialized data from untrusted clients.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: An application uses a auto-update feature that downloads binary updates over HTTP without checking their digital signature. An attacker intercepts the request and serves a malicious update.
- **Scenario #2**: Insecure Deserialization where a Java object containing user privileges is serialized into a cookie; an attacker modifies the byte-stream to grant themselves admin rights.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"WEB\",\"2025\",\"Tampering\",\"Data Integrity\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:05:10.11816', '2026-02-05 07:05:10.11816'),
	(1138, 13, 110, 'API1:2023', 'Broken Object Level Authorization', '### Description
APIs expose endpoints that handle object identifiers, creating a wide attack surface. This happens when an application doesn''t verify if the user has authorization for the specific object ID they provided.', '### How to Prevent
- Implement object-level authorization checks for every function accessing a data source using a user-provided ID.
- Validate that the authenticated user owns the resource.
- Prefer random GUIDs over sequential IDs.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: Peloton API flaw allowed users to access other members'' private profile data by simply modifying the user ID in the API request URL.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"API\",\"2023\",\"Tampering\",\"Elevation of Privilege\",\"BOLA\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:05:12.047752', '2026-02-05 07:05:12.047752'),
	(1143, 13, 110, 'API6:2023', 'Unrestricted Access to Sensitive Business Flows', '### Description
When an API exposes a business flow (like buying a ticket) without considering how automated, excessive use could harm the business.', '### How to Prevent
- Identify business flows vulnerable to abuse (e.g., scalping, spam).
- Implement device fingerprinting and human detection (CAPTCHAs).
- Add friction to sensitive operations.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: Bot farms buying up all limited-edition video cards in the first 2 seconds of availability by hammering the checkout API.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"API\",\"2023\",\"Tampering\",\"Business Logic\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:05:14.390219', '2026-02-05 07:05:14.390219'),
	(1149, 14, 111, 'ML02:2023', 'Data Poisoning Attack', '### Description
Manipulating the training data to cause the model to behave in an undesirable way or insert backdoors. This is especially dangerous when using open-source or crowdsourced datasets.', '### How to Prevent
- Ensure training data is carefully validated and secured.
- Use anomaly detection to identify malicious data points in the training set.
- Tighten data source controls and human-in-the-loop review for labeling.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: An attacker injects 10,000 mislabeled images into a public dataset used to train a malware detector, making the detector fail to recognize the attacker''s specific payload.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"ML\",\"2023\",\"AI\",\"Tampering\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:28:09.201151', '2026-02-05 07:28:09.201151'),
	(1151, 14, 111, 'ML04:2023', 'Membership Inference Attack', '### Description
Determining if a specific person''s data was used in the training set. This can leak sensitive info, such as participation in a cancer research trial.', '### How to Prevent
- Prevent overfitting using strong regularization.
- Apply differentially private training mechanisms.
- Aggregate model outputs to reduce precision.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: An attacker determines that a specific public figure''s sensitive health record was part of a clinical study model by observing a tiny ''memorization'' anomaly in the model''s output.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"ML\",\"2023\",\"AI\",\"Tampering\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:28:10.057178', '2026-02-05 07:28:10.057178'),
	(1152, 14, 111, 'ML05:2023', 'Model Theft', '### Description
Duplicating or extracting a model by querying it and using the responses to train a competitor''s ''substitute'' model.', '### How to Prevent
- Implement strict API throttling and authentication.
- Use behavioral fingerprinting to detect automated extraction patterns.
- Limit the precision of returned probability vectors.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: A competitor clones a proprietary stock trading model by sending millions of synthetic queries to its API and training a neural network on the responses.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"ML\",\"2023\",\"AI\",\"Tampering\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:28:10.475732', '2026-02-05 07:28:10.475732'),
	(1128, 12, 109, 'A01:2025', 'Broken Access Control', '### Background
Maintaining its position at #1, 100% of tested applications had some form of broken access control. Notable CWEs: CWE-200 (Exposure of Sensitive Information), CWE-918 (SSRF), CWE-352 (CSRF).

### Description
Access control enforces policy such that users cannot act outside of their intended permissions. Failures typically lead to unauthorized information disclosure, modification or destruction of all data, or performing a business function outside the user''s limits.', '### How to Prevent
- Deny by default except for public resources.
- Implement access control mechanisms once and reuse them throughout the application.
- Model access controls should enforce record ownership.
- Disable web server directory listing and ensure file metadata (e.g., .git) are not present.
- Log access control failures and alert admins.
- Implement rate limits on API and controller access.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: Changing the ''acct'' parameter in the URL (e.g., `?acct=12345` to `?acct=67890`) to view another user''s account details.
- **Scenario #2**: Forcing navigation to an admin page (e.g., `/app/admin_getappInfo`) as a regular user because the server fails to verify the session''s role.
- **Scenario #3**: Accessing administrative APIs directly via `curl` because the access control was only implemented in the frontend JavaScript.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"WEB\",\"2025\",\"Tampering\",\"Elevation of Privilege\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:05:06.845454', '2026-02-05 07:05:06.845454'),
	(1137, 12, 109, 'A10:2025', 'Mishandling of Exceptional Conditions', '### Background
New for 2025. Contains 24 CWEs. Notable CWEs: CWE-209 (Generation of Error Message with Sensitive Info), CWE-636 (Not Failing Securely).

### Description
When programs fail to prevent, detect, and respond to unusual situations, leading to crashes, unknown states, or vulnerabilities (failing open instead of closed).', '### How to Prevent
- Catch exceptions directly and roll back transactions (fail closed).
- Implement a global exception handler for unexpected cases.
- Use rate limiting to prevent resource exhaustion from repeatedly triggered errors.
- Provide generic error messages to users while logging details internally.

### Example Attack Scenarios
- **Scenario #1**: Resource exhaustion where an exception is caught but a database connection isn''t released. An attacker triggers 100 errors to lock the entire pool, causing a DoS.
- **Scenario #2**: A financial transaction is interrupted by a network error after the debit but before the credit. The system fails to roll back, causing a loss of funds.
- **Scenario #3**: A system ''fails open'' when it cannot reach the LDAP server, granting access to anyone because the default behavior was ''permit'' if the check crashed.', '"[\"OWASP\",\"WEB\",\"2025\",\"Tampering\",\"SSRF\"]"', '2026-02-05 07:05:11.06263', '2026-02-05 07:05:11.06263'),
	(1241, 15, 117, 'SCVS-3.8', 'Network Settings Integrity', 'Verify that application build pipeline prohibits alteration of DNS and network settings during build.', 'Implementation guidance for Network Settings Integrity based on OWASP Software Component Verification Standard v1.0.', NULL, '2026-02-06 15:17:37.081441', '2026-02-06 15:17:37.081441'),
	(1243, 15, 117, 'SCVS-3.10', 'Authentication Enforcement', 'Verify that application build pipeline enforces authentication and defaults to deny.', 'Implementation guidance for Authentication Enforcement based on OWASP Software Component Verification Standard v1.0.', NULL, '2026-02-06 15:17:37.514526', '2026-02-06 15:17:37.514526'),
	(1244, 15, 117, 'SCVS-3.11', 'Authorization Enforcement', 'Verify that application build pipeline enforces authorization and defaults to deny.', 'Implementation guidance for Authorization Enforcement based on OWASP Software Component Verification Standard v1.0.', NULL, '2026-02-06 15:17:37.729627', '2026-02-06 15:17:37.729627'),
	(1245, 15, 117, 'SCVS-3.12', 'Separation of Concerns', 'Verify that application build pipeline requires separation of concerns for the modification of system settings.', 'Implementation guidance for Separation of Concerns based on OWASP Software Component Verification Standard v1.0.', NULL, '2026-02-06 15:17:37.9451', '2026-02-06 15:17:37.9451'),
	(1246, 15, 117, 'SCVS-3.13', 'System Audit Log', 'Verify that application build pipeline maintains a verifiable audit log of all system changes.', 'Implementation guidance for System Audit Log based on OWASP Software Component Verification Standard v1.0.', NULL, '2026-02-06 15:17:38.163522', '2026-02-06 15:17:38.163522'),
	(1247, 15, 117, 'SCVS-3.14', 'Job Audit Log', 'Verify that application build pipeline maintains a verifiable audit log of all build job changes.', 'Implementation guidance for Job Audit Log based on OWASP Software Component Verification Standard v1.0.', NULL, '2026-02-06 15:17:38.38101', '2026-02-06 15:17:38.38101'),
	(1248, 15, 117, 'SCVS-3.15', 'Maintenance Cadence', 'Verify that application build pipeline has required maintenance cadence where the entire stack is updated, patched, and re-certified for use.', 'Implementation guidance for Maintenance Cadence based on OWASP Software Component Verification Standard v1.0.', NULL, '2026-02-06 15:17:38.596173', '2026-02-06 15:17:38.596173'),
	(1249, 15, 117, 'SCVS-3.16', 'Tool Analysis', 'Verify that compilers, version control clients, development utilities, and software development kits are analyzed and monitored for tampering, trojans, or malicious code.', 'Implementation guidance for Tool Analysis based on OWASP Software Component Verification Standard v1.0.', NULL, '2026-02-06 15:17:38.814006', '2026-02-06 15:17:38.814006'),
	(1250, 15, 117, 'SCVS-3.17', 'Manipulation Awareness', 'Verify that all build-time manipulations to source or binaries are known and well defined.', 'Implementation guidance for Manipulation Awareness based on OWASP Software Component Verification Standard v1.0.', NULL, '2026-02-06 15:17:39.036967', '2026-02-06 15:17:39.036967'),
	(1251, 15, 117, 'SCVS-3.18', 'Component Checksums', 'Verify that checksums of all first-party and third-party components are documented for every build.', 'Implementation guidance for Component Checksums based on OWASP Software Component Verification Standard v1.0.', NULL, '2026-02-06 15:17:39.255533', '2026-02-06 15:17:39.255533'),
	(1252, 15, 117, 'SCVS-3.19', 'Checksum Delivery', 'Verify that checksums of all components are accessible and delivered out-of-band whenever those components are packaged or distributed.', 'Implementation guidance for Checksum Delivery based on OWASP Software Component Verification Standard v1.0.', NULL, '2026-02-06 15:17:39.470661', '2026-02-06 15:17:39.470661'),
	(1253, 15, 117, 'SCVS-3.20', 'Unused Component ID', 'Verify that unused direct and transitive components have been identified.', 'Implementation guidance for Unused Component ID based on OWASP Software Component Verification Standard v1.0.', NULL, '2026-02-06 15:17:39.687061', '2026-02-06 15:17:39.687061'),
