	(100, 4, 'A.8.30', 'implemented', 'required', 'Asset inventory managed in Jira/Snipe-IT.', NULL, NULL, 'low', '2026-01-03 23:13:46.194007', '2026-01-03 23:13:46.194007', NULL, NULL);
Implement a policy and configure all systems to automatically lock user sessions and hide displayed information after a maximum of 15 minutes of inactivity. For Windows systems, configure the screen saver with a password and set the "Screen saver timeout" and "Wait time for screen saver" in Group Policy or local settings. For macOS, set "Start screen saver after" and require a password immediately in Security & Privacy settings. Apply similar configurations to all managed workstations, servers with local logins, and mobile devices.

A common pitfall is applying settings only to end-user workstations while neglecting administrative consoles, virtual machine consoles, or kiosk devices. Ensure the policy covers all system types. Another pitfall is setting an excessively long timeout (e.g., 30+ minutes), which does not meet the intent of the control. Avoid disabling the lock feature for convenience.

To demonstrate compliance, collect and retain the following evidence: 1) The written policy defining the maximum 15-minute inactivity period and requirement for pattern-hiding. 2) Screenshots or configuration exports (e.g., `gpedit.msc` settings, macOS system preferences) showing the applied timeout and password-on-resume settings. 3) A system inventory list with a sampling method, confirming the settings are deployed uniformly across the environment. 4) Procedure documents for how these settings are managed and enforced.', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-01-06 23:37:48.347408', '2026-01-06 23:38:18.931', NULL, NULL),
	(113, 5, '3.1.1', 'partially_implemented', 'required', '--- AI Remediation Plan ---
First, implement a formal access control policy that defines authorization procedures for users, processes, and devices. Technologically, enforce this by configuring your systems to require unique user accounts with strong authentication (like multi-factor authentication), implementing role-based access controls (RBAC) to assign permissions based on job function, and using network access control (NAC) or device certificates to authorize only known devices. Ensure all service accounts (processes) are documented and have their access reviewed.

A common pitfall is focusing only on user accounts while neglecting service accounts and device-level access, which creates significant vulnerabilities. Another is granting excessive initial permissions or failing to conduct regular access reviews, leading to privilege creep. Avoid using shared accounts, as they break accountability.

To demonstrate compliance, gather this evidence: your documented access control policy, screenshots of Active Directory or system settings showing RBAC groups and assigned permissions, a list of all service accounts with their purposes, network diagrams or NAC configuration showing device authorization rules, and reports from quarterly access reviews showing the recertification of user and service account privileges.', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-01-06 20:17:36.37675', '2026-01-06 23:41:39.861', NULL, NULL),
	(106, 3, 'DE.AE-03', 'partially_implemented', 'required', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-01-05 12:33:24.366682', '2026-01-05 12:33:34.048', NULL, NULL),
	(116, 9, 'A.5.1', 'partial', 'required', 'Policy exists but hasn''t been reviewed in 2 years.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', '2026-01-13 05:32:13.937079', '2026-01-13 05:32:13.937079', 70, NULL),
	(115, 7, 'A.5.3', 'partially_implemented', 'required', '--- AI Remediation Plan ---
First, conduct a formal Segregation of Duties (SoD) analysis by mapping all critical IT and business processes (e.g., user access provisioning, financial transactions, code deployment) to identify roles with conflicting duties [1]. Use this to define and document a clear SoD matrix that explicitly lists incompatible roles and functions, such as preventing a single person from requesting, approving, and implementing a system change.

Technologically, implement automated controls in your identity and access management (IAM) system to enforce the SoD matrix. Configure the system to automatically flag or deny access requests that would create a conflict, and ensure it is integrated with HR processes so role changes trigger access reviews.

A common pitfall is creating a matrix that is too theoretical and not aligned with actual business processes, leading to unworkable restrictions. Avoid relying solely on manual reviews, which are error-prone and not scalable. Also, ensure the SoD policy has executive sponsorship to address inevitable pushback from departments over new access restrictions.

To demonstrate compliance, gather this evidence: the approved SoD policy and matrix, screenshots or reports from the IAM system showing automated SoD rules and denial logs, records of access reviews that specifically check for SoD conflicts, and training records showing relevant personnel have been informed of their SoD responsibilities.', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-01-12 09:51:23.770945', '2026-01-12 09:54:16.982', NULL, NULL),
