<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, operated, or managed by [Company Name], including but not limited to: information systems, networks, applications, databases, data repositories, and physical facilities. It governs all personnel, including employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party agents who require access to these assets.</p>

<p><strong>Access Control:</strong> The process of granting or denying specific requests to: 1) obtain and use information and related information processing services; and 2) enter specific physical facilities.</p><p><strong>Least Privilege:</strong> The security principle that users and systems shall be granted the minimum levels of access—or permissions—necessary to perform their authorized tasks.</p><p><strong>Privileged Access:</strong> Any access that provides elevated capabilities beyond those of a standard user, including system administration, security administration, database administration, and access to sensitive data or critical systems.</p><p><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> An authentication method that requires the user to provide two or more verification factors to gain access to a resource.</p><p><strong>Role-Based Access Control (RBAC):</strong> A method of regulating access to computer or network resources based on the roles of individual users within an enterprise.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that access to its information assets shall be strictly controlled and managed through a formal, risk-based process. Access shall be granted based on the principles of least privilege, need-to-know, and explicit authorization. All access control activities must be documented, monitored, and auditable to ensure accountability and compliance with legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Access Provisioning & Authorization</strong><br>5.1.1 All access requests must be submitted via a formal, documented process and require approval from the data/system owner or their designated manager prior to provisioning.<br>5.1.2 Access rights shall be granted based on predefined job roles (RBAC) and the principle of least privilege. Justification for access exceeding baseline role permissions must be formally documented and approved.<br>5.1.3 A formal user access review for all systems handling sensitive or regulated data shall be conducted by data/system owners at least quarterly. Evidence of review and attestation must be retained.</p><p><strong>5.2 Authentication & Identity Management</strong><br>5.2.1 A unique user ID shall be assigned to each individual for accountability. Shared or generic accounts are strictly prohibited for human users, except where technically unavoidable for system functions, in which case they shall be tightly controlled and monitored.<br>5.2.2 Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) must be enforced for all remote network access (e.g., VPN), access to cloud administration consoles, and access to any system containing sensitive or regulated data as defined by the Data Classification Policy.<br>5.2.3 Password complexity and management shall adhere to the Password Policy. The use of password-less authentication (e.g., FIDO2 security keys) is encouraged where supported.</p><p><strong>5.3 Privileged Access Management (PAM)</strong><br>5.3.1 Privileged accounts (e.g., root, Administrator, SA) must be distinct from standard user accounts. Direct login using privileged accounts for routine tasks is forbidden.<br>5.3.2 Access to privileged accounts shall be brokered through a dedicated Privileged Access Management solution where technically feasible. Sessions must be recorded, logged, and subject to periodic review.<br>5.3.3 Just-in-Time (JIT) access provisioning for privileged tasks is mandated where supported, with elevated privileges granted for a limited, approved duration.</p><p><strong>5.4 Access Revocation & Modification</strong><br>5.4.1 Access rights for personnel shall be revoked or modified immediately upon role change or termination. The IT Department must be formally notified by Human Resources or management within 24 hours of such events.<br>5.4.2 Dormant accounts (inactive for more than 90 days) shall be automatically disabled. Accounts inactive for more than 120 days shall be deleted, pending archival of associated logs as required by the Data Retention Policy.</p><p><strong>5.5 Logging, Monitoring & Audit</strong><br>5.5.1 All successful and failed authentication attempts, privilege escalations, and access to sensitive data sets must be logged. Logs must include user ID, timestamp, action performed, and target system/asset.<br>5.5.2 Access logs for critical systems and all privileged access sessions shall be centrally aggregated, protected from tampering, and retained for a minimum of one year to support forensic investigation and audit requirements.<br>5.5.3 Automated monitoring and alerting shall be configured for anomalous access patterns, including after-hours access from unusual locations, repeated failed logins, and bulk data extraction.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to align with control objectives from ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.5.15, A.5.16, A.5.17, A.8.2, A.8.3, A.8.4) and relevant SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Confidentiality). Compliance with this policy is mandatory to satisfy contractual obligations, regulatory mandates (e.g., GDPR, HIPAA, CCPA), and industry standards. Deviations from this policy require a formal risk assessment and documented exception approved by the CISO.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimately responsible for the development, implementation, and maintenance of this policy. Approves major changes and policy exceptions.</p><p><strong>Data/System Owners:</strong> Responsible for authorizing access requests, conducting periodic access reviews, and classifying the data within their purview. They define appropriate access levels.</p><p><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Responsible for the technical implementation of access controls, provisioning/de-provisioning user accounts, maintaining identity management systems, and generating access review reports.</p><p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for safeguarding their authentication credentials, reporting suspected policy violations or unauthorized access attempts, and adhering to the access controls defined for their role.</p><p><strong>Human Resources (HR):</strong> Responsible for promptly notifying IT of personnel status changes (e.g., hiring, role change, termination) that trigger access modifications.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal prosecution. Violations by third-party agents may result in termination of the contractual agreement. The CISO or designated authority is responsible for investigating suspected violations and determining appropriate corrective actions.</p>

<p>This Access Control Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO or designated authority at least annually, or following significant security incidents, changes in technology, or updates to applicable laws and regulations. Proposed revisions must be formally documented, approved by the CISO, and communicated to all relevant stakeholders.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Access Control Policy establishes the mandatory framework for managing, controlling, and auditing logical and physical access to [Company Name]''s information assets. It defines the principles, standards, and procedures to ensure that access is granted based on business need, least privilege, and strict accountability, thereby protecting the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, operated, or managed by [Company Name], including but not limited to: information systems, networks, applications, databases, data repositories, and physical facilities. It governs all personnel, including employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party agents who require access to these assets.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Access Control:</strong> The process of granting or denying specific requests to: 1) obtain and use information and related information processing services; and 2) enter specific physical facilities.</p><p><strong>Least Privilege:</strong> The security principle that users and systems shall be granted the minimum levels of access—or permissions—necessary to perform their authorized tasks.</p><p><strong>Privileged Access:</strong> Any access that provides elevated capabilities beyond those of a standard user, including system administration, security administration, database administration, and access to sensitive data or critical systems.</p><p><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> An authentication method that requires the user to provide two or more verification factors to gain access to a resource.</p><p><strong>Role-Based Access Control (RBAC):</strong> A method of regulating access to computer or network resources based on the roles of individual users within an enterprise.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates that access to its information assets shall be strictly controlled and managed through a formal, risk-based process. Access shall be granted based on the principles of least privilege, need-to-know, and explicit authorization. All access control activities must be documented, monitored, and auditable to ensure accountability and compliance with legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>5.1 Access Provisioning & Authorization</strong><br>5.1.1 All access requests must be submitted via a formal, documented process and require approval from the data/system owner or their designated manager prior to provisioning.<br>5.1.2 Access rights shall be granted based on predefined job roles (RBAC) and the principle of least privilege. Justification for access exceeding baseline role permissions must be formally documented and approved.<br>5.1.3 A formal user access review for all systems handling sensitive or regulated data shall be conducted by data/system owners at least quarterly. Evidence of review and attestation must be retained.</p><p><strong>5.2 Authentication & Identity Management</strong><br>5.2.1 A unique user ID shall be assigned to each individual for accountability. Shared or generic accounts are strictly prohibited for human users, except where technically unavoidable for system functions, in which case they shall be tightly controlled and monitored.<br>5.2.2 Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) must be enforced for all remote network access (e.g., VPN), access to cloud administration consoles, and access to any system containing sensitive or regulated data as defined by the Data Classification Policy.<br>5.2.3 Password complexity and management shall adhere to the Password Policy. The use of password-less authentication (e.g., FIDO2 security keys) is encouraged where supported.</p><p><strong>5.3 Privileged Access Management (PAM)</strong><br>5.3.1 Privileged accounts (e.g., root, Administrator, SA) must be distinct from standard user accounts. Direct login using privileged accounts for routine tasks is forbidden.<br>5.3.2 Access to privileged accounts shall be brokered through a dedicated Privileged Access Management solution where technically feasible. Sessions must be recorded, logged, and subject to periodic review.<br>5.3.3 Just-in-Time (JIT) access provisioning for privileged tasks is mandated where supported, with elevated privileges granted for a limited, approved duration.</p><p><strong>5.4 Access Revocation & Modification</strong><br>5.4.1 Access rights for personnel shall be revoked or modified immediately upon role change or termination. The IT Department must be formally notified by Human Resources or management within 24 hours of such events.<br>5.4.2 Dormant accounts (inactive for more than 90 days) shall be automatically disabled. Accounts inactive for more than 120 days shall be deleted, pending archival of associated logs as required by the Data Retention Policy.</p><p><strong>5.5 Logging, Monitoring & Audit</strong><br>5.5.1 All successful and failed authentication attempts, privilege escalations, and access to sensitive data sets must be logged. Logs must include user ID, timestamp, action performed, and target system/asset.<br>5.5.2 Access logs for critical systems and all privileged access sessions shall be centrally aggregated, protected from tampering, and retained for a minimum of one year to support forensic investigation and audit requirements.<br>5.5.3 Automated monitoring and alerting shall be configured for anomalous access patterns, including after-hours access from unusual locations, repeated failed logins, and bulk data extraction.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy is designed to align with control objectives from ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.5.15, A.5.16, A.5.17, A.8.2, A.8.3, A.8.4) and relevant SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Confidentiality). Compliance with this policy is mandatory to satisfy contractual obligations, regulatory mandates (e.g., GDPR, HIPAA, CCPA), and industry standards. Deviations from this policy require a formal risk assessment and documented exception approved by the CISO.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimately responsible for the development, implementation, and maintenance of this policy. Approves major changes and policy exceptions.</p><p><strong>Data/System Owners:</strong> Responsible for authorizing access requests, conducting periodic access reviews, and classifying the data within their purview. They define appropriate access levels.</p><p><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Responsible for the technical implementation of access controls, provisioning/de-provisioning user accounts, maintaining identity management systems, and generating access review reports.</p><p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for safeguarding their authentication credentials, reporting suspected policy violations or unauthorized access attempts, and adhering to the access controls defined for their role.</p><p><strong>Human Resources (HR):</strong> Responsible for promptly notifying IT of personnel status changes (e.g., hiring, role change, termination) that trigger access modifications.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal prosecution. Violations by third-party agents may result in termination of the contractual agreement. The CISO or designated authority is responsible for investigating suspected violations and determining appropriate corrective actions.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This Access Control Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO or designated authority at least annually, or following significant security incidents, changes in technology, or updates to applicable laws and regulations. Proposed revisions must be formally documented, approved by the CISO, and communicated to all relevant stakeholders.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-19 06:15:54.505066', '"[\"ISO 27001 / SOC 2\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 20:03:23.409', NULL),
	(30, '40746', 'Capacity Management Policy', '<p>The purpose of this Capacity Management Policy is to establish a formal, documented, and proactive framework for ensuring that the information technology (IT) resources of [Company Name] possess sufficient capacity to meet current and future business requirements in a cost-effective and timely manner. This policy mandates the systematic planning, monitoring, analysis, and tuning of all IT infrastructure components to prevent performance degradation, service disruption, or security compromise due to resource exhaustion.</p>

<p>This Capacity Management Policy applies to all information technology resources owned, operated, or leased by [Company Name], including but not limited to: compute (servers, virtual machines, containers), storage (SAN, NAS, cloud object storage), network (bandwidth, switching/routing capacity), database systems, and critical application platforms. It encompasses all environments: production, development, testing, and disaster recovery. All personnel, including employees, contractors, and third-party service providers, who are involved in the design, provisioning, operation, or management of these resources, are bound by this policy.</p>

<p><strong>Business Capacity Management (BCM):</strong> The process of translating business plans and forecasts into quantifiable IT service capacity requirements to ensure future business needs are understood and planned for.</p><p><strong>Service Capacity Management (SCM):</strong> The process focused on managing the performance of live, operational IT services, ensuring they meet their agreed-upon service level targets.</p><p><strong>Component Capacity Management (CCM):</strong> The process focused on managing the performance, utilization, and capacity of individual technology components (e.g., CPU, memory, disk I/O, network interfaces).</p><p><strong>Capacity Plan:</strong> A forward-looking document that details current resource utilization, forecasts future demand based on business drivers, and specifies the initiatives required to ensure adequate capacity, including associated costs, resource requirements, and implementation timelines.</p><p><strong>Threshold:</strong> A predefined utilization or performance level which, when breached, triggers an alert and initiates a defined management action.</p><p><strong>Baselining:</strong> The process of establishing a known performance and utilization standard under normal operating conditions for comparison against future measurements.</p>

<p>[Company Name] shall implement and maintain a formal, documented, and proactive Capacity Management process aligned with the ISO/IEC 27001:2022 framework, specifically supporting control A.8.9 (Management of technical vulnerabilities). The organization is committed to ensuring IT service capacity aligns with current and agreed future business needs. Reactive, ad-hoc capacity management is strictly prohibited. All capacity-related decisions must be data-driven, based on monitored metrics, documented forecasts, and approved plans.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Formal Capacity Management Process:</strong> A documented Capacity Management process shall be established, maintained, and enforced. This process must integrate Business (BCM), Service (SCM), and Component (CCM) capacity management activities and define clear workflows for demand forecasting, modeling, planning, and implementation of changes.</p><p><strong>5.2 Proactive Monitoring and Baselining:</strong> All in-scope IT resources shall be continuously monitored for key performance and capacity metrics (e.g., CPU utilization, memory consumption, disk I/O, network throughput, transaction volumes). Historical baselines for normal and peak operational states must be established, documented, and reviewed quarterly.</p><p><strong>5.3 Threshold Management and Alerting:</strong> Proactive warning and critical thresholds shall be defined for all monitored metrics. Threshold breaches must trigger automated alerts to the responsible IT operations team via the approved monitoring and alerting platform. Alert response procedures must be documented.</p><p><strong>5.4 Regular Capacity Analysis and Reporting:</strong> Capacity and performance data shall be analyzed at least monthly. A formal Capacity Report must be produced quarterly, detailing current utilization trends, threshold breaches, forecasted demand against available capacity, and any identified risks of capacity shortfall.</p><p><strong>5.5 Annual Capacity Planning:</strong> A forward-looking Capacity Plan shall be developed, reviewed, and approved annually. The plan must forecast demand for a minimum 12-month horizon, identify required upgrades or new procurements, and include a cost-benefit analysis. The plan must be formally approved by the CIO or designated authority.</p><p><strong>5.6 Change Integration:</strong> All proposed changes to IT services, applications, or infrastructure (via the Change Management process) must include a capacity impact assessment. Changes with a significant or unknown capacity impact shall require explicit approval from the Capacity Manager or designated authority before implementation.</p><p><strong>5.7 Performance and Load Testing:</strong> Prior to deployment into production, new or significantly modified business-critical applications must undergo performance and load testing to validate capacity requirements and identify scaling characteristics under expected and peak loads.</p><p><strong>5.8 Supplier Capacity Management:</strong> For services dependent on third-party suppliers or cloud providers, contractual agreements (SLAs) must include explicit capacity and performance commitments. The responsible team shall monitor supplier performance against these commitments and manage supplier reviews to ensure capacity obligations are met.</p>

<p>Capacity Management activities shall be conducted in a manner that supports compliance with all relevant legal, statutory, regulatory, and contractual obligations of [Company Name]. This includes, but is not limited to, ensuring sufficient capacity to maintain data availability requirements stipulated in service level agreements (SLAs), data processing agreements (DPAs), and regulatory frameworks (e.g., data residency laws). The Capacity Management process shall provide documented evidence for audits demonstrating proactive control over resource availability, a key component of security and service continuity.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Officer (CIO) / Head of IT:</strong> Ultimately accountable for the effectiveness of the Capacity Management process. Responsible for approving the annual Capacity Plan and allocating necessary budget and resources.</p><p><strong>Capacity Manager (Designated Role):</strong> Responsible for the day-to-day operation of the Capacity Management process. Duties include producing capacity reports, maintaining the Capacity Plan, analyzing trends, chairing capacity review meetings, and acting as the central point of contact for all capacity-related issues.</p><p><strong>IT Operations Teams:</strong> Responsible for the implementation of monitoring, collection of capacity data, responding to capacity alerts, and executing approved capacity-related changes (e.g., upgrades, scaling actions).</p><p><strong>Application and Service Owners:</strong> Responsible for providing business demand forecasts, participating in capacity impact assessments for changes, and validating that service capacity meets business requirements.</p><p><strong>Procurement/Finance:</strong> Responsible for facilitating the procurement of hardware, software, or cloud resources as specified in the approved Capacity Plan.</p>

<p>Compliance with this policy is mandatory for all individuals and systems within its scope. Violations of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and may lead to civil or criminal liability. The CIO, in conjunction with Information Security, is authorized to monitor compliance and enforce this policy. Deliberate circumvention of capacity controls or willful neglect of capacity warnings that lead to a service outage shall be treated as a serious security and operational incident.</p>

<p>This Capacity Management Policy shall be reviewed by the CIO or designated authority at least annually, or following significant changes to the business model, technology landscape, or regulatory environment. Revisions must be formally approved and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. The policy version, revision date, and change history shall be meticulously maintained.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>The purpose of this Capacity Management Policy is to establish a formal, documented, and proactive framework for ensuring that the information technology (IT) resources of [Company Name] possess sufficient capacity to meet current and future business requirements in a cost-effective and timely manner. This policy mandates the systematic planning, monitoring, analysis, and tuning of all IT infrastructure components to prevent performance degradation, service disruption, or security compromise due to resource exhaustion.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This Capacity Management Policy applies to all information technology resources owned, operated, or leased by [Company Name], including but not limited to: compute (servers, virtual machines, containers), storage (SAN, NAS, cloud object storage), network (bandwidth, switching/routing capacity), database systems, and critical application platforms. It encompasses all environments: production, development, testing, and disaster recovery. All personnel, including employees, contractors, and third-party service providers, who are involved in the design, provisioning, operation, or management of these resources, are bound by this policy.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Business Capacity Management (BCM):</strong> The process of translating business plans and forecasts into quantifiable IT service capacity requirements to ensure future business needs are understood and planned for.</p><p><strong>Service Capacity Management (SCM):</strong> The process focused on managing the performance of live, operational IT services, ensuring they meet their agreed-upon service level targets.</p><p><strong>Component Capacity Management (CCM):</strong> The process focused on managing the performance, utilization, and capacity of individual technology components (e.g., CPU, memory, disk I/O, network interfaces).</p><p><strong>Capacity Plan:</strong> A forward-looking document that details current resource utilization, forecasts future demand based on business drivers, and specifies the initiatives required to ensure adequate capacity, including associated costs, resource requirements, and implementation timelines.</p><p><strong>Threshold:</strong> A predefined utilization or performance level which, when breached, triggers an alert and initiates a defined management action.</p><p><strong>Baselining:</strong> The process of establishing a known performance and utilization standard under normal operating conditions for comparison against future measurements.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] shall implement and maintain a formal, documented, and proactive Capacity Management process aligned with the ISO/IEC 27001:2022 framework, specifically supporting control A.8.9 (Management of technical vulnerabilities). The organization is committed to ensuring IT service capacity aligns with current and agreed future business needs. Reactive, ad-hoc capacity management is strictly prohibited. All capacity-related decisions must be data-driven, based on monitored metrics, documented forecasts, and approved plans.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>5.1 Formal Capacity Management Process:</strong> A documented Capacity Management process shall be established, maintained, and enforced. This process must integrate Business (BCM), Service (SCM), and Component (CCM) capacity management activities and define clear workflows for demand forecasting, modeling, planning, and implementation of changes.</p><p><strong>5.2 Proactive Monitoring and Baselining:</strong> All in-scope IT resources shall be continuously monitored for key performance and capacity metrics (e.g., CPU utilization, memory consumption, disk I/O, network throughput, transaction volumes). Historical baselines for normal and peak operational states must be established, documented, and reviewed quarterly.</p><p><strong>5.3 Threshold Management and Alerting:</strong> Proactive warning and critical thresholds shall be defined for all monitored metrics. Threshold breaches must trigger automated alerts to the responsible IT operations team via the approved monitoring and alerting platform. Alert response procedures must be documented.</p><p><strong>5.4 Regular Capacity Analysis and Reporting:</strong> Capacity and performance data shall be analyzed at least monthly. A formal Capacity Report must be produced quarterly, detailing current utilization trends, threshold breaches, forecasted demand against available capacity, and any identified risks of capacity shortfall.</p><p><strong>5.5 Annual Capacity Planning:</strong> A forward-looking Capacity Plan shall be developed, reviewed, and approved annually. The plan must forecast demand for a minimum 12-month horizon, identify required upgrades or new procurements, and include a cost-benefit analysis. The plan must be formally approved by the CIO or designated authority.</p><p><strong>5.6 Change Integration:</strong> All proposed changes to IT services, applications, or infrastructure (via the Change Management process) must include a capacity impact assessment. Changes with a significant or unknown capacity impact shall require explicit approval from the Capacity Manager or designated authority before implementation.</p><p><strong>5.7 Performance and Load Testing:</strong> Prior to deployment into production, new or significantly modified business-critical applications must undergo performance and load testing to validate capacity requirements and identify scaling characteristics under expected and peak loads.</p><p><strong>5.8 Supplier Capacity Management:</strong> For services dependent on third-party suppliers or cloud providers, contractual agreements (SLAs) must include explicit capacity and performance commitments. The responsible team shall monitor supplier performance against these commitments and manage supplier reviews to ensure capacity obligations are met.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>Capacity Management activities shall be conducted in a manner that supports compliance with all relevant legal, statutory, regulatory, and contractual obligations of [Company Name]. This includes, but is not limited to, ensuring sufficient capacity to maintain data availability requirements stipulated in service level agreements (SLAs), data processing agreements (DPAs), and regulatory frameworks (e.g., data residency laws). The Capacity Management process shall provide documented evidence for audits demonstrating proactive control over resource availability, a key component of security and service continuity.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Chief Information Officer (CIO) / Head of IT:</strong> Ultimately accountable for the effectiveness of the Capacity Management process. Responsible for approving the annual Capacity Plan and allocating necessary budget and resources.</p><p><strong>Capacity Manager (Designated Role):</strong> Responsible for the day-to-day operation of the Capacity Management process. Duties include producing capacity reports, maintaining the Capacity Plan, analyzing trends, chairing capacity review meetings, and acting as the central point of contact for all capacity-related issues.</p><p><strong>IT Operations Teams:</strong> Responsible for the implementation of monitoring, collection of capacity data, responding to capacity alerts, and executing approved capacity-related changes (e.g., upgrades, scaling actions).</p><p><strong>Application and Service Owners:</strong> Responsible for providing business demand forecasts, participating in capacity impact assessments for changes, and validating that service capacity meets business requirements.</p><p><strong>Procurement/Finance:</strong> Responsible for facilitating the procurement of hardware, software, or cloud resources as specified in the approved Capacity Plan.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Compliance with this policy is mandatory for all individuals and systems within its scope. Violations of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and may lead to civil or criminal liability. The CIO, in conjunction with Information Security, is authorized to monitor compliance and enforce this policy. Deliberate circumvention of capacity controls or willful neglect of capacity warnings that lead to a service outage shall be treated as a serious security and operational incident.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This Capacity Management Policy shall be reviewed by the CIO or designated authority at least annually, or following significant changes to the business model, technology landscape, or regulatory environment. Revisions must be formally approved and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. The policy version, revision date, and change history shall be meticulously maintained.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-20 21:17:22.403385', '"[\"ISO 27001\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 20:03:58.67', NULL),
	(8, '3', 'Incident Response Policy', '<h2>Purpose</h2><p>This Incident Response Policy establishes the formal authority, structure, and mandatory procedures for [Company Name] to detect, respond to, contain, eradicate, and recover from information security incidents in a timely, effective, and legally compliant manner. The policy mandates a consistent, documented approach to minimize operational, financial, reputational, and legal impact, and to ensure continuous improvement of the organization''s security posture.</p>

<h2>Scope</h2><p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, managed, entrusted to, or processed by [Company Name], including but not limited to data, systems, networks, applications, and physical facilities. It is binding for all personnel (employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party agents) who access, use, or manage these assets. The policy governs all phases of incident response, from preparation and detection through post-incident review.</p>

<h2>Definitions</h2><ul><li><strong>Security Incident:</strong> Any attempted or actual unauthorized access, use, disclosure, modification, or destruction of information, or interference with system operations in an information system, constituting a violation or imminent threat of violation of security policies, acceptable use policies, or standard security practices.</li><li><strong>Data Breach:</strong> A confirmed incident where sensitive, protected, or confidential data has been accessed, disclosed, or exfiltrated in an unauthorized manner.</li><li><strong>Incident Response Team (IRT):</strong> The formally designated, cross-functional team responsible for managing the response to a security incident.</li><li><strong>Containment:</strong> The immediate actions taken to limit the scope and impact of an incident.</li><li><strong>Forensic Evidence:</strong> Data collected and preserved in a legally admissible manner to support incident analysis, recovery, and potential legal proceedings.</li><li><strong>Mean Time to Acknowledge (MTTA):</strong> The target maximum elapsed time from incident detection to formal assignment and initiation of response actions.</li><li><strong>Mean Time to Resolve (MTTR):</strong> The target maximum elapsed time from incident detection to closure, including full containment, eradication, and recovery.</li></ul>

<h2>Policy Statement</h2><p>[Company Name] mandates a proactive, structured, and continuous approach to information security incident management. All personnel shall immediately report suspected or confirmed security incidents in accordance with this policy. The organization shall maintain a formal, tested Incident Response Plan (IRP) and dedicate appropriate resources to ensure rapid detection, effective response, thorough remediation, and organizational learning from all incidents.</p>

<h2>Policy Requirements</h2><h3>5.1 Incident Response Plan (IRP) Maintenance</h3><p>A comprehensive, written Incident Response Plan (IRP) shall be developed, approved by the CISO, and maintained by the Information Security team. The IRP must be reviewed and updated at least annually, or following any significant security incident, change in infrastructure, or regulatory update. The IRP shall be made available to all members of the Incident Response Team and key stakeholders.</p><h3>5.2 Incident Classification and Prioritization</h3><p>All detected incidents shall be classified according to a predefined severity matrix based on impact criteria (e.g., data sensitivity, system criticality, financial loss, regulatory impact) and urgency. Severity levels (e.g., Critical, High, Medium, Low) shall dictate escalation paths, response timelines, and resource allocation. Classification must occur within the MTTA target defined in the IRP.</p><h3>5.3 Detection and Reporting</h3><p>All personnel are required to report any suspected security incident immediately to the designated Security Operations Center (SOC) or via the approved incident reporting channel. Automated detection systems (SIEM, IDS/IPS, EDR) shall be configured to generate alerts for anomalous activity. Logs from all critical systems must be centrally collected and retained for a minimum of 90 days to facilitate detection and investigation.</p><h3>5.4 Response and Containment Procedures</h3><p>Upon declaration of an incident, the IRT shall execute predefined containment strategies to isolate affected systems and prevent further damage. Containment actions may include network segmentation, disabling accounts, taking systems offline, or applying firewall rules. All containment actions must be documented, and their potential business impact assessed prior to execution where feasible.</p><h3>5.5 Eradication, Recovery, and Forensics</h3><p>Following containment, the root cause of the incident shall be identified and eradicated. This may involve removing malware, patching vulnerabilities, or reconfiguring systems. Recovery shall restore systems and data from known-good, validated backups. A forensic evidence chain of custody shall be maintained for all incidents classified as High or Critical. Forensic images of affected systems must be created prior to eradication if legally or investigatively required.</p><h3>5.6 Communication and Notification</h3><p>Internal and external communications during an incident shall follow strict protocols defined in the IRP. Unauthorized public communication is strictly prohibited. Legal and Compliance teams must be engaged immediately to determine if regulatory, contractual, or legal notification obligations (e.g., to data subjects, regulators, partners) are triggered. All notifications must be documented.</p><h3>5.7 Post-Incident Analysis and Reporting</h3><p>A formal Post-Incident Review (PIR) report shall be completed for every High or Critical severity incident, and for Medium severity incidents at the CISO''s discretion. The report must include a timeline, root cause analysis, impact assessment, effectiveness of response actions, and specific corrective actions to prevent recurrence. Lessons learned shall be integrated into security controls, policies, and training programs.</p><h3>5.8 Training and Testing</h3><p>All members of the IRT shall receive annual role-specific training on incident response procedures. A tabletop exercise simulating a significant security incident shall be conducted at least annually. A full-scale technical simulation or red team exercise shall be conducted at least biennially. Results from all tests and exercises shall be used to update the IRP.</p>

<h2>Compliance and Legal Requirements</h2><p>All incident response activities shall be conducted in compliance with applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data breach notification laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, state laws), industry standards (e.g., PCI DSS), and contractual SLAs. Evidence preservation shall adhere to legal standards for admissibility. The Legal Department must be consulted prior to any external communications regarding an incident that may have legal or regulatory implications.</p>

<h2>Roles and Responsibilities</h2><p><strong>Executive Management / Board of Directors:</strong> Provides ultimate authority and resources for the IRP; is informed of Critical incidents.</p><p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Owns this policy and the IRP; declares major incidents; chairs the IRT; approves final PIR reports.</p><p><strong>Incident Response Team (IRT) Lead:</strong> Manages the tactical response during an incident; coordinates team activities; serves as primary point of contact.</p><p><strong>Information Security Analysts:</strong> Execute technical investigation, containment, eradication, and forensic analysis.</p><p><strong>IT Operations:</strong> Implements containment and recovery actions on infrastructure; provides system expertise.</p><p><strong>Legal & Compliance:</strong> Advises on regulatory notification requirements, legal risks, and evidence handling.</p><p><strong>Corporate Communications / PR:</strong> Manages all external and internal stakeholder communications as directed by the IRT Lead and Legal.</p><p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must complete security awareness training including incident reporting procedures; are required to report suspected incidents immediately.</p>

<h2>Enforcement</h2><p>Compliance with this Incident Response Policy is mandatory for all covered individuals and entities. Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and potential legal liability. Failure to report a suspected incident, or interference with an incident response investigation, is considered a serious violation.</p>

<h2>Review and Revision</h2><p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the Information Security Governance Committee at least annually. Revisions shall be made in response to changes in the threat landscape, legal and regulatory requirements, technology infrastructure, or lessons learned from incident response activities. The most current version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel through the company''s policy management system.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"Purpose\",\"content\":\"<h2>Purpose</h2><p>This Incident Response Policy establishes the formal authority, structure, and mandatory procedures for [Company Name] to detect, respond to, contain, eradicate, and recover from information security incidents in a timely, effective, and legally compliant manner. The policy mandates a consistent, documented approach to minimize operational, financial, reputational, and legal impact, and to ensure continuous improvement of the organization''s security posture.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Scope\",\"content\":\"<h2>Scope</h2><p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, managed, entrusted to, or processed by [Company Name], including but not limited to data, systems, networks, applications, and physical facilities. It is binding for all personnel (employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party agents) who access, use, or manage these assets. The policy governs all phases of incident response, from preparation and detection through post-incident review.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Definitions\",\"content\":\"<h2>Definitions</h2><ul><li><strong>Security Incident:</strong> Any attempted or actual unauthorized access, use, disclosure, modification, or destruction of information, or interference with system operations in an information system, constituting a violation or imminent threat of violation of security policies, acceptable use policies, or standard security practices.</li><li><strong>Data Breach:</strong> A confirmed incident where sensitive, protected, or confidential data has been accessed, disclosed, or exfiltrated in an unauthorized manner.</li><li><strong>Incident Response Team (IRT):</strong> The formally designated, cross-functional team responsible for managing the response to a security incident.</li><li><strong>Containment:</strong> The immediate actions taken to limit the scope and impact of an incident.</li><li><strong>Forensic Evidence:</strong> Data collected and preserved in a legally admissible manner to support incident analysis, recovery, and potential legal proceedings.</li><li><strong>Mean Time to Acknowledge (MTTA):</strong> The target maximum elapsed time from incident detection to formal assignment and initiation of response actions.</li><li><strong>Mean Time to Resolve (MTTR):</strong> The target maximum elapsed time from incident detection to closure, including full containment, eradication, and recovery.</li></ul>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<h2>Policy Statement</h2><p>[Company Name] mandates a proactive, structured, and continuous approach to information security incident management. All personnel shall immediately report suspected or confirmed security incidents in accordance with this policy. The organization shall maintain a formal, tested Incident Response Plan (IRP) and dedicate appropriate resources to ensure rapid detection, effective response, thorough remediation, and organizational learning from all incidents.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h2>Policy Requirements</h2><h3>5.1 Incident Response Plan (IRP) Maintenance</h3><p>A comprehensive, written Incident Response Plan (IRP) shall be developed, approved by the CISO, and maintained by the Information Security team. The IRP must be reviewed and updated at least annually, or following any significant security incident, change in infrastructure, or regulatory update. The IRP shall be made available to all members of the Incident Response Team and key stakeholders.</p><h3>5.2 Incident Classification and Prioritization</h3><p>All detected incidents shall be classified according to a predefined severity matrix based on impact criteria (e.g., data sensitivity, system criticality, financial loss, regulatory impact) and urgency. Severity levels (e.g., Critical, High, Medium, Low) shall dictate escalation paths, response timelines, and resource allocation. Classification must occur within the MTTA target defined in the IRP.</p><h3>5.3 Detection and Reporting</h3><p>All personnel are required to report any suspected security incident immediately to the designated Security Operations Center (SOC) or via the approved incident reporting channel. Automated detection systems (SIEM, IDS/IPS, EDR) shall be configured to generate alerts for anomalous activity. Logs from all critical systems must be centrally collected and retained for a minimum of 90 days to facilitate detection and investigation.</p><h3>5.4 Response and Containment Procedures</h3><p>Upon declaration of an incident, the IRT shall execute predefined containment strategies to isolate affected systems and prevent further damage. Containment actions may include network segmentation, disabling accounts, taking systems offline, or applying firewall rules. All containment actions must be documented, and their potential business impact assessed prior to execution where feasible.</p><h3>5.5 Eradication, Recovery, and Forensics</h3><p>Following containment, the root cause of the incident shall be identified and eradicated. This may involve removing malware, patching vulnerabilities, or reconfiguring systems. Recovery shall restore systems and data from known-good, validated backups. A forensic evidence chain of custody shall be maintained for all incidents classified as High or Critical. Forensic images of affected systems must be created prior to eradication if legally or investigatively required.</p><h3>5.6 Communication and Notification</h3><p>Internal and external communications during an incident shall follow strict protocols defined in the IRP. Unauthorized public communication is strictly prohibited. Legal and Compliance teams must be engaged immediately to determine if regulatory, contractual, or legal notification obligations (e.g., to data subjects, regulators, partners) are triggered. All notifications must be documented.</p><h3>5.7 Post-Incident Analysis and Reporting</h3><p>A formal Post-Incident Review (PIR) report shall be completed for every High or Critical severity incident, and for Medium severity incidents at the CISO''s discretion. The report must include a timeline, root cause analysis, impact assessment, effectiveness of response actions, and specific corrective actions to prevent recurrence. Lessons learned shall be integrated into security controls, policies, and training programs.</p><h3>5.8 Training and Testing</h3><p>All members of the IRT shall receive annual role-specific training on incident response procedures. A tabletop exercise simulating a significant security incident shall be conducted at least annually. A full-scale technical simulation or red team exercise shall be conducted at least biennially. Results from all tests and exercises shall be used to update the IRP.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h2>Compliance and Legal Requirements</h2><p>All incident response activities shall be conducted in compliance with applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data breach notification laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, state laws), industry standards (e.g., PCI DSS), and contractual SLAs. Evidence preservation shall adhere to legal standards for admissibility. The Legal Department must be consulted prior to any external communications regarding an incident that may have legal or regulatory implications.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<h2>Roles and Responsibilities</h2><p><strong>Executive Management / Board of Directors:</strong> Provides ultimate authority and resources for the IRP; is informed of Critical incidents.</p><p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Owns this policy and the IRP; declares major incidents; chairs the IRT; approves final PIR reports.</p><p><strong>Incident Response Team (IRT) Lead:</strong> Manages the tactical response during an incident; coordinates team activities; serves as primary point of contact.</p><p><strong>Information Security Analysts:</strong> Execute technical investigation, containment, eradication, and forensic analysis.</p><p><strong>IT Operations:</strong> Implements containment and recovery actions on infrastructure; provides system expertise.</p><p><strong>Legal & Compliance:</strong> Advises on regulatory notification requirements, legal risks, and evidence handling.</p><p><strong>Corporate Communications / PR:</strong> Manages all external and internal stakeholder communications as directed by the IRT Lead and Legal.</p><p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must complete security awareness training including incident reporting procedures; are required to report suspected incidents immediately.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<h2>Enforcement</h2><p>Compliance with this Incident Response Policy is mandatory for all covered individuals and entities. Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and potential legal liability. Failure to report a suspected incident, or interference with an incident response investigation, is considered a serious violation.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<h2>Review and Revision</h2><p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the Information Security Governance Committee at least annually. Revisions shall be made in response to changes in the threat landscape, legal and regulatory requirements, technology infrastructure, or lessons learned from incident response activities. The most current version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel through the company''s policy management system.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-19 06:15:54.71159', '"[\"ISO 27001 / SOC 2\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 20:04:38.515', NULL),
	(35, '38635', 'Firewall Configuration Policy', '<p>This Firewall Configuration Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the secure design, implementation, management, and monitoring of all firewall systems at [Company Name]. The policy enforces a defense-in-depth architecture to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate information assets and network infrastructure from unauthorized access, misuse, or compromise.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all network firewall devices, virtual firewalls, cloud-native firewall services (e.g., AWS Security Groups, Azure NSGs, GCP Firewall Rules), web application firewalls (WAFs), and next-generation firewalls (NGFWs) deployed within or protecting any [Company Name] network environment, including corporate data centers, cloud platforms, and branch offices. It encompasses all personnel, contractors, and third-party service providers responsible for the configuration, administration, or management of these systems.</p>

<p><strong>Firewall:</strong> A network security system that monitors and controls incoming and outgoing network traffic based on predetermined security rules.<br><strong>Principle of Least Privilege:</strong> A security concept in which a user or process is granted the minimum levels of access—or permissions—necessary to perform its function.<br><strong>Baseline Configuration:</strong> A standardized, secure configuration template for a specific type of firewall, against which all operational configurations must be assessed.<br><strong>Change Management:</strong> A formal process for controlling modifications to firewall rules, configurations, and firmware to ensure they are reviewed, approved, documented, and tested.<br><strong>Implicit Deny:</strong> A firewall rule that blocks all traffic not explicitly permitted by preceding allow rules.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all firewall configurations shall enforce a principle of least privilege and a default-deny posture. All rule changes must follow a formal change management process. Unauthorized modification, bypassing, or disabling of firewall protections is strictly prohibited.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Baseline Configuration & Hardening</strong><br>All firewalls shall be deployed with a company-approved, hardened baseline configuration. This baseline must disable all unnecessary services, use strong, unique administrative credentials, and implement role-based access control (RBAC) for management interfaces. Default passwords and accounts shall be changed immediately upon installation.</p><p><strong>5.2 Rule Set Management & Least Privilege</strong><br>Firewall rule sets shall be reviewed and pruned quarterly to remove obsolete rules. All rules must have a documented business justification, owner, and expiration date. Rules shall be as specific as possible, specifying source IP, destination IP, destination port, and protocol. The use of "ANY" in source, destination, or service fields is forbidden unless explicitly justified and approved by the CISO.</p><p><strong>5.3 Default-Deny Posture & Rule Order</strong><br>All firewalls must implement an implicit deny rule as the final rule in every rule base. Inbound internet traffic shall be restricted to only authorized services published through a DMZ architecture. Direct inbound connections from the internet to internal networks are prohibited.</p><p><strong>5.4 Change Management & Documentation</strong><br>All modifications to firewall rules, policies, or firmware must be submitted through the formal IT Change Management process. Changes require pre-implementation review, risk assessment, and approval from the Network Security team. A complete audit trail, including the requester, approver, date, time, and reason for change, must be maintained for a minimum of seven years.</p><p><strong>5.5 Logging, Monitoring & Alerting</strong><br>All firewalls shall be configured to generate detailed logs for all allowed and denied traffic, administrative access, and configuration changes. Logs must be forwarded in real-time to a centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system. Alerts shall be configured for critical security events, including but not limited to: policy modification, login failures, and detected intrusion attempts.</p><p><strong>5.6 Segmentation & Zone Defense</strong><br>Network segmentation shall be enforced using firewalls to create security zones (e.g., DMZ, Internal, Management, Production). Traffic between zones of differing trust levels must traverse a firewall and be explicitly permitted by rule. Direct network pathways that bypass firewall inspection are strictly forbidden.</p><p><strong>5.7 Vulnerability & Patch Management</strong><br>Firewall operating systems and firmware shall be included in the enterprise vulnerability management program. Critical and high-severity security patches must be applied within the timeframe defined by the corporate Patch Management Policy, following approved change procedures.</p><p><strong>5.8 Cryptographic Controls for Management</strong><br>All administrative access to firewalls (including GUI, CLI, and API) must be encrypted using TLS 1.2 or higher. Insecure management protocols such as Telnet and HTTP are prohibited.</p>

<p>Firewall configurations shall be designed and maintained to meet the control requirements of ISO 27001 (A.13.1 Network Security Management), SOC 2 (CC6.1, CC6.6, CC6.7), and PCI DSS (Requirements 1.1 - 1.4). Regular audits and evidence collection (e.g., rule sets, change logs, review reports) shall be performed to demonstrate compliance with these frameworks and any applicable data protection regulations.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for policy approval, enforcement, and overall firewall security posture.<br><strong>Network Security Team:</strong> Responsible for the operational implementation, daily management, rule review, logging, and incident response related to firewalls.<br><strong>System & Cloud Administrators:</strong> Responsible for adhering to this policy when configuring virtual or cloud-native firewall components within their environments.<br><strong>IT Change Advisory Board (CAB):</strong> Responsible for reviewing and approving all firewall change requests as part of the formal change management process.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment, and may face legal action. The Information Security Department is authorized to monitor compliance and conduct periodic technical audits of firewall configurations. Non-compliant configurations will be documented, and responsible parties will be required to remediate findings within a mandated timeframe.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Department at least annually or following significant security incidents or changes in the threat landscape. Revisions must be approved by the CISO. The latest version of this policy shall be made available to all relevant personnel.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Firewall Configuration Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the secure design, implementation, management, and monitoring of all firewall systems at [Company Name]. The policy enforces a defense-in-depth architecture to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate information assets and network infrastructure from unauthorized access, misuse, or compromise.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all network firewall devices, virtual firewalls, cloud-native firewall services (e.g., AWS Security Groups, Azure NSGs, GCP Firewall Rules), web application firewalls (WAFs), and next-generation firewalls (NGFWs) deployed within or protecting any [Company Name] network environment, including corporate data centers, cloud platforms, and branch offices. It encompasses all personnel, contractors, and third-party service providers responsible for the configuration, administration, or management of these systems.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Firewall:</strong> A network security system that monitors and controls incoming and outgoing network traffic based on predetermined security rules.<br><strong>Principle of Least Privilege:</strong> A security concept in which a user or process is granted the minimum levels of access—or permissions—necessary to perform its function.<br><strong>Baseline Configuration:</strong> A standardized, secure configuration template for a specific type of firewall, against which all operational configurations must be assessed.<br><strong>Change Management:</strong> A formal process for controlling modifications to firewall rules, configurations, and firmware to ensure they are reviewed, approved, documented, and tested.<br><strong>Implicit Deny:</strong> A firewall rule that blocks all traffic not explicitly permitted by preceding allow rules.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates that all firewall configurations shall enforce a principle of least privilege and a default-deny posture. All rule changes must follow a formal change management process. Unauthorized modification, bypassing, or disabling of firewall protections is strictly prohibited.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>5.1 Baseline Configuration & Hardening</strong><br>All firewalls shall be deployed with a company-approved, hardened baseline configuration. This baseline must disable all unnecessary services, use strong, unique administrative credentials, and implement role-based access control (RBAC) for management interfaces. Default passwords and accounts shall be changed immediately upon installation.</p><p><strong>5.2 Rule Set Management & Least Privilege</strong><br>Firewall rule sets shall be reviewed and pruned quarterly to remove obsolete rules. All rules must have a documented business justification, owner, and expiration date. Rules shall be as specific as possible, specifying source IP, destination IP, destination port, and protocol. The use of \\\"ANY\\\" in source, destination, or service fields is forbidden unless explicitly justified and approved by the CISO.</p><p><strong>5.3 Default-Deny Posture & Rule Order</strong><br>All firewalls must implement an implicit deny rule as the final rule in every rule base. Inbound internet traffic shall be restricted to only authorized services published through a DMZ architecture. Direct inbound connections from the internet to internal networks are prohibited.</p><p><strong>5.4 Change Management & Documentation</strong><br>All modifications to firewall rules, policies, or firmware must be submitted through the formal IT Change Management process. Changes require pre-implementation review, risk assessment, and approval from the Network Security team. A complete audit trail, including the requester, approver, date, time, and reason for change, must be maintained for a minimum of seven years.</p><p><strong>5.5 Logging, Monitoring & Alerting</strong><br>All firewalls shall be configured to generate detailed logs for all allowed and denied traffic, administrative access, and configuration changes. Logs must be forwarded in real-time to a centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system. Alerts shall be configured for critical security events, including but not limited to: policy modification, login failures, and detected intrusion attempts.</p><p><strong>5.6 Segmentation & Zone Defense</strong><br>Network segmentation shall be enforced using firewalls to create security zones (e.g., DMZ, Internal, Management, Production). Traffic between zones of differing trust levels must traverse a firewall and be explicitly permitted by rule. Direct network pathways that bypass firewall inspection are strictly forbidden.</p><p><strong>5.7 Vulnerability & Patch Management</strong><br>Firewall operating systems and firmware shall be included in the enterprise vulnerability management program. Critical and high-severity security patches must be applied within the timeframe defined by the corporate Patch Management Policy, following approved change procedures.</p><p><strong>5.8 Cryptographic Controls for Management</strong><br>All administrative access to firewalls (including GUI, CLI, and API) must be encrypted using TLS 1.2 or higher. Insecure management protocols such as Telnet and HTTP are prohibited.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>Firewall configurations shall be designed and maintained to meet the control requirements of ISO 27001 (A.13.1 Network Security Management), SOC 2 (CC6.1, CC6.6, CC6.7), and PCI DSS (Requirements 1.1 - 1.4). Regular audits and evidence collection (e.g., rule sets, change logs, review reports) shall be performed to demonstrate compliance with these frameworks and any applicable data protection regulations.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for policy approval, enforcement, and overall firewall security posture.<br><strong>Network Security Team:</strong> Responsible for the operational implementation, daily management, rule review, logging, and incident response related to firewalls.<br><strong>System & Cloud Administrators:</strong> Responsible for adhering to this policy when configuring virtual or cloud-native firewall components within their environments.<br><strong>IT Change Advisory Board (CAB):</strong> Responsible for reviewing and approving all firewall change requests as part of the formal change management process.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment, and may face legal action. The Information Security Department is authorized to monitor compliance and conduct periodic technical audits of firewall configurations. Non-compliant configurations will be documented, and responsible parties will be required to remediate findings within a mandated timeframe.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Department at least annually or following significant security incidents or changes in the threat landscape. Revisions must be approved by the CISO. The latest version of this policy shall be made available to all relevant personnel.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-20 21:17:22.92538', '"[\"ISO 27001\",\"SOC 2\",\"PCI DSS\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 20:05:10.158', NULL),
	(33, '507965', 'Password Policy', '<p>This Password Policy establishes the mandatory technical and administrative controls for the creation, management, protection, and secure disposal of authentication credentials at [Company Name]. Its primary objective is to safeguard information assets by ensuring that passwords are robust, confidential, and resistant to unauthorized access, thereby protecting the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate data and systems.</p>

<p>This policy applies universally to all personnel, including but not limited to employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party agents who access, create, or manage any [Company Name] information system, network, application, database, or data repository. This policy governs all authentication credentials, including but not limited to user passwords, service account passwords, application secrets, and API keys, irrespective of the system''s location (on-premises, cloud, or hybrid).</p>

<p><strong>Password:</strong> A confidential sequence of characters, known only to the user and the system, used to authenticate identity and authorize access to information resources.</p><p><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> An authentication method that requires the user to provide two or more verification factors to gain access to a resource.</p><p><strong>Password Manager:</strong> A software application designed to store and manage online credentials in an encrypted vault.</p><p><strong>Service Account:</strong> A non-human account used by an application or system to interact with other systems or resources.</p><p><strong>Password Hash:</strong> A cryptographically scrambled representation of a password, generated via a one-way function.</p><p><strong>Brute-Force Attack:</strong> An attempt to discover a password by systematically trying every possible combination of characters.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all authentication credentials shall be created, managed, and protected according to the stringent requirements defined in this policy. The use of weak, default, shared, or compromised passwords is strictly prohibited. All personnel are accountable for adhering to these controls to maintain the security posture of the organization.</p>

<h4>5.1 Password Composition and Complexity</h4><p>All user-created passwords must meet the following minimum complexity requirements:</p><ul><li><strong>Minimum Length:</strong> 14 characters for standard user accounts; 20 characters for privileged administrative accounts.</li><li><strong>Character Diversity:</strong> Must include characters from at least three of the following four categories: uppercase letters (A-Z), lowercase letters (a-z), numerals (0-9), and special characters (e.g., ! @ # $ % ^ & *).</li><li><strong>Prohibited Elements:</strong> Passwords must not contain the user''s account name, common dictionary words, simple patterns (e.g., "123456", "qwerty"), or repetitive characters (e.g., "aaaaaa").</li></ul><h4>5.2 Password Lifecycle Management</h4><p>The following controls govern password creation, change, and expiration:</p><ul><li><strong>Initial Provisioning:</strong> All initial passwords shall be system-generated, unique, and transmitted via a secure, out-of-band method. The user must change this password upon first login.</li><li><strong>Password Expiration:</strong> Standard user passwords must be changed at least every 90 days. Privileged account passwords must be changed at least every 60 days. Service account passwords shall be rotated at least every 180 days or immediately upon personnel change.</li><li><strong>Password History:</strong> Systems shall enforce a password history of at least the previous 10 passwords to prevent reuse.</li></ul><h4>5.3 Password Protection and Storage</h4><p>All passwords and secrets shall be protected according to the following technical mandates:</p><ul><li><strong>Storage:</strong> Passwords shall never be stored in plaintext. All passwords at rest must be hashed using a strong, industry-approved cryptographic hashing algorithm (e.g., bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2, or PBKDF2 with sufficient iteration counts).</li><li><strong>Transmission:</strong> Passwords shall only be transmitted over encrypted channels (e.g., TLS 1.2 or higher).</li><li><strong>Local Storage Prohibition:</strong> Writing down passwords on paper, storing them in unencrypted files (e.g., text files, spreadsheets), or saving them in browser password managers not integrated with the corporate-approved solution is strictly forbidden.</li></ul><h4>5.4 Authentication and Access Controls</h4><p>Authentication systems shall implement the following controls:</p><ul><li><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> MFA is mandatory for all remote network access (VPN), access to cloud administration portals, and for all users with privileged access rights (e.g., system administrators, database administrators).</li><li><strong>Account Lockout:</strong> After 5 consecutive failed authentication attempts, the user account shall be automatically locked for a minimum period of 15 minutes. Lockout events must be logged and alerted to the Security Operations Center (SOC).</li><li><strong>Session Management:</strong> Interactive user sessions shall automatically terminate after 15 minutes of inactivity. Session tokens must be cryptographically secure and invalidated upon logout.</li></ul><h4>5.5 Administrative and Technical Enforcement</h4><p>Technical systems must be configured to enforce this policy:</p><ul><li><strong>Centralized Identity Management:</strong> All authentication shall be managed through a centralized Identity and Access Management (IAM) system where technically feasible.</li><li><strong>Automated Compliance Checking:</strong> Automated tools shall be deployed to periodically scan for and alert on the use of default, weak, or shared passwords, particularly for service accounts.</li><li><strong>Logging and Monitoring:</strong> All authentication events (successes, failures, password changes, account lockouts) must be logged with sufficient detail (timestamp, source IP, user ID) and retained for a minimum of 90 days for analysis and audit purposes.</li></ul>

<p>This policy is designed to align with and support compliance with the following frameworks and regulations:</p><ul><li><strong>ISO 27001:2022:</strong> Controls A.5.15 (Identity Management), A.5.16 (Identity, Authentication & Access), A.5.17 (Authentication Information), and A.8.2 (Privileged Access Rights).</li><li><strong>SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria:</strong> Security (CC6.1 - Logical Access), Availability, and Confidentiality criteria pertaining to access controls and authentication.</li><li><strong>PCI DSS v4.0:</strong> Requirements 8.3 (Secure all individual non-console administrative access and all remote access using MFA) and 8.3.6 (Minimum password complexity and strength).</li><li><strong>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR):</strong> Article 32 (Security of Processing), mandating appropriate technical measures to ensure a level of security appropriate to the risk.</li></ul><p>Non-compliance with this policy may result in regulatory penalties, audit findings, and legal liability for [Company Name].</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority and accountability for the approval, implementation, and enforcement of this policy.</p><p><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for the development, maintenance, monitoring, and technical enforcement of this policy. This includes configuring IAM systems and reviewing authentication logs.</p><p><strong>IT Infrastructure Team:</strong> Responsible for implementing the technical controls specified in Section 5 across all systems, servers, and applications under their management.</p><p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for understanding, adhering to, and reporting any suspected violations of this policy. This includes creating strong passwords, safeguarding them, and using MFA where required.</p><p><strong>Human Resources (HR):</strong> Responsible for ensuring policy acknowledgment is part of the onboarding and offboarding processes.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement, and potential legal action. The Information Security Team is authorized to immediately disable accounts found in violation of this policy to mitigate risk. All violations shall be documented and reported in accordance with the Incident Response Policy.</p>

<p>This Password Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Team at least annually, or following any significant security incident, change in compliance landscape, or major technological shift. Proposed revisions must be submitted to the CISO for formal approval. The latest approved version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel through the corporate policy repository.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Password Policy establishes the mandatory technical and administrative controls for the creation, management, protection, and secure disposal of authentication credentials at [Company Name]. Its primary objective is to safeguard information assets by ensuring that passwords are robust, confidential, and resistant to unauthorized access, thereby protecting the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate data and systems.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies universally to all personnel, including but not limited to employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party agents who access, create, or manage any [Company Name] information system, network, application, database, or data repository. This policy governs all authentication credentials, including but not limited to user passwords, service account passwords, application secrets, and API keys, irrespective of the system''s location (on-premises, cloud, or hybrid).</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Password:</strong> A confidential sequence of characters, known only to the user and the system, used to authenticate identity and authorize access to information resources.</p><p><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> An authentication method that requires the user to provide two or more verification factors to gain access to a resource.</p><p><strong>Password Manager:</strong> A software application designed to store and manage online credentials in an encrypted vault.</p><p><strong>Service Account:</strong> A non-human account used by an application or system to interact with other systems or resources.</p><p><strong>Password Hash:</strong> A cryptographically scrambled representation of a password, generated via a one-way function.</p><p><strong>Brute-Force Attack:</strong> An attempt to discover a password by systematically trying every possible combination of characters.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates that all authentication credentials shall be created, managed, and protected according to the stringent requirements defined in this policy. The use of weak, default, shared, or compromised passwords is strictly prohibited. All personnel are accountable for adhering to these controls to maintain the security posture of the organization.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1 Password Composition and Complexity</h4><p>All user-created passwords must meet the following minimum complexity requirements:</p><ul><li><strong>Minimum Length:</strong> 14 characters for standard user accounts; 20 characters for privileged administrative accounts.</li><li><strong>Character Diversity:</strong> Must include characters from at least three of the following four categories: uppercase letters (A-Z), lowercase letters (a-z), numerals (0-9), and special characters (e.g., ! @ # $ % ^ & *).</li><li><strong>Prohibited Elements:</strong> Passwords must not contain the user''s account name, common dictionary words, simple patterns (e.g., \\\"123456\\\", \\\"qwerty\\\"), or repetitive characters (e.g., \\\"aaaaaa\\\").</li></ul><h4>5.2 Password Lifecycle Management</h4><p>The following controls govern password creation, change, and expiration:</p><ul><li><strong>Initial Provisioning:</strong> All initial passwords shall be system-generated, unique, and transmitted via a secure, out-of-band method. The user must change this password upon first login.</li><li><strong>Password Expiration:</strong> Standard user passwords must be changed at least every 90 days. Privileged account passwords must be changed at least every 60 days. Service account passwords shall be rotated at least every 180 days or immediately upon personnel change.</li><li><strong>Password History:</strong> Systems shall enforce a password history of at least the previous 10 passwords to prevent reuse.</li></ul><h4>5.3 Password Protection and Storage</h4><p>All passwords and secrets shall be protected according to the following technical mandates:</p><ul><li><strong>Storage:</strong> Passwords shall never be stored in plaintext. All passwords at rest must be hashed using a strong, industry-approved cryptographic hashing algorithm (e.g., bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2, or PBKDF2 with sufficient iteration counts).</li><li><strong>Transmission:</strong> Passwords shall only be transmitted over encrypted channels (e.g., TLS 1.2 or higher).</li><li><strong>Local Storage Prohibition:</strong> Writing down passwords on paper, storing them in unencrypted files (e.g., text files, spreadsheets), or saving them in browser password managers not integrated with the corporate-approved solution is strictly forbidden.</li></ul><h4>5.4 Authentication and Access Controls</h4><p>Authentication systems shall implement the following controls:</p><ul><li><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> MFA is mandatory for all remote network access (VPN), access to cloud administration portals, and for all users with privileged access rights (e.g., system administrators, database administrators).</li><li><strong>Account Lockout:</strong> After 5 consecutive failed authentication attempts, the user account shall be automatically locked for a minimum period of 15 minutes. Lockout events must be logged and alerted to the Security Operations Center (SOC).</li><li><strong>Session Management:</strong> Interactive user sessions shall automatically terminate after 15 minutes of inactivity. Session tokens must be cryptographically secure and invalidated upon logout.</li></ul><h4>5.5 Administrative and Technical Enforcement</h4><p>Technical systems must be configured to enforce this policy:</p><ul><li><strong>Centralized Identity Management:</strong> All authentication shall be managed through a centralized Identity and Access Management (IAM) system where technically feasible.</li><li><strong>Automated Compliance Checking:</strong> Automated tools shall be deployed to periodically scan for and alert on the use of default, weak, or shared passwords, particularly for service accounts.</li><li><strong>Logging and Monitoring:</strong> All authentication events (successes, failures, password changes, account lockouts) must be logged with sufficient detail (timestamp, source IP, user ID) and retained for a minimum of 90 days for analysis and audit purposes.</li></ul>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy is designed to align with and support compliance with the following frameworks and regulations:</p><ul><li><strong>ISO 27001:2022:</strong> Controls A.5.15 (Identity Management), A.5.16 (Identity, Authentication & Access), A.5.17 (Authentication Information), and A.8.2 (Privileged Access Rights).</li><li><strong>SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria:</strong> Security (CC6.1 - Logical Access), Availability, and Confidentiality criteria pertaining to access controls and authentication.</li><li><strong>PCI DSS v4.0:</strong> Requirements 8.3 (Secure all individual non-console administrative access and all remote access using MFA) and 8.3.6 (Minimum password complexity and strength).</li><li><strong>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR):</strong> Article 32 (Security of Processing), mandating appropriate technical measures to ensure a level of security appropriate to the risk.</li></ul><p>Non-compliance with this policy may result in regulatory penalties, audit findings, and legal liability for [Company Name].</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority and accountability for the approval, implementation, and enforcement of this policy.</p><p><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for the development, maintenance, monitoring, and technical enforcement of this policy. This includes configuring IAM systems and reviewing authentication logs.</p><p><strong>IT Infrastructure Team:</strong> Responsible for implementing the technical controls specified in Section 5 across all systems, servers, and applications under their management.</p><p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for understanding, adhering to, and reporting any suspected violations of this policy. This includes creating strong passwords, safeguarding them, and using MFA where required.</p><p><strong>Human Resources (HR):</strong> Responsible for ensuring policy acknowledgment is part of the onboarding and offboarding processes.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement, and potential legal action. The Information Security Team is authorized to immediately disable accounts found in violation of this policy to mitigate risk. All violations shall be documented and reported in accordance with the Incident Response Policy.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This Password Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Team at least annually, or following any significant security incident, change in compliance landscape, or major technological shift. Proposed revisions must be submitted to the CISO for formal approval. The latest approved version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel through the corporate policy repository.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-20 21:17:22.719889', '"[\"ISO 27001\",\"SOC 2\",\"PCI DSS\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 20:05:50.697', NULL),
	(34, '775612', 'Wireless Security Policy', '<p>This Wireless Security Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the secure deployment, configuration, management, and monitoring of all wireless networks and devices that access, transmit, or store [Company Name] information assets. The policy is designed to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate data and systems from threats inherent in wireless communications, in alignment with [Company Name]''s overall Information Security Management System (ISMS).</p>

<p>This policy applies universally across [Company Name], encompassing all subsidiaries, business units, and physical locations. It governs all individuals (employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties), all corporate-owned or managed wireless infrastructure (e.g., access points, controllers, bridges), and all devices (corporate-issued or personal) that connect to [Company Name] wireless networks or that create wireless connections (e.g., ad-hoc, tethering) to access corporate resources. This includes, but is not limited to, Wi-Fi (802.11 standards), Bluetooth, cellular data modems, and any emerging wireless technologies used for business purposes.</p>

<p><strong>Access Point (AP) / Wireless Access Point (WAP):</strong> A networking hardware device that allows Wi-Fi compliant devices to connect to a wired network.<br><br><strong>Enterprise Wireless Network:</strong> The centrally managed, authorized wireless network infrastructure deployed by [Company Name] IT for general corporate use.<br><br><strong>Guest Wireless Network:</strong> A segregated and controlled wireless network provisioned for visitors, contractors, or other non-employees, with strictly limited access to corporate resources.<br><br><strong>Rogue Access Point:</strong> Any wireless access point installed on the corporate network or within corporate facilities without explicit authorization from the Information Technology (IT) Department.<br><br><strong>WPA3-Enterprise (Wi-Fi Protected Access 3 Enterprise):</strong> The current mandatory security certification program for securing enterprise-grade Wi-Fi networks, utilizing 256-bit encryption and 802.1X/EAP for authentication.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all wireless network access serving corporate functions shall be implemented and maintained with security as the paramount design principle. The use of wireless technologies is a privilege granted under strict controls to mitigate risks of unauthorized access, data interception, and network compromise. All wireless communications involving [Company Name] data or systems must be encrypted, authenticated, and monitored. The deployment of unauthorized wireless infrastructure or the circumvention of security controls is strictly prohibited.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Authorization and Architecture</strong><br>5.1.1 All wireless infrastructure, including access points, controllers, and management systems, must be approved, procured, and deployed solely by the Information Technology (IT) Department.<br>5.1.2 A formal network architecture diagram detailing all wireless infrastructure, including coverage areas, channels, and power settings, shall be maintained and reviewed annually.<br>5.1.3 The implementation of personal wireless access points, wireless routers, or ad-hoc wireless networks within corporate facilities is expressly forbidden.<br><br><strong>5.2 Encryption and Authentication Standards</strong><br>5.2.1 All enterprise wireless networks shall utilize WPA3-Enterprise with AES-256-GCM encryption. Transitional use of WPA2-Enterprise is permitted only for legacy device support and must be documented with a risk-accepted migration plan.<br>5.2.2 The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted networks for any corporate purpose is forbidden.<br>5.2.3 Authentication for the enterprise network must leverage a centralized identity provider (e.g., RADIUS server) integrated with the corporate directory (e.g., Active Directory, Azure AD) using 802.1X/EAP-TLS or EAP-PEAP.<br>5.2.4 Guest network access shall be secured via a captive portal with unique, time-limited credentials or a separate PSK that is rotated at least quarterly.<br><br><strong>5.3 Device Configuration and Hardening</strong><br>5.3.1 All authorized access points shall have default administrative credentials changed, unnecessary services disabled, and firmware updated to vendor-supported versions within 30 days of release for critical security patches.<br>5.3.2 Service Set Identifiers (SSIDs) shall not contain information identifying [Company Name], its location, or function (e.g., "[Company Name]_Finance").<br>5.3.3 Wireless signal strength shall be tuned to minimize propagation beyond physical premises boundaries where feasible.<br><br><strong>5.4 Monitoring and Intrusion Detection</strong><br>5.4.1 A Wireless Intrusion Detection/Prevention System (WIDS/WIPS) shall be deployed to continuously monitor the radio frequency (RF) spectrum for rogue devices, misconfigured clients, and attack patterns.<br>5.4.2 All authentication successes and failures, as well as administrative access to wireless infrastructure, must be logged to a centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system with a minimum retention period of 90 days.<br>5.4.3 The IT Department shall conduct periodic wireless vulnerability assessments and penetration tests at least annually to identify security weaknesses.<br><br><strong>5.5 Endpoint and Client Security</strong><br>5.5.1 Corporate-managed devices must have wireless interfaces configured via a Mobile Device Management (MDM) or equivalent policy to automatically connect only to approved [Company Name] SSIDs.<br>5.5.2 Users are prohibited from configuring corporate devices to connect to untrusted or public wireless networks for accessing sensitive corporate data unless using a mandated, company-provided VPN.<br>5.5.3 The use of personal devices (BYOD) to connect to the enterprise wireless network requires enrollment in the BYOD program, which mandates device health checks and containerization of corporate data.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to support compliance with relevant legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA). Specific controls within this policy directly address ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A controls, particularly A.8.1 (Asset Management), A.8.2 (Information Classification), A.8.9 (Wireless Access Control), A.8.12 (Data Leakage Prevention), A.8.15 (Logging), and A.8.16 (Monitoring). For SOC 2, this policy supports the Trust Services Criteria of Security (CC6.1, CC6.3, CC6.6, CC6.7) and Confidentiality. Deviations from this policy for business necessity require a formal risk assessment and documented exception approved by the CISO.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Bears ultimate responsibility for the approval, oversight, and enforcement of this policy.<br><br><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department / Network Team:</strong> Responsible for the secure design, implementation, maintenance, and monitoring of all authorized wireless infrastructure. This includes managing the WIDS/WIPS, applying patches, and responding to incidents.<br><br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for policy review, conducting security assessments, analyzing WIDS/WIPS alerts, and investigating policy violations.<br><br><strong>All Users:</strong> Required to comply with all aspects of this policy, use only authorized wireless networks for business, and report any suspected security incidents or unauthorized wireless devices immediately.</p>

<p>Compliance with this policy will be enforced through a combination of technical controls, automated monitoring, and administrative procedures. Violations of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement, and may lead to civil or criminal liability. The IT Department is authorized to immediately disconnect any unauthorized wireless device or network posing a security risk. All users shall be required to formally acknowledge receipt and understanding of this policy upon hire and annually thereafter.</p>

<p>This Wireless Security Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security team at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in threat landscape, or updates to relevant standards (ISO 27001, NIST, etc.). Proposed revisions must be submitted to the CISO for approval. The latest approved version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel through the corporate policy repository.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Wireless Security Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the secure deployment, configuration, management, and monitoring of all wireless networks and devices that access, transmit, or store [Company Name] information assets. The policy is designed to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate data and systems from threats inherent in wireless communications, in alignment with [Company Name]''s overall Information Security Management System (ISMS).</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies universally across [Company Name], encompassing all subsidiaries, business units, and physical locations. It governs all individuals (employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties), all corporate-owned or managed wireless infrastructure (e.g., access points, controllers, bridges), and all devices (corporate-issued or personal) that connect to [Company Name] wireless networks or that create wireless connections (e.g., ad-hoc, tethering) to access corporate resources. This includes, but is not limited to, Wi-Fi (802.11 standards), Bluetooth, cellular data modems, and any emerging wireless technologies used for business purposes.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Access Point (AP) / Wireless Access Point (WAP):</strong> A networking hardware device that allows Wi-Fi compliant devices to connect to a wired network.<br><br><strong>Enterprise Wireless Network:</strong> The centrally managed, authorized wireless network infrastructure deployed by [Company Name] IT for general corporate use.<br><br><strong>Guest Wireless Network:</strong> A segregated and controlled wireless network provisioned for visitors, contractors, or other non-employees, with strictly limited access to corporate resources.<br><br><strong>Rogue Access Point:</strong> Any wireless access point installed on the corporate network or within corporate facilities without explicit authorization from the Information Technology (IT) Department.<br><br><strong>WPA3-Enterprise (Wi-Fi Protected Access 3 Enterprise):</strong> The current mandatory security certification program for securing enterprise-grade Wi-Fi networks, utilizing 256-bit encryption and 802.1X/EAP for authentication.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates that all wireless network access serving corporate functions shall be implemented and maintained with security as the paramount design principle. The use of wireless technologies is a privilege granted under strict controls to mitigate risks of unauthorized access, data interception, and network compromise. All wireless communications involving [Company Name] data or systems must be encrypted, authenticated, and monitored. The deployment of unauthorized wireless infrastructure or the circumvention of security controls is strictly prohibited.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>5.1 Authorization and Architecture</strong><br>5.1.1 All wireless infrastructure, including access points, controllers, and management systems, must be approved, procured, and deployed solely by the Information Technology (IT) Department.<br>5.1.2 A formal network architecture diagram detailing all wireless infrastructure, including coverage areas, channels, and power settings, shall be maintained and reviewed annually.<br>5.1.3 The implementation of personal wireless access points, wireless routers, or ad-hoc wireless networks within corporate facilities is expressly forbidden.<br><br><strong>5.2 Encryption and Authentication Standards</strong><br>5.2.1 All enterprise wireless networks shall utilize WPA3-Enterprise with AES-256-GCM encryption. Transitional use of WPA2-Enterprise is permitted only for legacy device support and must be documented with a risk-accepted migration plan.<br>5.2.2 The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted networks for any corporate purpose is forbidden.<br>5.2.3 Authentication for the enterprise network must leverage a centralized identity provider (e.g., RADIUS server) integrated with the corporate directory (e.g., Active Directory, Azure AD) using 802.1X/EAP-TLS or EAP-PEAP.<br>5.2.4 Guest network access shall be secured via a captive portal with unique, time-limited credentials or a separate PSK that is rotated at least quarterly.<br><br><strong>5.3 Device Configuration and Hardening</strong><br>5.3.1 All authorized access points shall have default administrative credentials changed, unnecessary services disabled, and firmware updated to vendor-supported versions within 30 days of release for critical security patches.<br>5.3.2 Service Set Identifiers (SSIDs) shall not contain information identifying [Company Name], its location, or function (e.g., \\\"[Company Name]_Finance\\\").<br>5.3.3 Wireless signal strength shall be tuned to minimize propagation beyond physical premises boundaries where feasible.<br><br><strong>5.4 Monitoring and Intrusion Detection</strong><br>5.4.1 A Wireless Intrusion Detection/Prevention System (WIDS/WIPS) shall be deployed to continuously monitor the radio frequency (RF) spectrum for rogue devices, misconfigured clients, and attack patterns.<br>5.4.2 All authentication successes and failures, as well as administrative access to wireless infrastructure, must be logged to a centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system with a minimum retention period of 90 days.<br>5.4.3 The IT Department shall conduct periodic wireless vulnerability assessments and penetration tests at least annually to identify security weaknesses.<br><br><strong>5.5 Endpoint and Client Security</strong><br>5.5.1 Corporate-managed devices must have wireless interfaces configured via a Mobile Device Management (MDM) or equivalent policy to automatically connect only to approved [Company Name] SSIDs.<br>5.5.2 Users are prohibited from configuring corporate devices to connect to untrusted or public wireless networks for accessing sensitive corporate data unless using a mandated, company-provided VPN.<br>5.5.3 The use of personal devices (BYOD) to connect to the enterprise wireless network requires enrollment in the BYOD program, which mandates device health checks and containerization of corporate data.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy is designed to support compliance with relevant legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA). Specific controls within this policy directly address ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A controls, particularly A.8.1 (Asset Management), A.8.2 (Information Classification), A.8.9 (Wireless Access Control), A.8.12 (Data Leakage Prevention), A.8.15 (Logging), and A.8.16 (Monitoring). For SOC 2, this policy supports the Trust Services Criteria of Security (CC6.1, CC6.3, CC6.6, CC6.7) and Confidentiality. Deviations from this policy for business necessity require a formal risk assessment and documented exception approved by the CISO.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Bears ultimate responsibility for the approval, oversight, and enforcement of this policy.<br><br><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department / Network Team:</strong> Responsible for the secure design, implementation, maintenance, and monitoring of all authorized wireless infrastructure. This includes managing the WIDS/WIPS, applying patches, and responding to incidents.<br><br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for policy review, conducting security assessments, analyzing WIDS/WIPS alerts, and investigating policy violations.<br><br><strong>All Users:</strong> Required to comply with all aspects of this policy, use only authorized wireless networks for business, and report any suspected security incidents or unauthorized wireless devices immediately.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Compliance with this policy will be enforced through a combination of technical controls, automated monitoring, and administrative procedures. Violations of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement, and may lead to civil or criminal liability. The IT Department is authorized to immediately disconnect any unauthorized wireless device or network posing a security risk. All users shall be required to formally acknowledge receipt and understanding of this policy upon hire and annually thereafter.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This Wireless Security Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security team at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in threat landscape, or updates to relevant standards (ISO 27001, NIST, etc.). Proposed revisions must be submitted to the CISO for approval. The latest approved version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel through the corporate policy repository.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-20 21:17:22.821891', '"[\"ISO 27001\",\"SOC 2\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 20:06:29.023', NULL),
	(36, '755984', 'Secure SDLC Policy', '<p>This Secure Software Development Lifecycle (SDLC) Policy establishes the mandatory security controls, processes, and governance framework for all software development activities at [Company Name]. It mandates the integration of security and privacy requirements throughout all phases of the software lifecycle to ensure the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of applications and data, in alignment with business objectives and regulatory obligations.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all personnel, contractors, and third-party vendors involved in the design, development, testing, deployment, maintenance, or procurement of software applications, systems, or services on behalf of [Company Name]. This includes, but is not limited to, software engineers, architects, quality assurance personnel, product managers, and DevOps engineers. The policy covers all internally developed applications, commercial off-the-shelf (COTS) software configurations, and integrations with third-party services.</p>

<p><strong>Secure SDLC:</strong> A structured process that integrates security activities and checkpoints into each phase of the software development lifecycle.</p><p><strong>Static Application Security Testing (SAST):</strong> Analysis of application source code, bytecode, or binary code for security vulnerabilities without executing the program.</p><p><strong>Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST):</strong> Analysis of a running application for security vulnerabilities by simulating external attacks.</p><p><strong>Software Composition Analysis (SCA):</strong> The process of identifying and managing open-source and third-party components within an application to detect known vulnerabilities and license compliance issues.</p><p><strong>Threat Model:</strong> A structured representation of all information that affects the security of an application, including assets, threats, vulnerabilities, and mitigations.</p><p><strong>Production Environment:</strong> The operational environment where end-users access the live application and data.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that security and privacy requirements shall be defined, implemented, and verified throughout the entire software development lifecycle. Security is a non-negotiable requirement and shall be integrated into all phases—from initial concept and design through deployment, operation, and decommissioning. The ad-hoc introduction of security controls as an afterthought is strictly prohibited.</p>

<h4>5.1 Requirements and Design Phase</h4><p>5.1.1 Security and privacy requirements shall be documented in formal requirement specifications and user stories, and shall be traceable through the development lifecycle.</p><p>5.1.2 A formal threat modeling exercise, utilizing a recognized methodology (e.g., STRIDE), shall be conducted for all new applications and for significant modifications to existing applications. Threat models shall be documented and approved by the development lead and a security representative.</p><p>5.1.3 All application architectures and high-level designs must be reviewed for security implications. The use of deprecated or insecure protocols (e.g., SSLv3, TLS 1.0/1.1) is forbidden.</p><p>5.1.4 Data classification must be performed, and handling requirements for sensitive data (e.g., PII, financial data, intellectual property) shall be explicitly defined in the design.</p><h4>5.2 Implementation Phase</h4><p>5.2.1 Developers shall adhere to the [Company Name] Secure Coding Standards, which prohibit common vulnerabilities (e.g., OWASP Top 10).</p><p>5.2.2 All code commits must be peer-reviewed with a focus on security. Reviews shall be documented within the version control system.</p><p>5.2.3 The use of open-source and third-party libraries must be governed by a formal Software Composition Analysis (SCA) process. Components with known critical or high-severity vulnerabilities shall not be approved for use.</p><p>5.2.4 Hard-coded secrets (e.g., passwords, API keys, cryptographic keys) are strictly prohibited. All secrets must be managed through a dedicated, approved secrets management solution.</p><h4>5.3 Verification and Testing Phase</h4><p>5.3.1 Static Application Security Testing (SAST) shall be performed on all application source code. Findings of critical or high severity must be remediated prior to release.</p><p>5.3.2 Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST) or Interactive Application Security Testing (IAST) shall be performed against all staging or pre-production application builds.</p><p>5.3.3 Software Composition Analysis (SCA) scans shall be run to generate a Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) and identify vulnerable dependencies. No application with unresolved critical or high-severity component vulnerabilities may be promoted to production.</p><p>5.3.4 Penetration testing by an independent, qualified third party shall be conducted for all internet-facing applications and applications handling sensitive data at least annually, or following any major release.</p><h4>5.4 Release and Deployment Phase</h4><p>5.4.1 A formal security approval gate, requiring sign-off from the Application Security team or designated security champion, is mandatory for all production deployments.</p><p>5.4.2 Deployment to production environments must be performed through automated, auditable pipelines. Manual deployments or direct access to production servers by developers is strictly prohibited, except for approved break-glass procedures.</p><p>5.4.3 All application infrastructure (e.g., containers, virtual machines, serverless functions) must be provisioned from hardened, security-approved templates or images.</p><h4>5.5 Operations and Maintenance Phase</h4><p>5.5.1 A continuous monitoring program for applications in production shall be implemented, including monitoring for anomalous behavior, unauthorized access attempts, and vulnerability alerts.</p><p>5.5.2 A formal vulnerability management process shall govern the triage, prioritization, and remediation of security vulnerabilities discovered in production applications. Critical vulnerabilities must be remediated within 7 days of notification; high-severity vulnerabilities within 30 days.</p><p>5.5.3 All changes to production applications, including patches and configuration modifications, must follow the formal change management process and be tested in a non-production environment first.</p><h4>5.6 Decommissioning Phase</h4><p>5.6.1 A decommissioning plan must be created for applications being retired, detailing the secure archival or destruction of all associated data and credentials.</p><p>5.6.2 All persistent data storage (e.g., databases, file stores, backups) associated with the decommissioned application shall be securely wiped or destroyed in accordance with data retention and destruction policies.</p>

<p>All software development activities must comply with relevant laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), financial industry standards (e.g., PCI DSS), and sector-specific requirements. The Secure SDLC process is designed to provide demonstrable evidence of due care for security and privacy, supporting audits for ISO 27001, SOC 2, and other compliance frameworks. Non-compliance with this policy may result in legal, financial, and reputational consequences for [Company Name].</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the policy, its enforcement, and its alignment with the corporate risk appetite.</p><p><strong>Head of Software Development/Engineering:</strong> Operational responsibility for implementing the Secure SDLC processes within development teams and ensuring resource allocation for security activities.</p><p><strong>Application Security Team:</strong> Responsible for defining secure coding standards, maintaining security testing tools, conducting security reviews, and providing expert guidance to development teams.</p><p><strong>Development Team Leads:</strong> Responsible for ensuring their teams adhere to this policy, facilitating threat modeling, enforcing peer reviews, and managing the remediation of security findings.</p><p><strong>Software Developers:</strong> Responsible for writing secure code, participating in security training, performing peer reviews, and remediating security vulnerabilities in their code.</p><p><strong>Quality Assurance (QA) Engineers:</strong> Responsible for integrating security test cases into QA processes and validating the remediation of security defects.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement. The CISO and Information Security team are authorized to block the deployment of any application that does not meet the security gates defined in this policy. Exceptions to this policy require a formal risk assessment and written approval from the CISO or their designated delegate.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Governance Committee at least annually, or following significant security incidents or changes in the threat landscape, technology stack, or regulatory environment. Proposed revisions must be formally documented, reviewed for risk impact, and approved by the CISO. The latest approved version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel and third parties to whom it applies.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Secure Software Development Lifecycle (SDLC) Policy establishes the mandatory security controls, processes, and governance framework for all software development activities at [Company Name]. It mandates the integration of security and privacy requirements throughout all phases of the software lifecycle to ensure the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of applications and data, in alignment with business objectives and regulatory obligations.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all personnel, contractors, and third-party vendors involved in the design, development, testing, deployment, maintenance, or procurement of software applications, systems, or services on behalf of [Company Name]. This includes, but is not limited to, software engineers, architects, quality assurance personnel, product managers, and DevOps engineers. The policy covers all internally developed applications, commercial off-the-shelf (COTS) software configurations, and integrations with third-party services.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Secure SDLC:</strong> A structured process that integrates security activities and checkpoints into each phase of the software development lifecycle.</p><p><strong>Static Application Security Testing (SAST):</strong> Analysis of application source code, bytecode, or binary code for security vulnerabilities without executing the program.</p><p><strong>Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST):</strong> Analysis of a running application for security vulnerabilities by simulating external attacks.</p><p><strong>Software Composition Analysis (SCA):</strong> The process of identifying and managing open-source and third-party components within an application to detect known vulnerabilities and license compliance issues.</p><p><strong>Threat Model:</strong> A structured representation of all information that affects the security of an application, including assets, threats, vulnerabilities, and mitigations.</p><p><strong>Production Environment:</strong> The operational environment where end-users access the live application and data.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates that security and privacy requirements shall be defined, implemented, and verified throughout the entire software development lifecycle. Security is a non-negotiable requirement and shall be integrated into all phases—from initial concept and design through deployment, operation, and decommissioning. The ad-hoc introduction of security controls as an afterthought is strictly prohibited.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1 Requirements and Design Phase</h4><p>5.1.1 Security and privacy requirements shall be documented in formal requirement specifications and user stories, and shall be traceable through the development lifecycle.</p><p>5.1.2 A formal threat modeling exercise, utilizing a recognized methodology (e.g., STRIDE), shall be conducted for all new applications and for significant modifications to existing applications. Threat models shall be documented and approved by the development lead and a security representative.</p><p>5.1.3 All application architectures and high-level designs must be reviewed for security implications. The use of deprecated or insecure protocols (e.g., SSLv3, TLS 1.0/1.1) is forbidden.</p><p>5.1.4 Data classification must be performed, and handling requirements for sensitive data (e.g., PII, financial data, intellectual property) shall be explicitly defined in the design.</p><h4>5.2 Implementation Phase</h4><p>5.2.1 Developers shall adhere to the [Company Name] Secure Coding Standards, which prohibit common vulnerabilities (e.g., OWASP Top 10).</p><p>5.2.2 All code commits must be peer-reviewed with a focus on security. Reviews shall be documented within the version control system.</p><p>5.2.3 The use of open-source and third-party libraries must be governed by a formal Software Composition Analysis (SCA) process. Components with known critical or high-severity vulnerabilities shall not be approved for use.</p><p>5.2.4 Hard-coded secrets (e.g., passwords, API keys, cryptographic keys) are strictly prohibited. All secrets must be managed through a dedicated, approved secrets management solution.</p><h4>5.3 Verification and Testing Phase</h4><p>5.3.1 Static Application Security Testing (SAST) shall be performed on all application source code. Findings of critical or high severity must be remediated prior to release.</p><p>5.3.2 Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST) or Interactive Application Security Testing (IAST) shall be performed against all staging or pre-production application builds.</p><p>5.3.3 Software Composition Analysis (SCA) scans shall be run to generate a Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) and identify vulnerable dependencies. No application with unresolved critical or high-severity component vulnerabilities may be promoted to production.</p><p>5.3.4 Penetration testing by an independent, qualified third party shall be conducted for all internet-facing applications and applications handling sensitive data at least annually, or following any major release.</p><h4>5.4 Release and Deployment Phase</h4><p>5.4.1 A formal security approval gate, requiring sign-off from the Application Security team or designated security champion, is mandatory for all production deployments.</p><p>5.4.2 Deployment to production environments must be performed through automated, auditable pipelines. Manual deployments or direct access to production servers by developers is strictly prohibited, except for approved break-glass procedures.</p><p>5.4.3 All application infrastructure (e.g., containers, virtual machines, serverless functions) must be provisioned from hardened, security-approved templates or images.</p><h4>5.5 Operations and Maintenance Phase</h4><p>5.5.1 A continuous monitoring program for applications in production shall be implemented, including monitoring for anomalous behavior, unauthorized access attempts, and vulnerability alerts.</p><p>5.5.2 A formal vulnerability management process shall govern the triage, prioritization, and remediation of security vulnerabilities discovered in production applications. Critical vulnerabilities must be remediated within 7 days of notification; high-severity vulnerabilities within 30 days.</p><p>5.5.3 All changes to production applications, including patches and configuration modifications, must follow the formal change management process and be tested in a non-production environment first.</p><h4>5.6 Decommissioning Phase</h4><p>5.6.1 A decommissioning plan must be created for applications being retired, detailing the secure archival or destruction of all associated data and credentials.</p><p>5.6.2 All persistent data storage (e.g., databases, file stores, backups) associated with the decommissioned application shall be securely wiped or destroyed in accordance with data retention and destruction policies.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>All software development activities must comply with relevant laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), financial industry standards (e.g., PCI DSS), and sector-specific requirements. The Secure SDLC process is designed to provide demonstrable evidence of due care for security and privacy, supporting audits for ISO 27001, SOC 2, and other compliance frameworks. Non-compliance with this policy may result in legal, financial, and reputational consequences for [Company Name].</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the policy, its enforcement, and its alignment with the corporate risk appetite.</p><p><strong>Head of Software Development/Engineering:</strong> Operational responsibility for implementing the Secure SDLC processes within development teams and ensuring resource allocation for security activities.</p><p><strong>Application Security Team:</strong> Responsible for defining secure coding standards, maintaining security testing tools, conducting security reviews, and providing expert guidance to development teams.</p><p><strong>Development Team Leads:</strong> Responsible for ensuring their teams adhere to this policy, facilitating threat modeling, enforcing peer reviews, and managing the remediation of security findings.</p><p><strong>Software Developers:</strong> Responsible for writing secure code, participating in security training, performing peer reviews, and remediating security vulnerabilities in their code.</p><p><strong>Quality Assurance (QA) Engineers:</strong> Responsible for integrating security test cases into QA processes and validating the remediation of security defects.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement. The CISO and Information Security team are authorized to block the deployment of any application that does not meet the security gates defined in this policy. Exceptions to this policy require a formal risk assessment and written approval from the CISO or their designated delegate.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Governance Committee at least annually, or following significant security incidents or changes in the threat landscape, technology stack, or regulatory environment. Proposed revisions must be formally documented, reviewed for risk impact, and approved by the CISO. The latest approved version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel and third parties to whom it applies.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-20 21:17:23.030157', '"[\"ISO 27001\",\"SOC 2\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 19:39:27.647', NULL),
	(18, '13', 'Change Management Policy', '<p>This Change Management Policy establishes the formal framework and mandatory procedures for controlling, tracking, and documenting all changes to [Company Name]''s information systems, technology infrastructure, applications, and associated processes. The purpose is to ensure that changes are introduced in a controlled, secure, and auditable manner to minimize risk, prevent unauthorized modifications, maintain system integrity, and ensure service availability in alignment with business objectives and compliance obligations.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party service providers ("Personnel") who are involved in the request, approval, development, testing, implementation, or review of any change to [Company Name]''s production environments, including but not limited to:</p><ul><li>Network infrastructure, hardware, and software configurations.</li><li>Operating systems, databases, and middleware.</li><li>Business applications, both developed in-house and third-party.</li><li>Security controls, policies, and rule sets (e.g., firewall rules, IDS/IPS signatures).</li><li>Cloud infrastructure and platform services (IaaS, PaaS).</li><li>Documentation, procedures, and configurations that directly impact system security or service delivery.</li></ul><p>Emergency changes, as defined in Section 3, are also within scope but follow a distinct, expedited process.</p>

<p><strong>Change:</strong> Any addition, modification, or removal of a component within the scope that could affect services, security, or compliance.</p><p><strong>Change Request (CR):</strong> The formal, documented submission proposing a change, containing all required information for assessment and approval.</p><p><strong>Change Advisory Board (CAB):</strong> The formally constituted group of stakeholders responsible for assessing, prioritizing, and approving changes. The CAB shall include, at minimum, representatives from IT, Security, and relevant business units.</p><p><strong>Emergency Change:</strong> A change required to resolve a critical incident, patch a severe security vulnerability, or prevent imminent and significant service disruption. It bypasses the standard CAB approval process but requires post-implementation review and documentation.</p><p><strong>Production Environment:</strong> The live operational environment that delivers services to end-users or supports critical business functions.</p><p><strong>Backout Plan:</strong> A documented, tested procedure to restore a system to its last known working state in the event of a failed change implementation.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all changes within the scope of this policy shall be managed through a formal, documented, and auditable process. Unauthorized, untested, or undocumented changes to production systems are strictly prohibited. The process shall ensure that all changes are assessed for risk, approved by appropriate authority, tested, implemented in a controlled manner, and reviewed post-implementation to confirm success and identify required improvements.</p>

<h4>5.1 Change Classification and Categorization</h4><p>All changes shall be classified based on risk, impact, and urgency at the time of request. A standard classification model (e.g., Major, Minor, Standard) with predefined approval paths and testing requirements shall be used. Security-related changes shall be automatically classified as at least "Minor" and require security team review.</p><h4>5.2 Formal Change Request Submission</h4><p>A Change Request (CR) must be submitted through the authorized ticketing or change management system. The CR shall, at minimum, include: a unique identifier; description and business justification; proposed implementation and backout plans; risk assessment; impact analysis on security, availability, and performance; list of affected configuration items (CIs); and designated implementation team and window.</p><h4>5.3 Risk Assessment and Approval</h4><p>Every CR shall undergo a formal risk assessment. The CAB shall review all non-emergency Major and Minor changes. Approval authority is delegated based on change classification. For Major changes, final approval authority rests with the CAB Chair or designated management. All approvals shall be documented within the change management system.</p><h4>5.4 Pre-Implementation Testing</h4><p>No change shall be deployed to production without successful testing in a representative, non-production environment. Testing must validate functionality, security controls, performance, and the effectiveness of the backout plan. Evidence of successful testing shall be attached to the CR prior to final approval.</p><h4>5.5 Implementation Controls</h4><p>Approved changes shall be implemented only during authorized maintenance windows by authorized personnel. Implementation must follow the approved plan. Direct access to production systems for change implementation shall be logged and monitored. Automated deployment tools are preferred, and their use shall be logged.</p><h4>5.6 Emergency Change Procedure</h4><p>Emergency changes require authorization from the on-call IT Manager and the Information Security Officer (or designates). Standard CAB approval is waived. The individual authorizing the emergency change assumes responsibility for its risk. A full CR with all required details, including justification for the emergency classification, must be documented within one (1) business day of implementation for post-facto CAB review.</p><h4>5.7 Post-Implementation Review (PIR)</h4><p>A Post-Implementation Review shall be conducted for all Major changes and any failed changes. The PIR shall verify that the change met its objectives, did not introduce adverse effects, and that documentation is updated. Results of the PIR shall be recorded in the CR.</p><h4>5.8 Documentation and Audit Trail</h4><p>The change management system shall maintain a complete, immutable audit trail for every CR, including all submissions, assessments, approvals, test results, implementation notes, and review outcomes. This log shall be retained for a minimum of seven (7) years and be readily available for internal and external audits.</p>

<p>The Change Management process shall be designed and operated to meet the control objectives of relevant frameworks, including but not limited to:</p><ul><li><strong>ISO 27001:2022 (A.8.32 Change Management):</strong> To ensure the integrity, availability, and confidentiality of information systems is maintained throughout changes.</li><li><strong>SOC 2 (CC7.1):</strong> To demonstrate that the system was developed, implemented, and modified in a controlled manner, and that logical access security is maintained.</li><li><strong>Regulatory Requirements:</strong> The process shall support compliance with data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, HIPAA) by ensuring changes do not inadvertently weaken security controls protecting sensitive data.</li></ul><p>Evidence of compliance with this policy shall be produced for internal and external audits upon request.</p>

<p><strong>Change Requester:</strong> Responsible for submitting complete and accurate CRs, including impact assessment and backout plans.</p><p><strong>Change Advisory Board (CAB):</strong> Responsible for evaluating, prioritizing, and approving changes based on business need, risk, and resource availability.</p><p><strong>CAB Chair / Change Manager:</strong> Responsible for overseeing the change management process, facilitating CAB meetings, and ensuring policy adherence.</p><p><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Responsible for implementing approved changes in accordance with the approved plan and maintaining the change management system.</p><p><strong>Information Security Officer (ISO):</strong> Responsible for reviewing all changes for security implications, classifying security-related changes, and authorizing emergency changes involving security vulnerabilities.</p><p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for adhering to this policy and reporting any observed unauthorized changes.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement, and legal action. Unauthorized changes that result in a security incident, data breach, or significant service outage will be treated with particular severity. The Information Security and IT Departments are authorized to monitor systems and logs for compliance with this policy and to roll back unauthorized changes.</p>

<p>This Change Management Policy shall be reviewed at least annually by the CAB Chair and the Information Security Officer. Revisions shall be made in response to changes in technology, business processes, regulatory landscape, or audit findings. All revisions require approval from the Chief Information Officer (CIO) or equivalent. The latest approved version shall be made accessible to all personnel covered by its scope.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Change Management Policy establishes the formal framework and mandatory procedures for controlling, tracking, and documenting all changes to [Company Name]''s information systems, technology infrastructure, applications, and associated processes. The purpose is to ensure that changes are introduced in a controlled, secure, and auditable manner to minimize risk, prevent unauthorized modifications, maintain system integrity, and ensure service availability in alignment with business objectives and compliance obligations.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party service providers (\\\"Personnel\\\") who are involved in the request, approval, development, testing, implementation, or review of any change to [Company Name]''s production environments, including but not limited to:</p><ul><li>Network infrastructure, hardware, and software configurations.</li><li>Operating systems, databases, and middleware.</li><li>Business applications, both developed in-house and third-party.</li><li>Security controls, policies, and rule sets (e.g., firewall rules, IDS/IPS signatures).</li><li>Cloud infrastructure and platform services (IaaS, PaaS).</li><li>Documentation, procedures, and configurations that directly impact system security or service delivery.</li></ul><p>Emergency changes, as defined in Section 3, are also within scope but follow a distinct, expedited process.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Change:</strong> Any addition, modification, or removal of a component within the scope that could affect services, security, or compliance.</p><p><strong>Change Request (CR):</strong> The formal, documented submission proposing a change, containing all required information for assessment and approval.</p><p><strong>Change Advisory Board (CAB):</strong> The formally constituted group of stakeholders responsible for assessing, prioritizing, and approving changes. The CAB shall include, at minimum, representatives from IT, Security, and relevant business units.</p><p><strong>Emergency Change:</strong> A change required to resolve a critical incident, patch a severe security vulnerability, or prevent imminent and significant service disruption. It bypasses the standard CAB approval process but requires post-implementation review and documentation.</p><p><strong>Production Environment:</strong> The live operational environment that delivers services to end-users or supports critical business functions.</p><p><strong>Backout Plan:</strong> A documented, tested procedure to restore a system to its last known working state in the event of a failed change implementation.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates that all changes within the scope of this policy shall be managed through a formal, documented, and auditable process. Unauthorized, untested, or undocumented changes to production systems are strictly prohibited. The process shall ensure that all changes are assessed for risk, approved by appropriate authority, tested, implemented in a controlled manner, and reviewed post-implementation to confirm success and identify required improvements.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1 Change Classification and Categorization</h4><p>All changes shall be classified based on risk, impact, and urgency at the time of request. A standard classification model (e.g., Major, Minor, Standard) with predefined approval paths and testing requirements shall be used. Security-related changes shall be automatically classified as at least \\\"Minor\\\" and require security team review.</p><h4>5.2 Formal Change Request Submission</h4><p>A Change Request (CR) must be submitted through the authorized ticketing or change management system. The CR shall, at minimum, include: a unique identifier; description and business justification; proposed implementation and backout plans; risk assessment; impact analysis on security, availability, and performance; list of affected configuration items (CIs); and designated implementation team and window.</p><h4>5.3 Risk Assessment and Approval</h4><p>Every CR shall undergo a formal risk assessment. The CAB shall review all non-emergency Major and Minor changes. Approval authority is delegated based on change classification. For Major changes, final approval authority rests with the CAB Chair or designated management. All approvals shall be documented within the change management system.</p><h4>5.4 Pre-Implementation Testing</h4><p>No change shall be deployed to production without successful testing in a representative, non-production environment. Testing must validate functionality, security controls, performance, and the effectiveness of the backout plan. Evidence of successful testing shall be attached to the CR prior to final approval.</p><h4>5.5 Implementation Controls</h4><p>Approved changes shall be implemented only during authorized maintenance windows by authorized personnel. Implementation must follow the approved plan. Direct access to production systems for change implementation shall be logged and monitored. Automated deployment tools are preferred, and their use shall be logged.</p><h4>5.6 Emergency Change Procedure</h4><p>Emergency changes require authorization from the on-call IT Manager and the Information Security Officer (or designates). Standard CAB approval is waived. The individual authorizing the emergency change assumes responsibility for its risk. A full CR with all required details, including justification for the emergency classification, must be documented within one (1) business day of implementation for post-facto CAB review.</p><h4>5.7 Post-Implementation Review (PIR)</h4><p>A Post-Implementation Review shall be conducted for all Major changes and any failed changes. The PIR shall verify that the change met its objectives, did not introduce adverse effects, and that documentation is updated. Results of the PIR shall be recorded in the CR.</p><h4>5.8 Documentation and Audit Trail</h4><p>The change management system shall maintain a complete, immutable audit trail for every CR, including all submissions, assessments, approvals, test results, implementation notes, and review outcomes. This log shall be retained for a minimum of seven (7) years and be readily available for internal and external audits.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>The Change Management process shall be designed and operated to meet the control objectives of relevant frameworks, including but not limited to:</p><ul><li><strong>ISO 27001:2022 (A.8.32 Change Management):</strong> To ensure the integrity, availability, and confidentiality of information systems is maintained throughout changes.</li><li><strong>SOC 2 (CC7.1):</strong> To demonstrate that the system was developed, implemented, and modified in a controlled manner, and that logical access security is maintained.</li><li><strong>Regulatory Requirements:</strong> The process shall support compliance with data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, HIPAA) by ensuring changes do not inadvertently weaken security controls protecting sensitive data.</li></ul><p>Evidence of compliance with this policy shall be produced for internal and external audits upon request.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Change Requester:</strong> Responsible for submitting complete and accurate CRs, including impact assessment and backout plans.</p><p><strong>Change Advisory Board (CAB):</strong> Responsible for evaluating, prioritizing, and approving changes based on business need, risk, and resource availability.</p><p><strong>CAB Chair / Change Manager:</strong> Responsible for overseeing the change management process, facilitating CAB meetings, and ensuring policy adherence.</p><p><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Responsible for implementing approved changes in accordance with the approved plan and maintaining the change management system.</p><p><strong>Information Security Officer (ISO):</strong> Responsible for reviewing all changes for security implications, classifying security-related changes, and authorizing emergency changes involving security vulnerabilities.</p><p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for adhering to this policy and reporting any observed unauthorized changes.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement, and legal action. Unauthorized changes that result in a security incident, data breach, or significant service outage will be treated with particular severity. The Information Security and IT Departments are authorized to monitor systems and logs for compliance with this policy and to roll back unauthorized changes.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This Change Management Policy shall be reviewed at least annually by the CAB Chair and the Information Security Officer. Revisions shall be made in response to changes in technology, business processes, regulatory landscape, or audit findings. All revisions require approval from the Chief Information Officer (CIO) or equivalent. The latest approved version shall be made accessible to all personnel covered by its scope.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-19 06:15:56.920562', '"[\"Both\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 19:40:07.084', NULL),
	(37, '270222', 'Source Code Protection Policy', '<p>This Source Code Protection Policy establishes the mandatory controls and procedures for safeguarding the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of all proprietary source code assets belonging to [Company Name]. The policy mandates a defense-in-depth approach to prevent unauthorized access, exfiltration, tampering, or loss of source code, which constitutes a critical business asset and intellectual property.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all source code, scripts, configuration files, infrastructure-as-code definitions, database schema definitions, and any other human-readable instructions written for the purpose of creating, configuring, or operating software, systems, or services owned by or developed for [Company Name]. This includes all development, staging, and production repositories, regardless of hosting location (on-premises, cloud, or third-party platforms) or development methodology employed.</p>

<p><strong>Source Code:</strong> Human-readable instructions written in a programming language that define the logic, functionality, and configuration of software applications, systems, or services, including associated comments and documentation.</p><p><strong>Repository:</strong> A centralized storage location for source code, managed by a Version Control System (VCS).</p><p><strong>Principle of Least Privilege (PoLP):</strong> The security principle that any user, system, or process shall be granted the minimum levels of access necessary to perform its authorized functions.</p><p><strong>Static Application Security Testing (SAST):</strong> The process of analyzing source code for security vulnerabilities without executing the program.</p><p><strong>Secrets:</strong> Confidential data such as passwords, API keys, tokens, certificates, and encryption keys that are embedded within or required by source code.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates the protection of all Source Code through stringent access controls, encryption, secure development practices, and continuous monitoring. The unauthorized access, disclosure, modification, or destruction of Source Code is strictly prohibited. All personnel and systems interacting with Source Code shall adhere to the requirements defined in this policy.</p>

<h4>5.1 Access Control and Authentication</h4><p>5.1.1 Access to Source Code repositories shall be secured using multi-factor authentication (MFA) for all human users. The use of password-only authentication is forbidden.</p><p>5.1.2 Access shall be provisioned strictly according to the Principle of Least Privilege (PoLP). Role-based access control (RBAC) models must be implemented and regularly reviewed.</p><p>5.1.3 All administrative or privileged access to repository management systems (e.g., organization, repository, or branch-level admin rights) must be justified, documented, and approved by the system owner and the Information Security Team.</p><p>5.1.4 Service accounts or automated processes (CI/CD pipelines) accessing repositories must use short-lived, machine-generated credentials or tokens, which must be stored in a secure secrets management solution and rotated at least every 90 days.</p><p>5.1.5 Session management for web-based repository interfaces shall enforce idle session timeouts not to exceed 15 minutes.</p><h4>5.2 Repository and Data Protection</h4><p>5.2.1 All source code repositories at rest shall be encrypted using strong, industry-accepted cryptographic standards (e.g., AES-256).</p><p>5.2.2 Source code in transit between clients and repositories, and between repository nodes, must be encrypted using TLS 1.2 or higher.</p><p>5.2.3 Public repository hosting is strictly prohibited for any source code classified as Confidential or Proprietary without explicit written authorization from the CISO.</p><p>5.2.4 Branch protection rules shall be enforced on all main/master and production release branches. At a minimum, these rules must require pull requests, successful status checks (e.g., build, SAST), and at least one approved code review from an authorized peer before merging.</p><p>5.2.5 A complete, immutable audit log of all repository activities (commits, pushes, merges, access, permission changes) must be enabled, centrally collected, and retained for a minimum of one year for analysis and forensic purposes.</p><h4>5.3 Secure Development and Code Integrity</h4><p>5.3.1 All commits pushed to shared repositories must be cryptographically signed using GPG or S/MIME signatures to verify author authenticity. Unsigned commits shall be rejected or flagged for review.</p><p>5.3.2 Static Application Security Testing (SAST) must be integrated into the CI/CD pipeline. Code commits that introduce critical or high-severity vulnerabilities, as defined by the OWASP Top 10 or equivalent standard, must automatically fail the build and prevent merge to protected branches.</p><p>5.3.3 The inclusion of hard-coded secrets (passwords, API keys, private keys) within source code is strictly forbidden. All secrets must be referenced via environment variables or a dedicated secrets management service.</p><p>5.3.4 Dependencies (libraries, packages, modules) must be sourced from official, vetted repositories. A Software Composition Analysis (SCA) tool must be used to inventory dependencies and alert on known vulnerabilities and license compliance issues.</p><h4>5.4 Backup, Retention, and Disposal</h4><p>5.4.1 All source code repositories must be backed up in their entirety, including full commit history and metadata, at least daily. Backups must be stored in a geographically separate location from the primary repository and encrypted.</p><p>5.4.2 Backup integrity must be verified through restoration testing at least semi-annually.</p><p>5.4.3 Upon project decommissioning or at the direction of legal counsel, source code must be securely archived or destroyed in accordance with data retention policies. Secure deletion methods must render data unrecoverable.</p>

<p>This policy supports compliance with ISO 27001 controls, specifically A.8.1.3 (Acceptable use of assets), A.8.2.1 (Information classification), A.8.2.3 (Handling of assets), A.9 (Access control), A.10 (Cryptography), A.12.1.2 (Change management), A.12.4 (Logging and monitoring), A.12.5 (Control of operational software), A.14.2 (Security in development and support processes), and A.18.1 (Compliance with legal and contractual requirements). It also aligns with SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security (CC6.1, CC6.3, CC6.6, CC6.7, CC6.8) and Availability. Adherence to this policy is mandatory to protect intellectual property rights and meet contractual obligations with clients and partners.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the policy''s approval, enforcement, and overall effectiveness. Responsible for overseeing the security architecture of source code management systems.</p><p><strong>Development/Engineering Leadership:</strong> Responsible for ensuring development teams understand and comply with this policy. Must allocate resources for implementing required security controls within the development lifecycle.</p><p><strong>Software Developers/Engineers:</strong> Responsible for adhering to all secure coding practices, access controls, and procedures defined herein. Must report any suspected policy violations or security incidents immediately.</p><p><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for implementing and monitoring technical controls, conducting audits and reviews, managing access reviews, and investigating incidents related to source code.</p><p><strong>System Owners/Repository Administrators:</strong> Responsible for the day-to-day configuration, maintenance, and access management of source code repositories in accordance with this policy.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal prosecution. The Information Security Team is authorized to immediately revoke access, quarantine repositories, or take other technical measures to mitigate risks arising from policy violations. Exceptions to this policy require a formal risk assessment and written approval from the CISO.</p>

<p>This Source Code Protection Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Team at least annually, or following any significant security incident or change in technology or regulatory landscape. Proposed revisions must be approved by the CISO. The latest version of this policy shall be made accessible to all relevant personnel.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Source Code Protection Policy establishes the mandatory controls and procedures for safeguarding the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of all proprietary source code assets belonging to [Company Name]. The policy mandates a defense-in-depth approach to prevent unauthorized access, exfiltration, tampering, or loss of source code, which constitutes a critical business asset and intellectual property.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all source code, scripts, configuration files, infrastructure-as-code definitions, database schema definitions, and any other human-readable instructions written for the purpose of creating, configuring, or operating software, systems, or services owned by or developed for [Company Name]. This includes all development, staging, and production repositories, regardless of hosting location (on-premises, cloud, or third-party platforms) or development methodology employed.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Source Code:</strong> Human-readable instructions written in a programming language that define the logic, functionality, and configuration of software applications, systems, or services, including associated comments and documentation.</p><p><strong>Repository:</strong> A centralized storage location for source code, managed by a Version Control System (VCS).</p><p><strong>Principle of Least Privilege (PoLP):</strong> The security principle that any user, system, or process shall be granted the minimum levels of access necessary to perform its authorized functions.</p><p><strong>Static Application Security Testing (SAST):</strong> The process of analyzing source code for security vulnerabilities without executing the program.</p><p><strong>Secrets:</strong> Confidential data such as passwords, API keys, tokens, certificates, and encryption keys that are embedded within or required by source code.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates the protection of all Source Code through stringent access controls, encryption, secure development practices, and continuous monitoring. The unauthorized access, disclosure, modification, or destruction of Source Code is strictly prohibited. All personnel and systems interacting with Source Code shall adhere to the requirements defined in this policy.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1 Access Control and Authentication</h4><p>5.1.1 Access to Source Code repositories shall be secured using multi-factor authentication (MFA) for all human users. The use of password-only authentication is forbidden.</p><p>5.1.2 Access shall be provisioned strictly according to the Principle of Least Privilege (PoLP). Role-based access control (RBAC) models must be implemented and regularly reviewed.</p><p>5.1.3 All administrative or privileged access to repository management systems (e.g., organization, repository, or branch-level admin rights) must be justified, documented, and approved by the system owner and the Information Security Team.</p><p>5.1.4 Service accounts or automated processes (CI/CD pipelines) accessing repositories must use short-lived, machine-generated credentials or tokens, which must be stored in a secure secrets management solution and rotated at least every 90 days.</p><p>5.1.5 Session management for web-based repository interfaces shall enforce idle session timeouts not to exceed 15 minutes.</p><h4>5.2 Repository and Data Protection</h4><p>5.2.1 All source code repositories at rest shall be encrypted using strong, industry-accepted cryptographic standards (e.g., AES-256).</p><p>5.2.2 Source code in transit between clients and repositories, and between repository nodes, must be encrypted using TLS 1.2 or higher.</p><p>5.2.3 Public repository hosting is strictly prohibited for any source code classified as Confidential or Proprietary without explicit written authorization from the CISO.</p><p>5.2.4 Branch protection rules shall be enforced on all main/master and production release branches. At a minimum, these rules must require pull requests, successful status checks (e.g., build, SAST), and at least one approved code review from an authorized peer before merging.</p><p>5.2.5 A complete, immutable audit log of all repository activities (commits, pushes, merges, access, permission changes) must be enabled, centrally collected, and retained for a minimum of one year for analysis and forensic purposes.</p><h4>5.3 Secure Development and Code Integrity</h4><p>5.3.1 All commits pushed to shared repositories must be cryptographically signed using GPG or S/MIME signatures to verify author authenticity. Unsigned commits shall be rejected or flagged for review.</p><p>5.3.2 Static Application Security Testing (SAST) must be integrated into the CI/CD pipeline. Code commits that introduce critical or high-severity vulnerabilities, as defined by the OWASP Top 10 or equivalent standard, must automatically fail the build and prevent merge to protected branches.</p><p>5.3.3 The inclusion of hard-coded secrets (passwords, API keys, private keys) within source code is strictly forbidden. All secrets must be referenced via environment variables or a dedicated secrets management service.</p><p>5.3.4 Dependencies (libraries, packages, modules) must be sourced from official, vetted repositories. A Software Composition Analysis (SCA) tool must be used to inventory dependencies and alert on known vulnerabilities and license compliance issues.</p><h4>5.4 Backup, Retention, and Disposal</h4><p>5.4.1 All source code repositories must be backed up in their entirety, including full commit history and metadata, at least daily. Backups must be stored in a geographically separate location from the primary repository and encrypted.</p><p>5.4.2 Backup integrity must be verified through restoration testing at least semi-annually.</p><p>5.4.3 Upon project decommissioning or at the direction of legal counsel, source code must be securely archived or destroyed in accordance with data retention policies. Secure deletion methods must render data unrecoverable.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy supports compliance with ISO 27001 controls, specifically A.8.1.3 (Acceptable use of assets), A.8.2.1 (Information classification), A.8.2.3 (Handling of assets), A.9 (Access control), A.10 (Cryptography), A.12.1.2 (Change management), A.12.4 (Logging and monitoring), A.12.5 (Control of operational software), A.14.2 (Security in development and support processes), and A.18.1 (Compliance with legal and contractual requirements). It also aligns with SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security (CC6.1, CC6.3, CC6.6, CC6.7, CC6.8) and Availability. Adherence to this policy is mandatory to protect intellectual property rights and meet contractual obligations with clients and partners.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the policy''s approval, enforcement, and overall effectiveness. Responsible for overseeing the security architecture of source code management systems.</p><p><strong>Development/Engineering Leadership:</strong> Responsible for ensuring development teams understand and comply with this policy. Must allocate resources for implementing required security controls within the development lifecycle.</p><p><strong>Software Developers/Engineers:</strong> Responsible for adhering to all secure coding practices, access controls, and procedures defined herein. Must report any suspected policy violations or security incidents immediately.</p><p><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for implementing and monitoring technical controls, conducting audits and reviews, managing access reviews, and investigating incidents related to source code.</p><p><strong>System Owners/Repository Administrators:</strong> Responsible for the day-to-day configuration, maintenance, and access management of source code repositories in accordance with this policy.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal prosecution. The Information Security Team is authorized to immediately revoke access, quarantine repositories, or take other technical measures to mitigate risks arising from policy violations. Exceptions to this policy require a formal risk assessment and written approval from the CISO.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This Source Code Protection Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Team at least annually, or following any significant security incident or change in technology or regulatory landscape. Proposed revisions must be approved by the CISO. The latest version of this policy shall be made accessible to all relevant personnel.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-20 21:17:23.13553', '"[\"ISO 27001\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 19:40:45.904', NULL),
	(20, '15', 'Privacy Policy', '<p>This Privacy Policy establishes the mandatory framework for the collection, use, disclosure, storage, and disposal of Personal Data by [Company Name]. It is designed to ensure compliance with applicable data protection laws and regulations, uphold the organization''s ethical commitments, and maintain the trust of data subjects. The policy mandates a risk-based approach to privacy, integrating data protection principles into all business processes and technical systems.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary workers, and other personnel (collectively, "Personnel") of [Company Name]. It governs all processing activities involving Personal Data, regardless of the data format (electronic, paper, or other), the location of processing (on-premises, cloud, or third-party), or the jurisdiction in which the data subject resides. This includes data processed by automated means and data forming part of a filing system.</p>

<p><strong>Personal Data:</strong> Any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person ("Data Subject"). An identifiable person is one who can be identified, directly or indirectly, in particular by reference to an identifier such as a name, identification number, location data, online identifier, or to one or more factors specific to the physical, physiological, genetic, mental, economic, cultural, or social identity of that person.</p><p><strong>Processing:</strong> Any operation or set of operations performed on Personal Data, whether or not by automated means, such as collection, recording, organization, structuring, storage, adaptation, alteration, retrieval, consultation, use, disclosure by transmission, dissemination, alignment, combination, restriction, erasure, or destruction.</p><p><strong>Data Controller:</strong> The entity that determines the purposes and means of the processing of Personal Data.</p><p><strong>Data Processor:</strong> The entity that processes Personal Data on behalf of the Data Controller.</p><p><strong>Sensitive Personal Data:</strong> A special category of Personal Data requiring heightened protection, including data revealing racial or ethnic origin, political opinions, religious or philosophical beliefs, trade union membership, genetic data, biometric data for the purpose of uniquely identifying a natural person, data concerning health, or data concerning a natural person''s sex life or sexual orientation.</p>

<p>[Company Name] is committed to protecting the privacy and confidentiality of Personal Data. All processing activities shall adhere to the principles of lawfulness, fairness, transparency, purpose limitation, data minimization, accuracy, storage limitation, integrity, and confidentiality (security). [Company Name] shall implement appropriate technical and organizational measures to ensure and demonstrate compliance with these principles and applicable legal obligations.</p>

<h4>5.1 Lawful Basis for Processing</h4><p>Processing of Personal Data shall only occur when a valid, documented lawful basis exists. The specific basis (e.g., consent, contract, legal obligation, vital interests, public task, legitimate interests) must be identified and recorded prior to processing initiation. For Sensitive Personal Data, an additional condition under applicable law must be satisfied. Reliance on consent requires a clear, affirmative action; pre-ticked boxes or inactivity does not constitute valid consent.</p><h4>5.2 Data Collection and Purpose Limitation</h4><p>Personal Data shall be collected for specified, explicit, and legitimate purposes and not further processed in a manner incompatible with those purposes. At the point of collection, Data Subjects shall be provided with a privacy notice containing all information required by law, including the identity of the controller, purposes of processing, legal basis, data retention periods, and Data Subject rights.</p><h4>5.3 Data Minimization and Accuracy</h4><p>Collected Personal Data shall be adequate, relevant, and limited to what is necessary for the stated purposes. Procedures shall be established to ensure Personal Data is kept accurate and, where necessary, up to date. Inaccurate data shall be rectified or erased without undue delay.</p><h4>5.4 Data Subject Rights Management</h4><p>A formal process shall be implemented to facilitate and log Data Subject rights requests (access, rectification, erasure, restriction, portability, objection). All requests shall be acknowledged within the timeframe mandated by law (e.g., one month under GDPR) and fulfilled using secure, verifiable methods. The ability to fulfill these rights shall be engineered into system design where feasible.</p><h4>5.5 Data Security and Confidentiality</h4><p>Personal Data shall be protected against unauthorized or unlawful processing and against accidental loss, destruction, or damage. This mandate requires the implementation of encryption for data at rest (using AES-256 or equivalent) and in transit (using TLS 1.2 or higher), strict access controls based on the principle of least privilege, comprehensive logging of access and modifications, and regular security testing of applications and infrastructure handling Personal Data.</p><h4>5.6 Data Retention and Secure Disposal</h4><p>Personal Data shall not be kept in a form which permits identification of Data Subjects for longer than is necessary for the purposes for which it is processed. A formal data retention schedule, mapping data types to specific retention periods and legal justifications, shall be maintained and enforced. At the end of the retention period, data shall be securely and irreversibly destroyed using cryptographic erasure for electronic media or cross-cut shredding for physical media.</p><h4>5.7 Third-Party and Cross-Border Data Transfers</h4><p>Any transfer of Personal Data to a third party (Processor or Controller) shall be governed by a written agreement that imposes data protection obligations equivalent to those in this policy. Transfers of Personal Data outside the jurisdiction of origin shall only occur if an approved transfer mechanism (e.g., Standard Contractual Clauses, Binding Corporate Rules, or an Adequacy Decision) is in place and documented prior to the transfer.</p><h4>5.8 Data Protection by Design and by Default</h4><p>Data protection measures shall be integrated into the development of business processes and information systems from the initial design stage (Privacy by Design). Default system settings shall ensure that, by default, only Personal Data necessary for each specific purpose is processed (Privacy by Default). Data Protection Impact Assessments (DPIAs) shall be conducted for any high-risk processing activity prior to its commencement.</p><h4>5.9 Breach Notification</h4><p>A documented incident response plan specific to data breaches shall be maintained. In the event of a suspected or confirmed breach of Personal Data, the DPO/Privacy Officer must be notified immediately. The organization shall, without undue delay and in accordance with legal timeframes (e.g., 72 hours under GDPR), assess the breach and, where required by law, notify the relevant supervisory authority and affected Data Subjects.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant data protection frameworks, including but not limited to the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA)/California Privacy Rights Act (CPRA), and other applicable global, federal, and state regulations. Adherence to the control requirements of SOC 2 (Trust Services Criteria for Security, Availability, Processing Integrity, Confidentiality, and Privacy) and the Annex A controls of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 is mandated. All Personnel are responsible for understanding and complying with the legal obligations relevant to their role.</p>

<p><strong>Data Protection Officer (DPO)/Privacy Officer:</strong> Oversees policy implementation, conducts DPIAs, serves as the point of contact for supervisory authorities and Data Subjects, and provides mandatory privacy training. The DPO shall report directly to the highest level of management.</p><p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Responsible for implementing and maintaining the technical and organizational security measures required by this policy, including encryption, access controls, and logging infrastructure.</p><p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must complete annual privacy and security training, process Personal Data only in accordance with authorized procedures, and immediately report any suspected policy violation or data breach to the DPO or their manager.</p><p><strong>Heads of Business Units:</strong> Accountable for ensuring privacy controls are integrated into their departmental processes and for authorizing the lawful basis for processing activities under their purview.</p>

<p>Violations of this policy will result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and may lead to legal prosecution. The DPO and CISO are authorized to suspend any processing activity deemed non-compliant until corrective actions are verified. Compliance with this policy is a condition of employment and continued engagement with [Company Name].</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed at least annually by the DPO and CISO, or more frequently in response to significant changes in law, technology, business operations, or the risk environment. All revisions must be approved by the executive leadership team. The current version of this policy shall be made readily available to all Personnel and, where appropriate, to Data Subjects.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Privacy Policy establishes the mandatory framework for the collection, use, disclosure, storage, and disposal of Personal Data by [Company Name]. It is designed to ensure compliance with applicable data protection laws and regulations, uphold the organization''s ethical commitments, and maintain the trust of data subjects. The policy mandates a risk-based approach to privacy, integrating data protection principles into all business processes and technical systems.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary workers, and other personnel (collectively, \\\"Personnel\\\") of [Company Name]. It governs all processing activities involving Personal Data, regardless of the data format (electronic, paper, or other), the location of processing (on-premises, cloud, or third-party), or the jurisdiction in which the data subject resides. This includes data processed by automated means and data forming part of a filing system.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Personal Data:</strong> Any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person (\\\"Data Subject\\\"). An identifiable person is one who can be identified, directly or indirectly, in particular by reference to an identifier such as a name, identification number, location data, online identifier, or to one or more factors specific to the physical, physiological, genetic, mental, economic, cultural, or social identity of that person.</p><p><strong>Processing:</strong> Any operation or set of operations performed on Personal Data, whether or not by automated means, such as collection, recording, organization, structuring, storage, adaptation, alteration, retrieval, consultation, use, disclosure by transmission, dissemination, alignment, combination, restriction, erasure, or destruction.</p><p><strong>Data Controller:</strong> The entity that determines the purposes and means of the processing of Personal Data.</p><p><strong>Data Processor:</strong> The entity that processes Personal Data on behalf of the Data Controller.</p><p><strong>Sensitive Personal Data:</strong> A special category of Personal Data requiring heightened protection, including data revealing racial or ethnic origin, political opinions, religious or philosophical beliefs, trade union membership, genetic data, biometric data for the purpose of uniquely identifying a natural person, data concerning health, or data concerning a natural person''s sex life or sexual orientation.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] is committed to protecting the privacy and confidentiality of Personal Data. All processing activities shall adhere to the principles of lawfulness, fairness, transparency, purpose limitation, data minimization, accuracy, storage limitation, integrity, and confidentiality (security). [Company Name] shall implement appropriate technical and organizational measures to ensure and demonstrate compliance with these principles and applicable legal obligations.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1 Lawful Basis for Processing</h4><p>Processing of Personal Data shall only occur when a valid, documented lawful basis exists. The specific basis (e.g., consent, contract, legal obligation, vital interests, public task, legitimate interests) must be identified and recorded prior to processing initiation. For Sensitive Personal Data, an additional condition under applicable law must be satisfied. Reliance on consent requires a clear, affirmative action; pre-ticked boxes or inactivity does not constitute valid consent.</p><h4>5.2 Data Collection and Purpose Limitation</h4><p>Personal Data shall be collected for specified, explicit, and legitimate purposes and not further processed in a manner incompatible with those purposes. At the point of collection, Data Subjects shall be provided with a privacy notice containing all information required by law, including the identity of the controller, purposes of processing, legal basis, data retention periods, and Data Subject rights.</p><h4>5.3 Data Minimization and Accuracy</h4><p>Collected Personal Data shall be adequate, relevant, and limited to what is necessary for the stated purposes. Procedures shall be established to ensure Personal Data is kept accurate and, where necessary, up to date. Inaccurate data shall be rectified or erased without undue delay.</p><h4>5.4 Data Subject Rights Management</h4><p>A formal process shall be implemented to facilitate and log Data Subject rights requests (access, rectification, erasure, restriction, portability, objection). All requests shall be acknowledged within the timeframe mandated by law (e.g., one month under GDPR) and fulfilled using secure, verifiable methods. The ability to fulfill these rights shall be engineered into system design where feasible.</p><h4>5.5 Data Security and Confidentiality</h4><p>Personal Data shall be protected against unauthorized or unlawful processing and against accidental loss, destruction, or damage. This mandate requires the implementation of encryption for data at rest (using AES-256 or equivalent) and in transit (using TLS 1.2 or higher), strict access controls based on the principle of least privilege, comprehensive logging of access and modifications, and regular security testing of applications and infrastructure handling Personal Data.</p><h4>5.6 Data Retention and Secure Disposal</h4><p>Personal Data shall not be kept in a form which permits identification of Data Subjects for longer than is necessary for the purposes for which it is processed. A formal data retention schedule, mapping data types to specific retention periods and legal justifications, shall be maintained and enforced. At the end of the retention period, data shall be securely and irreversibly destroyed using cryptographic erasure for electronic media or cross-cut shredding for physical media.</p><h4>5.7 Third-Party and Cross-Border Data Transfers</h4><p>Any transfer of Personal Data to a third party (Processor or Controller) shall be governed by a written agreement that imposes data protection obligations equivalent to those in this policy. Transfers of Personal Data outside the jurisdiction of origin shall only occur if an approved transfer mechanism (e.g., Standard Contractual Clauses, Binding Corporate Rules, or an Adequacy Decision) is in place and documented prior to the transfer.</p><h4>5.8 Data Protection by Design and by Default</h4><p>Data protection measures shall be integrated into the development of business processes and information systems from the initial design stage (Privacy by Design). Default system settings shall ensure that, by default, only Personal Data necessary for each specific purpose is processed (Privacy by Default). Data Protection Impact Assessments (DPIAs) shall be conducted for any high-risk processing activity prior to its commencement.</p><h4>5.9 Breach Notification</h4><p>A documented incident response plan specific to data breaches shall be maintained. In the event of a suspected or confirmed breach of Personal Data, the DPO/Privacy Officer must be notified immediately. The organization shall, without undue delay and in accordance with legal timeframes (e.g., 72 hours under GDPR), assess the breach and, where required by law, notify the relevant supervisory authority and affected Data Subjects.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant data protection frameworks, including but not limited to the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA)/California Privacy Rights Act (CPRA), and other applicable global, federal, and state regulations. Adherence to the control requirements of SOC 2 (Trust Services Criteria for Security, Availability, Processing Integrity, Confidentiality, and Privacy) and the Annex A controls of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 is mandated. All Personnel are responsible for understanding and complying with the legal obligations relevant to their role.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Data Protection Officer (DPO)/Privacy Officer:</strong> Oversees policy implementation, conducts DPIAs, serves as the point of contact for supervisory authorities and Data Subjects, and provides mandatory privacy training. The DPO shall report directly to the highest level of management.</p><p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Responsible for implementing and maintaining the technical and organizational security measures required by this policy, including encryption, access controls, and logging infrastructure.</p><p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must complete annual privacy and security training, process Personal Data only in accordance with authorized procedures, and immediately report any suspected policy violation or data breach to the DPO or their manager.</p><p><strong>Heads of Business Units:</strong> Accountable for ensuring privacy controls are integrated into their departmental processes and for authorizing the lawful basis for processing activities under their purview.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Violations of this policy will result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and may lead to legal prosecution. The DPO and CISO are authorized to suspend any processing activity deemed non-compliant until corrective actions are verified. Compliance with this policy is a condition of employment and continued engagement with [Company Name].</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy shall be reviewed at least annually by the DPO and CISO, or more frequently in response to significant changes in law, technology, business operations, or the risk environment. All revisions must be approved by the executive leadership team. The current version of this policy shall be made readily available to all Personnel and, where appropriate, to Data Subjects.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-19 06:15:57.352899', '"[\"SOC 2\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 20:07:09.749', NULL),
	(12, '7', 'Risk Management Policy', '<p>The purpose of this Risk Management Policy is to establish a formal, structured, and continuous process for identifying, assessing, treating, monitoring, and communicating information security risks across [Company Name]. This policy mandates a systematic approach to safeguard the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information assets, ensure business continuity, and provide a foundation for informed decision-making. It is designed to fulfill the requirements of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (Clause 6.1) and relevant SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Risk Assessment).</p>

<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, managed, or processed by [Company Name], including but not limited to hardware, software, data, networks, and personnel. It is binding for all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any third parties who access, manage, or support [Company Name]''s information systems and processes. The risk management process shall encompass all organizational activities, business processes, and technological environments.</p>

<p><strong>Risk:</strong> The effect of uncertainty on objectives, expressed in terms of likelihood and consequence of an information security event.<br><strong>Risk Assessment:</strong> The overall process of risk identification, analysis, and evaluation.<br><strong>Risk Treatment:</strong> The process to modify risk through risk avoidance, mitigation, transfer, or acceptance.<br><strong>Residual Risk:</strong> The level of risk remaining after risk treatment measures have been applied.<br><strong>Risk Owner:</strong> The individual or entity with the accountability and authority to manage a specific risk.<br><strong>Inherent Risk:</strong> The level of risk before any controls are applied.<br><strong>Risk Appetite:</strong> The amount and type of risk that [Company Name] is willing to pursue or retain.</p>

<p>[Company Name] shall implement and maintain a comprehensive, documented Information Security Risk Management process. This process shall be integrated into all significant business decisions and operational activities. All identified risks shall be formally assessed, treated according to established criteria, and continuously monitored. Risk acceptance decisions shall be made only by authorized personnel and shall be formally documented. Failure to adhere to this policy and its associated procedures is strictly prohibited.</p>

<h4>5.1 Risk Assessment Framework</h4><p>A formal risk assessment methodology, approved by the CISO, shall be documented and utilized. This methodology must define scales for likelihood, impact, and risk level calculation (e.g., 5x5 matrix). Risk assessments shall be conducted at planned intervals and upon significant changes to the organization, assets, or threat landscape.</p><h4>5.2 Risk Identification</h4><p>All information assets shall be inventoried and classified. Threats and vulnerabilities relevant to these assets shall be identified using sources including, but not limited to, internal audits, threat intelligence feeds, vulnerability scans, and incident reports. This process shall consider internal and external context as defined in ISO 27001.</p><h4>5.3 Risk Analysis and Evaluation</h4><p>Identified risks shall be analyzed to determine their inherent likelihood and impact on confidentiality, integrity, and availability. Risks shall be evaluated against the organization''s defined risk acceptance criteria and risk appetite. All risks evaluated above the acceptance threshold shall be documented in the Risk Register.</p><h4>5.4 Risk Treatment</h4><p>For each risk requiring treatment, a risk treatment plan shall be documented. Treatment options must be selected from: a) <strong>Mitigate</strong> (implementing security controls), b) <strong>Transfer</strong> (e.g., cyber insurance), c) <strong>Avoid</strong> (discontinuing the activity), or d) <strong>Accept</strong> (formal, documented acceptance by the risk owner and CISO). The selection of controls to mitigate risk shall be justified and mapped to the Statement of Applicability (SoA).</p><h4>5.5 Risk Register Maintenance</h4><p>A centralized Risk Register shall be maintained. Each entry shall include, at minimum: risk description, asset(s) affected, inherent risk rating, risk owner, treatment plan, target residual risk, current status, and review date. The Risk Register shall be a controlled document with access restricted to authorized personnel.</p><h4>5.6 Residual Risk and Acceptance</h4><p>Following the implementation of treatment plans, the residual risk level shall be re-assessed. Any decision to accept residual risk that remains above the defined risk appetite must be formally documented, including justification, and approved by the CISO and the relevant business executive. Such acceptances shall be reviewed at least annually.</p><h4>5.7 Monitoring and Review</h4><p>The risk environment and the effectiveness of implemented controls shall be monitored continuously. The entire Risk Management process and the Risk Register shall be formally reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant incidents or changes. Results of these reviews shall be reported to senior management.</p>

<p>The Risk Management process shall be designed to ensure compliance with all applicable legal, statutory, regulatory, and contractual obligations related to information security and data protection (e.g., GDPR, CCPA, HIPAA where applicable). Risk assessments must explicitly consider compliance failures as a source of risk with significant financial and reputational impact. Evidence of the risk management process (assessments, treatment plans, review records) shall be maintained to demonstrate compliance with ISO 27001 and SOC 2 requirements for audits.</p>

<p><strong>Board of Directors / Senior Management:</strong> Establish and approve the organization''s risk appetite and provide overall governance and resources for the risk management program.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Owns and administers this policy, defines the risk methodology, maintains the Risk Register, and reports on risk status. Final authority for operational risk acceptance.<br><strong>Risk Owners (Business/System Owners):</strong> Responsible for identifying risks within their domain, implementing approved treatment plans, and managing accepted risks.<br><strong>Information Technology & Security Teams:</strong> Responsible for executing technical risk assessments (e.g., vulnerability scans), implementing technical controls, and providing subject matter expertise.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to report identified security risks or weaknesses to their manager or through the designated incident reporting channel.</p>

<p>Any employee, contractor, or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal action. Deliberate failure to report significant risks, or the falsification of risk assessment data, is considered gross misconduct. The Information Security Department is authorized to monitor compliance with this policy and conduct periodic reviews of risk management activities.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or following significant security incidents, changes in business operations, or updates to relevant standards and regulations. Revisions must be approved by the CISO and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. The latest version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel covered by its scope.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>The purpose of this Risk Management Policy is to establish a formal, structured, and continuous process for identifying, assessing, treating, monitoring, and communicating information security risks across [Company Name]. This policy mandates a systematic approach to safeguard the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information assets, ensure business continuity, and provide a foundation for informed decision-making. It is designed to fulfill the requirements of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (Clause 6.1) and relevant SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Risk Assessment).</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, managed, or processed by [Company Name], including but not limited to hardware, software, data, networks, and personnel. It is binding for all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any third parties who access, manage, or support [Company Name]''s information systems and processes. The risk management process shall encompass all organizational activities, business processes, and technological environments.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Risk:</strong> The effect of uncertainty on objectives, expressed in terms of likelihood and consequence of an information security event.<br><strong>Risk Assessment:</strong> The overall process of risk identification, analysis, and evaluation.<br><strong>Risk Treatment:</strong> The process to modify risk through risk avoidance, mitigation, transfer, or acceptance.<br><strong>Residual Risk:</strong> The level of risk remaining after risk treatment measures have been applied.<br><strong>Risk Owner:</strong> The individual or entity with the accountability and authority to manage a specific risk.<br><strong>Inherent Risk:</strong> The level of risk before any controls are applied.<br><strong>Risk Appetite:</strong> The amount and type of risk that [Company Name] is willing to pursue or retain.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] shall implement and maintain a comprehensive, documented Information Security Risk Management process. This process shall be integrated into all significant business decisions and operational activities. All identified risks shall be formally assessed, treated according to established criteria, and continuously monitored. Risk acceptance decisions shall be made only by authorized personnel and shall be formally documented. Failure to adhere to this policy and its associated procedures is strictly prohibited.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1 Risk Assessment Framework</h4><p>A formal risk assessment methodology, approved by the CISO, shall be documented and utilized. This methodology must define scales for likelihood, impact, and risk level calculation (e.g., 5x5 matrix). Risk assessments shall be conducted at planned intervals and upon significant changes to the organization, assets, or threat landscape.</p><h4>5.2 Risk Identification</h4><p>All information assets shall be inventoried and classified. Threats and vulnerabilities relevant to these assets shall be identified using sources including, but not limited to, internal audits, threat intelligence feeds, vulnerability scans, and incident reports. This process shall consider internal and external context as defined in ISO 27001.</p><h4>5.3 Risk Analysis and Evaluation</h4><p>Identified risks shall be analyzed to determine their inherent likelihood and impact on confidentiality, integrity, and availability. Risks shall be evaluated against the organization''s defined risk acceptance criteria and risk appetite. All risks evaluated above the acceptance threshold shall be documented in the Risk Register.</p><h4>5.4 Risk Treatment</h4><p>For each risk requiring treatment, a risk treatment plan shall be documented. Treatment options must be selected from: a) <strong>Mitigate</strong> (implementing security controls), b) <strong>Transfer</strong> (e.g., cyber insurance), c) <strong>Avoid</strong> (discontinuing the activity), or d) <strong>Accept</strong> (formal, documented acceptance by the risk owner and CISO). The selection of controls to mitigate risk shall be justified and mapped to the Statement of Applicability (SoA).</p><h4>5.5 Risk Register Maintenance</h4><p>A centralized Risk Register shall be maintained. Each entry shall include, at minimum: risk description, asset(s) affected, inherent risk rating, risk owner, treatment plan, target residual risk, current status, and review date. The Risk Register shall be a controlled document with access restricted to authorized personnel.</p><h4>5.6 Residual Risk and Acceptance</h4><p>Following the implementation of treatment plans, the residual risk level shall be re-assessed. Any decision to accept residual risk that remains above the defined risk appetite must be formally documented, including justification, and approved by the CISO and the relevant business executive. Such acceptances shall be reviewed at least annually.</p><h4>5.7 Monitoring and Review</h4><p>The risk environment and the effectiveness of implemented controls shall be monitored continuously. The entire Risk Management process and the Risk Register shall be formally reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant incidents or changes. Results of these reviews shall be reported to senior management.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>The Risk Management process shall be designed to ensure compliance with all applicable legal, statutory, regulatory, and contractual obligations related to information security and data protection (e.g., GDPR, CCPA, HIPAA where applicable). Risk assessments must explicitly consider compliance failures as a source of risk with significant financial and reputational impact. Evidence of the risk management process (assessments, treatment plans, review records) shall be maintained to demonstrate compliance with ISO 27001 and SOC 2 requirements for audits.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Board of Directors / Senior Management:</strong> Establish and approve the organization''s risk appetite and provide overall governance and resources for the risk management program.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Owns and administers this policy, defines the risk methodology, maintains the Risk Register, and reports on risk status. Final authority for operational risk acceptance.<br><strong>Risk Owners (Business/System Owners):</strong> Responsible for identifying risks within their domain, implementing approved treatment plans, and managing accepted risks.<br><strong>Information Technology & Security Teams:</strong> Responsible for executing technical risk assessments (e.g., vulnerability scans), implementing technical controls, and providing subject matter expertise.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to report identified security risks or weaknesses to their manager or through the designated incident reporting channel.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any employee, contractor, or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal action. Deliberate failure to report significant risks, or the falsification of risk assessment data, is considered gross misconduct. The Information Security Department is authorized to monitor compliance with this policy and conduct periodic reviews of risk management activities.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or following significant security incidents, changes in business operations, or updates to relevant standards and regulations. Revisions must be approved by the CISO and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. The latest version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel covered by its scope.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-19 06:15:55.645013', '"[\"ISO 27001 / SOC 2\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 19:41:19.196', NULL),
	(14, '9', 'Human Resources Security Policy', '<p>This Human Resources Security Policy establishes the mandatory security controls and procedures for managing personnel throughout the employment lifecycle at [Company Name]. Its purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information assets by mitigating risks associated with human factors, ensuring compliance with contractual and regulatory obligations, and embedding security responsibilities into all roles.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all individuals who have, or will have, access to [Company Name] information assets, information processing facilities, or sensitive data. This includes, but is not limited to: full-time and part-time employees, contractors, temporary staff, consultants, interns, and third-party personnel ("Covered Personnel"). The policy governs all phases of the employment relationship: pre-employment, active employment, and termination or change of employment.</p>

<p><strong>Information Assets:</strong> Any data, system, hardware, software, or service owned or used by [Company Name] that has value to the organization.<br/><strong>Least Privilege:</strong> The security principle that any user, process, or program shall be granted the minimum levels of access necessary to perform its function.<br/><strong>Segregation of Duties (SoD):</strong> The principle of separating critical functions among different individuals to reduce the risk of error or fraud.<br/><strong>Formal Sanction Process:</strong> A documented disciplinary procedure for personnel who violate security policies.<br/><strong>Confidentiality Agreement:</strong> A legally binding contract wherein the signee agrees to protect confidential information.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that security responsibilities shall be integrated into all human resource management practices. All Covered Personnel shall be subject to defined security controls prior to, during, and upon termination of their engagement to ensure they are suitable for the roles they are assigned, understand their security obligations, and are prevented from causing malicious or accidental damage to information assets.</p>

<h4>5.1 Pre-Employment Screening</h4><p>All candidates for employment, contractors, and third-party users shall undergo background verification checks commensurate with the business requirements, the classification of information to be accessed, and perceived risks. Checks must include, at a minimum: verification of academic and professional qualifications, validation of employment history for the past three years, and identity verification. For roles with access to highly sensitive systems or data, additional checks such as credit history or enhanced criminal record checks shall be conducted with candidate consent and in accordance with applicable laws.</p><h4>5.2 Terms and Conditions of Employment</h4><p>The contractual terms and conditions for all Covered Personnel shall explicitly state their and [Company Name]''s responsibilities for information security. This shall include: agreement to comply with all applicable security policies and procedures, a legally enforceable confidentiality or non-disclosure agreement (NDA), and acknowledgment that access rights are granted based on the principle of least privilege and may be monitored in accordance with the law.</p><h4>5.3 Management Responsibilities</h4><p>Department managers and project supervisors are responsible for: defining and documenting the security roles and responsibilities for their staff; ensuring personnel understand their security duties before granting access to systems or data; and applying the principles of least privilege and segregation of duties in the design of all roles and access profiles.</p><h4>5.4 Information Security Awareness, Education, and Training</h4><p>All Covered Personnel shall receive formal information security awareness training upon induction and annually thereafter. Training content must be role-specific and cover: acceptable use of assets, password hygiene, incident reporting procedures, clear desk/clear screen policy, secure remote working, and social engineering threats. Completion of training shall be recorded and monitored for compliance.</p><h4>5.5 Disciplinary Process</h4><p>A formal disciplinary process shall be established and communicated to address security breaches committed by personnel. The process shall provide for graduated sanctions, up to and including termination of employment and legal action, and shall be applied consistently and fairly in all cases of policy violation.</p><h4>5.6 Termination or Change of Employment</h4><p>Upon termination of employment or change of job responsibilities, the following controls shall be enforced: all access rights to information assets, premises, and systems shall be revoked immediately, synchronously with the HR notification; all company-owned physical and logical assets (e.g., laptops, mobile devices, ID badges, access tokens, software licenses) shall be returned and accounted for; and a formal exit interview shall be conducted to reiterate ongoing confidentiality obligations.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to satisfy the requirements of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A controls, specifically A.5 (Information Security Policies), A.6 (Organization of Information Security), A.7 (Human Resource Security), and A.13 (Communications Security). It also aligns with SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security and Confidentiality. All personnel screening and disciplinary actions shall be carried out in full compliance with relevant labor laws, data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), and privacy statutes.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority for the approval, implementation, and enforcement of this policy.<br/><strong>Human Resources (HR) Department:</strong> Responsible for executing pre-employment screening, managing the contractual process including NDAs, facilitating security training, and initiating access revocation upon termination in coordination with IT.<br/><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department / System Owners:</strong> Responsible for provisioning and de-provisioning access rights based on authorized requests from HR and management, in accordance with least privilege.<br/><strong>All Managers/Supervisors:</strong> Responsible for defining role-based security requirements, ensuring their team''s compliance with this policy, and reporting security incidents involving their personnel.<br/><strong>All Covered Personnel:</strong> Responsible for understanding and adhering to all aspects of this policy and associated procedures.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and potential legal liability. The IT Department is authorized to immediately suspend or revoke system access for any individual suspected of a serious policy violation pending investigation. Monitoring of system access and user activity will be conducted to ensure compliance with this policy.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the HR Director at least annually, or following significant security incidents or changes in business operations, technology, or regulatory landscape. Revisions must be approved by the CISO. The latest version of this policy shall be made accessible to all Covered Personnel through the company''s official policy repository.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Human Resources Security Policy establishes the mandatory security controls and procedures for managing personnel throughout the employment lifecycle at [Company Name]. Its purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information assets by mitigating risks associated with human factors, ensuring compliance with contractual and regulatory obligations, and embedding security responsibilities into all roles.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all individuals who have, or will have, access to [Company Name] information assets, information processing facilities, or sensitive data. This includes, but is not limited to: full-time and part-time employees, contractors, temporary staff, consultants, interns, and third-party personnel (\\\"Covered Personnel\\\"). The policy governs all phases of the employment relationship: pre-employment, active employment, and termination or change of employment.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Information Assets:</strong> Any data, system, hardware, software, or service owned or used by [Company Name] that has value to the organization.<br/><strong>Least Privilege:</strong> The security principle that any user, process, or program shall be granted the minimum levels of access necessary to perform its function.<br/><strong>Segregation of Duties (SoD):</strong> The principle of separating critical functions among different individuals to reduce the risk of error or fraud.<br/><strong>Formal Sanction Process:</strong> A documented disciplinary procedure for personnel who violate security policies.<br/><strong>Confidentiality Agreement:</strong> A legally binding contract wherein the signee agrees to protect confidential information.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates that security responsibilities shall be integrated into all human resource management practices. All Covered Personnel shall be subject to defined security controls prior to, during, and upon termination of their engagement to ensure they are suitable for the roles they are assigned, understand their security obligations, and are prevented from causing malicious or accidental damage to information assets.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1 Pre-Employment Screening</h4><p>All candidates for employment, contractors, and third-party users shall undergo background verification checks commensurate with the business requirements, the classification of information to be accessed, and perceived risks. Checks must include, at a minimum: verification of academic and professional qualifications, validation of employment history for the past three years, and identity verification. For roles with access to highly sensitive systems or data, additional checks such as credit history or enhanced criminal record checks shall be conducted with candidate consent and in accordance with applicable laws.</p><h4>5.2 Terms and Conditions of Employment</h4><p>The contractual terms and conditions for all Covered Personnel shall explicitly state their and [Company Name]''s responsibilities for information security. This shall include: agreement to comply with all applicable security policies and procedures, a legally enforceable confidentiality or non-disclosure agreement (NDA), and acknowledgment that access rights are granted based on the principle of least privilege and may be monitored in accordance with the law.</p><h4>5.3 Management Responsibilities</h4><p>Department managers and project supervisors are responsible for: defining and documenting the security roles and responsibilities for their staff; ensuring personnel understand their security duties before granting access to systems or data; and applying the principles of least privilege and segregation of duties in the design of all roles and access profiles.</p><h4>5.4 Information Security Awareness, Education, and Training</h4><p>All Covered Personnel shall receive formal information security awareness training upon induction and annually thereafter. Training content must be role-specific and cover: acceptable use of assets, password hygiene, incident reporting procedures, clear desk/clear screen policy, secure remote working, and social engineering threats. Completion of training shall be recorded and monitored for compliance.</p><h4>5.5 Disciplinary Process</h4><p>A formal disciplinary process shall be established and communicated to address security breaches committed by personnel. The process shall provide for graduated sanctions, up to and including termination of employment and legal action, and shall be applied consistently and fairly in all cases of policy violation.</p><h4>5.6 Termination or Change of Employment</h4><p>Upon termination of employment or change of job responsibilities, the following controls shall be enforced: all access rights to information assets, premises, and systems shall be revoked immediately, synchronously with the HR notification; all company-owned physical and logical assets (e.g., laptops, mobile devices, ID badges, access tokens, software licenses) shall be returned and accounted for; and a formal exit interview shall be conducted to reiterate ongoing confidentiality obligations.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy is designed to satisfy the requirements of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A controls, specifically A.5 (Information Security Policies), A.6 (Organization of Information Security), A.7 (Human Resource Security), and A.13 (Communications Security). It also aligns with SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security and Confidentiality. All personnel screening and disciplinary actions shall be carried out in full compliance with relevant labor laws, data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), and privacy statutes.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority for the approval, implementation, and enforcement of this policy.<br/><strong>Human Resources (HR) Department:</strong> Responsible for executing pre-employment screening, managing the contractual process including NDAs, facilitating security training, and initiating access revocation upon termination in coordination with IT.<br/><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department / System Owners:</strong> Responsible for provisioning and de-provisioning access rights based on authorized requests from HR and management, in accordance with least privilege.<br/><strong>All Managers/Supervisors:</strong> Responsible for defining role-based security requirements, ensuring their team''s compliance with this policy, and reporting security incidents involving their personnel.<br/><strong>All Covered Personnel:</strong> Responsible for understanding and adhering to all aspects of this policy and associated procedures.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and potential legal liability. The IT Department is authorized to immediately suspend or revoke system access for any individual suspected of a serious policy violation pending investigation. Monitoring of system access and user activity will be conducted to ensure compliance with this policy.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the HR Director at least annually, or following significant security incidents or changes in business operations, technology, or regulatory landscape. Revisions must be approved by the CISO. The latest version of this policy shall be made accessible to all Covered Personnel through the company''s official policy repository.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-19 06:15:56.064036', '"[\"ISO 27001\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 19:41:50.681', NULL),
	(22, '30001', 'Data Classification Policy', '<p>This Data Classification Policy establishes the framework for categorizing [Company Name] information assets based on their sensitivity, value, and criticality to the organization. The purpose is to ensure that information receives an appropriate level of protection in accordance with legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations, thereby safeguarding confidentiality, integrity, and availability.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, managed, or processed by [Company Name], regardless of format (e.g., electronic, physical, verbal) or location (e.g., on-premises, cloud, third-party). It is mandatory for all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other personnel who create, access, handle, store, or transmit [Company Name] data.</p>

<p><strong>Confidential Information:</strong> Data whose unauthorized disclosure could cause severe or catastrophic damage to [Company Name], including but not limited to trade secrets, source code, pre-release financial data, and certain personal data. This classification aligns with ISO 27001 ''confidential'' and SOC 2 ''restricted'' categories.<br><br><strong>Internal Use Information:</strong> Data intended for use within [Company Name] and authorized third parties. Unauthorized disclosure could cause moderate risk or inconvenience. Examples include internal policies, non-sensitive project plans, and operational procedures.<br><br><strong>Public Information:</strong> Data explicitly approved for public release. Unauthorized disclosure poses no material risk to [Company Name]. Examples include marketing materials and published press releases.<br><br><strong>Information Asset:</strong> Any data, system, or repository that stores, processes, or transmits information valuable to [Company Name].<br><br><strong>Data Owner:</strong> The business executive or department head accountable for a specific information asset, responsible for defining its classification and protection requirements.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all information assets shall be classified according to their sensitivity and business impact. Protection controls shall be implemented commensurate with the assigned classification level. Failure to classify and protect information in accordance with this policy is strictly prohibited and may result in disciplinary action.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Classification Levels and Criteria</strong><br>All information shall be classified into one of three levels:<br>• <strong>Confidential:</strong> Data protected by statute, regulation, or contract (e.g., GDPR, HIPAA); trade secrets; authentication credentials; and any data whose compromise could cause severe financial, reputational, or legal harm.<br>• <strong>Internal Use:</strong> Operational business data, internal communications, and information not intended for public disclosure but whose exposure would not cause severe harm.<br>• <strong>Public:</strong> Information explicitly approved for public dissemination.<br><br><strong>5.2 Classification and Labeling Procedures</strong><br>Data Owners shall formally classify information at the time of creation or significant modification. Electronic documents and data repositories shall be digitally labeled with the classification (e.g., via metadata headers, watermarking, or folder naming conventions). Physical media and hardcopy documents shall bear visible classification labels (e.g., stamps, headers).<br><br><strong>5.3 Handling and Storage Requirements</strong><br>• <strong>Confidential Data:</strong> Must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 or equivalent and in transit using TLS 1.2 or higher. Storage is permitted only on approved, access-controlled systems. Physical copies must be stored in locked containers.<br>• <strong>Internal Use Data:</strong> Shall be stored on authorized [Company Name] managed systems. Encryption is recommended for data in transit across untrusted networks.<br>• <strong>Public Data:</strong> No specific storage encryption is required, but integrity controls must be applied to prevent unauthorized modification.<br><br><strong>5.4 Access Control and Authorization</strong><br>Access to information shall be granted on a need-to-know basis. Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) shall be implemented for all systems storing Confidential or Internal Use data. Access reviews for Confidential data shall be conducted quarterly. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for all access to systems housing Confidential data.<br><br><strong>5.5 Transmission and Sharing Controls</strong><br>The transmission of Confidential data over public networks is forbidden unless encrypted via approved enterprise VPN or TLS 1.2+. Sharing Confidential data with external parties requires a valid Non-Disclosure Agreement (NDA) and the use of approved secure file transfer mechanisms. Email of Confidential data is strictly prohibited unless the message and attachments are encrypted end-to-end.<br><br><strong>5.6 Retention and Disposal</strong><br>All data shall be retained in accordance with the Records Retention Schedule. Confidential data shall be disposed of using secure deletion methods that render data unrecoverable (e.g., NIST 800-88 media sanitization for electronic data, cross-cut shredding for physical documents). Logs of disposal activities for Confidential data shall be maintained.<br><br><strong>5.7 Monitoring and Logging</strong><br>All access attempts, modifications, and transfers of Confidential data shall be logged. Logs shall include user identity, timestamp, action, and data identifier. Logs shall be centrally aggregated and protected from tampering, retained for a minimum of 90 days, and reviewed for anomalies weekly.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to align with and support compliance with relevant frameworks and regulations, including but not limited to:<br>• <strong>ISO/IEC 27001:2022:</strong> Controls A.5.9 (Inventory of information and other associated assets), A.5.10 (Classification of information), A.5.12 (Classification of information), and A.8.2 (Information classification).<br>• <strong>SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security):</strong> CC6.1 (Logical and physical access controls), CC6.6 (Logical access security measures to protect against threats), and CC6.8 (Data classification).<br>• <strong>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR):</strong> Principles of data minimization and integrity and confidentiality (Article 5).<br>• <strong>Other Jurisdictional Laws:</strong> All personnel are responsible for understanding and adhering to data protection laws applicable to the data they handle.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority for this policy; oversees implementation and enforcement.<br><br><strong>Data Owners (Business Function Heads):</strong> Responsible for classifying information assets under their purview; defining access requirements; and authorizing exceptions.<br><br><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Responsible for implementing and maintaining the technical controls (encryption, access management, logging) specified in Section 5.<br><br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to handle all [Company Name] information according to its classification; complete mandatory data handling training; and report any suspected mishandling or loss of data.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal prosecution. The IT Department is authorized to technically enforce controls (e.g., block unencrypted transfers, revoke access) to prevent policy violations. All violations shall be reported to the CISO and investigated per the Incident Response Policy.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually or in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, business operations, or applicable regulations. Proposed revisions must be approved by the CISO and General Counsel. The latest approved version is the only authoritative document.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Data Classification Policy establishes the framework for categorizing [Company Name] information assets based on their sensitivity, value, and criticality to the organization. The purpose is to ensure that information receives an appropriate level of protection in accordance with legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations, thereby safeguarding confidentiality, integrity, and availability.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, managed, or processed by [Company Name], regardless of format (e.g., electronic, physical, verbal) or location (e.g., on-premises, cloud, third-party). It is mandatory for all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other personnel who create, access, handle, store, or transmit [Company Name] data.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Confidential Information:</strong> Data whose unauthorized disclosure could cause severe or catastrophic damage to [Company Name], including but not limited to trade secrets, source code, pre-release financial data, and certain personal data. This classification aligns with ISO 27001 ''confidential'' and SOC 2 ''restricted'' categories.<br><br><strong>Internal Use Information:</strong> Data intended for use within [Company Name] and authorized third parties. Unauthorized disclosure could cause moderate risk or inconvenience. Examples include internal policies, non-sensitive project plans, and operational procedures.<br><br><strong>Public Information:</strong> Data explicitly approved for public release. Unauthorized disclosure poses no material risk to [Company Name]. Examples include marketing materials and published press releases.<br><br><strong>Information Asset:</strong> Any data, system, or repository that stores, processes, or transmits information valuable to [Company Name].<br><br><strong>Data Owner:</strong> The business executive or department head accountable for a specific information asset, responsible for defining its classification and protection requirements.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates that all information assets shall be classified according to their sensitivity and business impact. Protection controls shall be implemented commensurate with the assigned classification level. Failure to classify and protect information in accordance with this policy is strictly prohibited and may result in disciplinary action.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>5.1 Classification Levels and Criteria</strong><br>All information shall be classified into one of three levels:<br>• <strong>Confidential:</strong> Data protected by statute, regulation, or contract (e.g., GDPR, HIPAA); trade secrets; authentication credentials; and any data whose compromise could cause severe financial, reputational, or legal harm.<br>• <strong>Internal Use:</strong> Operational business data, internal communications, and information not intended for public disclosure but whose exposure would not cause severe harm.<br>• <strong>Public:</strong> Information explicitly approved for public dissemination.<br><br><strong>5.2 Classification and Labeling Procedures</strong><br>Data Owners shall formally classify information at the time of creation or significant modification. Electronic documents and data repositories shall be digitally labeled with the classification (e.g., via metadata headers, watermarking, or folder naming conventions). Physical media and hardcopy documents shall bear visible classification labels (e.g., stamps, headers).<br><br><strong>5.3 Handling and Storage Requirements</strong><br>• <strong>Confidential Data:</strong> Must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 or equivalent and in transit using TLS 1.2 or higher. Storage is permitted only on approved, access-controlled systems. Physical copies must be stored in locked containers.<br>• <strong>Internal Use Data:</strong> Shall be stored on authorized [Company Name] managed systems. Encryption is recommended for data in transit across untrusted networks.<br>• <strong>Public Data:</strong> No specific storage encryption is required, but integrity controls must be applied to prevent unauthorized modification.<br><br><strong>5.4 Access Control and Authorization</strong><br>Access to information shall be granted on a need-to-know basis. Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) shall be implemented for all systems storing Confidential or Internal Use data. Access reviews for Confidential data shall be conducted quarterly. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for all access to systems housing Confidential data.<br><br><strong>5.5 Transmission and Sharing Controls</strong><br>The transmission of Confidential data over public networks is forbidden unless encrypted via approved enterprise VPN or TLS 1.2+. Sharing Confidential data with external parties requires a valid Non-Disclosure Agreement (NDA) and the use of approved secure file transfer mechanisms. Email of Confidential data is strictly prohibited unless the message and attachments are encrypted end-to-end.<br><br><strong>5.6 Retention and Disposal</strong><br>All data shall be retained in accordance with the Records Retention Schedule. Confidential data shall be disposed of using secure deletion methods that render data unrecoverable (e.g., NIST 800-88 media sanitization for electronic data, cross-cut shredding for physical documents). Logs of disposal activities for Confidential data shall be maintained.<br><br><strong>5.7 Monitoring and Logging</strong><br>All access attempts, modifications, and transfers of Confidential data shall be logged. Logs shall include user identity, timestamp, action, and data identifier. Logs shall be centrally aggregated and protected from tampering, retained for a minimum of 90 days, and reviewed for anomalies weekly.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy is designed to align with and support compliance with relevant frameworks and regulations, including but not limited to:<br>• <strong>ISO/IEC 27001:2022:</strong> Controls A.5.9 (Inventory of information and other associated assets), A.5.10 (Classification of information), A.5.12 (Classification of information), and A.8.2 (Information classification).<br>• <strong>SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security):</strong> CC6.1 (Logical and physical access controls), CC6.6 (Logical access security measures to protect against threats), and CC6.8 (Data classification).<br>• <strong>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR):</strong> Principles of data minimization and integrity and confidentiality (Article 5).<br>• <strong>Other Jurisdictional Laws:</strong> All personnel are responsible for understanding and adhering to data protection laws applicable to the data they handle.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority for this policy; oversees implementation and enforcement.<br><br><strong>Data Owners (Business Function Heads):</strong> Responsible for classifying information assets under their purview; defining access requirements; and authorizing exceptions.<br><br><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Responsible for implementing and maintaining the technical controls (encryption, access management, logging) specified in Section 5.<br><br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to handle all [Company Name] information according to its classification; complete mandatory data handling training; and report any suspected mishandling or loss of data.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal prosecution. The IT Department is authorized to technically enforce controls (e.g., block unencrypted transfers, revoke access) to prevent policy violations. All violations shall be reported to the CISO and investigated per the Incident Response Policy.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually or in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, business operations, or applicable regulations. Proposed revisions must be approved by the CISO and General Counsel. The latest approved version is the only authoritative document.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-19 06:15:57.770065', '"[\"Both\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 19:42:25.922', NULL),
	(23, '60001', 'Mobile Device and BYOD Policy', '<p>This Mobile Device and Bring Your Own Device (BYOD) Policy establishes the mandatory security and management controls for all mobile devices that access, store, process, or transmit [Company Name] information assets. The purpose is to protect corporate data from unauthorized access, loss, or compromise while enabling secure productivity on approved mobile platforms, in alignment with [Company Name]''s risk management framework.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties ("Users") who use any mobile device to connect to [Company Name]''s internal networks, systems, applications, or data. It encompasses both company-provided mobile devices and personally owned devices (BYOD) used for business purposes. This policy covers all forms of mobile computing and communication devices including, but not limited to: smartphones, tablets, portable storage media (e.g., USB drives), and wearable technology with data connectivity.</p>

<p><strong>Mobile Device:</strong> Any portable computing device capable of storing, processing, or transmitting data wirelessly.<br/><strong>BYOD (Bring Your Own Device):</strong> A personally owned mobile device authorized for limited business use.<br/><strong>Mobile Device Management (MDM):</strong> A centralized software platform used to enforce security policies, configure settings, and manage mobile devices.<br/><strong>Containerization:</strong> A security method that isolates corporate applications and data within a secure, encrypted container on a device.<br/><strong>Enterprise Mobility Management (EMM):</strong> An integrated suite of services, typically including MDM, Mobile Application Management (MAM), and identity management, for securing the mobile enterprise.<br/><strong>Rooted/Jailbroken Device:</strong> A device where the operating system''s inherent security restrictions have been removed, compromising its integrity.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all mobile access to corporate resources shall be strictly controlled and secured. The use of mobile devices for business purposes is a privilege contingent upon full compliance with this policy. [Company Name] reserves the right to monitor, manage, and, if necessary, remotely wipe data from any device registered under this policy to protect corporate information assets.</p>

<h4>5.1 Device Registration and Management</h4><p>All devices used to access [Company Name] resources must be registered with and managed by the approved Enterprise Mobility Management (EMM) solution. Enrollment is mandatory prior to the first access attempt. Rooted or jailbroken devices are strictly prohibited from accessing any corporate resource.</p><h4>5.2 Authentication and Access Control</h4><p>Device-level authentication via a strong passcode, biometric lock, or PIN of at least six alphanumeric characters is mandatory. Inactivity lock must be configured to engage after a maximum of five minutes. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is required for all remote access to corporate email, VPNs, and cloud applications.</p><h4>5.3 Encryption Requirements</h4><p>Full-disk encryption (FDE) or file-based encryption must be enabled on all mobile devices storing [Company Name] data. For data in transit, TLS 1.2 or higher must be used. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted public Wi-Fi networks for transmitting corporate data is strictly forbidden; an approved corporate VPN must be utilized on untrusted networks.</p><h4>5.4 Application Security and Management</h4><p>Only applications vetted and provisioned through the official corporate application catalog or public app stores (Google Play, Apple App Store) may be installed on devices used for business. Side-loading of applications from unofficial sources is prohibited. Corporate data shall be confined within managed, encrypted application containers where technically feasible.</p><h4>5.5 Physical Security and Loss Procedures</h4><p>Users are personally responsible for the physical security of their mobile devices. Any loss, theft, or suspected compromise of a registered device must be reported to the Information Security Department immediately, triggering a potential remote wipe procedure.</p><h4>5.6 Monitoring and Logging</h4><p>All access to corporate data from mobile devices is subject to logging and monitoring. The EMM/MDM solution shall maintain audit logs of device compliance status, security incidents, application installations, and data access patterns for a minimum of 90 days.</p><h4>5.7 BYOD-Specific Provisions</h4><p>For BYOD participants, a clear separation between personal and corporate data must be maintained, typically through containerization. [Company Name]''s right to perform a selective remote wipe of the corporate container and data only, upon separation or security incident, must be formally acknowledged by the user during enrollment.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to meet the control objectives of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.6.2.1, A.8.1.1, A.8.2.1) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Confidentiality). Compliance with this policy is mandatory. Non-compliance may result in access revocation, disciplinary action, and legal liability for the user, particularly concerning data breach notification laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA) triggered by policy violations.</p>

<p><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Responsible for policy development, EMM/MDM management, security monitoring, and incident response.<br/><strong>IT Department:</strong> Responsible for technical implementation, device provisioning, and user support for the EMM/MDM platform.<br/><strong>Users:</strong> Responsible for adhering to all policy requirements, safeguarding devices, and reporting security incidents immediately.<br/><strong>Department Heads/Managers:</strong> Responsible for enforcing policy compliance within their teams and authorizing business use of mobile devices.</p>

<p>Any user found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal action. The Information Security Department is authorized to immediately and without prior notice disable access or initiate a remote wipe of any device deemed non-compliant or posing an imminent security risk to [Company Name].</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Department at least annually, or following significant changes to the threat landscape, technology, or relevant regulations. Revisions must be approved by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO). The latest version of this policy is the only version that is in effect.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Mobile Device and Bring Your Own Device (BYOD) Policy establishes the mandatory security and management controls for all mobile devices that access, store, process, or transmit [Company Name] information assets. The purpose is to protect corporate data from unauthorized access, loss, or compromise while enabling secure productivity on approved mobile platforms, in alignment with [Company Name]''s risk management framework.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties (\\\"Users\\\") who use any mobile device to connect to [Company Name]''s internal networks, systems, applications, or data. It encompasses both company-provided mobile devices and personally owned devices (BYOD) used for business purposes. This policy covers all forms of mobile computing and communication devices including, but not limited to: smartphones, tablets, portable storage media (e.g., USB drives), and wearable technology with data connectivity.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Mobile Device:</strong> Any portable computing device capable of storing, processing, or transmitting data wirelessly.<br/><strong>BYOD (Bring Your Own Device):</strong> A personally owned mobile device authorized for limited business use.<br/><strong>Mobile Device Management (MDM):</strong> A centralized software platform used to enforce security policies, configure settings, and manage mobile devices.<br/><strong>Containerization:</strong> A security method that isolates corporate applications and data within a secure, encrypted container on a device.<br/><strong>Enterprise Mobility Management (EMM):</strong> An integrated suite of services, typically including MDM, Mobile Application Management (MAM), and identity management, for securing the mobile enterprise.<br/><strong>Rooted/Jailbroken Device:</strong> A device where the operating system''s inherent security restrictions have been removed, compromising its integrity.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates that all mobile access to corporate resources shall be strictly controlled and secured. The use of mobile devices for business purposes is a privilege contingent upon full compliance with this policy. [Company Name] reserves the right to monitor, manage, and, if necessary, remotely wipe data from any device registered under this policy to protect corporate information assets.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1 Device Registration and Management</h4><p>All devices used to access [Company Name] resources must be registered with and managed by the approved Enterprise Mobility Management (EMM) solution. Enrollment is mandatory prior to the first access attempt. Rooted or jailbroken devices are strictly prohibited from accessing any corporate resource.</p><h4>5.2 Authentication and Access Control</h4><p>Device-level authentication via a strong passcode, biometric lock, or PIN of at least six alphanumeric characters is mandatory. Inactivity lock must be configured to engage after a maximum of five minutes. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is required for all remote access to corporate email, VPNs, and cloud applications.</p><h4>5.3 Encryption Requirements</h4><p>Full-disk encryption (FDE) or file-based encryption must be enabled on all mobile devices storing [Company Name] data. For data in transit, TLS 1.2 or higher must be used. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted public Wi-Fi networks for transmitting corporate data is strictly forbidden; an approved corporate VPN must be utilized on untrusted networks.</p><h4>5.4 Application Security and Management</h4><p>Only applications vetted and provisioned through the official corporate application catalog or public app stores (Google Play, Apple App Store) may be installed on devices used for business. Side-loading of applications from unofficial sources is prohibited. Corporate data shall be confined within managed, encrypted application containers where technically feasible.</p><h4>5.5 Physical Security and Loss Procedures</h4><p>Users are personally responsible for the physical security of their mobile devices. Any loss, theft, or suspected compromise of a registered device must be reported to the Information Security Department immediately, triggering a potential remote wipe procedure.</p><h4>5.6 Monitoring and Logging</h4><p>All access to corporate data from mobile devices is subject to logging and monitoring. The EMM/MDM solution shall maintain audit logs of device compliance status, security incidents, application installations, and data access patterns for a minimum of 90 days.</p><h4>5.7 BYOD-Specific Provisions</h4><p>For BYOD participants, a clear separation between personal and corporate data must be maintained, typically through containerization. [Company Name]''s right to perform a selective remote wipe of the corporate container and data only, upon separation or security incident, must be formally acknowledged by the user during enrollment.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy is designed to meet the control objectives of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.6.2.1, A.8.1.1, A.8.2.1) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Confidentiality). Compliance with this policy is mandatory. Non-compliance may result in access revocation, disciplinary action, and legal liability for the user, particularly concerning data breach notification laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA) triggered by policy violations.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Responsible for policy development, EMM/MDM management, security monitoring, and incident response.<br/><strong>IT Department:</strong> Responsible for technical implementation, device provisioning, and user support for the EMM/MDM platform.<br/><strong>Users:</strong> Responsible for adhering to all policy requirements, safeguarding devices, and reporting security incidents immediately.<br/><strong>Department Heads/Managers:</strong> Responsible for enforcing policy compliance within their teams and authorizing business use of mobile devices.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any user found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal action. The Information Security Department is authorized to immediately and without prior notice disable access or initiate a remote wipe of any device deemed non-compliant or posing an imminent security risk to [Company Name].</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Department at least annually, or following significant changes to the threat landscape, technology, or relevant regulations. Revisions must be approved by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO). The latest version of this policy is the only version that is in effect.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-19 06:15:57.989549', '"[\"Both\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 19:42:56.379', NULL),
	(28, '593939', 'Backup and Recovery Policy', '<p>This Backup and Recovery Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for creating, managing, testing, and restoring backups of [Company Name]''s information assets. The purpose is to ensure the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of data in the event of data loss, corruption, system failure, or disaster, thereby supporting business continuity and compliance with regulatory obligations.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all information systems, applications, databases, and data repositories owned, operated, or managed by [Company Name], or hosted by third-party service providers on behalf of [Company Name]. It encompasses all data classified as Confidential, Internal Use, or higher, including but not limited to corporate data, customer data, employee data, and intellectual property. All personnel, contractors, and third parties with access to [Company Name] systems are bound by this policy.</p>

<p><strong>Backup:</strong> A copy of data and system configurations created and retained for the purpose of restoration.<br><strong>Recovery Point Objective (RPO):</strong> The maximum acceptable amount of data loss measured in time.<br><strong>Recovery Time Objective (RTO):</strong> The maximum acceptable delay for restoring operations after an incident.<br><strong>Full Backup:</strong> A complete copy of all specified data.<br><strong>Incremental Backup:</strong> A copy of only the data that has changed since the last backup of any type.<br><strong>Differential Backup:</strong> A copy of data that has changed since the last full backup.<br><strong>Off-site Storage:</strong> A geographically separate location from the primary data center, used for storing backup media.<br><strong>Data Restoration:</strong> The process of returning data from a backup to an operational state.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates a formal, risk-based backup and recovery program to protect its critical information assets. All systems handling data covered under this policy''s scope shall have defined Recovery Point Objectives (RPO) and Recovery Time Objectives (RTO). The creation, storage, security, and testing of backups are strictly required activities. Unauthorized access to, modification of, or destruction of backup data is expressly prohibited.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Backup Strategy and Scheduling</strong><br>All critical systems and data shall be backed up according to a documented schedule aligned with their business impact assessment. Full system backups must be performed at least weekly. Incremental or differential backups must be performed at least daily. Backup schedules and methods must be documented in system-specific runbooks.</p><p><strong>5.2 Data Coverage and Retention</strong><br>Backups must capture all application data, databases, system configurations, and security settings necessary for full restoration. Backup retention periods shall be defined based on legal, regulatory, and business requirements, but shall not be less than 30 days for operational recovery and 7 years for data subject to specific compliance mandates (e.g., HIPAA, financial regulations).</p><p><strong>5.3 Encryption and Security of Backup Data</strong><br>All backup data, whether in transit or at rest, must be encrypted using strong, industry-accepted cryptographic standards (e.g., AES-256). Encryption keys shall be managed separately from the encrypted backup data. Access to backup systems, management consoles, and restoration capabilities shall be restricted based on the principle of least privilege and require multi-factor authentication (MFA).</p><p><strong>5.4 Storage and Geographic Separation</strong><br>A minimum of one copy of backup data must be stored in a geographically separate location (off-site) from the primary production data center to mitigate risks from local disasters. The integrity of off-site backups must be verified periodically. The use of immutable or write-once-read-many (WORM) storage for at least one backup copy is required to protect against ransomware and malicious deletion.</p><p><strong>5.5 Backup Integrity and Monitoring</strong><br>Automated monitoring must be implemented to verify the success or failure of all backup jobs. Backup logs must be retained and reviewed regularly for anomalies. Integrity checks (e.g., checksum validation) must be performed on backup data sets at least monthly to ensure data is not corrupted and is recoverable.</p><p><strong>5.6 Restoration Testing and Validation</strong><br>A formal restoration testing program shall be executed. Full restoration tests for critical systems must be conducted at least annually. Test results, including achieved RTO and RPO, must be documented, reviewed by management, and used to refine procedures. The ability to restore individual files or data objects must be tested quarterly.</p><p><strong>5.7 Third-Party and Cloud Services</strong><br>For data processed or stored by third-party providers (e.g., SaaS, IaaS), [Company Name] shall ensure contractual agreements mandate compliance with this policy''s backup, encryption, and testing requirements. The company retains the right to audit or receive evidence of the provider''s backup and recovery capabilities.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to align with control objectives from ISO 27001:2022 (A.5.29 Information security during disruption, A.8.13 Information backup, A.8.14 Redundancy of information processing facilities) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Availability, Confidentiality, Security). For environments processing Protected Health Information (PHI), backup procedures shall enforce the HIPAA Security Rule requirements for data backup, disaster recovery, and emergency mode operation plans (45 CFR § 164.308(a)(7)(ii)(A) & § 164.310(a)(2)(i)). All activities must comply with relevant data protection laws regarding the storage and cross-border transfer of backup data.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the approval and oversight of this policy.<br><strong>IT Operations/System Administrators:</strong> Responsible for the implementation, execution, and daily monitoring of backup jobs and infrastructure.<br><strong>Data Owners/Business Unit Heads:</strong> Responsible for defining the criticality (RPO/RTO) and legal retention requirements for their data.<br><strong>Compliance Officer:</strong> Responsible for ensuring backup and recovery processes meet applicable legal and regulatory frameworks.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for storing business-critical data only on systems covered by this policy and reporting any suspected data loss immediately.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment, and may face legal action. Deliberate circumvention of backup procedures, unauthorized restoration attempts, or tampering with backup data is considered a serious security incident and will be investigated accordingly. Third-party providers in violation of contractual obligations derived from this policy may be subject to penalties and contract termination.</p>

<p>This Backup and Recovery Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO or designated authority at least annually, or following significant changes to the IT environment, business processes, or applicable regulations. Revisions must be approved by the CISO. All changes shall be communicated to relevant stakeholders, and personnel shall be required to re-acknowledge the policy as stipulated by the company''s security awareness program.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Backup and Recovery Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for creating, managing, testing, and restoring backups of [Company Name]''s information assets. The purpose is to ensure the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of data in the event of data loss, corruption, system failure, or disaster, thereby supporting business continuity and compliance with regulatory obligations.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all information systems, applications, databases, and data repositories owned, operated, or managed by [Company Name], or hosted by third-party service providers on behalf of [Company Name]. It encompasses all data classified as Confidential, Internal Use, or higher, including but not limited to corporate data, customer data, employee data, and intellectual property. All personnel, contractors, and third parties with access to [Company Name] systems are bound by this policy.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Backup:</strong> A copy of data and system configurations created and retained for the purpose of restoration.<br><strong>Recovery Point Objective (RPO):</strong> The maximum acceptable amount of data loss measured in time.<br><strong>Recovery Time Objective (RTO):</strong> The maximum acceptable delay for restoring operations after an incident.<br><strong>Full Backup:</strong> A complete copy of all specified data.<br><strong>Incremental Backup:</strong> A copy of only the data that has changed since the last backup of any type.<br><strong>Differential Backup:</strong> A copy of data that has changed since the last full backup.<br><strong>Off-site Storage:</strong> A geographically separate location from the primary data center, used for storing backup media.<br><strong>Data Restoration:</strong> The process of returning data from a backup to an operational state.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates a formal, risk-based backup and recovery program to protect its critical information assets. All systems handling data covered under this policy''s scope shall have defined Recovery Point Objectives (RPO) and Recovery Time Objectives (RTO). The creation, storage, security, and testing of backups are strictly required activities. Unauthorized access to, modification of, or destruction of backup data is expressly prohibited.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>5.1 Backup Strategy and Scheduling</strong><br>All critical systems and data shall be backed up according to a documented schedule aligned with their business impact assessment. Full system backups must be performed at least weekly. Incremental or differential backups must be performed at least daily. Backup schedules and methods must be documented in system-specific runbooks.</p><p><strong>5.2 Data Coverage and Retention</strong><br>Backups must capture all application data, databases, system configurations, and security settings necessary for full restoration. Backup retention periods shall be defined based on legal, regulatory, and business requirements, but shall not be less than 30 days for operational recovery and 7 years for data subject to specific compliance mandates (e.g., HIPAA, financial regulations).</p><p><strong>5.3 Encryption and Security of Backup Data</strong><br>All backup data, whether in transit or at rest, must be encrypted using strong, industry-accepted cryptographic standards (e.g., AES-256). Encryption keys shall be managed separately from the encrypted backup data. Access to backup systems, management consoles, and restoration capabilities shall be restricted based on the principle of least privilege and require multi-factor authentication (MFA).</p><p><strong>5.4 Storage and Geographic Separation</strong><br>A minimum of one copy of backup data must be stored in a geographically separate location (off-site) from the primary production data center to mitigate risks from local disasters. The integrity of off-site backups must be verified periodically. The use of immutable or write-once-read-many (WORM) storage for at least one backup copy is required to protect against ransomware and malicious deletion.</p><p><strong>5.5 Backup Integrity and Monitoring</strong><br>Automated monitoring must be implemented to verify the success or failure of all backup jobs. Backup logs must be retained and reviewed regularly for anomalies. Integrity checks (e.g., checksum validation) must be performed on backup data sets at least monthly to ensure data is not corrupted and is recoverable.</p><p><strong>5.6 Restoration Testing and Validation</strong><br>A formal restoration testing program shall be executed. Full restoration tests for critical systems must be conducted at least annually. Test results, including achieved RTO and RPO, must be documented, reviewed by management, and used to refine procedures. The ability to restore individual files or data objects must be tested quarterly.</p><p><strong>5.7 Third-Party and Cloud Services</strong><br>For data processed or stored by third-party providers (e.g., SaaS, IaaS), [Company Name] shall ensure contractual agreements mandate compliance with this policy''s backup, encryption, and testing requirements. The company retains the right to audit or receive evidence of the provider''s backup and recovery capabilities.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy is designed to align with control objectives from ISO 27001:2022 (A.5.29 Information security during disruption, A.8.13 Information backup, A.8.14 Redundancy of information processing facilities) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Availability, Confidentiality, Security). For environments processing Protected Health Information (PHI), backup procedures shall enforce the HIPAA Security Rule requirements for data backup, disaster recovery, and emergency mode operation plans (45 CFR § 164.308(a)(7)(ii)(A) & § 164.310(a)(2)(i)). All activities must comply with relevant data protection laws regarding the storage and cross-border transfer of backup data.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the approval and oversight of this policy.<br><strong>IT Operations/System Administrators:</strong> Responsible for the implementation, execution, and daily monitoring of backup jobs and infrastructure.<br><strong>Data Owners/Business Unit Heads:</strong> Responsible for defining the criticality (RPO/RTO) and legal retention requirements for their data.<br><strong>Compliance Officer:</strong> Responsible for ensuring backup and recovery processes meet applicable legal and regulatory frameworks.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for storing business-critical data only on systems covered by this policy and reporting any suspected data loss immediately.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment, and may face legal action. Deliberate circumvention of backup procedures, unauthorized restoration attempts, or tampering with backup data is considered a serious security incident and will be investigated accordingly. Third-party providers in violation of contractual obligations derived from this policy may be subject to penalties and contract termination.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This Backup and Recovery Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO or designated authority at least annually, or following significant changes to the IT environment, business processes, or applicable regulations. Revisions must be approved by the CISO. All changes shall be communicated to relevant stakeholders, and personnel shall be required to re-acknowledge the policy as stipulated by the company''s security awareness program.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-20 21:17:22.194158', '"[\"ISO 27001\",\"SOC 2\",\"HIPAA\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 19:43:29.947', NULL),
	(24, '927462', 'Clean Desk and Clear Screen Policy', '<p>This Clean Desk and Clear Screen Policy establishes mandatory physical and logical security controls to prevent unauthorized access to, or disclosure of, [Company Name]''s sensitive and confidential information. The policy mandates procedures to secure workspaces and information systems when unattended, thereby mitigating risks of data breach, theft, or visual hacking in alignment with ISO 27001 (A.11.2.9) and SOC 2 (CC6.1) requirements.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other personnel (collectively "Personnel") who access [Company Name] facilities, information assets, or systems. It encompasses all physical workspaces (e.g., offices, cubicles, meeting rooms, hot desks) and all logical access points (e.g., workstations, laptops, mobile devices, virtual desktops) owned, leased, or operated by [Company Name].</p>

<p><strong>Clean Desk:</strong> A state where all physical sensitive or confidential materials are removed from a workspace and secured when the workspace is unattended.<br/><strong>Clear Screen:</strong> A state where an information system session is locked, requiring re-authentication, or terminated when the device is unattended.<br/><strong>Sensitive Information:</strong> Any data classified as Confidential or Restricted per the Data Classification Policy, including but not limited to financial records, intellectual property, customer data, employee records, and strategic plans.<br/><strong>Unattended Workspace:</strong> Any workspace where the authorized user is not physically present and cannot directly observe or control access to the workspace or device.<br/><strong>Session Lock:</strong> A mechanism that conceals all information on a display and requires re-authentication to resume activity.</p>

<p>[Company Name] strictly prohibits the leaving of sensitive information in an unsecured state in any unattended workspace. All Personnel shall maintain a clean desk and enforce a clear screen as defined in this policy. Failure to comply constitutes a security violation and may result in disciplinary action.</p>

<h4>5.1 Clean Desk Requirements</h4><p>5.1.1 At the end of the workday and whenever leaving a workspace unattended for any period, Personnel shall remove all physical documents containing sensitive information from the desk surface and store them in a locked filing cabinet or other approved secure storage.<br/>5.1.2 Sensitive information shall not be left on printers, copiers, fax machines, or in shared work areas. Documents must be retrieved immediately after printing or processing.<br/>5.1.3 Whiteboards, flip charts, or other writing surfaces in meeting rooms or workspaces shall be erased of all sensitive information immediately following use.<br/>5.1.4 Portable storage media (e.g., USB drives, external hard disks) containing sensitive information shall not be left unattended on desks and must be stored securely when not in active use.</p><h4>5.2 Clear Screen Requirements</h4><p>5.2.1 Personnel shall manually initiate a session lock (e.g., Windows Key + L, Ctrl+Alt+Del Lock) or enable a screensaver with password protection that activates after a maximum of <strong>15 minutes</strong> of inactivity.<br/>5.2.2 All workstations, laptops, and mobile devices used for company business must be configured to automatically lock after a period of inactivity not to exceed 15 minutes. Exceptions require written approval from the Information Security team.<br/>5.2.3 When leaving a device unattended, even for short periods, Personnel must manually lock the session. Devices left logged in and unattended are strictly prohibited.<br/>5.2.4 For shared workstations or kiosks, the session must be fully logged off at the conclusion of use.</p><h4>5.3 Workspace Security</h4><p>5.3.1 Laptops and mobile devices shall be physically secured with a locking cable when left at a desk in a secure office, or stored in a locked drawer or cabinet when left in an unsecured or public area.<br/>5.3.2 Keys to locked storage, and access cards/badges, shall not be left unattended on desks or in unsecured locations.<br/>5.3.3 Personnel are responsible for ensuring meeting rooms are cleared of all sensitive materials and whiteboards are erased at the conclusion of meetings.</p><h4>5.4 Remote Work and Flexible Workspaces</h4><p>5.4.1 This policy applies with equal force to home offices, co-working spaces, and other remote work locations.<br/>5.4.2 Personnel working remotely are responsible for ensuring family members, visitors, or cleaning staff cannot access sensitive physical or digital information.<br/>5.4.3 Screens must be positioned to prevent "shoulder surfing" and windows or blinds should be used to obscure the view from outside the residence.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA, HIPAA where applicable) by implementing appropriate technical and organizational measures to protect personal data. Adherence supports audit requirements for ISO 27001 control A.11.2.9 (Clear desk and clear screen policy) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security (CC6.1) and Confidentiality. Non-compliance may result in regulatory penalties, audit findings, and reputational damage to [Company Name].</p>

<p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Are responsible for daily compliance with all clean desk and clear screen procedures for their assigned workspaces and devices.<br/><strong>Managers and Team Leads:</strong> Are responsible for promoting awareness, monitoring compliance within their teams, and addressing violations.<br/><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Is responsible for defining the policy, configuring automatic session lock settings on corporate devices, conducting periodic spot checks/audits, and investigating violations.<br/><strong>Facilities/Office Management:</strong> Are responsible for providing adequate secure storage (locked cabinets) and supporting the enforcement of clean desk procedures in common areas.</p>

<p>Violations of this policy will be treated as a security incident. The Information Security Department shall conduct periodic unannounced physical audits of workspaces. Personnel found in violation may be subject to corrective action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, in accordance with the Acceptable Use Policy and disciplinary procedures. Repeated or willful violations will result in escalated disciplinary measures.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Department at least annually, or in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, technology, or regulatory requirements. Revisions must be approved by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) or designated authority. The latest version of this policy is authoritative and will be communicated to all Personnel.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Clean Desk and Clear Screen Policy establishes mandatory physical and logical security controls to prevent unauthorized access to, or disclosure of, [Company Name]''s sensitive and confidential information. The policy mandates procedures to secure workspaces and information systems when unattended, thereby mitigating risks of data breach, theft, or visual hacking in alignment with ISO 27001 (A.11.2.9) and SOC 2 (CC6.1) requirements.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other personnel (collectively \\\"Personnel\\\") who access [Company Name] facilities, information assets, or systems. It encompasses all physical workspaces (e.g., offices, cubicles, meeting rooms, hot desks) and all logical access points (e.g., workstations, laptops, mobile devices, virtual desktops) owned, leased, or operated by [Company Name].</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Clean Desk:</strong> A state where all physical sensitive or confidential materials are removed from a workspace and secured when the workspace is unattended.<br/><strong>Clear Screen:</strong> A state where an information system session is locked, requiring re-authentication, or terminated when the device is unattended.<br/><strong>Sensitive Information:</strong> Any data classified as Confidential or Restricted per the Data Classification Policy, including but not limited to financial records, intellectual property, customer data, employee records, and strategic plans.<br/><strong>Unattended Workspace:</strong> Any workspace where the authorized user is not physically present and cannot directly observe or control access to the workspace or device.<br/><strong>Session Lock:</strong> A mechanism that conceals all information on a display and requires re-authentication to resume activity.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] strictly prohibits the leaving of sensitive information in an unsecured state in any unattended workspace. All Personnel shall maintain a clean desk and enforce a clear screen as defined in this policy. Failure to comply constitutes a security violation and may result in disciplinary action.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1 Clean Desk Requirements</h4><p>5.1.1 At the end of the workday and whenever leaving a workspace unattended for any period, Personnel shall remove all physical documents containing sensitive information from the desk surface and store them in a locked filing cabinet or other approved secure storage.<br/>5.1.2 Sensitive information shall not be left on printers, copiers, fax machines, or in shared work areas. Documents must be retrieved immediately after printing or processing.<br/>5.1.3 Whiteboards, flip charts, or other writing surfaces in meeting rooms or workspaces shall be erased of all sensitive information immediately following use.<br/>5.1.4 Portable storage media (e.g., USB drives, external hard disks) containing sensitive information shall not be left unattended on desks and must be stored securely when not in active use.</p><h4>5.2 Clear Screen Requirements</h4><p>5.2.1 Personnel shall manually initiate a session lock (e.g., Windows Key + L, Ctrl+Alt+Del Lock) or enable a screensaver with password protection that activates after a maximum of <strong>15 minutes</strong> of inactivity.<br/>5.2.2 All workstations, laptops, and mobile devices used for company business must be configured to automatically lock after a period of inactivity not to exceed 15 minutes. Exceptions require written approval from the Information Security team.<br/>5.2.3 When leaving a device unattended, even for short periods, Personnel must manually lock the session. Devices left logged in and unattended are strictly prohibited.<br/>5.2.4 For shared workstations or kiosks, the session must be fully logged off at the conclusion of use.</p><h4>5.3 Workspace Security</h4><p>5.3.1 Laptops and mobile devices shall be physically secured with a locking cable when left at a desk in a secure office, or stored in a locked drawer or cabinet when left in an unsecured or public area.<br/>5.3.2 Keys to locked storage, and access cards/badges, shall not be left unattended on desks or in unsecured locations.<br/>5.3.3 Personnel are responsible for ensuring meeting rooms are cleared of all sensitive materials and whiteboards are erased at the conclusion of meetings.</p><h4>5.4 Remote Work and Flexible Workspaces</h4><p>5.4.1 This policy applies with equal force to home offices, co-working spaces, and other remote work locations.<br/>5.4.2 Personnel working remotely are responsible for ensuring family members, visitors, or cleaning staff cannot access sensitive physical or digital information.<br/>5.4.3 Screens must be positioned to prevent \\\"shoulder surfing\\\" and windows or blinds should be used to obscure the view from outside the residence.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA, HIPAA where applicable) by implementing appropriate technical and organizational measures to protect personal data. Adherence supports audit requirements for ISO 27001 control A.11.2.9 (Clear desk and clear screen policy) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security (CC6.1) and Confidentiality. Non-compliance may result in regulatory penalties, audit findings, and reputational damage to [Company Name].</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Are responsible for daily compliance with all clean desk and clear screen procedures for their assigned workspaces and devices.<br/><strong>Managers and Team Leads:</strong> Are responsible for promoting awareness, monitoring compliance within their teams, and addressing violations.<br/><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Is responsible for defining the policy, configuring automatic session lock settings on corporate devices, conducting periodic spot checks/audits, and investigating violations.<br/><strong>Facilities/Office Management:</strong> Are responsible for providing adequate secure storage (locked cabinets) and supporting the enforcement of clean desk procedures in common areas.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Violations of this policy will be treated as a security incident. The Information Security Department shall conduct periodic unannounced physical audits of workspaces. Personnel found in violation may be subject to corrective action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, in accordance with the Acceptable Use Policy and disciplinary procedures. Repeated or willful violations will result in escalated disciplinary measures.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Department at least annually, or in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, technology, or regulatory requirements. Revisions must be approved by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) or designated authority. The latest version of this policy is authoritative and will be communicated to all Personnel.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-20 21:17:21.737347', '"[\"ISO 27001\",\"SOC 2\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 19:44:02.16', NULL),
	(25, '147380', 'Malware Protection Policy', '<p>The purpose of this Malware Protection Policy is to establish the mandatory controls and procedures for [Company Name] to prevent, detect, contain, and eradicate malicious software (malware) across all information systems and assets. This policy is designed to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate data and systems, and to ensure compliance with relevant legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties (collectively, "Personnel") who access, manage, or operate any [Company Name] information asset. It encompasses all company-owned or managed endpoints (e.g., workstations, laptops, servers, mobile devices), network infrastructure, and cloud-based environments, regardless of location.</p>

<p><strong>Malware:</strong> Malicious software designed to infiltrate, damage, or disable computer systems, including but not limited to viruses, worms, trojans, ransomware, spyware, adware, rootkits, and keyloggers.</p><p><strong>Endpoint Protection Platform (EPP):</strong> A centralized security solution deployed on endpoints to prevent file-based malware, detect malicious activity, and provide investigation and remediation capabilities.</p><p><strong>Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR):</strong> A security solution that continuously monitors and collects endpoint data, uses rules-based automated response, and provides deep visibility for analysis of security incidents.</p><p><strong>Signature-Based Detection:</strong> A method of identifying malware by comparing files against a database of known malicious signatures or patterns.</p><p><strong>Heuristic/Behavioral Analysis:</strong> A method of detecting malware by analyzing the behavior or characteristics of software to identify suspicious activity indicative of new or unknown threats.</p>

<p>[Company Name] strictly prohibits the intentional introduction of malware into its information systems and environments. The organization mandates a defense-in-depth strategy, employing multiple, layered technical and administrative controls to mitigate the risk of malware infection. All Personnel are responsible for adhering to the security practices defined in this policy to safeguard organizational assets.</p>

<h4>5.1. Mandatory Endpoint Protection</h4><p>All company-managed endpoints (servers, workstations, laptops) must have the officially sanctioned Endpoint Protection Platform (EPP) installed, actively running, and kept up-to-date. The disabling, tampering with, or unauthorized removal of this software is strictly prohibited.</p><h4>5.2. Signature and Engine Updates</h4><p>The malware detection signatures and scanning engines for all protective software must be configured to update automatically at a minimum frequency of every 24 hours. Manual updates shall be performed if automatic updates fail.</p><h4>5.3. Real-Time Scanning</h4><p>Real-time (on-access) scanning must be enabled on all endpoints. This scanning shall inspect files upon creation, opening, closing, or execution to prevent malware from being written to or run from local storage, network drives, or removable media.</p><h4>5.4. Scheduled Full System Scans</h4><p>A comprehensive, scheduled full-system scan must be performed on all endpoints at least weekly. Scans must be configured to run during off-peak hours to minimize performance impact. Logs of all scans, including items detected and actions taken, must be retained.</p><h4>5.5. Automated Quarantine and Remediation</h4><p>All protective software must be configured to automatically quarantine any file identified as confirmed malware. Quarantined files must be moved to a secure, isolated location where they cannot be executed. Remediation actions (cleaning, deletion) shall be performed according to a defined process managed by the IT Security team.</p><h4>5.6. Email and Web Gateway Filtering</h4><p>All inbound and outbound email must pass through a gateway solution that scans attachments and links for malware. All web traffic (HTTP/HTTPS) must be proxied and filtered through a solution capable of detecting and blocking malware from web downloads and drive-by downloads.</p><h4>5.7. Application Control and Execution Restriction</h4><p>Where technically feasible, application whitelisting or execution control policies shall be implemented on critical servers and endpoints (e.g., point-of-sale systems, domain controllers) to prevent the execution of unauthorized software, including malware.</p><h4>5.8. Patch Management Integration</h4><p>The malware protection strategy shall be integrated with the enterprise patch management program. All operating systems and applications must be patched promptly in accordance with the Patch Management Policy to remediate vulnerabilities that could be exploited by malware.</p><h4>5.9. Centralized Logging and Alerting</h4><p>All malware detection events, including blocked attempts, quarantined files, and scan results, must be logged centrally to a Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system. Alerts for critical detections (e.g., ransomware, worm activity) must be configured for immediate notification of the Security Operations Center (SOC) or IT Security team.</p><h4>5.10. Removable Media Controls</h4><p>The use of removable media (USB drives, external hard drives) must be controlled. All such media must be automatically scanned for malware upon insertion into any company endpoint. The use of unapproved or un-scanned removable media on company systems is forbidden.</p>

<p>This policy supports compliance with control objectives from ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.8.7 Protection against malware), SOC 2 (CC6.1, CC7.1), and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (PR.IP-1, DE.CM-4). Adherence is mandatory to meet contractual obligations with clients and partners, and to satisfy regulatory requirements concerning data protection and breach notification.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the approval and effectiveness of this policy.<br><strong>IT Security Team:</strong> Responsible for the selection, deployment, configuration, monitoring, and tuning of all malware protection technologies. They shall investigate and respond to all malware incidents.<br><strong>IT Operations/Endpoint Management:</strong> Responsible for ensuring the mandated protection software is installed, functional, and updated on all assets under their management.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must exercise caution with email attachments and web links, report any suspected malware activity immediately to the IT Service Desk, and shall not attempt to disable or circumvent security controls.</p>

<p>Any Personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and may face legal action. Deliberate acts that introduce malware or disable protective controls will be treated as a serious security violation.</p>

<p>This Malware Protection Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO or designated representative at least annually, or following significant security incidents or changes in the threat landscape. Revisions must be approved by the CISO and communicated to all relevant stakeholders.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>The purpose of this Malware Protection Policy is to establish the mandatory controls and procedures for [Company Name] to prevent, detect, contain, and eradicate malicious software (malware) across all information systems and assets. This policy is designed to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate data and systems, and to ensure compliance with relevant legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties (collectively, \\\"Personnel\\\") who access, manage, or operate any [Company Name] information asset. It encompasses all company-owned or managed endpoints (e.g., workstations, laptops, servers, mobile devices), network infrastructure, and cloud-based environments, regardless of location.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Malware:</strong> Malicious software designed to infiltrate, damage, or disable computer systems, including but not limited to viruses, worms, trojans, ransomware, spyware, adware, rootkits, and keyloggers.</p><p><strong>Endpoint Protection Platform (EPP):</strong> A centralized security solution deployed on endpoints to prevent file-based malware, detect malicious activity, and provide investigation and remediation capabilities.</p><p><strong>Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR):</strong> A security solution that continuously monitors and collects endpoint data, uses rules-based automated response, and provides deep visibility for analysis of security incidents.</p><p><strong>Signature-Based Detection:</strong> A method of identifying malware by comparing files against a database of known malicious signatures or patterns.</p><p><strong>Heuristic/Behavioral Analysis:</strong> A method of detecting malware by analyzing the behavior or characteristics of software to identify suspicious activity indicative of new or unknown threats.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] strictly prohibits the intentional introduction of malware into its information systems and environments. The organization mandates a defense-in-depth strategy, employing multiple, layered technical and administrative controls to mitigate the risk of malware infection. All Personnel are responsible for adhering to the security practices defined in this policy to safeguard organizational assets.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1. Mandatory Endpoint Protection</h4><p>All company-managed endpoints (servers, workstations, laptops) must have the officially sanctioned Endpoint Protection Platform (EPP) installed, actively running, and kept up-to-date. The disabling, tampering with, or unauthorized removal of this software is strictly prohibited.</p><h4>5.2. Signature and Engine Updates</h4><p>The malware detection signatures and scanning engines for all protective software must be configured to update automatically at a minimum frequency of every 24 hours. Manual updates shall be performed if automatic updates fail.</p><h4>5.3. Real-Time Scanning</h4><p>Real-time (on-access) scanning must be enabled on all endpoints. This scanning shall inspect files upon creation, opening, closing, or execution to prevent malware from being written to or run from local storage, network drives, or removable media.</p><h4>5.4. Scheduled Full System Scans</h4><p>A comprehensive, scheduled full-system scan must be performed on all endpoints at least weekly. Scans must be configured to run during off-peak hours to minimize performance impact. Logs of all scans, including items detected and actions taken, must be retained.</p><h4>5.5. Automated Quarantine and Remediation</h4><p>All protective software must be configured to automatically quarantine any file identified as confirmed malware. Quarantined files must be moved to a secure, isolated location where they cannot be executed. Remediation actions (cleaning, deletion) shall be performed according to a defined process managed by the IT Security team.</p><h4>5.6. Email and Web Gateway Filtering</h4><p>All inbound and outbound email must pass through a gateway solution that scans attachments and links for malware. All web traffic (HTTP/HTTPS) must be proxied and filtered through a solution capable of detecting and blocking malware from web downloads and drive-by downloads.</p><h4>5.7. Application Control and Execution Restriction</h4><p>Where technically feasible, application whitelisting or execution control policies shall be implemented on critical servers and endpoints (e.g., point-of-sale systems, domain controllers) to prevent the execution of unauthorized software, including malware.</p><h4>5.8. Patch Management Integration</h4><p>The malware protection strategy shall be integrated with the enterprise patch management program. All operating systems and applications must be patched promptly in accordance with the Patch Management Policy to remediate vulnerabilities that could be exploited by malware.</p><h4>5.9. Centralized Logging and Alerting</h4><p>All malware detection events, including blocked attempts, quarantined files, and scan results, must be logged centrally to a Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system. Alerts for critical detections (e.g., ransomware, worm activity) must be configured for immediate notification of the Security Operations Center (SOC) or IT Security team.</p><h4>5.10. Removable Media Controls</h4><p>The use of removable media (USB drives, external hard drives) must be controlled. All such media must be automatically scanned for malware upon insertion into any company endpoint. The use of unapproved or un-scanned removable media on company systems is forbidden.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy supports compliance with control objectives from ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.8.7 Protection against malware), SOC 2 (CC6.1, CC7.1), and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (PR.IP-1, DE.CM-4). Adherence is mandatory to meet contractual obligations with clients and partners, and to satisfy regulatory requirements concerning data protection and breach notification.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the approval and effectiveness of this policy.<br><strong>IT Security Team:</strong> Responsible for the selection, deployment, configuration, monitoring, and tuning of all malware protection technologies. They shall investigate and respond to all malware incidents.<br><strong>IT Operations/Endpoint Management:</strong> Responsible for ensuring the mandated protection software is installed, functional, and updated on all assets under their management.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must exercise caution with email attachments and web links, report any suspected malware activity immediately to the IT Service Desk, and shall not attempt to disable or circumvent security controls.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any Personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and may face legal action. Deliberate acts that introduce malware or disable protective controls will be treated as a serious security violation.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This Malware Protection Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO or designated representative at least annually, or following significant security incidents or changes in the threat landscape. Revisions must be approved by the CISO and communicated to all relevant stakeholders.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-20 21:17:21.867899', '"[\"ISO 27001\",\"SOC 2\",\"NIST CSF\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 19:44:35.68', NULL),
	(26, '751705', 'Vulnerability Management Policy', '<p>This Vulnerability Management Policy establishes the mandatory framework for the systematic identification, assessment, prioritization, remediation, and reporting of security vulnerabilities within [Company Name]''s information assets, systems, and networks. The policy is designed to proactively manage risk, protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of data, and ensure compliance with regulatory obligations and contractual commitments.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all information systems, network infrastructure, applications (including third-party and cloud-hosted), and data owned, operated, or managed by [Company Name]. It encompasses all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties who develop, deploy, manage, or have access to [Company Name]''s technological environment. This policy is global in nature and applies to all subsidiaries and business units.</p>

<p><strong>Vulnerability:</strong> A weakness in an information system, system security procedures, internal controls, or implementation that could be exploited by a threat source.<br><strong>Threat:</strong> Any circumstance or event with the potential to adversely impact organizational operations, assets, or individuals through unauthorized access, destruction, disclosure, or modification of information.<br><strong>Risk:</strong> The potential for loss, damage, or destruction of an asset as a result of a threat exploiting a vulnerability.<br><strong>Remediation:</strong> The process of fixing a vulnerability to eliminate or mitigate the associated risk. This includes patching, configuration changes, or implementing compensating controls.<br><strong>Critical/High-Risk Vulnerability:</strong> A vulnerability with a CVSS base score of 7.0 or higher, or otherwise designated as such by the Information Security team based on contextual business risk.<br><strong>Asset Owner:</strong> The business unit or individual responsible for the business function supported by an information asset and accountable for its security.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates a continuous and risk-based vulnerability management program. All technology assets within scope shall be regularly assessed for security vulnerabilities. Identified vulnerabilities shall be evaluated, prioritized based on severity and business impact, and remediated within defined timeframes. The failure to adhere to remediation timelines for critical and high-risk vulnerabilities is strictly prohibited.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Vulnerability Discovery and Identification</strong><br>5.1.1 Automated vulnerability scanning of all in-scope internal and external network assets shall be conducted at least monthly.<br>5.1.2 Authenticated scanning, where technically feasible, shall be performed to identify vulnerabilities at the host and application layer.<br>5.1.3 All new or significantly modified applications must undergo static application security testing (SAST) and/or dynamic application security testing (DAST) prior to deployment to production.<br>5.1.4 External penetration tests, conducted by a qualified independent third party, shall be performed at least annually, or after any significant change to the external attack surface.<br><br><strong>5.2 Vulnerability Assessment and Prioritization</strong><br>5.2.1 All discovered vulnerabilities shall be cataloged in a centralized vulnerability management system.<br>5.2.2 Vulnerabilities shall be assigned a risk rating based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS) and contextual business factors, including asset criticality and threat intelligence.<br>5.2.3 A formal risk acceptance process is required for any vulnerability that will not be remediated within the standard timeframe. Risk acceptance must be documented, approved by the Asset Owner and the CISO (or delegate), and reviewed quarterly.<br><br><strong>5.3 Vulnerability Remediation</strong><br>5.3.1 Critical and High-Risk vulnerabilities (CVSS >= 7.0) must be remediated within 30 calendar days of identification or vendor patch availability, whichever is later.<br>5.3.2 Medium-Risk vulnerabilities (CVSS 4.0 - 6.9) must be remediated within 90 calendar days.<br>5.3.3 Low-Risk vulnerabilities (CVSS < 4.0) shall be remediated based on a scheduled review or as part of normal maintenance cycles.<br>5.3.4 Emergency patches for critical, actively exploited vulnerabilities (e.g., zero-days) shall be applied within 7 calendar days, following an expedited change management process.<br>5.3.5 When immediate remediation is not possible, compensating controls (e.g., network segmentation, intrusion prevention rules) must be implemented and documented until a permanent fix is deployed.<br><br><strong>5.4 Verification and Reporting</strong><br>5.4.1 All remediation actions must be verified through re-scanning or other validation techniques to confirm closure.<br>5.4.2 A comprehensive vulnerability management report, detailing scan coverage, vulnerability metrics, remediation status, and aging of open vulnerabilities, shall be generated and reviewed by senior management at least quarterly.<br>5.4.3 All scanning, assessment, and remediation activities shall be logged, with logs retained for a minimum of one year to support audit requirements.</p>

<p>The Vulnerability Management Program shall be designed and operated to meet the control objectives of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.8.8, A.5.7, A.5.29), SOC 2 (CC6.1, CC7.1), and PCI DSS (Requirements 6.1, 6.2, 11.2). The program must also consider and align with other applicable legal, regulatory, and contractual requirements pertaining to information security and data protection. Evidence of compliance, including scan reports, risk acceptance forms, and management reviews, shall be maintained for audit purposes.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the policy and program. Approves risk exceptions and provides management oversight.<br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Operates the vulnerability management program; conducts scans, coordinates testing, manages the vulnerability database, and tracks remediation.<br><strong>Asset Owners / System Administrators:</strong> Responsible for implementing approved remediation actions (e.g., applying patches) within mandated timeframes for assets under their control.<br><strong>IT Operations / Change Advisory Board (CAB):</strong> Responsible for facilitating the timely testing and deployment of security patches through the change management process.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to report suspected security vulnerabilities to the Information Security team immediately.</p>

<p>Any employee, contractor, or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract. Deliberate failure to remediate a known critical vulnerability, or the circumvention of security controls to avoid detection, will be treated as a serious security violation. The Information Security team is authorized to isolate or disconnect non-compliant systems from the network to prevent potential compromise.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the Information Security Steering Committee at least annually, or in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, technology environment, or regulatory requirements. Revisions must be formally approved and communicated to all relevant stakeholders.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Vulnerability Management Policy establishes the mandatory framework for the systematic identification, assessment, prioritization, remediation, and reporting of security vulnerabilities within [Company Name]''s information assets, systems, and networks. The policy is designed to proactively manage risk, protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of data, and ensure compliance with regulatory obligations and contractual commitments.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all information systems, network infrastructure, applications (including third-party and cloud-hosted), and data owned, operated, or managed by [Company Name]. It encompasses all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties who develop, deploy, manage, or have access to [Company Name]''s technological environment. This policy is global in nature and applies to all subsidiaries and business units.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Vulnerability:</strong> A weakness in an information system, system security procedures, internal controls, or implementation that could be exploited by a threat source.<br><strong>Threat:</strong> Any circumstance or event with the potential to adversely impact organizational operations, assets, or individuals through unauthorized access, destruction, disclosure, or modification of information.<br><strong>Risk:</strong> The potential for loss, damage, or destruction of an asset as a result of a threat exploiting a vulnerability.<br><strong>Remediation:</strong> The process of fixing a vulnerability to eliminate or mitigate the associated risk. This includes patching, configuration changes, or implementing compensating controls.<br><strong>Critical/High-Risk Vulnerability:</strong> A vulnerability with a CVSS base score of 7.0 or higher, or otherwise designated as such by the Information Security team based on contextual business risk.<br><strong>Asset Owner:</strong> The business unit or individual responsible for the business function supported by an information asset and accountable for its security.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates a continuous and risk-based vulnerability management program. All technology assets within scope shall be regularly assessed for security vulnerabilities. Identified vulnerabilities shall be evaluated, prioritized based on severity and business impact, and remediated within defined timeframes. The failure to adhere to remediation timelines for critical and high-risk vulnerabilities is strictly prohibited.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>5.1 Vulnerability Discovery and Identification</strong><br>5.1.1 Automated vulnerability scanning of all in-scope internal and external network assets shall be conducted at least monthly.<br>5.1.2 Authenticated scanning, where technically feasible, shall be performed to identify vulnerabilities at the host and application layer.<br>5.1.3 All new or significantly modified applications must undergo static application security testing (SAST) and/or dynamic application security testing (DAST) prior to deployment to production.<br>5.1.4 External penetration tests, conducted by a qualified independent third party, shall be performed at least annually, or after any significant change to the external attack surface.<br><br><strong>5.2 Vulnerability Assessment and Prioritization</strong><br>5.2.1 All discovered vulnerabilities shall be cataloged in a centralized vulnerability management system.<br>5.2.2 Vulnerabilities shall be assigned a risk rating based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS) and contextual business factors, including asset criticality and threat intelligence.<br>5.2.3 A formal risk acceptance process is required for any vulnerability that will not be remediated within the standard timeframe. Risk acceptance must be documented, approved by the Asset Owner and the CISO (or delegate), and reviewed quarterly.<br><br><strong>5.3 Vulnerability Remediation</strong><br>5.3.1 Critical and High-Risk vulnerabilities (CVSS >= 7.0) must be remediated within 30 calendar days of identification or vendor patch availability, whichever is later.<br>5.3.2 Medium-Risk vulnerabilities (CVSS 4.0 - 6.9) must be remediated within 90 calendar days.<br>5.3.3 Low-Risk vulnerabilities (CVSS < 4.0) shall be remediated based on a scheduled review or as part of normal maintenance cycles.<br>5.3.4 Emergency patches for critical, actively exploited vulnerabilities (e.g., zero-days) shall be applied within 7 calendar days, following an expedited change management process.<br>5.3.5 When immediate remediation is not possible, compensating controls (e.g., network segmentation, intrusion prevention rules) must be implemented and documented until a permanent fix is deployed.<br><br><strong>5.4 Verification and Reporting</strong><br>5.4.1 All remediation actions must be verified through re-scanning or other validation techniques to confirm closure.<br>5.4.2 A comprehensive vulnerability management report, detailing scan coverage, vulnerability metrics, remediation status, and aging of open vulnerabilities, shall be generated and reviewed by senior management at least quarterly.<br>5.4.3 All scanning, assessment, and remediation activities shall be logged, with logs retained for a minimum of one year to support audit requirements.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>The Vulnerability Management Program shall be designed and operated to meet the control objectives of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.8.8, A.5.7, A.5.29), SOC 2 (CC6.1, CC7.1), and PCI DSS (Requirements 6.1, 6.2, 11.2). The program must also consider and align with other applicable legal, regulatory, and contractual requirements pertaining to information security and data protection. Evidence of compliance, including scan reports, risk acceptance forms, and management reviews, shall be maintained for audit purposes.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the policy and program. Approves risk exceptions and provides management oversight.<br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Operates the vulnerability management program; conducts scans, coordinates testing, manages the vulnerability database, and tracks remediation.<br><strong>Asset Owners / System Administrators:</strong> Responsible for implementing approved remediation actions (e.g., applying patches) within mandated timeframes for assets under their control.<br><strong>IT Operations / Change Advisory Board (CAB):</strong> Responsible for facilitating the timely testing and deployment of security patches through the change management process.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to report suspected security vulnerabilities to the Information Security team immediately.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any employee, contractor, or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract. Deliberate failure to remediate a known critical vulnerability, or the circumvention of security controls to avoid detection, will be treated as a serious security violation. The Information Security team is authorized to isolate or disconnect non-compliant systems from the network to prevent potential compromise.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the Information Security Steering Committee at least annually, or in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, technology environment, or regulatory requirements. Revisions must be formally approved and communicated to all relevant stakeholders.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-20 21:17:21.977925', '"[\"ISO 27001\",\"SOC 2\",\"PCI DSS\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 19:45:08.683', NULL),
	(41, '33245', 'Data Breach Notification Policy', '<p>This Data Breach Notification Policy establishes the mandatory procedures for [Company Name] to detect, report, assess, and respond to data breaches involving Personal Data or Protected Health Information (PHI). The purpose is to ensure timely and compliant notification to regulatory authorities, affected individuals, and other stakeholders as required by applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, thereby mitigating legal, financial, and reputational harm.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other personnel (collectively, "Personnel") of [Company Name] who have access to company information systems or data. It covers all Personal Data and PHI processed by [Company Name], regardless of the data format (electronic, paper, or other) or location (on-premises, cloud, or third-party services). This policy extends to all subsidiaries and business units under the control of [Company Name].</p>

<p><strong>Data Breach:</strong> A security incident leading to the accidental or unlawful destruction, loss, alteration, unauthorized disclosure of, or access to Personal Data or PHI transmitted, stored, or otherwise processed.<br><strong>Personal Data:</strong> Any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person as defined under the GDPR and other applicable data protection laws.<br><strong>Protected Health Information (PHI):</strong> Individually identifiable health information as defined by HIPAA.<br><strong>Data Controller:</strong> The entity that determines the purposes and means of processing Personal Data.<br><strong>Data Processor:</strong> The entity that processes Personal Data on behalf of the Data Controller.<br><strong>Supervisory Authority:</strong> An independent public authority established under the GDPR (e.g., a Data Protection Authority).<br><strong>Notification Timeline:</strong> The legally mandated period within which a breach must be reported to authorities and/or affected individuals.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates a zero-tolerance approach to the mismanagement of data breach incidents. All Personnel shall immediately report any suspected or confirmed data breach. [Company Name] shall investigate all reported incidents promptly, document findings rigorously, and execute notification procedures in strict compliance with all applicable legal and regulatory frameworks, including but not limited to GDPR, HIPAA, and state-level breach notification laws. Failure to adhere to this policy constitutes a serious violation of company rules and may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Breach Detection and Initial Reporting:</strong> Any Personnel who suspects a data breach shall immediately, and in no case later than one (1) hour after discovery, report the incident to the Information Security Team via the designated secure channel (e.g., a dedicated ticketing system or hotline). Initial reports must include the time of discovery, systems/data involved, and the nature of the suspected breach.<br><br><strong>5.2 Incident Triage and Classification:</strong> Upon notification, the Information Security Team shall initiate a formal incident response. A preliminary assessment must be completed within four (4) hours to classify the incident''s severity (e.g., Low, Medium, High, Critical) based on data sensitivity, volume of records impacted, and likelihood of harm to data subjects.<br><br><strong>5.3 Formal Investigation and Documentation:</strong> For all confirmed breaches, a formal investigation shall be conducted. This investigation must document: the date, time, and duration of the breach; the categories and approximate number of data subjects and records concerned; the likely consequences of the breach; and the measures taken or proposed to address the breach. All investigative actions and findings shall be logged in the organization''s Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system.<br><br><strong>5.4 Breach Assessment and Legal Determination:</strong> The Data Protection Officer (DPO) or Legal Counsel, in conjunction with the CISO, shall determine within forty-eight (48) hours of a confirmed breach whether the incident is likely to result in a risk to the rights and freedoms of individuals. This assessment shall dictate the notification requirements under GDPR Article 33/34, HIPAA, and other relevant laws.<br><br><strong>5.5 Regulatory Notification Procedures:</strong> If notification is required, the DPO shall ensure notification is made to the relevant Supervisory Authority (e.g., for GDPR) within seventy-two (72) hours of becoming aware of the breach. Notifications to the Secretary of Health and Human Services (for HIPAA) shall be made without unreasonable delay and no later than sixty (60) calendar days after discovery. All notifications must include the information documented per section 5.3.<br><br><strong>5.6 Individual Notification Procedures:</strong> Where a breach is likely to result in a high risk to individuals, affected data subjects shall be notified without undue delay. Notifications shall be delivered in clear and plain language, describing the nature of the breach, the categories of data involved, recommended mitigation steps for the individual, and contact details for [Company Name]''s DPO. Communication methods shall be direct (e.g., email, letter) unless demonstrably disproportionate effort is required, in which case a public communication may be authorized by the DPO.<br><br><strong>5.7 Third-Party and Media Communication:</strong> All external communications regarding a data breach, including press releases and notifications to business partners or data processors, must be pre-approved by the CISO, DPO, and Legal Counsel. A single, authorized spokesperson shall be designated for all media inquiries.<br><br><strong>5.8 Post-Breach Remediation and Review:</strong> Following containment and notification, the Information Security Team shall conduct a root cause analysis. Corrective and preventive actions (e.g., patching systems, updating access controls, enhancing monitoring) shall be implemented. A full incident report, including lessons learned, shall be presented to senior management within thirty (30) days of breach closure.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with, but not limited to, the following frameworks and regulations:<br>- <strong>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR):</strong> Articles 33 and 34 (Notification obligations).<br>- <strong>Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA):</strong> Breach Notification Rule (45 CFR §§ 164.400-414).<br>- <strong>ISO/IEC 27001:2022:</strong> Control objectives A.5.24 (Information security incident response planning), A.5.25 (Assessment and decision on information security events), A.5.26 (Response to information security incidents), and A.5.27 (Learning from information security incidents).<br>- <strong>State-Level Laws:</strong> Applicable state data breach notification statutes (e.g., California Consumer Privacy Act/CPRA, New York SHIELD Act).<br>In the event of conflicting requirements, the most stringent applicable law shall govern [Company Name]''s actions.</p>

<p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must immediately report any suspected data breach as defined in Section 5.1.<br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for initial triage, investigation, containment, technical remediation, and documentation of the breach.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Has overall authority for the execution of this policy, coordinates the incident response, and is the primary liaison with executive management.<br><strong>Data Protection Officer (DPO) / Legal Counsel:</strong> Responsible for the legal assessment of the breach, determining notification obligations, and managing all regulatory and individual communications.<br><strong>Executive Management / Board of Directors:</strong> Must be informed of significant breaches without delay and are responsible for approving resource allocation for breach response and remediation.</p>

<p>Personnel found to have violated this policy—including, but not limited to, failing to report a suspected breach in a timely manner, impeding an investigation, or making unauthorized external communications—shall be subject to disciplinary action. Such action may include performance improvement plans, suspension, revocation of system access, termination of employment, and pursuit of legal action. Compliance with this policy is a condition of employment and continued access to [Company Name] information assets.</p>

<p>This Data Breach Notification Policy shall be reviewed annually by the CISO and DPO, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in business operations, or updates to applicable laws and regulations. All revisions must be approved by the CISO and General Counsel. The latest approved version of this policy shall be made accessible to all Personnel through the company''s policy management portal.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Data Breach Notification Policy establishes the mandatory procedures for [Company Name] to detect, report, assess, and respond to data breaches involving Personal Data or Protected Health Information (PHI). The purpose is to ensure timely and compliant notification to regulatory authorities, affected individuals, and other stakeholders as required by applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, thereby mitigating legal, financial, and reputational harm.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other personnel (collectively, \\\"Personnel\\\") of [Company Name] who have access to company information systems or data. It covers all Personal Data and PHI processed by [Company Name], regardless of the data format (electronic, paper, or other) or location (on-premises, cloud, or third-party services). This policy extends to all subsidiaries and business units under the control of [Company Name].</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Data Breach:</strong> A security incident leading to the accidental or unlawful destruction, loss, alteration, unauthorized disclosure of, or access to Personal Data or PHI transmitted, stored, or otherwise processed.<br><strong>Personal Data:</strong> Any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person as defined under the GDPR and other applicable data protection laws.<br><strong>Protected Health Information (PHI):</strong> Individually identifiable health information as defined by HIPAA.<br><strong>Data Controller:</strong> The entity that determines the purposes and means of processing Personal Data.<br><strong>Data Processor:</strong> The entity that processes Personal Data on behalf of the Data Controller.<br><strong>Supervisory Authority:</strong> An independent public authority established under the GDPR (e.g., a Data Protection Authority).<br><strong>Notification Timeline:</strong> The legally mandated period within which a breach must be reported to authorities and/or affected individuals.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates a zero-tolerance approach to the mismanagement of data breach incidents. All Personnel shall immediately report any suspected or confirmed data breach. [Company Name] shall investigate all reported incidents promptly, document findings rigorously, and execute notification procedures in strict compliance with all applicable legal and regulatory frameworks, including but not limited to GDPR, HIPAA, and state-level breach notification laws. Failure to adhere to this policy constitutes a serious violation of company rules and may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>5.1 Breach Detection and Initial Reporting:</strong> Any Personnel who suspects a data breach shall immediately, and in no case later than one (1) hour after discovery, report the incident to the Information Security Team via the designated secure channel (e.g., a dedicated ticketing system or hotline). Initial reports must include the time of discovery, systems/data involved, and the nature of the suspected breach.<br><br><strong>5.2 Incident Triage and Classification:</strong> Upon notification, the Information Security Team shall initiate a formal