ement authorization.<br>5.2.2. Access shall be controlled via an approved electronic access control system (e.g., card, biometric). The use of mechanical keys as a primary control is forbidden for high-security areas; where used, key issuance and inventory shall be strictly logged.<br>5.2.3. All access events (entries and exits) to secure areas shall be automatically logged. Logs shall be retained for a minimum of 90 days and reviewed for anomalies.<br>5.2.4. All visitors shall be pre-authorized, logged, required to present identification, and escorted by authorized personnel at all times while in secure areas. Visitor badges shall be distinctly different from employee badges.</p><h4>5.3 Securing Offices, Rooms, and Facilities</h4><p>5.3.1. A Clean Desk and Clear Screen Policy shall be enforced. Sensitive information shall not be left unattended on desks, printers, or screens.<br>5.3.2. Physical locks (e.g., cable locks) shall be used to secure portable IT equipment (e.g., laptops, projectors) when left unattended in offices or temporary work locations.<br>5.3.3. Sensitive media and information shall be stored in locked filing cabinets, safes, or other secure storage when not in use.</p><h4>5.4 Protection Against Environmental Threats</h4><p>5.4.1. Critical information processing facilities shall be protected by fire detection and automatic suppression systems appropriate for the environment (e.g., inert gas, water mist).<br>5.4.2. Water detection sensors shall be installed in data centers and server rooms to provide early warning of leaks or flooding.<br>5.4.3. Temperature and humidity levels in data centers and server rooms shall be continuously monitored and maintained within manufacturer-specified tolerances.</p><h4>5.5 Equipment Security and Maintenance</h4><p>5.5.1. Equipment shall be sited to minimize risks from environmental hazards and unauthorized access. Cabling for power and data shall be protected from interception, damage, and electrical interference.<br>5.5.2. A documented process for the secure disposal or reuse of equipment containing storage media shall be followed to prevent leakage of information. All media shall be sanitized or physically destroyed prior to disposal.<br>5.5.3. Supporting utilities (e.g., electricity, telecommunications) shall be secured and have adequate capacity. Critical equipment shall be connected to Uninterruptible Power Supplies (UPS) and/or backup generators.</p><h4>5.6 Removal of Assets</h4><p>5.6.1. The removal of information processing equipment, software, or media from company premises shall require explicit authorization from the Information Technology (IT) Department and the employee''s manager.<br>5.6.2. A formal asset removal log shall be maintained, recording the item, individual, authorization, and date.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with ISO 27001:2022 control objectives A.7.1 (Secure areas), A.7.2 (Equipment), A.7.3 (Cabling security), and A.8.1 (Responsibility for assets), as well as SOC 2 CC6.1 (Logical and Physical Access Controls) and CC7.1 (System Operations). Failure to comply may result in violations of data protection laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), contractual obligations, and regulatory standards, exposing [Company Name] to legal liability, financial penalties, and reputational damage.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the approval, implementation, and oversight of this policy.<br><strong>Facilities/Security Manager:</strong> Responsible for the implementation and maintenance of physical security controls (access systems, CCTV, alarms), visitor management, and environmental monitoring systems.<br><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Responsible for the security of information processing equipment, media sanitization/disposal, and technical implementation of controls within server rooms and data centers.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for complying with this policy, including the Clean Desk Policy, securing equipment, challenging unescorted strangers in secure areas, and reporting physical security incidents.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any employee found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment. Contractors and vendors found in violation may have their agreements terminated. Unauthorized physical access, theft, or willful damage to company assets will be reported to law enforcement authorities. Exceptions to this policy require a formal risk assessment and written approval from the CISO.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This Physical and Environmental Security Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and relevant stakeholders at least annually, or following any significant security incident or change to the physical operating environment. Revisions shall be made to ensure ongoing effectiveness and compliance with evolving standards and threats. The latest version of this policy is authoritative and shall be made available to all personnel covered by its scope.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-19 06:15:56.282998', '"[\"ISO 27001\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 20:00:21.268', NULL),
	(64, 'nist_ai_aup_001', 'AI Acceptable Use Policy (AUP)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Master Policy: AI Acceptable Use Policy (AUP)</title>
    <style>
        body { font-family: ''Segoe UI'', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif; line-height: 1.6; color: #333; max-width: 1200px; margin: 40px auto; padding: 20px; }
        h1 { color: #2c3e50; border-bottom: 3px solid #3498db; padding-bottom: 10px; }
        h2 { color: #2980b9; margin-top: 30px; border-left: 4px solid #3498db; padding-left: 10px; }
        h3 { color: #16a085; margin-top: 25px; }
        h4 { color: #27ae60; }
        .section { margin-bottom: 40px; }
        .requirement { background-color: #f8f9fa; border-left: 4px solid #e74c3c; padding: 15px; margin: 15px 0; }
        .definition-term { font-weight: bold; color: #8e44ad; }
        ul, ol { padding-left: 25px; }
        li { margin-bottom: 8px; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Master Policy: AI Acceptable Use Policy (AUP)</h1>
    <p><strong>Policy ID:</strong> INFOSEC-AI-AUP-001<br>
    <strong>Version:</strong> 2.0<br>
    <strong>Effective Date:</strong> [Date]<br>
    <strong>Classification:</strong> [Company Name] Confidential</p>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>1. Purpose</h2>
        <p>This AI Acceptable Use Policy (AUP) establishes the mandatory principles, rules, and controls governing the secure, ethical, and compliant acquisition, development, deployment, and use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) systems, models, and data within [Company Name]. Its purpose is to mitigate risks associated with AI, including security vulnerabilities, data poisoning, model theft, biased outputs, and regulatory non-compliance, thereby protecting organizational assets, reputation, and stakeholder trust.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>2. Scope</h2>
        <p>This policy applies universally to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any third-party agents (collectively, "Users") who interact with, develop, manage, or are responsible for any AI/ML system. It encompasses all AI/ML models (proprietary or third-party), training and operational datasets, associated infrastructure (cloud or on-premises), software development lifecycles, and AI-generated outputs used in the course of [Company Name]''s business operations, irrespective of deployment environment.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>3. Definitions</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><span class="definition-term">AI/ML System:</span> Any software system that utilizes machine learning, deep learning, neural networks, generative AI, or related techniques to perform tasks without explicit, deterministic programming.</li>
            <li><span class="definition-term">Model Artifacts:</span> The trained model files, weights, parameters, configuration files, and code required to execute an AI/ML system.</li>
            <li><span class="definition-term">Training Data:</span> The dataset used to train, validate, or tune an AI/ML model.</li>
            <li><span class="definition-term">Production Deployment:</span> The operational state where an AI/ML system is integrated into a business process or customer-facing application.</li>
            <li><span class="definition-term">Prompt Injection:</span> A security exploit where malicious input is crafted to manipulate an AI system''s behavior, leading to unauthorized data disclosure or actions.</li>
            <li><span class="definition-term">Model Card:</span> A standardized document detailing an AI model''s performance characteristics, limitations, intended use, and ethical considerations.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>4. Policy Statement</h2>
        <p>[Company Name] mandates that all use of Artificial Intelligence shall be lawful, ethical, secure, and aligned with business objectives. The development and operation of AI systems shall adhere to principles of fairness, accountability, transparency, and data integrity. Any use of AI that poses an unacceptable risk to security, privacy, regulatory compliance, or [Company Name]''s reputation is strictly prohibited.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>5. Policy Requirements</h2>
        <p>The following technical and procedural controls are mandatory for all AI/ML activities.</p>

        <div class="requirement">
            <h3>5.1. AI System Authorization & Inventory</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>All AI/ML systems intended for development, testing, or production use <strong>must</strong> undergo a formal authorization process managed by the AI Governance Committee prior to any data processing or model training.</li>
                <li>A centralized inventory of all authorized AI/ML systems, including model artifacts, versioning, data sources, and responsible owners, <strong>shall</strong> be maintained and reviewed quarterly.</li>
                <li>The use of unauthorized or "shadow AI" systems (e.g., unvetted third-party APIs, open-source models deployed without review) is <strong>expressly forbidden</strong>.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="requirement">
            <h3>5.2. Data Governance for AI</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Training and operational data for AI systems <strong>must</strong> be sourced, processed, and stored in compliance with the Data Classification and Handling Policy. Use of Personal Identifiable Information (PII) requires explicit privacy impact assessment and legal approval.</li>
                <li>All training datasets <strong>shall</strong> be version-controlled, and their provenance (origin, collection method, transformations) documented to ensure reproducibility and auditability.</li>
                <li>Data labeling and annotation activities <strong>must</strong> implement quality assurance checks to minimize bias and error. Annotation guidelines <strong>shall</strong> be documented and retained.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="requirement">
            <h3>5.3. Model Development & Security</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Model development <strong>must</strong> occur in isolated, secure environments (e.g., dedicated VPCs, air-gapped research clusters). Direct development on production systems or data is <strong>prohibited</strong>.</li>
                <li>All model code and artifacts <strong>shall</strong> be stored in a secure, access-controlled repository (e.g., private Git repository) with commit signing and peer review requirements enabled.</li>
                <li>Models must be evaluated for security vulnerabilities, including susceptibility to adversarial attacks, prompt injection (for generative AI), and data leakage, prior to promotion to any staging environment.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="requirement">
            <h3>5.4. Production Deployment & Monitoring</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Deployment to production <strong>must</strong> be gated by a formal model promotion procedure, including sign-off from Security, Legal, and the business owner.</li>
                <li>All production AI systems <strong>shall</strong> have comprehensive, immutable logging enabled. Logs must capture all input queries (sanitized of PII), model responses, system performance metrics, and error rates, retained for a minimum of [e.g., 90] days.</li>
                <li>Automated monitoring for model drift, performance degradation, and anomalous output patterns <strong>must</strong> be implemented. Alerts for threshold breaches <strong>shall</strong> be configured and routed to the responsible AI Operations team.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="requirement">
            <h3>5.5. Acceptable Use of Third-Party & Generative AI</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Use of external AI/ML APIs (e.g., OpenAI GPT, Google Vertex AI) <strong>requires</strong> pre-approval and a completed Third-Party AI Risk Assessment. All API keys <strong>must</strong> be managed via a secure enterprise secrets manager.</li>
                <li>Employees interacting with public generative AI tools (e.g., ChatGPT, Copilot) <strong>are strictly prohibited</strong> from submitting any [Company Name] Confidential, Internal, or PII data. Use for general research or public data is permitted only after completing mandatory AI awareness training.</li>
                <li>Output from generative AI systems <strong>shall not</strong> be considered a primary authoritative source. All factual assertions, code, or legal text generated must be validated and verified by a qualified human expert.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="requirement">
            <h3>5.6. Ethical Use & Human Oversight</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>AI systems shall not be used for deceptive practices, automated discrimination, creating malicious content, or any activity that violates applicable laws or [Company Name]''s Code of Conduct.</li>
                <li>High-risk AI systems (e.g., those impacting hiring, credit, healthcare, or public safety) <strong>must</strong> implement a "human-in-the-loop" (HITL) control, where a qualified human reviews and approves critical decisions before action.</li>
                <li>A Model Card or equivalent documentation <strong>shall</strong> be created for every production AI system, detailing its intended use, limitations, known biases, and performance metrics.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>6. Compliance and Legal Requirements</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>All AI activities <strong>must</strong> comply with relevant data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA/CPRA), sector-specific laws (e.g., HIPAA, GLBA), and emerging AI governance frameworks (e.g., EU AI Act, NIST AI RMF).</li>
            <li>The Legal Department <strong>shall</strong> review all contracts for third-party AI services to ensure appropriate data processing, liability, and intellectual property terms are in place.</li>
            <li>Any AI system that creates content (text, image, code) must have its intellectual property ownership and licensing implications assessed prior to use.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>7. Roles and Responsibilities</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>AI Governance Committee:</strong> Owns this policy, defines AI risk appetite, and provides final authorization for high-risk AI systems.</li>
            <li><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Responsible for the security controls outlined in this policy, conducting threat modeling, and overseeing security audits of AI systems.</li>
            <li><strong>Data Protection Officer (DPO) / Legal:</strong> Ensures AI use complies with privacy and other relevant laws, and reviews data processing agreements.</li>
            <li><strong>AI/ML System Owners:</strong> Accountable for the secure development, deployment, monitoring, and documented compliance of their specific AI systems.</li>
            <li><strong>All Users:</strong> Required to complete AI AUP training, use AI systems only for authorized purposes, and immediately report any suspected policy violations or security incidents.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>8. Enforcement</h2>
        <p>Violations of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement, and potential legal action. The Information Security Department is authorized to monitor, investigate, and, if necessary, immediately disconnect or quarantine any AI system found to be in violation of this policy to prevent harm to [Company Name].</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>9. Review and Revision</h2>
        <p>This policy shall be reviewed by the AI Governance Committee and the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant changes in technology, threat landscape, or regulatory requirements. All revisions must be formally approved and communicated to all stakeholders.</p>
        <p><strong>Approved By:</strong> [Name, Title]<br>
        <strong>Date of Approval:</strong> [Date]</p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>', NULL, '2026-01-31 19:00:14.318921', NULL, 1, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 20:01:15.053', NULL),
	(65, 'nist_ai_sdlc_001', 'AI System Development & Lifecycle Policy', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>AI System Development & Lifecycle Policy</title>
    <style>
        body { font-family: ''Segoe UI'', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif; line-height: 1.6; color: #333; max-width: 1200px; margin: 40px auto; padding: 20px; }
        h1 { color: #2c3e50; border-bottom: 3px solid #3498db; padding-bottom: 10px; }
        h2 { color: #2c3e50; margin-top: 30px; border-left: 4px solid #3498db; padding-left: 10px; }
        h3 { color: #34495e; margin-top: 25px; }
        h4 { color: #7f8c8d; }
        .policy-section { margin-bottom: 40px; }
        .requirement-item { margin-bottom: 15px; }
        .definition-term { font-weight: bold; }
        .legal-ref { font-style: italic; color: #555; }
        .placeholder { background-color: #fffacd; padding: 2px 4px; border-radius: 3px; font-weight: bold; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>AI System Development & Lifecycle Policy</h1>
    <p><strong>Policy ID:</strong> AI-DLCY-001<br><strong>Version:</strong> 1.0<br><strong>Effective Date:</strong> [Date]<br><strong>Classification:</strong> [Company Name] Confidential</p>

    <div class="policy-section">
        <h2>1. Purpose</h2>
        <p>This policy establishes the mandatory governance, security, and compliance framework for the development, deployment, operation, and decommissioning of all Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) systems at <span class="placeholder">[Company Name]</span>. Its purpose is to ensure that AI systems are designed and managed responsibly, with rigorous attention to data integrity, algorithmic fairness, security, privacy, and regulatory compliance throughout their entire lifecycle.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-section">
        <h2>2. Scope</h2>
        <p>This policy applies universally to all <span class="placeholder">[Company Name]</span> personnel (including employees, contractors, and third-party vendors), assets, and processes involved in the conception, design, development, testing, deployment, monitoring, maintenance, and retirement of any AI/ML system. This includes, but is not limited to, supervised/unsupervised learning models, generative AI, neural networks, and automated decision-making systems, regardless of deployment environment (cloud, on-premise, or hybrid).</p>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-section">
        <h2>3. Definitions</h2>
        <p><span class="definition-term">AI System Lifecycle:</span> The end-to-end process encompassing design, data collection, model development, validation, deployment, monitoring, maintenance, and decommissioning.<br>
        <span class="definition-term">Model Artifacts:</span> All components of a trained model, including weights, architecture, hyperparameters, and code.<br>
        <span class="definition-term">Production Environment:</span> Any system where the AI model is executing live inferences that impact business operations or customer decisions.<br>
        <span class="definition-term">Algorithmic Bias:</span> Systematic and unfair discrimination in outputs, resulting from prejudiced assumptions in the algorithmic process or training data.<br>
        <span class="definition-term">Model Drift:</span> The degradation of a model''s predictive performance over time due to changes in real-world data.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-section">
        <h2>4. Policy Statement</h2>
        <p><span class="placeholder">[Company Name]</span> mandates that all AI system development and operations shall be conducted within a controlled governance framework. This framework shall enforce principles of security-by-design, privacy-by-design, fairness, transparency, and accountability. Any deviation from the controls specified in this policy is strictly prohibited.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-section">
        <h2>5. Policy Requirements</h2>

        <h3>5.1 Governance & Risk Assessment</h3>
        <div class="requirement-item">5.1.1. A formal AI System Impact Assessment (AISIA) <strong>must</strong> be completed and approved by the AI Governance Committee prior to the initiation of any development project. This assessment shall classify the system''s risk tier (e.g., High, Medium, Low) based on its potential impact on safety, privacy, fairness, and business continuity.</div>
        <div class="requirement-item">5.1.2. All High-Risk AI systems (e.g., those used in hiring, credit scoring, or clinical diagnostics) <strong>shall</strong> undergo a mandatory third-party algorithmic audit for bias and fairness before deployment.</div>

        <h3>5.2 Data Management & Provenance</h3>
        <div class="requirement-item">5.2.1. All training, validation, and test datasets <strong>must</strong> be cataloged in a centralized Data Registry, with documented provenance, collection methods, and applicable data use agreements.</div>
        <div class="requirement-item">5.2.2. Personally Identifiable Information (PII) used in training <strong>shall</strong> be pseudonymized or anonymized. The use of sensitive data categories (e.g., biometric, genetic) requires explicit approval from the Data Protection Officer (DPO).</div>
        <div class="requirement-item">5.2.3. Data pipelines feeding AI systems <strong>must</strong> implement integrity checks and validation rules to detect and alert on data poisoning or significant distribution shifts.</div>

        <h3>5.3 Model Development & Security</h3>
        <div class="requirement-item">5.3.1. All model development code <strong>shall</strong> be managed in a version-controlled repository (e.g., Git), with mandatory peer review and static application security testing (SAST) integrated into the commit pipeline.</div>
        <div class="requirement-item">5.3.2. Model artifacts <strong>must</strong> be stored in a secure, access-controlled Model Registry. Access to production model artifacts is restricted to authorized MLOps personnel only.</div>
        <div class="requirement-item">5.3.3. Adversarial robustness testing <strong>shall</strong> be performed on all models prior to deployment to evaluate resilience against evasion, poisoning, and extraction attacks.</div>

        <h3>5.4 Testing, Validation & Approval</h3>
        <div class="requirement-item">5.4.1. A comprehensive Model Validation Report, including performance metrics, fairness assessments (e.g., disparate impact analysis), and explainability outputs, <strong>must</strong> be signed off by the Model Validator and the business process owner.</div>
        <div class="requirement-item">5.4.2. All models <strong>shall</strong> undergo penetration testing and vulnerability scanning of their deployment environment as part of the release process.</div>

        <h3>5.5 Deployment & Operations</h3>
        <div class="requirement-item">5.5.1. Deployment to any production environment <strong>must</strong> be executed via an automated CI/CD pipeline with mandatory approval gates. Manual deployments are forbidden.</div>
        <div class="requirement-item">5.5.2. All production inference endpoints <strong>shall</strong> be protected by robust authentication (e.g., OAuth 2.0, API keys) and encrypted in transit using TLS 1.2 or higher.</div>

        <h3>5.6 Monitoring, Maintenance & Decommissioning</h3>
        <div class="requirement-item">5.6.1. Continuous monitoring of model performance, data drift, and concept drift <strong>must</strong> be implemented. Alerts <strong>shall</strong> be configured for performance degradation beyond pre-defined thresholds.</div>
        <div class="requirement-item">5.6.2. A formal model retraining or retirement procedure <strong>shall</strong> be triggered when monitoring indicates sustained drift or performance decay.</div>
        <div class="requirement-item">5.6.3. Upon decommissioning, all model artifacts, associated data, and logs <strong>must</strong> be securely archived or destroyed in accordance with the Data Retention and Disposal Policy.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-section">
        <h2>6. Compliance and Legal Requirements</h2>
        <p>All AI systems must comply with applicable laws, regulations, and standards, including but not limited to:
        <ul>
            <li>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) and any applicable national data protection laws.</li>
            <li>Sector-specific regulations (e.g., HIPAA, PCI-DSS, SOX) where applicable.</li>
            <li>Emerging AI regulations (e.g., EU AI Act, US Executive Order on AI).</li>
            <li>ISO 27001 controls for information security management.</li>
            <li>SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for security, availability, processing integrity, confidentiality, and privacy.</li>
        </ul>
        The Legal & Compliance Department is responsible for interpreting these requirements for each AI initiative.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-section">
        <h2>7. Roles and Responsibilities</h2>
        <p><strong>AI Governance Committee:</strong> Approves AISIAs, sets policy, and oversees high-risk AI projects.<br>
        <strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority for the security controls mandated in this policy.<br>
        <strong>Data Protection Officer (DPO):</strong> Ensures compliance with data privacy regulations throughout the AI lifecycle.<br>
        <strong>AI/ML Development Teams:</strong> Responsible for implementing all technical controls during design, development, and testing phases.<br>
        <strong>MLOps Engineers:</strong> Responsible for secure deployment, monitoring, maintenance, and decommissioning of production models.<br>
        <strong>Model Validator:</strong> An independent role responsible for reviewing and signing off on Model Validation Reports.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-section">
        <h2>8. Enforcement</h2>
        <p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement, and may face legal prosecution. The Information Security Department is authorized to immediately isolate or shut down any AI system found to be operating in violation of this policy.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-section">
        <h2>9. Review and Revision</h2>
        <p>This policy shall be reviewed and updated by the AI Governance Committee and the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant changes in technology, threat landscape, or regulatory environment. All revisions must be formally communicated and documented.</p>
        <p><strong>Approved By:</strong> [CISO Name & Signature]<br>
        <strong>Date of Last Review:</strong> [Date]<br>
        <strong>Next Scheduled Review:</strong> [Date]</p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>', NULL, '2026-01-31 19:00:14.592014', NULL, 1, true, NULL, '2026-02-19 20:02:03.897', NULL),
	(9, '4', 'Data Retention Policy', '<p>This Data Retention Policy establishes the mandatory framework for the systematic management, storage, and secure disposal of all data assets created, processed, or stored by [Company Name]. Its purpose is to ensure legal and regulatory compliance, mitigate risks associated with data over-retention, optimize storage resources, and enforce data minimization principles in alignment with ISO 27001 (A.8.2.3, A.8.3.1) and SOC 2 (CC6.1, CC6.8) requirements.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all data classified as Confidential, Internal Use, or Public, regardless of format (structured, unstructured, or semi-structured) or storage medium (electronic, physical, or cloud-based). It encompasses all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party service providers who create, access, process, or manage data on behalf of [Company Name]. This includes, but is not limited to, data residing in: production databases, backup systems, archives, file shares, email systems, endpoint devices, and physical records.</p>

<p><strong>Data Retention Period:</strong> The legally mandated or business-justified duration for which a specific data class must be retained before mandatory secure disposal.<br/><strong>Data Disposal:</strong> The irreversible and secure destruction of data, rendering it unrecoverable by any means.<br/><strong>Legal Hold:</strong> A formal suspension of normal retention and disposal rules for data relevant to actual or reasonably anticipated litigation, audit, or investigation.<br/><strong>Data Owner:</strong> The business unit leader or designated individual accountable for defining the classification, retention period, and authorized access for a specific data set.<br/><strong>Custodian:</strong> The Information Technology (IT) Department or system administrator responsible for implementing and enforcing the technical controls specified in this policy.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all data shall be retained only for a defined, justified period and subsequently disposed of in a secure, auditable manner. Indefinite or unnecessary data retention is strictly prohibited. All retention schedules and disposal activities must comply with applicable laws, regulations, contractual obligations, and the principle of data minimization.</p>

<h4>5.1 Retention Schedule Establishment and Maintenance</h4><p>A comprehensive, documented Data Retention Schedule, approved by Legal and Compliance, shall be maintained. This schedule must map all data classes to specific retention periods, citing the legal or business rationale (e.g., "7 years for financial records per [Jurisdiction] Tax Code"). The schedule shall be reviewed and updated at least annually.</p><h4>5.2 Technical Implementation of Retention Controls</h4><p>Retention periods defined in the Retention Schedule shall be enforced through technical controls wherever feasible. This includes, but is not limited to: automated archival flags in databases, immutable retention locks on object storage, and time-based lifecycle policies in cloud platforms (e.g., AWS S3 Lifecycle, Azure Blob Storage tiers). Manual processes are only permitted where automation is technically infeasible and require documented compensating controls.</p><h4>5.3 Secure Disposal Methods</h4><p>Upon expiry of the retention period, data must be disposed of using methods commensurate with its sensitivity. For electronic data, this requires cryptographic erasure (NIST 800-88 Clear/Purge) or physical destruction of media. For physical records, cross-cut shredding or incineration by a certified vendor is required. All disposal actions must generate an audit log entry.</p><h4>5.4 Legal Hold Process</h4><p>A formal Legal Hold procedure, managed by the Legal Department, shall be established. Upon notification of a hold, IT Custodians must immediately suspend automated disposal for implicated data sets. Held data must be isolated and protected from modification. The hold must be documented, including scope, date initiated, and responsible party, and may only be released upon written authorization from Legal.</p><h4>5.5 Backup and Archive Media Retention</h4><p>Backup media are subject to the same retention schedules as primary data. Backup systems must support granular recovery to avoid unnecessary restoration of expired data. Long-term archival systems shall employ Write-Once-Read-Many (WORM) or functionally equivalent technology to prevent tampering or premature deletion.</p><h4>5.6 Logging and Auditability</h4><p>All data disposal activities, whether automated or manual, shall be logged. Logs must include, at minimum: data identifier, disposal method, date/time, and initiating user or system. These logs shall be retained for a period exceeding the longest retention period of any corporate data to facilitate forensic investigation and compliance audits.</p>

<p>Retention periods shall be established in compliance with all applicable laws and regulations, including but not limited to: GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, HIPAA, SOX, FINRA, and FTC rules. The Legal Department is responsible for identifying and communicating changes in legal retention obligations. This policy directly supports compliance with ISO 27001 Annex A controls 8.2.3 (Handling of assets), 8.3.1 (Management of removable media), and A.12.3 (Backup), and SOC 2 Criteria CC6.1 (Logical Access) and CC6.8 (System Operations).</p>

<p><strong>Data Owner:</strong> Define data classification and approved retention period; authorize exceptions and access.<br/><strong>Legal & Compliance Department:</strong> Define legal retention obligations; issue and manage Legal Holds; approve the Retention Schedule.<br/><strong>Information Technology (Custodian):</strong> Implement and maintain technical controls for retention and disposal; execute secure disposal; manage Legal Hold technical suspension.<br/><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Adhere to retention and disposal procedures for data under their control; promptly notify Legal of potential litigation.<br/><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Overall policy enforcement, audit coordination, and risk management oversight.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment, and may face civil or criminal penalties. Deliberate circumvention of retention controls or unauthorized disposal of data under Legal Hold is considered a severe security incident. Third-party vendors in violation of contractual data retention obligations shall be subject to penalties per the agreement and possible termination of the business relationship.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and Legal Department at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant changes in the legal landscape, technology infrastructure, or business operations. Revisions must be approved by the Information Security Steering Committee. The latest approved version is the only version considered authoritative.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Data Retention Policy establishes the mandatory framework for the systematic management, storage, and secure disposal of all data assets created, processed, or stored by [Company Name]. Its purpose is to ensure legal and regulatory compliance, mitigate risks associated with data over-retention, optimize storage resources, and enforce data minimization principles in alignment with ISO 27001 (A.8.2.3, A.8.3.1) and SOC 2 (CC6.1, CC6.8) requirements.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all data classified as Confidential, Internal Use, or Public, regardless of format (structured, unstructured, or semi-structured) or storage medium (electronic, physical, or cloud-based). It encompasses all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party service providers who create, access, process, or manage data on behalf of [Company Name]. This includes, but is not limited to, data residing in: production databases, backup systems, archives, file shares, email systems, endpoint devices, and physical records.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Data Retention Period:</strong> The legally mandated or business-justified duration for which a specific data class must be retained before mandatory secure disposal.<br/><strong>Data Disposal:</strong> The irreversible and secure destruction of data, rendering it unrecoverable by any means.<br/><strong>Legal Hold:</strong> A formal suspension of normal retention and disposal rules for data relevant to actual or reasonably anticipated litigation, audit, or investigation.<br/><strong>Data Owner:</strong> The business unit leader or designated individual accountable for defining the classification, retention period, and authorized access for a specific data set.<br/><strong>Custodian:</strong> The Information Technology (IT) Department or system administrator responsible for implementing and enforcing the technical controls specified in this policy.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates that all data shall be retained only for a defined, justified period and subsequently disposed of in a secure, auditable manner. Indefinite or unnecessary data retention is strictly prohibited. All retention schedules and disposal activities must comply with applicable laws, regulations, contractual obligations, and the principle of data minimization.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1 Retention Schedule Establishment and Maintenance</h4><p>A comprehensive, documented Data Retention Schedule, approved by Legal and Compliance, shall be maintained. This schedule must map all data classes to specific retention periods, citing the legal or business rationale (e.g., \\\"7 years for financial records per [Jurisdiction] Tax Code\\\"). The schedule shall be reviewed and updated at least annually.</p><h4>5.2 Technical Implementation of Retention Controls</h4><p>Retention periods defined in the Retention Schedule shall be enforced through technical controls wherever feasible. This includes, but is not limited to: automated archival flags in databases, immutable retention locks on object storage, and time-based lifecycle policies in cloud platforms (e.g., AWS S3 Lifecycle, Azure Blob Storage tiers). Manual processes are only permitted where automation is technically infeasible and require documented compensating controls.</p><h4>5.3 Secure Disposal Methods</h4><p>Upon expiry of the retention period, data must be disposed of using methods commensurate with its sensitivity. For electronic data, this requires cryptographic erasure (NIST 800-88 Clear/Purge) or physical destruction of media. For physical records, cross-cut shredding or incineration by a certified vendor is required. All disposal actions must generate an audit log entry.</p><h4>5.4 Legal Hold Process</h4><p>A formal Legal Hold procedure, managed by the Legal Department, shall be established. Upon notification of a hold, IT Custodians must immediately suspend automated disposal for implicated data sets. Held data must be isolated and protected from modification. The hold must be documented, including scope, date initiated, and responsible party, and may only be released upon written authorization from Legal.</p><h4>5.5 Backup and Archive Media Retention</h4><p>Backup media are subject to the same retention schedules as primary data. Backup systems must support granular recovery to avoid unnecessary restoration of expired data. Long-term archival systems shall employ Write-Once-Read-Many (WORM) or functionally equivalent technology to prevent tampering or premature deletion.</p><h4>5.6 Logging and Auditability</h4><p>All data disposal activities, whether automated or manual, shall be logged. Logs must include, at minimum: data identifier, disposal method, date/time, and initiating user or system. These logs shall be retained for a period exceeding the longest retention period of any corporate data to facilitate forensic investigation and compliance audits.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>Retention periods shall be established in compliance with all applicable laws and regulations, including but not limited to: GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, HIPAA, SOX, FINRA, and FTC rules. The Legal Department is responsible for identifying and communicating changes in legal retention obligations. This policy directly supports compliance with ISO 27001 Annex A controls 8.2.3 (Handling of assets), 8.3.1 (Management of removable media), and A.12.3 (Backup), and SOC 2 Criteria CC6.1 (Logical Access) and CC6.8 (System Operations).</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Data Owner:</strong> Define data classification and approved retention period; authorize exceptions and access.<br/><strong>Legal & Compliance Department:</strong> Define legal retention obligations; issue and manage Legal Holds; approve the Retention Schedule.<br/><strong>Information Technology (Custodian):</strong> Implement and maintain technical controls for retention and disposal; execute secure disposal; manage Legal Hold technical suspension.<br/><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Adhere to retention and disposal procedures for data under their control; promptly notify Legal of potential litigation.<br/><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Overall policy enforcement, audit coordination, and risk management oversight.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment, and may face civil or criminal penalties. Deliberate circumvention of retention controls or unauthorized disposal of data under Legal Hold is considered a severe security incident. Third-party vendors in violation of contractual data retention obligations shall be subject to penalties per the agreement and possible termination of the business relationship.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and Legal Department at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant changes in the legal landscape, technology infrastructure, or business operations. Revisions must be approved by the Information Security Steering Committee. The latest approved version is the only version considered authoritative.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-19 06:15:54.931012', '"[\"ISO 27001 / SOC 2\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-20 05:58:26.735', NULL),
	(38, '492314', 'API Security Policy', '<p>This API Security Policy establishes the mandatory security controls and governance framework for the design, development, deployment, and operation of all Application Programming Interfaces (APIs) at [Company Name]. The purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of API-accessible data and services, mitigate risks associated with API-based attacks, and ensure compliance with regulatory obligations and contractual commitments.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all APIs owned, managed, or operated by [Company Name], including public-facing, partner, and internal APIs. It encompasses all stages of the API lifecycle, from design and development through testing, deployment, production operation, and eventual decommissioning. All personnel, contractors, and third-party service providers involved in the creation, management, or consumption of [Company Name] APIs are bound by this policy.</p>

<p><strong>API (Application Programming Interface):</strong> A set of defined rules and specifications that allows software applications to communicate with each other.<br/><strong>API Gateway:</strong> A management tool that sits between a client and a collection of backend services, acting as a reverse proxy to accept all API calls.<br/><strong>OAuth 2.0:</strong> An authorization framework that enables applications to obtain limited access to user accounts on an HTTP service.<br/><strong>JSON Web Token (JWT):</strong> An open standard (RFC 7519) that defines a compact and self-contained way for securely transmitting information between parties as a JSON object.<br/><strong>Rate Limiting:</strong> A technique for controlling the rate of traffic sent or received by a network interface controller.<br/><strong>API Key:</strong> A unique identifier used to authenticate a calling program to an API.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all APIs shall be developed and operated with security as a foundational principle. All API interactions must be authenticated, authorized, and logged. The exposure of sensitive data via APIs is strictly prohibited unless protected by robust encryption and access controls. Non-compliance with this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contracts, and legal prosecution.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Authentication and Authorization</strong><br/>5.1.1 All API endpoints, without exception, must implement strong authentication. The use of basic authentication over unencrypted channels is forbidden.<br/>5.1.2 For machine-to-machine (M2M) communication, OAuth 2.0 Client Credentials grant or mutual TLS (mTLS) with certificate-based authentication shall be used.<br/>5.1.3 For user-authorized access, the OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code grant with Proof Key for Code Exchange (PKCE) must be implemented.<br/>5.1.4 All authorization decisions must be enforced at the API endpoint level based on the principle of least privilege, using scopes, roles, or attribute-based access control (ABAC).<br/><br/><strong>5.2 Transport Security</strong><br/>5.2.1 All API communications must be encrypted in transit using TLS 1.2 or higher. TLS 1.0 and 1.1 are strictly prohibited.<br/>5.2.2 TLS certificates must be obtained from a trusted Certificate Authority (CA) and shall be configured to use strong cipher suites.<br/>5.2.3 Certificate validity must be monitored and renewed prior to expiration.<br/><br/><strong>5.3 Input Validation and Output Encoding</strong><br/>5.3.1 All API inputs (headers, parameters, payloads) must be validated against a strict schema (e.g., JSON Schema, OpenAPI Schema) upon receipt.<br/>5.3.2 Positive (allow-list) validation is required for all input fields where feasible.<br/>5.3.3 All data returned by APIs must be properly encoded to mitigate injection and cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks.<br/><br/><strong>5.4 API Management and Gateway Controls</strong><br/>5.4.1 All external-facing APIs must be routed through a dedicated, security-hardened API Gateway.<br/>5.4.2 The API Gateway must enforce global policies including, but not limited to: authentication, rate limiting, request/response logging, and threat protection (e.g., SQLi, XSS filters).<br/>5.4.3 API rate limiting must be implemented per client/API key to prevent denial-of-service and brute-force attacks.<br/><br/><strong>5.5 Secrets Management</strong><br/>5.5.1 API keys, client secrets, and cryptographic keys must never be hard-coded in source code, configuration files, or client-side applications.<br/>5.5.2 All secrets must be stored, managed, and retrieved dynamically from a centralized, secure secrets management service (e.g., HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager).<br/>5.5.3 Secrets must be rotated immediately upon suspicion of compromise and according to a defined schedule (e.g., annually for keys, quarterly for client secrets).<br/><br/><strong>5.6 Logging and Monitoring</strong><br/>5.6.1 All API transactions must generate detailed audit logs. Logs must include, at minimum: timestamp, source IP, authenticated principal (user/service), API endpoint, HTTP method, request/response size, and HTTP status code.<br/>5.6.2 All authentication successes and failures, authorization failures, and input validation failures must be logged as security events.<br/>5.6.3 API logs must be aggregated into a centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system and retained for a minimum of 90 days to support incident investigation and compliance audits.<br/>5.6.4 Real-time monitoring for anomalous activity (e.g., spike in 4xx/5xx errors, abnormal data egress) must be configured with defined alerting thresholds.</p>

<p>API implementations must adhere to all applicable laws, regulations, and industry standards, including but not limited to: the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA), Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS) where applicable, and the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) where applicable. This policy is designed to align with the control objectives of SOC 2 (Security, Availability, Confidentiality), the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, Recover), and ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A controls, specifically A.5.36, A.8.24, A.8.25, and A.9.4.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the approval and enforcement of this policy.<br/><strong>API Development Teams:</strong> Responsible for implementing the security controls defined in Section 5 during the design, coding, and testing phases.<br/><strong>Platform/Cloud Engineering:</strong> Responsible for the secure configuration, maintenance, and monitoring of the API Gateway and underlying infrastructure.<br/><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for defining security standards, conducting design reviews and penetration tests on APIs, monitoring for threats, and investigating incidents.<br/><strong>Product Owners/Business Units:</strong> Responsible for ensuring API functionality requests include security requirements and for approving data classification for API payloads.</p>

<p>Any API found to be in violation of this policy will be subject to immediate remediation. The Information Security Team is authorized to disconnect or block access to non-compliant APIs from the production network until all security deficiencies are corrected. Violations by employees or contractors will be handled according to the [Company Name] Human Resources policies and may constitute grounds for disciplinary action. Violations by third-party providers will be escalated per the terms of the relevant service agreement.</p>

<p>This API Security Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Team at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in the threat landscape, or updates to relevant compliance frameworks. All revisions require formal approval by the CISO. The latest approved version of this policy must be made accessible to all relevant personnel.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This API Security Policy establishes the mandatory security controls and governance framework for the design, development, deployment, and operation of all Application Programming Interfaces (APIs) at [Company Name]. The purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of API-accessible data and services, mitigate risks associated with API-based attacks, and ensure compliance with regulatory obligations and contractual commitments.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all APIs owned, managed, or operated by [Company Name], including public-facing, partner, and internal APIs. It encompasses all stages of the API lifecycle, from design and development through testing, deployment, production operation, and eventual decommissioning. All personnel, contractors, and third-party service providers involved in the creation, management, or consumption of [Company Name] APIs are bound by this policy.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>API (Application Programming Interface):</strong> A set of defined rules and specifications that allows software applications to communicate with each other.<br/><strong>API Gateway:</strong> A management tool that sits between a client and a collection of backend services, acting as a reverse proxy to accept all API calls.<br/><strong>OAuth 2.0:</strong> An authorization framework that enables applications to obtain limited access to user accounts on an HTTP service.<br/><strong>JSON Web Token (JWT):</strong> An open standard (RFC 7519) that defines a compact and self-contained way for securely transmitting information between parties as a JSON object.<br/><strong>Rate Limiting:</strong> A technique for controlling the rate of traffic sent or received by a network interface controller.<br/><strong>API Key:</strong> A unique identifier used to authenticate a calling program to an API.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates that all APIs shall be developed and operated with security as a foundational principle. All API interactions must be authenticated, authorized, and logged. The exposure of sensitive data via APIs is strictly prohibited unless protected by robust encryption and access controls. Non-compliance with this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contracts, and legal prosecution.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>5.1 Authentication and Authorization</strong><br/>5.1.1 All API endpoints, without exception, must implement strong authentication. The use of basic authentication over unencrypted channels is forbidden.<br/>5.1.2 For machine-to-machine (M2M) communication, OAuth 2.0 Client Credentials grant or mutual TLS (mTLS) with certificate-based authentication shall be used.<br/>5.1.3 For user-authorized access, the OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code grant with Proof Key for Code Exchange (PKCE) must be implemented.<br/>5.1.4 All authorization decisions must be enforced at the API endpoint level based on the principle of least privilege, using scopes, roles, or attribute-based access control (ABAC).<br/><br/><strong>5.2 Transport Security</strong><br/>5.2.1 All API communications must be encrypted in transit using TLS 1.2 or higher. TLS 1.0 and 1.1 are strictly prohibited.<br/>5.2.2 TLS certificates must be obtained from a trusted Certificate Authority (CA) and shall be configured to use strong cipher suites.<br/>5.2.3 Certificate validity must be monitored and renewed prior to expiration.<br/><br/><strong>5.3 Input Validation and Output Encoding</strong><br/>5.3.1 All API inputs (headers, parameters, payloads) must be validated against a strict schema (e.g., JSON Schema, OpenAPI Schema) upon receipt.<br/>5.3.2 Positive (allow-list) validation is required for all input fields where feasible.<br/>5.3.3 All data returned by APIs must be properly encoded to mitigate injection and cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks.<br/><br/><strong>5.4 API Management and Gateway Controls</strong><br/>5.4.1 All external-facing APIs must be routed through a dedicated, security-hardened API Gateway.<br/>5.4.2 The API Gateway must enforce global policies including, but not limited to: authentication, rate limiting, request/response logging, and threat protection (e.g., SQLi, XSS filters).<br/>5.4.3 API rate limiting must be implemented per client/API key to prevent denial-of-service and brute-force attacks.<br/><br/><strong>5.5 Secrets Management</strong><br/>5.5.1 API keys, client secrets, and cryptographic keys must never be hard-coded in source code, configuration files, or client-side applications.<br/>5.5.2 All secrets must be stored, managed, and retrieved dynamically from a centralized, secure secrets management service (e.g., HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager).<br/>5.5.3 Secrets must be rotated immediately upon suspicion of compromise and according to a defined schedule (e.g., annually for keys, quarterly for client secrets).<br/><br/><strong>5.6 Logging and Monitoring</strong><br/>5.6.1 All API transactions must generate detailed audit logs. Logs must include, at minimum: timestamp, source IP, authenticated principal (user/service), API endpoint, HTTP method, request/response size, and HTTP status code.<br/>5.6.2 All authentication successes and failures, authorization failures, and input validation failures must be logged as security events.<br/>5.6.3 API logs must be aggregated into a centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system and retained for a minimum of 90 days to support incident investigation and compliance audits.<br/>5.6.4 Real-time monitoring for anomalous activity (e.g., spike in 4xx/5xx errors, abnormal data egress) must be configured with defined alerting thresholds.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>API implementations must adhere to all applicable laws, regulations, and industry standards, including but not limited to: the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA), Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS) where applicable, and the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) where applicable. This policy is designed to align with the control objectives of SOC 2 (Security, Availability, Confidentiality), the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, Recover), and ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A controls, specifically A.5.36, A.8.24, A.8.25, and A.9.4.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the approval and enforcement of this policy.<br/><strong>API Development Teams:</strong> Responsible for implementing the security controls defined in Section 5 during the design, coding, and testing phases.<br/><strong>Platform/Cloud Engineering:</strong> Responsible for the secure configuration, maintenance, and monitoring of the API Gateway and underlying infrastructure.<br/><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for defining security standards, conducting design reviews and penetration tests on APIs, monitoring for threats, and investigating incidents.<br/><strong>Product Owners/Business Units:</strong> Responsible for ensuring API functionality requests include security requirements and for approving data classification for API payloads.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any API found to be in violation of this policy will be subject to immediate remediation. The Information Security Team is authorized to disconnect or block access to non-compliant APIs from the production network until all security deficiencies are corrected. Violations by employees or contractors will be handled according to the [Company Name] Human Resources policies and may constitute grounds for disciplinary action. Violations by third-party providers will be escalated per the terms of the relevant service agreement.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This API Security Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Team at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in the threat landscape, or updates to relevant compliance frameworks. All revisions require formal approval by the CISO. The latest approved version of this policy must be made accessible to all relevant personnel.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-20 21:17:23.236925', '"[\"SOC 2\",\"NIST CSF\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-20 05:59:05.029', NULL),
	(10, '5', 'Vendor Risk Management Policy', '<p>This Vendor Risk Management Policy establishes the mandatory framework for [Company Name] to identify, assess, monitor, and mitigate risks associated with third-party vendors that have access to, process, store, or transmit [Company Name] information assets or provide critical services. The policy ensures that vendor relationships are managed in a manner consistent with [Company Name]''s security, compliance, and business continuity objectives, and in alignment with ISO 27001 and SOC 2 trust service criteria.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, and subsidiaries involved in the procurement, management, or oversight of third-party vendors ("Vendors"). It encompasses all Vendor relationships where the Vendor has, or could have, access to [Company Name]''s information systems, non-public data (including Confidential, Proprietary, and Personal Data), or provides a service integral to [Company Name]''s operations. This includes, but is not limited to, cloud service providers (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS), IT outsourcing, managed service providers, and business process outsourcing.</p>

<p><strong>Vendor:</strong> Any external entity providing goods, services, or technology to [Company Name].<br/><strong>Critical Vendor:</strong> A Vendor whose failure or security breach would cause significant operational disruption, financial loss, legal/regulatory impact, or reputational damage to [Company Name].<br/><strong>Due Diligence:</strong> The comprehensive assessment process conducted prior to engaging a Vendor to evaluate their security, financial, and operational controls.<br/><strong>Risk Assessment:</strong> The formal analysis to determine the level of risk a Vendor relationship poses, based on data sensitivity, service criticality, and control adequacy.<br/><strong>Service Level Agreement (SLA):</strong> A contract defining the specific performance and availability metrics a Vendor must meet.<br/><strong>Right-to-Audit Clause:</strong> A contractual provision granting [Company Name] the right to assess the Vendor''s controls, either directly or via independent reports (e.g., SOC 2, ISO 27001).</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all Vendor relationships shall be governed by a formal risk management lifecycle. No department shall engage a Vendor that will have access to [Company Name] information assets without prior completion of a risk-based due diligence and approval process. Vendor contracts must incorporate explicit security, confidentiality, and compliance obligations. Ongoing monitoring and periodic re-assessment of Vendors, especially Critical Vendors, are strictly required.</p>

<h4>5.1 Vendor Classification and Risk Tiering</h4><p>All prospective Vendors shall be classified into risk tiers (e.g., High, Medium, Low) based on a standardized assessment of:<br/>• The sensitivity and classification of data to be accessed (e.g., PII, PHI, intellectual property).<br/>• The criticality of the service to business operations.<br/>• The level of integration with [Company Name]''s internal systems and networks.<br/>The risk tier shall dictate the rigor of the subsequent due diligence and ongoing monitoring requirements.</p><h4>5.2 Pre-Engagement Due Diligence</h4><p>Prior to contract signing, the responsible business unit, in conjunction with Information Security and Legal, must complete a Vendor Security Questionnaire (VSQ). For High and Medium-risk Vendors, this must be supplemented by one or more of the following:<br/>• Review of a current, Type II SOC 2 report (or equivalent) with a clean opinion.<br/>• Review of a valid ISO 27001 certification certificate and scope statement.<br/>• Completion of a third-party security assessment.<br/>Evidence of due diligence shall be formally documented and retained.</p><h4>5.3 Contractual Security Requirements</h4><p>All Vendor contracts, including amendments and statements of work, must include, at a minimum:<br/>• Explicit data protection and confidentiality clauses.<br/>• A Right-to-Audit clause permitting [Company Name] to request a SOC 2 report or conduct a security assessment.<br/>• Notification requirements for security incidents, including a defined timeframe (e.g., within 24 hours).<br/>• Data breach response and liability provisions.<br/>• Data return and destruction obligations upon contract termination.<br/>• Subcontractor management obligations, requiring Vendor to flow down equivalent security requirements.</p><h4>5.4 Continuous Monitoring</h4><p>High-risk (Critical) Vendors shall be subject to annual re-assessment. Monitoring activities shall include:<br/>• Annual review of the Vendor''s most recent SOC 2 report or equivalent.<br/>• Verification of ongoing compliance with contractual SLAs and security requirements.<br/>• Tracking of Vendor-related security incidents and their resolution.<br/>• Re-assessment of the Vendor''s risk tier following significant changes to the service or [Company Name]''s use of it.</p><h4>5.5 Termination and Offboarding</h4><p>Upon termination of a Vendor relationship, the responsible business unit must coordinate with IT and Information Security to:<br/>• Ensure all [Company Name] data is securely returned or verifiably destroyed, with evidence provided.<br/>• Revoke all system, network, and physical access privileges granted to the Vendor.<br/>• Archive contractual and due diligence documentation in accordance with records retention policy.</p>

<p>Vendor management activities shall be conducted to ensure compliance with all applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, HIPAA (if applicable), and PCI-DSS. The policy is designed to satisfy control objectives within ISO 27001:2022 Annex A (e.g., A.15.1 & A.15.2 - Supplier Relationships) and relevant SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity). Evidence of compliance with this policy, including completed assessments, contracts, and monitoring reports, shall be maintained for audit purposes.</p>

<p><strong>Business Unit/Procurement:</strong> Responsible for initial Vendor identification, completing the business justification, and initiating the due diligence process.<br/><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Responsible for defining the risk assessment methodology, reviewing security due diligence artifacts, classifying Vendor risk, and approving security controls.<br/><strong>Legal/Compliance Department:</strong> Responsible for ensuring contractual terms meet policy and regulatory requirements.<br/><strong>Vendor Risk Management Committee (or designated authority):</strong> Responsible for final approval of High-risk Vendor engagements and overseeing the policy''s implementation.<br/><strong>All Employees:</strong> Prohibited from bypassing the formal Vendor onboarding process or sharing sensitive data with unauthorized Vendors.</p>

<p>Any employee or contractor found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal action. Engaging a Vendor without completing the mandated due diligence and approval process is strictly prohibited. The Information Security Department is authorized to suspend or terminate any Vendor connection that poses an immediate and severe risk to [Company Name]''s information assets.</p>

<p>This Vendor Risk Management Policy shall be reviewed at least annually by the Information Security Department and the Vendor Risk Management Committee. Revisions shall be made in response to changes in the threat landscape, regulatory environment, business operations, or audit findings. All revisions require formal approval from the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) or designated executive.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Vendor Risk Management Policy establishes the mandatory framework for [Company Name] to identify, assess, monitor, and mitigate risks associated with third-party vendors that have access to, process, store, or transmit [Company Name] information assets or provide critical services. The policy ensures that vendor relationships are managed in a manner consistent with [Company Name]''s security, compliance, and business continuity objectives, and in alignment with ISO 27001 and SOC 2 trust service criteria.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, and subsidiaries involved in the procurement, management, or oversight of third-party vendors (\\\"Vendors\\\"). It encompasses all Vendor relationships where the Vendor has, or could have, access to [Company Name]''s information systems, non-public data (including Confidential, Proprietary, and Personal Data), or provides a service integral to [Company Name]''s operations. This includes, but is not limited to, cloud service providers (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS), IT outsourcing, managed service providers, and business process outsourcing.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Vendor:</strong> Any external entity providing goods, services, or technology to [Company Name].<br/><strong>Critical Vendor:</strong> A Vendor whose failure or security breach would cause significant operational disruption, financial loss, legal/regulatory impact, or reputational damage to [Company Name].<br/><strong>Due Diligence:</strong> The comprehensive assessment process conducted prior to engaging a Vendor to evaluate their security, financial, and operational controls.<br/><strong>Risk Assessment:</strong> The formal analysis to determine the level of risk a Vendor relationship poses, based on data sensitivity, service criticality, and control adequacy.<br/><strong>Service Level Agreement (SLA):</strong> A contract defining the specific performance and availability metrics a Vendor must meet.<br/><strong>Right-to-Audit Clause:</strong> A contractual provision granting [Company Name] the right to assess the Vendor''s controls, either directly or via independent reports (e.g., SOC 2, ISO 27001).</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates that all Vendor relationships shall be governed by a formal risk management lifecycle. No department shall engage a Vendor that will have access to [Company Name] information assets without prior completion of a risk-based due diligence and approval process. Vendor contracts must incorporate explicit security, confidentiality, and compliance obligations. Ongoing monitoring and periodic re-assessment of Vendors, especially Critical Vendors, are strictly required.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1 Vendor Classification and Risk Tiering</h4><p>All prospective Vendors shall be classified into risk tiers (e.g., High, Medium, Low) based on a standardized assessment of:<br/>• The sensitivity and classification of data to be accessed (e.g., PII, PHI, intellectual property).<br/>• The criticality of the service to business operations.<br/>• The level of integration with [Company Name]''s internal systems and networks.<br/>The risk tier shall dictate the rigor of the subsequent due diligence and ongoing monitoring requirements.</p><h4>5.2 Pre-Engagement Due Diligence</h4><p>Prior to contract signing, the responsible business unit, in conjunction with Information Security and Legal, must complete a Vendor Security Questionnaire (VSQ). For High and Medium-risk Vendors, this must be supplemented by one or more of the following:<br/>• Review of a current, Type II SOC 2 report (or equivalent) with a clean opinion.<br/>• Review of a valid ISO 27001 certification certificate and scope statement.<br/>• Completion of a third-party security assessment.<br/>Evidence of due diligence shall be formally documented and retained.</p><h4>5.3 Contractual Security Requirements</h4><p>All Vendor contracts, including amendments and statements of work, must include, at a minimum:<br/>• Explicit data protection and confidentiality clauses.<br/>• A Right-to-Audit clause permitting [Company Name] to request a SOC 2 report or conduct a security assessment.<br/>• Notification requirements for security incidents, including a defined timeframe (e.g., within 24 hours).<br/>• Data breach response and liability provisions.<br/>• Data return and destruction obligations upon contract termination.<br/>• Subcontractor management obligations, requiring Vendor to flow down equivalent security requirements.</p><h4>5.4 Continuous Monitoring</h4><p>High-risk (Critical) Vendors shall be subject to annual re-assessment. Monitoring activities shall include:<br/>• Annual review of the Vendor''s most recent SOC 2 report or equivalent.<br/>• Verification of ongoing compliance with contractual SLAs and security requirements.<br/>• Tracking of Vendor-related security incidents and their resolution.<br/>• Re-assessment of the Vendor''s risk tier following significant changes to the service or [Company Name]''s use of it.</p><h4>5.5 Termination and Offboarding</h4><p>Upon termination of a Vendor relationship, the responsible business unit must coordinate with IT and Information Security to:<br/>• Ensure all [Company Name] data is securely returned or verifiably destroyed, with evidence provided.<br/>• Revoke all system, network, and physical access privileges granted to the Vendor.<br/>• Archive contractual and due diligence documentation in accordance with records retention policy.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>Vendor management activities shall be conducted to ensure compliance with all applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, HIPAA (if applicable), and PCI-DSS. The policy is designed to satisfy control objectives within ISO 27001:2022 Annex A (e.g., A.15.1 & A.15.2 - Supplier Relationships) and relevant SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity). Evidence of compliance with this policy, including completed assessments, contracts, and monitoring reports, shall be maintained for audit purposes.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Business Unit/Procurement:</strong> Responsible for initial Vendor identification, completing the business justification, and initiating the due diligence process.<br/><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Responsible for defining the risk assessment methodology, reviewing security due diligence artifacts, classifying Vendor risk, and approving security controls.<br/><strong>Legal/Compliance Department:</strong> Responsible for ensuring contractual terms meet policy and regulatory requirements.<br/><strong>Vendor Risk Management Committee (or designated authority):</strong> Responsible for final approval of High-risk Vendor engagements and overseeing the policy''s implementation.<br/><strong>All Employees:</strong> Prohibited from bypassing the formal Vendor onboarding process or sharing sensitive data with unauthorized Vendors.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any employee or contractor found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal action. Engaging a Vendor without completing the mandated due diligence and approval process is strictly prohibited. The Information Security Department is authorized to suspend or terminate any Vendor connection that poses an immediate and severe risk to [Company Name]''s information assets.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This Vendor Risk Management Policy shall be reviewed at least annually by the Information Security Department and the Vendor Risk Management Committee. Revisions shall be made in response to changes in the threat landscape, regulatory environment, business operations, or audit findings. All revisions require formal approval from the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) or designated executive.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-19 06:15:55.223023', '"[\"ISO 27001 / SOC 2\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-20 05:59:40.731', NULL),
	(48, '924745', 'Visitor Access Policy', '<h1>Visitor Access Policy</h1>
<h2>1. Purpose</h2>
<p>The purpose of this Visitor Access Policy is to establish formal standards, procedures, and controls governing the authorization, monitoring, and management of all non-employee individuals granted physical or logical access to [Company Name] facilities, information systems, or sensitive data. This policy ensures that all visitor access aligns with the organization&#39;s security, safety, and compliance objectives.</p>
<h2>2. Scope</h2>
<p>This policy applies universally to all employees, contractors, interns, vendors, and third-party representatives of [Company Name]. It further governs all visitors, including but not limited to clients, guests, service providers, and temporary personnel, who are granted access to company premises, networks, systems, or data resources. Compliance is mandatory for all individuals interacting with [Company Name] assets.</p>
<h2>3. Policy Statement</h2>
<p>[Company Name] is committed to maintaining the security, integrity, and confidentiality of its physical assets, information systems, and proprietary data. All visitor access must be pre-authorized, properly logged, actively monitored, and strictly limited to the minimum necessary for conducting approved business. Unauthorized access is expressly prohibited, and all visitors must adhere to company security protocols during their presence.</p>
<h2>4. Visitor Access Procedures</h2>
<h3>4.1 Pre-Arrival Authorization</h3>
<p>All visitors must receive advance authorization from an authorized employee sponsor. The sponsor must submit a visitor access request through the designated system, specifying the visitor&#39;s name, company, purpose, duration, and required access areas.</p>
<h3>4.2 Registration and Identification</h3>
<p>Upon arrival, all visitors must register at the designated reception or security point. They must present valid government-issued photo identification, which will be verified and logged. Visitors will be issued a temporary badge that must be visibly worn at all times while on premises.</p>
<h3>4.3 Escort Requirements</h3>
<p>Unless explicitly pre-authorized for unescorted access, visitors must be accompanied by their sponsor or a designated employee at all times. The escort is responsible for the visitor&#39;s conduct and adherence to all company policies.</p>
<h3>4.4 Access Limitations</h3>
<p>Visitor access is restricted to approved areas only. Access to sensitive zones, data centers, server rooms, and areas containing confidential information is prohibited unless specifically authorized in writing by the relevant department head.</p>
<h3>4.5 System and Network Access</h3>
<p>Logical access to [Company Name] networks, systems, or data by visitors is generally prohibited. Exceptions require formal approval from the Information Security Officer and must utilize segregated guest networks with appropriate security controls.</p>
<h3>4.6 Departure Procedures</h3>
<p>Upon departure, visitors must return all issued access badges, equipment, or materials to the reception or security point. The sponsor must confirm the visitor&#39;s departure and the return of all assets.</p>
<h2>5. Roles and Responsibilities</h2>
<h3>5.1 Employees/Sponsors</h3>
<p>Initiate and authorize visitor requests; escort visitors as required; ensure visitor compliance with policies; and confirm proper departure.</p>
<h3>5.2 Security/Reception Personnel</h3>
<p>Verify visitor identity; manage the registration and badging process; maintain access logs; and monitor visitor movement in accordance with this policy.</p>
<h3>5.3 Information Security Team</h3>
<p>Define technical controls for logical access; audit compliance; and investigate any policy violations related to system or data access.</p>
<h3>5.4 All Visitors</h3>
<p>Comply with all stated procedures; wear identification visibly; respect access restrictions; and report any security concerns immediately.</p>
<h2>6. Enforcement</h2>
<p>Violations of this policy by employees, sponsors, or visitors will result in appropriate disciplinary action, which may include revocation of access, termination of contracts, legal action, or reporting to relevant authorities. [Company Name] will investigate all suspected breaches and apply corrective measures to prevent recurrence.</p>
<h2>7. Policy Review and Maintenance</h2>
<p>This policy shall be reviewed annually by the Security and Compliance Committee to ensure its continued relevance, effectiveness, and alignment with industry standards and regulatory requirements. Updates will be communicated to all stakeholders upon approval.</p>
', '"[\"Purpose\",\"Scope\",\"Terms and Definitions\",\"Policy Requirements/Statements\",\"Sign-in\",\"Escorts\",\"Badging\",\"Enforcement/Violations\",\"Review/Approval\"]"', '2025-12-20 21:17:24.302394', '"[\"ISO 27001\",\"SOC 2\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-11 08:47:22.552259', NULL),
	(11, '6', 'Business Continuity Policy', '<p>This Business Continuity Policy establishes the mandatory framework for [Company Name] to ensure the resilience, continuity, and timely recovery of critical business operations, information assets, and supporting infrastructure in the event of a disruptive incident. The policy mandates the development, implementation, maintenance, and testing of a Business Continuity Management System (BCMS) to protect against, reduce the likelihood of, and ensure recovery from such incidents, thereby safeguarding stakeholder interests and organizational reputation.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all personnel (employees, contractors, and third-party agents), business processes, information systems, data, facilities, and technologies owned, operated, or under the contractual control of [Company Name] that are deemed critical to organizational operations. It encompasses all locations from which [Company Name] conducts business.</p>

<p><strong>Business Continuity Management System (BCMS):</strong> The holistic management process that identifies potential threats to the organization and provides a framework for building resilience and the capability for an effective response.<br><br><strong>Business Impact Analysis (BIA):</strong> A systematic process to determine and evaluate the potential effects of an interruption to critical business operations as a result of a disaster, accident, or emergency.<br><br><strong>Recovery Time Objective (RTO):</strong> The maximum acceptable length of time that a business process or system can be unavailable following a disruptive event.<br><br><strong>Recovery Point Objective (RPO):</strong> The maximum acceptable amount of data loss measured in time, defining the point to which information must be restored.<br><br><strong>Disruptive Incident:</strong> Any event, whether anticipated or unanticipated, that causes an interruption to the normal operation of a critical business process or system.</p>

<p>[Company Name] is committed to maintaining operational resilience through a formally documented and regularly tested Business Continuity Management System. The organization shall proactively identify risks, establish recovery objectives, and implement robust continuity and recovery strategies to ensure the availability of critical services and the protection of information assets in alignment with business priorities, contractual obligations, and regulatory requirements.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Governance and Program Management</strong><br>A formal Business Continuity Management System (BCMS) shall be established, documented, and maintained. A designated Business Continuity Manager shall be appointed with the authority to oversee the BCMS lifecycle.</p><p><strong>5.2 Business Impact Analysis (BIA) and Risk Assessment</strong><br>A documented BIA shall be conducted at least annually, or upon significant organizational change, to identify and prioritize critical business processes, their interdependencies, and supporting resources. The BIA must define Recovery Time Objectives (RTO) and Recovery Point Objectives (RPO) for each critical process. A complementary risk assessment shall identify threats to these processes.</p><p><strong>5.3 Business Continuity Strategy and Plans</strong><br>Based on the BIA, documented recovery strategies shall be developed and approved to meet the defined RTOs and RPOs. This includes, but is not limited to, strategies for data backup, system redundancy, alternate work locations, and third-party service continuity. Detailed, actionable Business Continuity Plans (BCPs) and Disaster Recovery Plans (DRPs) shall be developed for all critical functions.</p><p><strong>5.4 Backup and Recovery Controls</strong><br>All data and system configurations deemed critical by the BIA shall be backed up according to their RPO. Backups must be encrypted, stored in a geographically separate location from the primary data center, and tested for integrity and recoverability. The use of immutable or write-once-read-many (WORM) storage for critical backups is mandated where feasible to protect against ransomware or malicious deletion.</p><p><strong>5.5 Testing and Exercises</strong><br>BCPs and DRPs shall be tested through structured exercises at least annually. Testing scenarios must be comprehensive, ranging from table-top walkthroughs to full-scale functional recovery tests. All test results, including successes and failures, shall be documented, reviewed by management, and used to update plans.</p><p><strong>5.6 Training and Awareness</strong><br>All personnel with defined roles in the BCP/DRP shall receive role-specific training upon plan assignment and following any significant plan update. General business continuity awareness training shall be provided to all employees annually.</p><p><strong>5.7 Communication and Notification</strong><br>A documented crisis communication plan shall be maintained, specifying internal and external notification procedures, communication channels, and spokespersons during a disruptive incident. Contact lists for key personnel, emergency services, and critical third parties must be reviewed and updated quarterly.</p><p><strong>5.8 Third-Party and Supply Chain Continuity</strong><br>Critical third-party service providers and suppliers shall be contractually obligated to maintain business continuity plans that meet or exceed [Company Name]''s RTO and RPO requirements for the services provided. Their compliance and test results shall be subject to review as part of vendor risk management.</p><p><strong>5.9 Plan Maintenance and Documentation</strong><br>All BCMS documentation, including the BIA, BCPs, DRPs, test reports, and training records, shall be maintained under version control. Plans shall be formally reviewed and updated at least annually, or immediately following a test, exercise, actual incident, or significant organizational change.</p>

<p>The BCMS shall be designed and operated to ensure compliance with all applicable legal, statutory, regulatory, and contractual obligations related to operational resilience and data availability. This policy aligns with the requirements of ISO 27001 (Annex A.17) and SOC 2 (Availability and Security Criteria). Evidence of BIA, plan testing, and management review shall be maintained for audit purposes.</p>

<p><strong>Executive Management / Board:</strong> Ultimate responsibility for approving this policy, providing resources for the BCMS, and ensuring organizational commitment.<br><br><strong>Business Continuity Manager:</strong> Responsible for the day-to-day management, coordination, and maintenance of the BCMS, including overseeing the BIA, plan development, testing, and reporting.<br><br><strong>Process / System Owners:</strong> Responsible for participating in the BIA, providing input for recovery strategies, and executing specific recovery procedures for their owned processes/systems during an incident.<br><br><strong>IT Department:</strong> Responsible for implementing and maintaining the technical recovery controls, including backup systems, redundant infrastructure, and the execution of the Disaster Recovery Plan.<br><br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for familiarizing themselves with general continuity procedures, participating in training and exercises, and following management directives during a disruptive incident.</p>

<p>Any violation of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and may lead to legal or financial penalties for the individual and [Company Name]. Failure to participate in required BCP/DRP testing or training is considered a violation of this policy.</p>

<p>This Business Continuity Policy shall be reviewed by the Business Continuity Manager and relevant stakeholders at least annually. Revisions must be approved by Executive Management. The policy version history shall be maintained to track all changes.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Business Continuity Policy establishes the mandatory framework for [Company Name] to ensure the resilience, continuity, and timely recovery of critical business operations, information assets, and supporting infrastructure in the event of a disruptive incident. The policy mandates the development, implementation, maintenance, and testing of a Business Continuity Management System (BCMS) to protect against, reduce the likelihood of, and ensure recovery from such incidents, thereby safeguarding stakeholder interests and organizational reputation.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all personnel (employees, contractors, and third-party agents), business processes, information systems, data, facilities, and technologies owned, operated, or under the contractual control of [Company Name] that are deemed critical to organizational operations. It encompasses all locations from which [Company Name] conducts business.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Business Continuity Management System (BCMS):</strong> The holistic management process that identifies potential threats to the organization and provides a framework for building resilience and the capability for an effective response.<br><br><strong>Business Impact Analysis (BIA):</strong> A systematic process to determine and evaluate the potential effects of an interruption to critical business operations as a result of a disaster, accident, or emergency.<br><br><strong>Recovery Time Objective (RTO):</strong> The maximum acceptable length of time that a business process or system can be unavailable following a disruptive event.<br><br><strong>Recovery Point Objective (RPO):</strong> The maximum acceptable amount of data loss measured in time, defining the point to which information must be restored.<br><br><strong>Disruptive Incident:</strong> Any event, whether anticipated or unanticipated, that causes an interruption to the normal operation of a critical business process or system.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] is committed to maintaining operational resilience through a formally documented and regularly tested Business Continuity Management System. The organization shall proactively identify risks, establish recovery objectives, and implement robust continuity and recovery strategies to ensure the availability of critical services and the protection of information assets in alignment with business priorities, contractual obligations, and regulatory requirements.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>5.1 Governance and Program Management</strong><br>A formal Business Continuity Management System (BCMS) shall be established, documented, and maintained. A designated Business Continuity Manager shall be appointed with the authority to oversee the BCMS lifecycle.</p><p><strong>5.2 Business Impact Analysis (BIA) and Risk Assessment</strong><br>A documented BIA shall be conducted at least annually, or upon significant organizational change, to identify and prioritize critical business processes, their interdependencies, and supporting resources. The BIA must define Recovery Time Objectives (RTO) and Recovery Point Objectives (RPO) for each critical process. A complementary risk assessment shall identify threats to these processes.</p><p><strong>5.3 Business Continuity Strategy and Plans</strong><br>Based on the BIA, documented recovery strategies shall be developed and approved to meet the defined RTOs and RPOs. This includes, but is not limited to, strategies for data backup, system redundancy, alternate work locations, and third-party service continuity. Detailed, actionable Business Continuity Plans (BCPs) and Disaster Recovery Plans (DRPs) shall be developed for all critical functions.</p><p><strong>5.4 Backup and Recovery Controls</strong><br>All data and system configurations deemed critical by the BIA shall be backed up according to their RPO. Backups must be encrypted, stored in a geographically separate location from the primary data center, and tested for integrity and recoverability. The use of immutable or write-once-read-many (WORM) storage for critical backups is mandated where feasible to protect against ransomware or malicious deletion.</p><p><strong>5.5 Testing and Exercises</strong><br>BCPs and DRPs shall be tested through structured exercises at least annually. Testing scenarios must be comprehensive, ranging from table-top walkthroughs to full-scale functional recovery tests. All test results, including successes and failures, shall be documented, reviewed by management, and used to update plans.</p><p><strong>5.6 Training and Awareness</strong><br>All personnel with defined roles in the BCP/DRP shall receive role-specific training upon plan assignment and following any significant plan update. General business continuity awareness training shall be provided to all employees annually.</p><p><strong>5.7 Communication and Notification</strong><br>A documented crisis communication plan shall be maintained, specifying internal and external notification procedures, communication channels, and spokespersons during a disruptive incident. Contact lists for key personnel, emergency services, and critical third parties must be reviewed and updated quarterly.</p><p><strong>5.8 Third-Party and Supply Chain Continuity</strong><br>Critical third-party service providers and suppliers shall be contractually obligated to maintain business continuity plans that meet or exceed [Company Name]''s RTO and RPO requirements for the services provided. Their compliance and test results shall be subject to review as part of vendor risk management.</p><p><strong>5.9 Plan Maintenance and Documentation</strong><br>All BCMS documentation, including the BIA, BCPs, DRPs, test reports, and training records, shall be maintained under version control. Plans shall be formally reviewed and updated at least annually, or immediately following a test, exercise, actual incident, or significant organizational change.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>The BCMS shall be designed and operated to ensure compliance with all applicable legal, statutory, regulatory, and contractual obligations related to operational resilience and data availability. This policy aligns with the requirements of ISO 27001 (Annex A.17) and SOC 2 (Availability and Security Criteria). Evidence of BIA, plan testing, and management review shall be maintained for audit purposes.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Executive Management / Board:</strong> Ultimate responsibility for approving this policy, providing resources for the BCMS, and ensuring organizational commitment.<br><br><strong>Business Continuity Manager:</strong> Responsible for the day-to-day management, coordination, and maintenance of the BCMS, including overseeing the BIA, plan development, testing, and reporting.<br><br><strong>Process / System Owners:</strong> Responsible for participating in the BIA, providing input for recovery strategies, and executing specific recovery procedures for their owned processes/systems during an incident.<br><br><strong>IT Department:</strong> Responsible for implementing and maintaining the technical recovery controls, including backup systems, redundant infrastructure, and the execution of the Disaster Recovery Plan.<br><br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for familiarizing themselves with general continuity procedures, participating in training and exercises, and following management directives during a disruptive incident.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any violation of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and may lead to legal or financial penalties for the individual and [Company Name]. Failure to participate in required BCP/DRP testing or training is considered a violation of this policy.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This Business Continuity Policy shall be reviewed by the Business Continuity Manager and relevant stakeholders at least annually. Revisions must be approved by Executive Management. The policy version history shall be maintained to track all changes.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-19 06:15:55.434025', '"[\"ISO 27001 / SOC 2\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-20 06:00:17.133', NULL),
	(13, '8', 'Asset Management Policy', '<p>The purpose of this Asset Management Policy is to establish a comprehensive framework for identifying, classifying, protecting, and managing all information assets owned, controlled, or processed by [Company Name]. This policy ensures that assets are appropriately protected throughout their lifecycle, commensurate with their value, sensitivity, and criticality to business operations. It provides the foundation for accountability, risk management, and compliance with ISO 27001, SOC 2, and other regulatory requirements.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all information assets, regardless of format or location, that are owned, leased, managed, or otherwise under the custodianship of [Company Name]. This includes, but is not limited to, all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party service providers who create, access, process, store, transmit, or dispose of company information assets. The scope encompasses physical assets (e.g., hardware, media), logical assets (e.g., software, data, licenses), and intangible assets (e.g., intellectual property, reputation).</p>

<p><strong>Asset:</strong> Any item that has value to the organization, including information, software, physical devices, and services.<br><strong>Asset Owner:</strong> An individual or entity with approved management responsibility for an asset, accountable for its protection and lifecycle.<br><strong>Asset Custodian:</strong> An individual or entity responsible for the day-to-day safeguarding and operational management of an asset on behalf of the Owner.<br><strong>Information Asset:</strong> A body of information, defined and managed as a single unit, that has value to the organization.<br><strong>Asset Inventory:</strong> A formal register that records and tracks all assets, their attributes, classification, ownership, and location.<br><strong>Asset Lifecycle:</strong> The stages an asset passes through from planning and acquisition through to disposal.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all information assets shall be formally identified, classified, and managed according to their business value, sensitivity, and legal requirements. A centralized, accurate, and up-to-date asset inventory shall be maintained. All assets shall have a designated Owner responsible for defining protection requirements and authorizing access. Protection controls shall be implemented and maintained throughout the asset lifecycle to ensure confidentiality, integrity, and availability.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Asset Identification and Inventory</strong><br>5.1.1 A comprehensive inventory of all information assets shall be established and maintained.<br>5.1.2 The inventory must, at a minimum, record: unique identifier, asset name, type, classification, owner, custodian, location, and acquisition/retirement dates.<br>5.1.3 The asset inventory shall be reviewed and verified for accuracy at least quarterly.<br><br><strong>5.2 Asset Classification and Labeling</strong><br>5.2.1 All information assets shall be classified according to the corporate data classification scheme (e.g., Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted).<br>5.2.2 Classification must be based on legal requirements, value, criticality, and sensitivity to unauthorized disclosure or modification.<br>5.2.3 Assets containing or processing Confidential or Restricted data must be clearly labeled, either physically or electronically.<br><br><strong>5.3 Asset Ownership and Accountability</strong><br>5.3.1 Every asset recorded in the inventory must have a formally designated and documented Asset Owner.<br>5.3.2 The Asset Owner is responsible for approving access rights, defining acceptable use, and authorizing changes to the asset.<br>5.3.3 Ownership assignments shall be reviewed annually and upon any significant organizational change.<br><br><strong>5.4 Acceptable Use of Assets</strong><br>5.4.1 All use of company assets must be for authorized business purposes only.<br>5.4.2 Personal use of company information assets is strictly prohibited unless explicitly authorized in writing.<br>5.4.3 Users shall not install unapproved hardware or software on company assets.<br><br><strong>5.5 Asset Lifecycle Management</strong><br>5.5.1 Formal procedures shall govern the secure acquisition, deployment, maintenance, and disposal of all assets.<br>5.5.2 Prior to disposal, transfer, or reassignment, all data must be securely erased using approved methods (e.g., NIST 800-88 media sanitization).<br>5.5.3 Physical assets shall be disposed of in a manner that prevents recovery of information and complies with environmental regulations.<br><br><strong>5.6 Protection of Mobile and Removable Assets</strong><br>5.6.1 Company-owned mobile devices (laptops, tablets, smartphones) and removable media (USB drives) must employ full-disk encryption.<br>5.6.2 The use of removable media for storing Confidential or Restricted information is forbidden unless encrypted and explicitly authorized by the Asset Owner.<br>5.6.3 All mobile assets must be capable of being remotely wiped if lost or stolen.<br><br><strong>5.7 Software Asset Management</strong><br>5.7.1 The installation and use of all software must comply with licensing agreements.<br>5.7.2 The use of unlicensed, trial, or shareware software for business purposes is strictly prohibited.<br>5.7.3 A software inventory shall be maintained and reconciled against purchase records at least annually.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant legal, statutory, regulatory, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data protection laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), intellectual property rights, and software licensing agreements. Asset management practices shall support audit requirements for ISO 27001 (Annex A 8.1, 8.2) and SOC 2 (CC6.1, CC6.6). Non-compliance with this policy may result in legal action, regulatory penalties, and reputational damage to [Company Name].</p>

<p><strong>Senior Management / Board of Directors:</strong> Provide ultimate accountability and resource commitment for the asset management program.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Responsible for the development, implementation, and oversight of this policy.<br><strong>Asset Owners:</strong> Define protection requirements, authorize access, and are accountable for their assigned assets.<br><strong>IT Department / Asset Custodians:</strong> Implement and maintain technical controls, manage the asset inventory, and execute day-to-day operational procedures.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Comply with this policy, use assets responsibly, and report any loss, theft, or misuse of assets immediately.</p>

<p>Any employee, contractor, or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal prosecution. Violations include, but are not limited to: failure to maintain an accurate asset inventory, unauthorized use or modification of assets, removal of assets without authorization, and negligent handling leading to asset loss or data breach. The Information Security team is authorized to monitor compliance and investigate suspected violations.</p>

<p>This Asset Management Policy shall be reviewed at least annually by the CISO or designated authority. Revisions shall be made in response to significant security incidents, changes in business processes, technological developments, or updates to applicable laws and standards. All revisions require formal approval from the CISO and must be communicated to all relevant stakeholders.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>The purpose of this Asset Management Policy is to establish a comprehensive framework for identifying, classifying, protecting, and managing all information assets owned, controlled, or processed by [Company Name]. This policy ensures that assets are appropriately protected throughout their lifecycle, commensurate with their value, sensitivity, and criticality to business operations. It provides the foundation for accountability, risk management, and compliance with ISO 27001, SOC 2, and other regulatory requirements.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all information assets, regardless of format or location, that are owned, leased, managed, or otherwise under the custodianship of [Company Name]. This includes, but is not limited to, all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party service providers who create, access, process, store, transmit, or dispose of company information assets. The scope encompasses physical assets (e.g., hardware, media), logical assets (e.g., software, data, licenses), and intangible assets (e.g., intellectual property, reputation).</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Asset:</strong> Any item that has value to the organization, including information, software, physical devices, and services.<br><strong>Asset Owner:</strong> An individual or entity with approved management responsibility for an asset, accountable for its protection and lifecycle.<br><strong>Asset Custodian:</strong> An individual or entity responsible for the day-to-day safeguarding and operational management of an asset on behalf of the Owner.<br><strong>Information Asset:</strong> A body of information, defined and managed as a single unit, that has value to the organization.<br><strong>Asset Inventory:</strong> A formal register that records and tracks all assets, their attributes, classification, ownership, and location.<br><strong>Asset Lifecycle:</strong> The stages an asset passes through from planning and acquisition through to disposal.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates that all information assets shall be formally identified, classified, and managed according to their business value, sensitivity, and legal requirements. A centralized, accurate, and up-to-date asset inventory shall be maintained. All assets shall have a designated Owner responsible for defining protection requirements and authorizing access. Protection controls shall be implemented and maintained throughout the asset lifecycle to ensure confidentiality, integrity, and availability.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>5.1 Asset Identification and Inventory</strong><br>5.1.1 A comprehensive inventory of all information assets shall be established and maintained.<br>5.1.2 The inventory must, at a minimum, record: unique identifier, asset name, type, classification, owner, custodian, location, and acquisition/retirement dates.<br>5.1.3 The asset inventory shall be reviewed and verified for accuracy at least quarterly.<br><br><strong>5.2 Asset Classification and Labeling</strong><br>5.2.1 All information assets shall be classified according to the corporate data classification scheme (e.g., Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted).<br>5.2.2 Classification must be based on legal requirements, value, criticality, and sensitivity to unauthorized disclosure or modification.<br>5.2.3 Assets containing or processing Confidential or Restricted data must be clearly labeled, either physically or electronically.<br><br><strong>5.3 Asset Ownership and Accountability</strong><br>5.3.1 Every asset recorded in the inventory must have a formally designated and documented Asset Owner.<br>5.3.2 The Asset Owner is responsible for approving access rights, defining acceptable use, and authorizing changes to the asset.<br>5.3.3 Ownership assignments shall be reviewed annually and upon any significant organizational change.<br><br><strong>5.4 Acceptable Use of Assets</strong><br>5.4.1 All use of company assets must be for authorized business purposes only.<br>5.4.2 Personal use of company information assets is strictly prohibited unless explicitly authorized in writing.<br>5.4.3 Users shall not install unapproved hardware or software on company assets.<br><br><strong>5.5 Asset Lifecycle Management</strong><br>5.5.1 Formal procedures shall govern the secure acquisition, deployment, maintenance, and disposal of all assets.<br>5.5.2 Prior to disposal, transfer, or reassignment, all data must be securely erased using approved methods (e.g., NIST 800-88 media sanitization).<br>5.5.3 Physical assets shall be disposed of in a manner that prevents recovery of information and complies with environmental regulations.<br><br><strong>5.6 Protection of Mobile and Removable Assets</strong><br>5.6.1 Company-owned mobile devices (laptops, tablets, smartphones) and removable media (USB drives) must employ full-disk encryption.<br>5.6.2 The use of removable media for storing Confidential or Restricted information is forbidden unless encrypted and explicitly authorized by the Asset Owner.<br>5.6.3 All mobile assets must be capable of being remotely wiped if lost or stolen.<br><br><strong>5.7 Software Asset Management</strong><br>5.7.1 The installation and use of all software must comply with licensing agreements.<br>5.7.2 The use of unlicensed, trial, or shareware software for business purposes is strictly prohibited.<br>5.7.3 A software inventory shall be maintained and reconciled against purchase records at least annually.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant legal, statutory, regulatory, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data protection laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), intellectual property rights, and software licensing agreements. Asset management practices shall support audit requirements for ISO 27001 (Annex A 8.1, 8.2) and SOC 2 (CC6.1, CC6.6). Non-compliance with this policy may result in legal action, regulatory penalties, and reputational damage to [Company Name].</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Senior Management / Board of Directors:</strong> Provide ultimate accountability and resource commitment for the asset management program.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Responsible for the development, implementation, and oversight of this policy.<br><strong>Asset Owners:</strong> Define protection requirements, authorize access, and are accountable for their assigned assets.<br><strong>IT Department / Asset Custodians:</strong> Implement and maintain technical controls, manage the asset inventory, and execute day-to-day operational procedures.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Comply with this policy, use assets responsibly, and report any loss, theft, or misuse of assets immediately.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any employee, contractor, or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal prosecution. Violations include, but are not limited to: failure to maintain an accurate asset inventory, unauthorized use or modification of assets, removal of assets without authorization, and negligent handling leading to asset loss or data breach. The Information Security team is authorized to monitor compliance and investigate suspected violations.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This Asset Management Policy shall be reviewed at least annually by the CISO or designated authority. Revisions shall be made in response to significant security incidents, changes in business processes, technological developments, or updates to applicable laws and standards. All revisions require formal approval from the CISO and must be communicated to all relevant stakeholders.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-19 06:15:55.851538', '"[\"ISO 27001\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-20 06:00:53.88', NULL),
	(19, '14', 'Acceptable Use Policy', '<p>This Acceptable Use Policy (AUP) establishes the mandatory rules and behavioral standards for the appropriate and secure use of [Company Name]''s Information Assets, including but not limited to information systems, networks, data, software, and hardware. The purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate and customer data, mitigate risks, ensure compliance with legal and regulatory obligations, and define the boundaries of acceptable conduct for all Users.</p>

<p>This policy applies universally to all Users, including employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, and any other individuals or entities granted access to [Company Name]''s Information Assets, irrespective of location or device ownership. It encompasses all company-owned or managed systems, networks, applications, data, and services, as well as the use of personal devices (BYOD) when accessing corporate resources or data.</p>

<p><strong>Information Assets:</strong> Any data, system, network, software, hardware, or service owned, leased, operated by, or entrusted to [Company Name] that stores, processes, or transmits information.<br><strong>User:</strong> Any individual or system entity authorized to access [Company Name]''s Information Assets.<br><strong>Authorized Use:</strong> Use of Information Assets that is directly related to an individual''s job responsibilities and in accordance with this policy and all other applicable policies.<br><strong>Confidential Information:</strong> Any non-public information pertaining to [Company Name], its employees, customers, partners, or operations, the disclosure of which could cause harm.<br><strong>BYOD (Bring Your Own Device):</strong> A personally owned computing device used to access corporate resources.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that access to and use of its Information Assets is a privilege granted solely for conducting authorized business. All Users shall adhere to the principles of least privilege, data protection, and responsible conduct. Any use of Information Assets that violates this policy, compromises security, or is illegal, unethical, or for personal gain is strictly prohibited and may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment/contract and legal prosecution.</p>

<h4>5.1 General Use and Access Controls</h4><p>5.1.1 Users shall only access Information Assets for which they have received explicit authorization. Sharing of credentials (usernames, passwords, tokens) is forbidden.<br>5.1.2 Multi-factor authentication (MFA) must be enabled for all remote access and privileged administrative accounts without exception.<br>5.1.3 Users must lock workstations and secure mobile devices with a password/PIN/biometric lock when unattended.<br>5.1.4 The installation of unauthorized software, including freeware, shareware, or trial versions, on company-owned devices is prohibited without prior written approval from IT Security.</p><h4>5.2 Data Protection and Handling</h4><p>5.2.1 Confidential Information must be classified and handled according to the Data Classification and Handling Policy. Transmission of Confidential Information over public networks requires encryption using company-approved protocols (e.g., TLS 1.2+).<br>5.2.2 Storing Confidential Information on unauthorized cloud storage services (e.g., personal Dropbox, Google Drive) or removable media without encryption is forbidden.<br>5.2.3 Data must be disposed of securely using approved methods (e.g., certified data destruction, secure deletion tools) when no longer required for business purposes.</p><h4>5.3 Network and System Security</h4><p>5.3.1 Users shall not attempt to bypass network security controls, including firewalls, proxies, or intrusion prevention systems.<br>5.3.2 The connection of unauthorized network devices (e.g., wireless access points, routers, network switches) to the corporate network is strictly prohibited.<br>5.3.3 All wireless access to corporate networks must use company-provisioned and IT-managed infrastructure. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted wireless networks for corporate access is forbidden.</p><h4>5.4 Acceptable and Prohibited Activities</h4><p>5.4.1 <strong>Prohibited:</strong> Accessing, downloading, storing, or transmitting unlawful, offensive, or harassing material. Engaging in activities for personal financial gain. Running non-business related servers (e.g., game, file, web servers). Deliberately introducing malware or engaging in hacking activities.<br>5.4.2 <strong>Permitted with Caution:</strong> Limited personal use of email and internet is permitted provided it does not interfere with work duties, consume excessive bandwidth, or violate any other policy clause.</p><h4>5.5 Monitoring and Privacy</h4><p>5.5.1 Users acknowledge that [Company Name] reserves the right to monitor, audit, inspect, and log all activity on its Information Assets without prior notice to ensure policy compliance, security, and operational integrity.<br>5.5.2 Users shall have no expectation of privacy regarding data stored on or transmitted through company-owned systems and networks.</p>

<p>All use of Information Assets must comply with applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data protection laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), copyright laws, and industry-specific regulations. Users are responsible for understanding the legal requirements pertinent to their role. Any suspected violation of law must be reported immediately to the Legal or Compliance department.</p>

<p><strong>All Users:</strong> Are responsible for reading, understanding, and complying with this policy, safeguarding credentials, and reporting security incidents.<br><strong>Department Heads/Managers:</strong> Are responsible for ensuring their staff are aware of and adhere to this policy and for enforcing compliance within their teams.<br><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Is responsible for implementing technical controls, monitoring compliance, and managing access rights in line with this policy.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Is the owner of this policy, responsible for its maintenance, interpretation, and overall enforcement.</p>

<p>Violations of this policy will be treated with the utmost seriousness. Depending on the severity and circumstances, enforcement actions may include, but are not limited to: formal reprimand, suspension of system access, disciplinary action up to and including termination of employment or contract, and pursuit of legal remedies. All violations will be investigated by the IT Security and Human Resources departments.</p>

<p>This Acceptable Use Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, technology, or regulatory environment. Revisions must be approved by executive management. The most current version is the authoritative version and is made available to all Users through the company policy portal.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Acceptable Use Policy (AUP) establishes the mandatory rules and behavioral standards for the appropriate and secure use of [Company Name]''s Information Assets, including but not limited to information systems, networks, data, software, and hardware. The purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate and customer data, mitigate risks, ensure compliance with legal and regulatory obligations, and define the boundaries of acceptable conduct for all Users.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies universally to all Users, including employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, and any other individuals or entities granted access to [Company Name]''s Information Assets, irrespective of location or device ownership. It encompasses all company-owned or managed systems, networks, applications, data, and services, as well as the use of personal devices (BYOD) when accessing corporate resources or data.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Information Assets:</strong> Any data, system, network, software, hardware, or service owned, leased, operated by, or entrusted to [Company Name] that stores, processes, or transmits information.<br><strong>User:</strong> Any individual or system entity authorized to access [Company Name]''s Information Assets.<br><strong>Authorized Use:</strong> Use of Information Assets that is directly related to an individual''s job responsibilities and in accordance with this policy and all other applicable policies.<br><strong>Confidential Information:</strong> Any non-public information pertaining to [Company Name], its employees, customers, partners, or operations, the disclosure of which could cause harm.<br><strong>BYOD (Bring Your Own Device):</strong> A personally owned computing device used to access corporate resources.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates that access to and use of its Information Assets is a privilege granted solely for conducting authorized business. All Users shall adhere to the principles of least privilege, data protection, and responsible conduct. Any use of Information Assets that violates this policy, compromises security, or is illegal, unethical, or for personal gain is strictly prohibited and may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment/contract and legal prosecution.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1 General Use and Access Controls</h4><p>5.1.1 Users shall only access Information Assets for which they have received explicit authorization. Sharing of credentials (usernames, passwords, tokens) is forbidden.<br>5.1.2 Multi-factor authentication (MFA) must be enabled for all remote access and privileged administrative accounts without exception.<br>5.1.3 Users must lock workstations and secure mobile devices with a password/PIN/biometric lock when unattended.<br>5.1.4 The installation of unauthorized software, including freeware, shareware, or trial versions, on company-owned devices is prohibited without prior written approval from IT Security.</p><h4>5.2 Data Protection and Handling</h4><p>5.2.1 Confidential Information must be classified and handled according to the Data Classification and Handling Policy. Transmission of Confidential Information over public networks requires encryption using company-approved protocols (e.g., TLS 1.2+).<br>5.2.2 Storing Confidential Information on unauthorized cloud storage services (e.g., personal Dropbox, Google Drive) or removable media without encryption is forbidden.<br>5.2.3 Data must be disposed of securely using approved methods (e.g., certified data destruction, secure deletion tools) when no longer required for business purposes.</p><h4>5.3 Network and System Security</h4><p>5.3.1 Users shall not attempt to bypass network security controls, including firewalls, proxies, or intrusion prevention systems.<br>5.3.2 The connection of unauthorized network devices (e.g., wireless access points, routers, network switches) to the corporate network is strictly prohibited.<br>5.3.3 All wireless access to corporate networks must use company-provisioned and IT-managed infrastructure. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted wireless networks for corporate access is forbidden.</p><h4>5.4 Acceptable and Prohibited Activities</h4><p>5.4.1 <strong>Prohibited:</strong> Accessing, downloading, storing, or transmitting unlawful, offensive, or harassing material. Engaging in activities for personal financial gain. Running non-business related servers (e.g., game, file, web servers). Deliberately introducing malware or engaging in hacking activities.<br>5.4.2 <strong>Permitted with Caution:</strong> Limited personal use of email and internet is permitted provided it does not interfere with work duties, consume excessive bandwidth, or violate any other policy clause.</p><h4>5.5 Monitoring and Privacy</h4><p>5.5.1 Users acknowledge that [Company Name] reserves the right to monitor, audit, inspect, and log all activity on its Information Assets without prior notice to ensure policy compliance, security, and operational integrity.<br>5.5.2 Users shall have no expectation of privacy regarding data stored on or transmitted through company-owned systems and networks.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>All use of Information Assets must comply with applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data protection laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), copyright laws, and industry-specific regulations. Users are responsible for understanding the legal requirements pertinent to their role. Any suspected violation of law must be reported immediately to the Legal or Compliance department.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>All Users:</strong> Are responsible for reading, understanding, and complying with this policy, safeguarding credentials, and reporting security incidents.<br><strong>Department Heads/Managers:</strong> Are responsible for ensuring their staff are aware of and adhere to this policy and for enforcing compliance within their teams.<br><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Is responsible for implementing technical controls, monitoring compliance, and managing access rights in line with this policy.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Is the owner of this policy, responsible for its maintenance, interpretation, and overall enforcement.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Violations of this policy will be treated with the utmost seriousness. Depending on the severity and circumstances, enforcement actions may include, but are not limited to: formal reprimand, suspension of system access, disciplinary action up to and including termination of employment or contract, and pursuit of legal remedies. All violations will be investigated by the IT Security and Human Resources departments.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This Acceptable Use Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, technology, or regulatory environment. Revisions must be approved by executive management. The most current version is the authoritative version and is made available to all Users through the company policy portal.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-19 06:15:57.141012', '"[\"Both\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-20 06:01:27.204', NULL),
	(29, '569126', 'Secure Disposal Policy', '<p>This Secure Disposal Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the secure and permanent destruction of all [Company Name] information assets, regardless of media type, to prevent unauthorized disclosure, ensure compliance with legal and regulatory obligations, and mitigate risks associated with data remanence. The policy mandates a consistent, auditable process for sanitizing and disposing of information that has reached the end of its lifecycle.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties acting on behalf of [Company Name] ("Personnel"). It governs the secure disposal of all information assets owned, leased, or managed by [Company Name], including but not limited to: electronic media (hard drives, SSDs, USB drives, mobile devices, backup tapes, optical media), paper records, and any hardware containing non-volatile memory (e.g., printers, multifunction devices, networking equipment).</p>

<p><strong>Clear:</strong> A logical sanitization technique using standard Read and Write commands to overwrite data with non-sensitive values (e.g., all zeros).<br><strong>Cryptographic Erasure:</strong> The process of securely deleting or rendering inaccessible the encryption key used to protect data on self-encrypting drives (SEDs), effectively destroying the data.<br><strong>Degaussing:</strong> The process of reducing or eliminating the magnetic field of storage media, rendering it unusable and data unrecoverable.<br><strong>Destruction:</strong> The physical disintegration, shredding, pulverizing, or incineration of media such that data recovery is infeasible.<br><strong>Media Sanitization:</strong> A general term referring to the process of irreversibly removing data from storage media.<br><strong>Purge:</strong> A sanitization method that protects against laboratory attack, typically involving multiple overwrite passes, degaussing, or cryptographic erasure.<br><strong>Removable Electronic Media:</strong> Storage devices designed to be easily removed from a computer, including USB flash drives, external hard drives, SD cards, and CDs/DVDs.</p>

<p>[Company Name] shall ensure that all information assets are disposed of in a secure, controlled, and documented manner commensurate with their data classification. The unauthorized disposal, discarding, or transfer of any company information asset to an unapproved entity is strictly prohibited. All disposal activities must be performed in accordance with the requirements specified in this policy and associated procedures.</p>

<h4>5.1 Classification-Based Disposal Standard</h4><p>All media must be sanitized according to its highest classification level. The following standards shall be applied:<br><strong>Public/Internal Use Only:</strong> Media may be cleared via a single overwrite pass or securely erased using a software utility that meets NIST SP 800-88 Revision 1 guidelines.<br><strong>Confidential/Restricted:</strong> Media must be purged. This requires either cryptographic erasure (for SEDs) or overwriting with a minimum of three passes using a recognized algorithm (e.g., DoD 5220.22-M). Degaussing is required for magnetic media where overwriting is not feasible.<br><strong>Highly Confidential/Regulated (e.g., PHI, PII, Financial Data):</strong> Media containing such data must be physically destroyed. Hard drives and SSDs shall be shredded or disintegrated. Paper records shall be cross-cut shredded to a particle size not exceeding 5mm x 40mm.</p><h4>5.2 Inventory and Chain of Custody</h4><p>A formal, documented inventory of all assets pending disposal must be maintained. The chain of custody for media from decommissioning through final destruction shall be recorded and include: asset identifier, serial number, custodian name, date of transfer, and destruction certificate reference. Gaps in the chain of custody constitute a security incident.</p><h4>5.3 Approved Sanitization and Destruction Methods</h4><p>The use of unapproved software tools or ad-hoc destruction methods is forbidden. All sanitization software must be validated to produce verifiable logs. Physical destruction must be performed by an NAID AAA-certified vendor or using on-site equipment that meets DIN 66399 Level P-5 (paper) or E-3 (hard drives) standards. Degaussers must meet NSA/CSS EPL standards for the media type being sanitized.</p><h4>5.4 Verification and Certification</h4><p>For software-based sanitization, a verification log confirming successful completion must be generated and retained. For physical destruction, a formal Certificate of Destruction (CoD) must be obtained from the service provider for each batch. The CoD must include the destruction date, method, serial numbers (or range), and be signed by an authorized representative of the destruction vendor.</p><h4>5.5 Removable Media and End-of-Life Hardware</h4><p>All end-user removable electronic media issued by [Company Name] must be returned to IT for centralized, secure disposal. Personnel are strictly prohibited from disposing of such media through municipal waste or recycling. All hardware containing non-volatile memory (e.g., printers, copiers) must have internal storage media sanitized or physically removed and destroyed prior to disposal, donation, or return to a lessor.</p><h4>5.6 Retention of Documentation</h4><p>All disposal records, including inventories, chain of custody logs, sanitization verification reports, and Certificates of Destruction, shall be retained for a minimum period of seven (7) years, or as required by specific regulatory mandates (e.g., HIPAA, GDPR).</p>

<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant sections of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.8.10, A.8.12, A.8.14), SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Confidentiality), HIPAA Security Rule (164.310(d)(1) Device and Media Controls), GDPR (Article 32, Security of Processing), and other data protection regulations. Failure to comply may result in regulatory penalties, legal liability, and reputational damage for [Company Name].</p>

<p><strong>Information Security Officer (ISO):</strong> Responsible for the development, maintenance, and oversight of this policy; approving disposal vendors and methods.<br><strong>IT Department/Asset Management:</strong> Responsible for executing the technical disposal process, maintaining the asset inventory and chain of custody, and securely storing media pending destruction.<br><strong>Department Heads/Business Unit Managers:</strong> Responsible for ensuring all personnel within their area understand and comply with this policy, and for initiating the disposal request process for assets under their control.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for safeguarding all [Company Name] information assets in their possession and returning them to IT for secure disposal at end-of-life.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal action. Deliberate or negligent disposal of information assets in an unsecure manner, or the failure to report a suspected breach of this policy, shall be treated as a serious security incident.</p>

<p>This Secure Disposal Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Officer at least annually, or following significant changes to technology, business processes, or applicable regulations. Revisions must be approved by the [Company Name] executive management team. The latest approved version is the only version considered valid.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Secure Disposal Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the secure and permanent destruction of all [Company Name] information assets, regardless of media type, to prevent unauthorized disclosure, ensure compliance with legal and regulatory obligations, and mitigate risks associated with data remanence. The policy mandates a consistent, auditable process for sanitizing and disposing of information that has reached the end of its lifecycle.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties acting on behalf of [Company Name] (\\\"Personnel\\\"). It governs the secure disposal of all information assets owned, leased, or managed by [Company Name], including but not limited to: electronic media (hard drives, SSDs, USB drives, mobile devices, backup tapes, optical media), paper records, and any hardware containing non-volatile memory (e.g., printers, multifunction devices, networking equipment).</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Clear:</strong> A logical sanitization technique using standard Read and Write commands to overwrite data with non-sensitive values (e.g., all zeros).<br><strong>Cryptographic Erasure:</strong> The process of securely deleting or rendering inaccessible the encryption key used to protect data on self-encrypting drives (SEDs), effectively destroying the data.<br><strong>Degaussing:</strong> The process of reducing or eliminating the magnetic field of storage media, rendering it unusable and data unrecoverable.<br><strong>Destruction:</strong> The physical disintegration, shredding, pulverizing, or incineration of media such that data recovery is infeasible.<br><strong>Media Sanitization:</strong> A general term referring to the process of irreversibly removing data from storage media.<br><strong>Purge:</strong> A sanitization method that protects against laboratory attack, typically involving multiple overwrite passes, degaussing, or cryptographic erasure.<br><strong>Removable Electronic Media:</strong> Storage devices designed to be easily removed from a computer, including USB flash drives, external hard drives, SD cards, and CDs/DVDs.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] shall ensure that all information assets are disposed of in a secure, controlled, and documented manner commensurate with their data classification. The unauthorized disposal, discarding, or transfer of any company information asset to an unapproved entity is strictly prohibited. All disposal activities must be performed in accordance with the requirements specified in this policy and associated procedures.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1 Classification-Based Disposal Standard</h4><p>All media must be sanitized according to its highest classification level. The following standards shall be applied:<br><strong>Public/Internal Use Only:</strong> Media may be cleared via a single overwrite pass or securely erased using a software utility that meets NIST SP 800-88 Revision 1 guidelines.<br><strong>Confidential/Restricted:</strong> Media must be purged. This requires either cryptographic erasure (for SEDs) or overwriting with a minimum of three passes using a recognized algorithm (e.g., DoD 5220.22-M). Degaussing is required for magnetic media where overwriting is not feasible.<br><strong>Highly Confidential/Regulated (e.g., PHI, PII, Financial Data):</strong> Media containing such data must be physically destroyed. Hard drives and SSDs shall be shredded or disintegrated. Paper records shall be cross-cut shredded to a particle size not exceeding 5mm x 40mm.</p><h4>5.2 Inventory and Chain of Custody</h4><p>A formal, documented inventory of all assets pending disposal must be maintained. The chain of custody for media from decommissioning through final destruction shall be recorded and include: asset identifier, serial number, custodian name, date of transfer, and destruction certificate reference. Gaps in the chain of custody constitute a security incident.</p><h4>5.3 Approved Sanitization and Destruction Methods</h4><p>The use of unapproved software tools or ad-hoc destruction methods is forbidden. All sanitization software must be validated to produce verifiable logs. Physical destruction must be performed by an NAID AAA-certified vendor or using on-site equipment that meets DIN 66399 Level P-5 (paper) or E-3 (hard drives) standards. Degaussers must meet NSA/CSS EPL standards for the media type being sanitized.</p><h4>5.4 Verification and Certification</h4><p>For software-based sanitization, a verification log confirming successful completion must be generated and retained. For physical destruction, a formal Certificate of Destruction (CoD) must be obtained from the service provider for each batch. The CoD must include the destruction date, method, serial numbers (or range), and be signed by an authorized representative of the destruction vendor.</p><h4>5.5 Removable Media and End-of-Life Hardware</h4><p>All end-user removable electronic media issued by [Company Name] must be returned to IT for centralized, secure disposal. Personnel are strictly prohibited from disposing of such media through municipal waste or recycling. All hardware containing non-volatile memory (e.g., printers, copiers) must have internal storage media sanitized or physically removed and destroyed prior to disposal, donation, or return to a lessor.</p><h4>5.6 Retention of Documentation</h4><p>All disposal records, including inventories, chain of custody logs, sanitization verification reports, and Certificates of Destruction, shall be retained for a minimum period of seven (7) years, or as required by specific regulatory mandates (e.g., HIPAA, GDPR).</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant sections of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.8.10, A.8.12, A.8.14), SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Confidentiality), HIPAA Security Rule (164.310(d)(1) Device and Media Controls), GDPR (Article 32, Security of Processing), and other data protection regulations. Failure to comply may result in regulatory penalties, legal liability, and reputational damage for [Company Name].</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Information Security Officer (ISO):</strong> Responsible for the development, maintenance, and oversight of this policy; approving disposal vendors and methods.<br><strong>IT Department/Asset Management:</strong> Responsible for executing the technical disposal process, maintaining the asset inventory and chain of custody, and securely storing media pending destruction.<br><strong>Department Heads/Business Unit Managers:</strong> Responsible for ensuring all personnel within their area understand and comply with this policy, and for initiating the disposal request process for assets under their control.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for safeguarding all [Company Name] information assets in their possession and returning them to IT for secure disposal at end-of-life.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal action. Deliberate or negligent disposal of information assets in an unsecure manner, or the failure to report a suspected breach of this policy, shall be treated as a serious security incident.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This Secure Disposal Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Officer at least annually, or following significant changes to technology, business processes, or applicable regulations. Revisions must be approved by the [Company Name] executive management team. The latest approved version is the only version considered valid.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-20 21:17:22.296933', '"[\"ISO 27001\",\"SOC 2\",\"HIPAA\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-20 06:02:05.015', NULL),
	(47, '902632', 'AI Acceptable Use Policy', '<p>This AI Acceptable Use Policy (AUP) establishes the mandatory governance, security, and ethical framework for the acquisition, development, deployment, and use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) systems by [Company Name]. The policy is designed to ensure that all AI/ML activities align with organizational values, comply with legal and regulatory obligations, and mitigate risks associated with data integrity, model bias, security, and operational reliability. It serves as the authoritative control document for AI governance, satisfying core requirements of ISO 27001 (A.6, A.8, A.12, A.14, A.15, A.16) and SOC 2 (Security, Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity, Privacy) trust service criteria.</p>

<p>This policy applies universally to all personnel of [Company Name] (including employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and interns), all organizational units, and all third-party partners who access, develop, manage, or interact with [Company Name]''s AI/ML systems, data, or infrastructure. It encompasses all stages of the AI lifecycle: data sourcing and preparation, model development (including training, fine-tuning, and testing), model deployment (in production or pilot environments), model inference/operation, and model decommissioning. This policy governs all AI/ML systems, whether developed in-house, acquired as commercial-off-the-shelf (COTS) software, accessed via third-party APIs (e.g., OpenAI, Anthropic), or embedded within other enterprise applications.</p>

<p><strong>AI/ML System:</strong> Any software system that employs machine learning (including deep learning, natural language processing, computer vision), statistical, or rule-based algorithms to perform tasks typically requiring human intelligence, such as prediction, classification, recommendation, or content generation.<br><br><strong>Production Model:</strong> An AI/ML model that is deployed in a live environment and whose outputs are used to inform business decisions, automate processes, or interact with customers or internal users.<br><br><strong>Training Data:</strong> The dataset used to train or fine-tune an AI/ML model. This includes both structured and unstructured data.<br><br><strong>Model Artifacts:</strong> The final outputs of the model development process, including the trained model weights, configuration files, and associated metadata.<br><br><strong>Shadow IT AI:</strong> The unauthorized use, procurement, or development of AI tools or services not approved by the designated governance body (e.g., AI Governance Committee).<br><br><strong>High-Risk AI Application:</strong> An AI/ML system whose failure or error could result in significant harm, including but not limited to: physical injury, substantial financial loss, legal liability, violation of individual rights, or material damage to [Company Name]''s reputation.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning activities shall be conducted in a secure, ethical, compliant, and accountable manner. The use of AI/ML technologies is a privilege granted to support business objectives, not a right. All personnel and third parties are strictly prohibited from engaging in any AI-related activity that violates this policy, applicable laws, or ethical norms. [Company Name] reserves the right to monitor, audit, and immediately suspend any AI/ML system or user activity that is non-compliant.</p>

<h4>5.1 Governance & Pre-Approval</h4><p>5.1.1. All new AI/ML projects, including the procurement of third-party AI services, must undergo a formal risk assessment and receive written pre-approval from the AI Governance Committee prior to any development, procurement, or data collection activities.<br>5.1.2. A designated business owner and a technical owner must be assigned for every approved AI/ML system in production, responsible for its ongoing performance, compliance, and risk management.<br>5.1.3. The use of personal or "shadow IT" AI tools (e.g., unauthorized use of public generative AI chatbots for company data) on [Company Name] devices or networks is strictly forbidden.</p><h4>5.2 Data Governance & Integrity</h4><p>5.2.1. Training and operational data for AI/ML systems must be sourced in accordance with the Data Classification and Handling Policy. The use of Sensitive or Confidential data requires explicit authorization and must be documented in the system''s data provenance record.<br>5.2.2. All training datasets must be version-controlled. A data card or similar artifact must be maintained, documenting the dataset''s origin, composition, preprocessing steps, and any known biases.<br>5.2.3. Data used for model training must be rigorously validated for quality, accuracy, and representativeness. Sampling bias that could lead to discriminatory outcomes must be identified and mitigated prior to model training.</p><h4>5.3 Model Development & Security</h4><p>5.3.1. Model development must occur in isolated, secure environments (e.g., approved cloud AI platforms or air-gapped research clusters). Development on local workstations using production data is prohibited.<br>5.3.2. All model code, configurations, and training scripts shall be managed within the organization''s approved version control system (e.g., Git). Commit history must be preserved and shall not be altered.<br>5.3.3. Model artifacts must be cryptographically signed and stored in a secure, access-controlled model registry. Access to production model artifacts shall be restricted based on the principle of least privilege.</p><h4>5.4 Testing, Validation & Bias Mitigation</h4><p>5.4.1. Prior to deployment, all models must pass a comprehensive validation suite, including performance metrics (accuracy, precision, recall), robustness testing (for adversarial examples), and fairness assessments across protected attributes (e.g., race, gender, age) as relevant.<br>5.4.2. For High-Risk AI Applications, an independent third-party audit or red-team exercise must be conducted, and the results approved by the AI Governance Committee.<br>5.4.3. A model card must be created and maintained for every production model, detailing its intended use, performance characteristics, limitations, and known ethical considerations.</p><h4>5.5 Secure Deployment & Operations</h4><p>5.5.1. AI/ML models shall only be deployed to environments that meet [Company Name]''s hardened infrastructure standards, including network segmentation, intrusion detection, and vulnerability management.<br>5.5.2. All model inference endpoints (APIs) must be protected by strong authentication (OAuth 2.0, API keys) and encrypted in transit using TLS 1.2 or higher.<br>5.5.3. Inputs to and outputs from production models must be logged in a secure, immutable audit log. Logs must include a unique session/user ID, timestamp, and a hash of the input/output data for non-repudiation.</p><h4>5.6 Monitoring, Observability & Human Oversight</h4><p>5.6.1. Continuous monitoring of model performance (e.g., prediction drift, data drift) and system health (latency, error rates) must be implemented. Automated alerts must be configured for significant degradation.<br>5.6.2. A human-in-the-loop (HITL) review process is mandatory for High-Risk AI Applications, where a qualified human must review and approve critical model outputs before action is taken.<br>5.6.3. All AI/ML systems must have a documented and tested rollback procedure to a previous known-good state in the event of failure or compromise.</p><h4>5.7 Third-Party & External AI Services</h4><p>5.7.1. The use of external AI/ML APIs or services (e.g., cloud AI, generative AI platforms) requires a completed vendor security assessment and a signed Data Processing Agreement (DPA) that addresses data ownership, confidentiality, and prohibits the vendor from using [Company Name] data for model training.<br>5.7.2. Prompt engineering for generative AI services must follow secure coding practices. Prompts must not contain Sensitive or Confidential information unless the service is explicitly approved for such data and is configured with data isolation guarantees.</p>

<p>All AI/ML activities must comply with applicable international, federal, state, and local laws and regulations, including but not limited to: data protection and privacy laws (GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, etc.), sector-specific regulations (HIPAA, GLBA, PCI-DSS where applicable), and emerging AI-specific legislation (e.g., EU AI Act, US Executive Order on AI). Systems that process personal data must implement Privacy by Design principles, including data minimization and purpose limitation. [Company Name] shall maintain records of AI system risk assessments, model cards, data cards, and audit logs as evidence of due diligence and compliance for a minimum period defined by the Records Retention Policy, or as required by law.</p>

<p><strong>AI Governance Committee:</strong> Responsible for setting strategic direction, approving projects, defining risk tolerance, and overseeing policy compliance. Chaired by the CISO or designated executive.<br><br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority for the security of all AI/ML systems. Responsible for policy enforcement, security architecture review, and incident response.<br><br><strong>Data Owners & Business Unit Leaders:</strong> Accountable for the business use, ethical application, and risk management of AI systems within their domain. Must ensure adequate resources for monitoring and maintenance.<br><br><strong>AI/ML Developers & Data Scientists:</strong> Responsible for adhering to secure development practices, documenting their work, performing bias testing, and collaborating with security teams during design and deployment.<br><br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to complete mandatory AI ethics and security awareness training. Must report any suspected policy violations, security incidents, or unethical AI behavior through the established reporting channel.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and may face legal action. Violations include, but are not limited to: deploying an AI system without approval, using prohibited data, circumventing security controls, or failing to perform required monitoring. The Information Security Department is authorized to immediately disconnect, quarantine, or disable any AI/ML system that poses an imminent security threat or is operating in severe violation of this policy.</p>

<p>This AI Acceptable Use Policy shall be reviewed and updated by the AI Governance Committee at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant changes in technology, threat landscape, or regulatory requirements. All revisions must be formally approved and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. A version history of this policy shall be maintained to demonstrate continuous improvement and governance.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This AI Acceptable Use Policy (AUP) establishes the mandatory governance, security, and ethical framework for the acquisition, development, deployment, and use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) systems by [Company Name]. The policy is designed to ensure that all AI/ML activities align with organizational values, comply with legal and regulatory obligations, and mitigate risks associated with data integrity, model bias, security, and operational reliability. It serves as the authoritative control document for AI governance, satisfying core requirements of ISO 27001 (A.6, A.8, A.12, A.14, A.15, A.16) and SOC 2 (Security, Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity, Privacy) trust service criteria.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies universally to all personnel of [Company Name] (including employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and interns), all organizational units, and all third-party partners who access, develop, manage, or interact with [Company Name]''s AI/ML systems, data, or infrastructure. It encompasses all stages of the AI lifecycle: data sourcing and preparation, model development (including training, fine-tuning, and testing), model deployment (in production or pilot environments), model inference/operation, and model decommissioning. This policy governs all AI/ML systems, whether developed in-house, acquired as commercial-off-the-shelf (COTS) software, accessed via third-party APIs (e.g., OpenAI, Anthropic), or embedded within other enterprise applications.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>AI/ML System:</strong> Any software system that employs machine learning (including deep learning, natural language processing, computer vision), statistical, or rule-based algorithms to perform tasks typically requiring human intelligence, such as prediction, classification, recommendation, or content generation.<br><br><strong>Production Model:</strong> An AI/ML model that is deployed in a live environment and whose outputs are used to inform business decisions, automate processes, or interact with customers or internal users.<br><br><strong>Training Data:</strong> The dataset used to train or fine-tune an AI/ML model. This includes both structured and unstructured data.<br><br><strong>Model Artifacts:</strong> The final outputs of the model development process, including the trained model weights, configuration files, and associated metadata.<br><br><strong>Shadow IT AI:</strong> The unauthorized use, procurement, or development of AI tools or services not approved by the designated governance body (e.g., AI Governance Committee).<br><br><strong>High-Risk AI Application:</strong> An AI/ML system whose failure or error could result in significant harm, including but not limited to: physical injury, substantial financial loss, legal liability, violation of individual rights, or material damage to [Company Name]''s reputation.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates that all Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning activities shall be conducted in a secure, ethical, compliant, and accountable manner. The use of AI/ML technologies is a privilege granted to support business objectives, not a right. All personnel and third parties are strictly prohibited from engaging in any AI-related activity that violates this policy, applicable laws, or ethical norms. [Company Name] reserves the right to monitor, audit, and immediately suspend any AI/ML system or user activity that is non-compliant.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1 Governance & Pre-Approval</h4><p>5.1.1. All new AI/ML projects, including the procurement of third-party AI services, must undergo a formal risk assessment and receive written pre-approval from the AI Governance Committee prior to any development, procurement, or data collection activities.<br>5.1.2. A designated business owner and a technical owner must be assigned for every approved AI/ML system in production, responsible for its ongoing performance, compliance, and risk management.<br>5.1.3. The use of personal or \\\"shadow IT\\\" AI tools (e.g., unauthorized use of public generative AI chatbots for company data) on [Company Name] devices or networks is strictly forbidden.</p><h4>5.2 Data Governance & Integrity</h4><p>5.2.1. Training and operational data for AI/ML systems must be sourced in accordance with the Data Classification and Handling Policy. The use of Sensitive or Confidential data requires explicit authorization and must be documented in the system''s data provenance record.<br>5.2.2. All training datasets must be version-controlled. A data card or similar artifact must be maintained, documenting the dataset''s origin, composition, preprocessing steps, and any known biases.<br>5.2.3. Data used for model training must be rigorously validated for quality, accuracy, and representativeness. Sampling bias that could lead to discriminatory outcomes must be identified and mitigated prior to model training.</p><h4>5.3 Model Development & Security</h4><p>5.3.1. Model development must occur in isolated, secure environments (e.g., approved cloud AI platforms or air-gapped research clusters). Development on local workstations using production data is prohibited.<br>5.3.2. All model code, configurations, and training scripts shall be managed within the organization''s approved version control system (e.g., Git). Commit history must be preserved and shall not be altered.<br>5.3.3. Model artifacts must be cryptographically signed and stored in a secure, access-controlled model registry. Access to production model artifacts shall be restricted based on the principle of least privilege.</p><h4>5.4 Testing, Validation & Bias Mitigation</h4><p>5.4.1. Prior to deployment, all models must pass a comprehensive validation suite, including performance metrics (accuracy, precision, recall), robustness testing (for adversarial examples), and fairness assessments across protected attributes (e.g., race, gender, age) as relevant.<br>5.4.2. For High-Risk AI Applications, an independent third-party audit or red-team exercise must be conducted, and the results approved by the AI Governance Committee.<br>5.4.3. A model card must be created and maintained for every production model, detailing its intended use, performance characteristics, limitations, and known ethical considerations.</p><h4>5.5 Secure Deployment & Operations</h4><p>5.5.1. AI/ML models shall only be deployed to environments that meet [Company Name]''s hardened infrastructure standards, including network segmentation, intrusion detection, and vulnerability management.<br>5.5.2. All model inference endpoints (APIs) must be protected by strong authentication (OAuth 2.0, API keys) and encrypted in transit using TLS 1.2 or higher.<br>5.5.3. Inputs to and outputs from production models must be logged in a secure, immutable audit log. Logs must include a unique session/user ID, timestamp, and a hash of the input/output data for non-repudiation.</p><h4>5.6 Monitoring, Observability & Human Oversight</h4><p>5.6.1. Continuous monitoring of model performance (e.g., prediction drift, data drift) and system health (latency, error rates) must be implemented. Automated alerts must be configured for significant degradation.<br>5.6.2. A human-in-the-loop (HITL) review process is mandatory for High-Risk AI Applications, where a qualified human must review and approve critical model outputs before action is taken.<br>5.6.3. All AI/ML systems must have a documented and tested rollback procedure to a previous known-good state in the event of failure or compromise.</p><h4>5.7 Third-Party & External AI Services</h4><p>5.7.1. The use of external AI/ML APIs or services (e.g., cloud AI, generative AI platforms) requires a completed vendor security assessment and a signed Data Processing Agreement (DPA) that addresses data ownership, confidentiality, and prohibits the vendor from using [Company Name] data for model training.<br>5.7.2. Prompt engineering for generative AI services must follow secure coding practices. Prompts must not contain Sensitive or Confidential information unless the service is explicitly approved for such data and is configured with data isolation guarantees.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>All AI/ML activities must comply with applicable international, federal, state, and local laws and regulations, including but not limited to: data protection and privacy laws (GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, etc.), sector-specific regulations (HIPAA, GLBA, PCI-DSS where applicable), and emerging AI-specific legislation (e.g., EU AI Act, US Executive Order on AI). Systems that process personal data must implement Privacy by Design principles, including data minimization and purpose limitation. [Company Name] shall maintain records of AI system risk assessments, model cards, data cards, and audit logs as evidence of due diligence and compliance for a minimum period defined by the Records Retention Policy, or as required by law.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>AI Governance Committee:</strong> Responsible for setting strategic direction, approving projects, defining risk tolerance, and overseeing policy compliance. Chaired by the CISO or designated executive.<br><br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority for the security of all AI/ML systems. Responsible for policy enforcement, security architecture review, and incident response.<br><br><strong>Data Owners & Business Unit Leaders:</strong> Accountable for the business use, ethical application, and risk management of AI systems within their domain. Must ensure adequate resources for monitoring and maintenance.<br><br><strong>AI/ML Developers & Data Scientists:</strong> Responsible for adhering to secure development practices, documenting their work, performing bias testing, and collaborating with security teams during design and deployment.<br><br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to complete mandatory AI ethics and security awareness training. Must report any suspected policy violations, security incidents, or unethical AI behavior through the established reporting channel.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and may face legal action. Violations include, but are not limited to: deploying an AI system without approval, using prohibited data, circumventing security controls, or failing to perform required monitoring. The Information Security Department is authorized to immediately disconnect, quarantine, or disable any AI/ML system that poses an imminent security threat or is operating in severe violation of this policy.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This AI Acceptable Use Policy shall be reviewed and updated by the AI Governance Committee at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant changes in technology, threat landscape, or regulatory requirements. All revisions must be formally approved and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. A version history of this policy shall be maintained to demonstrate continuous improvement and governance.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-20 21:17:24.199895', '"[\"ISO 27001\",\"SOC 2\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-20 06:02:56.071', NULL),
	(16, '11', 'Communications Security Policy', '<p>This Communications Security Policy establishes the mandatory controls and procedures to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of [Company Name]''s information assets during electronic transmission over internal and external networks. The policy ensures that all communications are secured against unauthorized access, interception, modification, or denial of service, in alignment with business requirements and regulatory obligations.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties (collectively, "Personnel") who access, use, or manage [Company Name]''s information systems, networks, or data. It encompasses all forms of electronic communication, including but not limited to: data transmitted over internal networks (wired and wireless), virtual private networks (VPNs), internet connections, email, instant messaging, voice-over-IP (VoIP), and any communication with external entities.</p>

<p><strong>Cryptographic Controls:</strong> The use of cryptographic techniques (e.g., encryption, digital signatures) to protect information.<br><strong>Electronic Communication:</strong> Any transmission of data, voice, or video via electronic means.<br><strong>Network Perimeter:</strong> The boundary between [Company Name]''s internal trusted network and external untrusted networks.<br><strong>Secure Channel:</strong> A communication path protected by encryption and authentication mechanisms.<br><strong>TLS/SSL:</strong> Transport Layer Security/Secure Sockets Layer, protocols for securing communications over a network.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all electronic communications containing sensitive or confidential information shall be protected using industry-standard cryptographic and security controls. The use of unsecured or unauthorized communication channels is strictly prohibited. All network infrastructure and communication services must be designed, configured, and operated to prevent unauthorized disclosure, alteration, or destruction of information.</p>

<h4>5.1 Network Encryption</h4><p>All sensitive data transmitted across internal networks, wide area networks (WANs), or the internet shall be encrypted using approved protocols. For web applications and services, TLS 1.2 or higher must be enforced, with weak cipher suites (e.g., RC4, DES, 3DES) explicitly disabled. Internal administrative traffic (e.g., SSH, RDP) must utilize strong encryption.</p><h4>5.2 Wireless Network Security</h4><p>All wireless infrastructure and access points must be approved and managed by the Information Security or IT Department. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted networks for business purposes is forbidden. Corporate wireless networks shall implement WPA2-Enterprise or WPA3-Enterprise with 802.1X authentication. Guest wireless access must be segregated from the corporate network via a dedicated VLAN and firewall rules.</p><h4>5.3 Virtual Private Networks (VPNs)</h4><p>Remote access to [Company Name]''s internal network shall only be permitted via an approved corporate VPN. The VPN solution must support multi-factor authentication (MFA) and use strong encryption (e.g., AES-256). Split-tunneling is prohibited; all remote user traffic must be routed through the VPN gateway for inspection and logging.</p><h4>5.4 Email Security</h4><p>All external email containing sensitive information must be encrypted in transit using TLS. For high-sensitivity data, end-to-end encryption or secure email gateways with encryption capabilities shall be employed. Email systems must be configured with anti-spoofing controls (SPF, DKIM, DMARC) and robust anti-malware filtering.</p><h4>5.5 Voice and Video Communications</h4><p>Voice-over-IP (VoIP) and video conferencing systems used for business discussions involving confidential information must utilize encryption for both signaling and media streams. The use of consumer-grade, unsecured services for sensitive meetings is prohibited.</p><h4>5.6 Network Segmentation and Isolation</h4><p>Critical systems (e.g., databases, payment systems) shall be placed in logically isolated network segments (e.g., DMZs, private subnets) protected by firewalls. Communication between segments must be explicitly allowed based on the principle of least privilege and logged.</p><h4>5.7 Logging and Monitoring</h4><p>All perimeter security devices (firewalls, VPN concentrators, proxies) and critical network infrastructure shall have logging enabled. Logs must capture source/destination IP, port, protocol, timestamp, and action taken (allow/deny). These logs shall be centrally collected, retained for a minimum of 90 days, and monitored for anomalous or malicious activity.</p>

<p>This policy supports compliance with ISO/IEC 27001:2022 controls, specifically A.8.21 (Security of network services), A.8.22 (Segregation of networks), A.8.23 (Web filtering), A.8.24 (Use of cryptography), and A.8.25 (Secure development life cycle). It also addresses relevant requirements of SOC 2 (Security and Confidentiality criteria), GDPR, HIPAA, and other data protection regulations pertaining to data in transit. All personnel are responsible for understanding and adhering to the legal and contractual obligations related to the information they handle.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the policy''s approval, implementation, and oversight.<br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for designing technical controls, monitoring compliance, and investigating security incidents related to communications.<br><strong>IT/Network Operations:</strong> Responsible for the secure configuration, maintenance, and monitoring of all network and communication infrastructure as defined in this policy.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must use only approved communication channels and services, report suspected security weaknesses or incidents, and complete required security awareness training.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement, and legal prosecution. The Information Security Team is authorized to block, disconnect, or restrict any network device, service, or connection that is found to be non-compliant and poses an immediate risk to [Company Name].</p>

<p>This Communications Security Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO or designated authority at least annually, or following significant changes to the threat landscape, technology infrastructure, or applicable regulations. Revisions must be formally approved and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. The version control and revision history shall be maintained by the Information Security Team.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Communications Security Policy establishes the mandatory controls and procedures to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of [Company Name]''s information assets during electronic transmission over internal and external networks. The policy ensures that all communications are secured against unauthorized access, interception, modification, or denial of service, in alignment with business requirements and regulatory obligations.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties (collectively, \\\"Personnel\\\") who access, use, or manage [Company Name]''s information systems, networks, or data. It encompasses all forms of electronic communication, including but not limited to: data transmitted over internal networks (wired and wireless), virtual private networks (VPNs), internet connections, email, instant messaging, voice-over-IP (VoIP), and any communication with external entities.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Cryptographic Controls:</strong> The use of cryptographic techniques (e.g., encryption, digital signatures) to protect information.<br><strong>Electronic Communication:</strong> Any transmission of data, voice, or video via electronic means.<br><strong>Network Perimeter:</strong> The boundary between [Company Name]''s internal trusted network and external untrusted networks.<br><strong>Secure Channel:</strong> A communication path protected by encryption and authentication mechanisms.<br><strong>TLS/SSL:</strong> Transport Layer Security/Secure Sockets Layer, protocols for securing communications over a network.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates that all electronic communications containing sensitive or confidential information shall be protected using industry-standard cryptographic and security controls. The use of unsecured or unauthorized communication channels is strictly prohibited. All network infrastructure and communication services must be designed, configured, and operated to prevent unauthorized disclosure, alteration, or destruction of information.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1 Network Encryption</h4><p>All sensitive data transmitted across internal networks, wide area networks (WANs), or the internet shall be encrypted using approved protocols. For web applications and services, TLS 1.2 or higher must be enforced, with weak cipher suites (e.g., RC4, DES, 3DES) explicitly disabled. Internal administrative traffic (e.g., SSH, RDP) must utilize strong encryption.</p><h4>5.2 Wireless Network Security</h4><p>All wireless infrastructure and access points must be approved and managed by the Information Security or IT Department. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted networks for business purposes is forbidden. Corporate wireless networks shall implement WPA2-Enterprise or WPA3-Enterprise with 802.1X authentication. Guest wireless access must be segregated from the corporate network via a dedicated VLAN and firewall rules.</p><h4>5.3 Virtual Private Networks (VPNs)</h4><p>Remote access to [Company Name]''s internal network shall only be permitted via an approved corporate VPN. The VPN solution must support multi-factor authentication (MFA) and use strong encryption (e.g., AES-256). Split-tunneling is prohibited; all remote user traffic must be routed through the VPN gateway for inspection and logging.</p><h4>5.4 Email Security</h4><p>All external email containing sensitive information must be encrypted in transit using TLS. For high-sensitivity data, end-to-end encryption or secure email gateways with encryption capabilities shall be employed. Email systems must be configured with anti-spoofing controls (SPF, DKIM, DMARC) and robust anti-malware filtering.</p><h4>5.5 Voice and Video Communications</h4><p>Voice-over-IP (VoIP) and video conferencing systems used for business discussions involving confidential information must utilize encryption for both signaling and media streams. The use of consumer-grade, unsecured services for sensitive meetings is prohibited.</p><h4>5.6 Network Segmentation and Isolation</h4><p>Critical systems (e.g., databases, payment systems) shall be placed in logically isolated network segments (e.g., DMZs, private subnets) protected by firewalls. Communication between segments must be explicitly allowed based on the principle of least privilege and logged.</p><h4>5.7 Logging and Monitoring</h4><p>All perimeter security devices (firewalls, VPN concentrators, proxies) and critical network infrastructure shall have logging enabled. Logs must capture source/destination IP, port, protocol, timestamp, and action taken (allow/deny). These logs shall be centrally collected, retained for a minimum of 90 days, and monitored for anomalous or malicious activity.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy supports compliance with ISO/IEC 27001:2022 controls, specifically A.8.21 (Security of network services), A.8.22 (Segregation of networks), A.8.23 (Web filtering), A.8.24 (Use of cryptography), and A.8.25 (Secure development life cycle). It also addresses relevant requirements of SOC 2 (Security and Confidentiality criteria), GDPR, HIPAA, and other data protection regulations pertaining to data in transit. All personnel are responsible for understanding and adhering to the legal and contractual obligations related to the information they handle.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the policy''s approval, implementation, and oversight.<br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for designing technical controls, monitoring compliance, and investigating security incidents related to communications.<br><strong>IT/Network Operations:</strong> Responsible for the secure configuration, maintenance, and monitoring of all network and communication infrastructure as defined in this policy.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must use only approved communication channels and services, report suspected security weaknesses or incidents, and complete required security awareness training.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement, and legal prosecution. The Information Security Team is authorized to block, disconnect, or restrict any network device, service, or connection that is found to be non-compliant and poses an immediate risk to [Company Name].</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This Communications Security Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO or designated authority at least annually, or following significant changes to the threat landscape, technology infrastructure, or applicable regulations. Revisions must be formally approved and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. The version control and revision history shall be maintained by the Information Security Team.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-19 06:15:56.491566', '"[\"ISO 27001\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-20 06:03:28.638', NULL),
	(57, '235550', 'Start-up Security Policy', '<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>This policy establishes the mandatory security objectives, principles, and governance framework for [Company Name]. Its purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of all information assets, systems, and networks against unauthorized access, disclosure, modification, destruction, or disruption. This policy serves as the foundational directive for all security-related activities and forms the basis for compliance with SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria and ISO/IEC 27001 requirements.</p>

<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>This policy applies universally to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third-party personnel (collectively, "Personnel") who access, process, store, or transmit [Company Name] information or utilize its information systems. It encompasses all information assets, regardless of format (electronic, physical, verbal), all company-owned or managed information technology resources (including endpoints, servers, network infrastructure, cloud services, and software), and all company facilities. This policy is in effect at all times and in all locations where company business is conducted.</p>

<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><ul><li><strong>Information Asset:</strong> Any data, system, hardware, software, or service that has value to [Company Name].</li><li><strong>Confidential Information:</strong> Any non-public information pertaining to [Company Name], its customers, employees, or business operations, the disclosure of which could cause harm.</li><li><strong>Information System:</strong> A discrete set of information resources organized for the collection, processing, maintenance, use, sharing, dissemination, or disposition of information.</li><li><strong>Risk Appetite:</strong> The types and amount of risk, on a broad level, that [Company Name] is willing to accept in pursuit of its strategic objectives.</li><li><strong>Baseline Security Controls:</strong> The minimum set of protective measures that shall be implemented across all in-scope systems and environments.</li><li><strong>Least Privilege:</strong> The security principle that grants users and systems the minimum levels of access—or permissions—necessary to perform their authorized tasks.</li></ul>

<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>[Company Name] is committed to implementing and maintaining a comprehensive information security management system (ISMS). Security is a shared responsibility integrated into all business processes. All Personnel shall adhere to the principles of least privilege, defense-in-depth, and continuous monitoring. The unauthorized access, use, disclosure, disruption, modification, or destruction of information assets is strictly prohibited. Management mandates full compliance with this policy and all supporting standards and procedures.</p>

<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><h3>5.1 Access Control & Authentication</h3><p>5.1.1 All user accounts shall be provisioned based on documented authorization and the principle of least privilege. Generic or shared accounts are forbidden for individual human users in production environments.<br>5.1.2 Multi-factor authentication (MFA) must be enforced for all remote access (e.g., VPN, cloud consoles) and for all administrative access to critical systems.<br>5.1.3 Password complexity shall meet a minimum of 12 characters, combining uppercase, lowercase, numerals, and special symbols. Passwords must be hashed using a strong, adaptive algorithm (e.g., bcrypt, Argon2).<br>5.1.4 Access rights shall be reviewed and formally re-certified by system/data owners at least quarterly, with immediate revocation upon role termination.</p><h3>5.2 Data Protection & Encryption</h3><p>5.2.1 All Confidential Information at rest shall be encrypted using AES-256 or equivalent. Encryption keys must be managed through a dedicated key management service, separate from the encrypted data.<br>5.2.2 All data in transit over public or untrusted networks must be protected using TLS 1.2 or higher with strong cipher suites. The use of SSLv3, TLS 1.0, or TLS 1.1 is forbidden.<br>5.2.3 A formal data classification scheme (e.g., Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted) shall be defined and applied to all information assets. Handling procedures must be defined per classification level.</p><h3>5.3 System Hardening & Configuration Management</h3><p>5.3.1 All systems (servers, workstations, network devices) shall be hardened against a recognized industry benchmark (e.g., CIS Benchmarks) before deployment into any environment.<br>5.3.2 A formal change management process must govern all modifications to production systems, applications, and security configurations. Emergency changes shall be documented and reviewed post-implementation.<br>5.3.3 Unnecessary services, ports, protocols, and software shall be disabled or removed from all systems. Only explicitly authorized software may be installed on company-managed endpoints.</p><h3>5.4 Network Security</h3><p>5.4.1 All network infrastructure devices (routers, switches, firewalls) must be configured to deny-all by default, with rules explicitly allowing only necessary traffic. Rule bases shall be reviewed semi-annually.<br>5.4.2 All wireless network access must be secured using WPA2-Enterprise or WPA3. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted networks is strictly prohibited.<br>5.4.3 Production environments shall be logically segmented from development, testing, and corporate networks using firewalls or software-defined perimeters.</p><h3>5.5 Vulnerability & Patch Management</h3><p>5.5.1 All in-scope systems and applications shall be scanned for vulnerabilities using automated tools at least monthly. External penetration tests must be conducted annually by a qualified third party.<br>5.5.2 A risk-based patch management process shall be implemented. Critical and High severity security patches must be applied within 30 days of release or according to a stricter, risk-assessed timeline.<br>5.5.3 An inventory of all hardware and software assets, including dependencies, shall be maintained and updated automatically or reviewed quarterly.</p><h3>5.6 Logging, Monitoring, and Incident Response</h3><p>5.6.1 Security-relevant events (authentication successes/failures, configuration changes, data access, firewall denies) shall be logged centrally with immutable storage. Logs must be retained for a minimum of 90 days, with one year for critical systems.<br>5.6.2 A formal Security Operations Center (SOC) function or equivalent 24/7 monitoring capability must be established to detect and analyze anomalous activities.<br>5.6.3 A documented incident response plan shall be maintained, tested annually, and include procedures for containment, eradication, recovery, and communication. All incidents must be tracked to resolution.</p>

<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>All security activities shall be conducted in accordance with applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations. This includes, but is not limited to, data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), industry-specific mandates, and the requirements of the SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity, Privacy) and ISO/IEC 27001:2022. The Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) is responsible for interpreting these requirements and ensuring controls are designed to meet them. Regular compliance assessments and internal audits shall be performed to validate control effectiveness.</p>

<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><ul><li><strong>Board of Directors / Executive Leadership:</strong> Ultimate accountability for information security; approves risk appetite and provides strategic resources.</li><li><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Owns this policy and the overall security program; reports on security posture and risks to leadership.</li><li><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Implements and maintains technical security controls as defined in this policy and supporting standards.</li><li><strong>System & Data Owners:</strong> Classify information assets, authorize access, and are accountable for the security of their assigned systems/data.</li><li><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Mandated to comply with all security policies and procedures; required to report security incidents or concerns immediately.</li></ul>

<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>Any Personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal action. The CISO, in coordination with Human Resources and Legal departments, is authorized to investigate suspected violations. Corrective actions may include mandatory re-training, revocation of system access, or other measures deemed necessary to remediate risk and ensure future compliance. Enforcement actions shall be applied consistently and documented.</p>

<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in the threat landscape, or major business or regulatory changes. Proposed revisions must be approved by the executive leadership team. The official version of this policy shall be maintained in a centralized document repository accessible to all Personnel. All revisions shall be communicated to relevant stakeholders.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"Purpose\",\"content\":\"<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>This policy establishes the mandatory security objectives, principles, and governance framework for [Company Name]. Its purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of all information assets, systems, and networks against unauthorized access, disclosure, modification, destruction, or disruption. This policy serves as the foundational directive for all security-related activities and forms the basis for compliance with SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria and ISO/IEC 27001 requirements.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Scope\",\"content\":\"<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>This policy applies universally to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third-party personnel (collectively, \\\"Personnel\\\") who access, process, store, or transmit [Company Name] information or utilize its information systems. It encompasses all information assets, regardless of format (electronic, physical, verbal), all company-owned or managed information technology resources (including endpoints, servers, network infrastructure, cloud services, and software), and all company facilities. This policy is in effect at all times and in all locations where company business is conducted.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Definitions\",\"content\":\"<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><ul><li><strong>Information Asset:</strong> Any data, system, hardware, software, or service that has value to [Company Name].</li><li><strong>Confidential Information:</strong> Any non-public information pertaining to [Company Name], its customers, employees, or business operations, the disclosure of which could cause harm.</li><li><strong>Information System:</strong> A discrete set of information resources organized for the collection, processing, maintenance, use, sharing, dissemination, or disposition of information.</li><li><strong>Risk Appetite:</strong> The types and amount of risk, on a broad level, that [Company Name] is willing to accept in pursuit of its strategic objectives.</li><li><strong>Baseline Security Controls:</strong> The minimum set of protective measures that shall be implemented across all in-scope systems and environments.</li><li><strong>Least Privilege:</strong> The security principle that grants users and systems the minimum levels of access—or permissions—necessary to perform their authorized tasks.</li></ul>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>[Company Name] is committed to implementing and maintaining a comprehensive information security management system (ISMS). Security is a shared responsibility integrated into all business processes. All Personnel shall adhere to the principles of least privilege, defense-in-depth, and continuous monitoring. The unauthorized access, use, disclosure, disruption, modification, or destruction of information assets is strictly prohibited. Management mandates full compliance with this policy and all supporting standards and procedures.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><h3>5.1 Access Control & Authentication</h3><p>5.1.1 All user accounts shall be provisioned based on documented authorization and the principle of least privilege. Generic or shared accounts are forbidden for individual human users in production environments.<br>5.1.2 Multi-factor authentication (MFA) must be enforced for all remote access (e.g., VPN, cloud consoles) and for all administrative access to critical systems.<br>5.1.3 Password complexity shall meet a minimum of 12 characters, combining uppercase, lowercase, numerals, and special symbols. Passwords must be hashed using a strong, adaptive algorithm (e.g., bcrypt, Argon2).<br>5.1.4 Access rights shall be reviewed and formally re-certified by system/data owners at least quarterly, with immediate revocation upon role termination.</p><h3>5.2 Data Protection & Encryption</h3><p>5.2.1 All Confidential Information at rest shall be encrypted using AES-256 or equivalent. Encryption keys must be managed through a dedicated key management service, separate from the encrypted data.<br>5.2.2 All data in transit over public or untrusted networks must be protected using TLS 1.2 or higher with strong cipher suites. The use of SSLv3, TLS 1.0, or TLS 1.1 is forbidden.<br>5.2.3 A formal data classification scheme (e.g., Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted) shall be defined and applied to all information assets. Handling procedures must be defined per classification level.</p><h3>5.3 System Hardening & Configuration Management</h3><p>5.3.1 All systems (servers, workstations, network devices) shall be hardened against a recognized industry benchmark (e.g., CIS Benchmarks) before deployment into any environment.<br>5.3.2 A formal change management process must govern all modifications to production systems, applications, and security configurations. Emergency changes shall be documented and reviewed post-implementation.<br>5.3.3 Unnecessary services, ports, protocols, and software shall be disabled or removed from all systems. Only explicitly authorized software may be installed on company-managed endpoints.</p><h3>5.4 Network Security</h3><p>5.4.1 All network infrastructure devices (routers, switches, firewalls) must be configured to deny-all by default, with rules explicitly allowing only necessary traffic. Rule bases shall be reviewed semi-annually.<br>5.4.2 All wireless network access must be secured using WPA2-Enterprise or WPA3. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted networks is strictly prohibited.<br>5.4.3 Production environments shall be logically segmented from development, testing, and corporate networks using firewalls or software-defined perimeters.</p><h3>5.5 Vulnerability & Patch Management</h3><p>5.5.1 All in-scope systems and applications shall be scanned for vulnerabilities using automated tools at least monthly. External penetration tests must be conducted annually by a qualified third party.<br>5.5.2 A risk-based patch management process shall be implemented. Critical and High severity security patches must be applied within 30 days of release or according to a stricter, risk-assessed timeline.<br>5.5.3 An inventory of all hardware and software assets, including dependencies, shall be maintained and updated automatically or reviewed quarterly.</p><h3>5.6 Logging, Monitoring, and Incident Response</h3><p>5.6.1 Security-relevant events (authentication successes/failures, configuration changes, data access, firewall denies) shall be logged centrally with immutable storage. Logs must be retained for a minimum of 90 days, with one year for critical systems.<br>5.6.2 A formal Security Operations Center (SOC) function or equivalent 24/7 monitoring capability must be established to detect and analyze anomalous activities.<br>5.6.3 A documented incident response plan shall be maintained, tested annually, and include procedures for containment, eradication, recovery, and communication. All incidents must be tracked to resolution.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>All security activities shall be conducted in accordance with applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations. This includes, but is not limited to, data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), industry-specific mandates, and the requirements of the SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity, Privacy) and ISO/IEC 27001:2022. The Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) is responsible for interpreting these requirements and ensuring controls are designed to meet them. Regular compliance assessments and internal audits shall be performed to validate control effectiveness.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><ul><li><strong>Board of Directors / Executive Leadership:</strong> Ultimate accountability for information security; approves risk appetite and provides strategic resources.</li><li><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Owns this policy and the overall security program; reports on security posture and risks to leadership.</li><li><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Implements and maintains technical security controls as defined in this policy and supporting standards.</li><li><strong>System & Data Owners:</strong> Classify information assets, authorize access, and are accountable for the security of their assigned systems/data.</li><li><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Mandated to comply with all security policies and procedures; required to report security incidents or concerns immediately.</li></ul>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>Any Personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal action. The CISO, in coordination with Human Resources and Legal departments, is authorized to investigate suspected violations. Corrective actions may include mandatory re-training, revocation of system access, or other measures deemed necessary to remediate risk and ensure future compliance. Enforcement actions shall be applied consistently and documented.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in the threat landscape, or major business or regulatory changes. Proposed revisions must be approved by the executive leadership team. The official version of this policy shall be maintained in a centralized document repository accessible to all Personnel. All revisions shall be communicated to relevant stakeholders.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-22 09:39:18.056999', '"[\"SOC 2\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-20 06:04:13.04', NULL),
	(17, '12', 'Cryptography Policy', '<p>This Cryptography Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the use, management, and protection of cryptographic controls at [Company Name]. Its purpose is to ensure the confidentiality, integrity, authenticity, and non-repudiation of information assets in alignment with business objectives and regulatory obligations. The policy provides the authoritative framework for implementing cryptographic safeguards against unauthorized access, data breaches, and tampering.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, managed, or processed by [Company Name], including data at rest, in transit, and in use. It encompasses all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party service providers who develop, implement, manage, or interact with cryptographic systems or protected data. This includes, but is not limited to, applications, databases, network communications, cloud services, endpoints, and portable media.</p>

<p><strong>Cryptography:</strong> The discipline that embodies the principles, means, and methods for transforming data to hide its information content, establish its authenticity, prevent its undetected modification, and prevent its repudiation.<br><strong>Cryptographic Key:</strong> A parameter used in conjunction with a cryptographic algorithm that determines its operation (e.g., transformation of plaintext to ciphertext).<br><strong>Key Management:</strong> The activities involving the handling of cryptographic keys and related metadata during their entire lifecycle, including generation, storage, distribution, rotation, archival, and destruction.<br><strong>TLS/SSL:</strong> Transport Layer Security / Secure Sockets Layer, cryptographic protocols designed to provide communications security over a computer network.<br><strong>FIPS 140-2/3:</strong> Federal Information Processing Standards Publication 140-2/3, a U.S. government standard that specifies security requirements for cryptographic modules.<br><strong>Strong Cryptography:</strong> Cryptography that is based on industry-tested and accepted algorithms, along with strong key lengths and proper implementation.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates the use of strong, approved cryptography to protect the confidentiality and integrity of its sensitive information. The implementation of cryptographic controls shall be risk-based, consistently applied, and managed through a formal lifecycle. The use of weak, deprecated, or non-compliant cryptographic algorithms, protocols, or key lengths is strictly prohibited.</p>

<h4>5.1 Algorithm and Protocol Standards</h4><p>All cryptographic implementations shall use algorithms and protocols approved by [Company Name]''s Information Security Department. As a minimum baseline:<br>- Symmetric encryption shall use AES with a key length of 256 bits.<br>- Asymmetric encryption and digital signatures shall use RSA (3072-bit or higher) or Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) with curves of at least 256 bits (e.g., P-256).<br>- Cryptographic hash functions shall use SHA-256 or stronger (e.g., SHA-384, SHA-512).<br>- The use of deprecated algorithms (e.g., MD5, SHA-1, DES, 3DES, RC4) for any security purpose is forbidden.</p><h4>5.2 Transport Security</h4><p>All network communications transmitting sensitive or confidential data shall be encrypted in transit.<br>- External web applications and APIs must use TLS 1.2 or higher. TLS 1.0 and 1.1 are prohibited.<br>- TLS configurations must disable weak cipher suites (e.g., those using CBC mode, NULL, or anonymous authentication) and shall support perfect forward secrecy (PFS).<br>- Internal service-to-service communications within production environments must also be encrypted using mutually authenticated TLS (mTLS) or an equivalent approved protocol.</p><h4>5.3 Data at Rest Encryption</h4><p>Sensitive data stored on any medium must be encrypted.<br>- All databases containing sensitive information (e.g., PII, financial data, credentials) shall utilize application-layer encryption, transparent data encryption (TDE), or full-disk encryption.<br>- Encryption keys for data at rest shall not be stored on the same logical or physical system as the encrypted data they protect.<br>- Laptops, mobile devices, and removable media (e.g., USB drives) containing company data must employ full-disk encryption (e.g., BitLocker, FileVault) with a pre-boot authentication mechanism.</p><h4>5.4 Cryptographic Key Management</h4><p>A formal key management lifecycle policy shall be established and followed for all cryptographic keys.<br>- Keys shall be generated using approved, cryptographically secure random number generators.<br>- Secret and private keys shall be stored within a certified Hardware Security Module (HSM), cloud key management service (KMS), or a secure, dedicated key management server. Storage in configuration files, source code, or plaintext databases is strictly prohibited.<br>- Key distribution shall use secure channels. Symmetric keys shall never be transmitted in plaintext.<br>- Keys shall be rotated at least annually, or immediately upon suspicion of compromise. Key archival and secure destruction procedures must be documented and followed.</p><h4>5.5 Certificate Management</h4><p>All digital certificates used for authentication or encryption must be managed through a formal process.<br>- Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) certificates shall be issued by a trusted, managed Certificate Authority (CA). Self-signed certificates are prohibited in production environments except for specific, approved internal use cases.<br>- Certificate lifetimes shall not exceed 13 months (398 days).<br>- A process for monitoring, renewing, and revoking certificates must be implemented to prevent service disruption or use of expired certificates.</p><h4>5.6 Development and Implementation</h4><p>Cryptographic functions shall not be implemented using custom or "home-grown" algorithms. Only vetted, industry-standard cryptographic libraries (e.g., OpenSSL, Bouncy Castle, platform-native APIs) shall be used.<br>- All cryptographic implementations, including selection of algorithms, modes of operation (e.g., GCM, CBC), and initialization vectors (IVs), must be reviewed by the Information Security Department prior to deployment.<br>- Cryptographic modules used for protecting regulated data should be FIPS 140-2/3 validated where applicable.</p>

<p>Cryptographic controls shall be implemented to ensure compliance with all applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to GDPR, CCPA, HIPAA, PCI DSS, and SOX. The policy aligns with ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A controls A.10 (Cryptography) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security and Confidentiality. The Information Security Department is responsible for interpreting the cryptographic requirements of such frameworks and mapping them to this policy''s controls.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the approval, enforcement, and overall effectiveness of this policy.<br><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Responsible for defining cryptographic standards, maintaining the approved algorithms list, conducting security reviews of implementations, and auditing compliance.<br><strong>IT Operations/Cloud Teams:</strong> Responsible for the secure configuration, management, and monitoring of cryptographic systems (e.g., HSMs, KMS, TLS terminators) in accordance with this policy.<br><strong>Development Teams:</strong> Responsible for integrating approved cryptographic libraries and following secure coding practices as defined in section 5.6.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to use cryptographic systems as mandated (e.g., encrypted VPNs, encrypted drives) and to report any suspected misuse or weakness in cryptographic controls.</p>

<p>Any employee, contractor, or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal prosecution. The Information Security Department shall conduct regular audits and technical assessments (e.g., vulnerability scans, configuration reviews) to detect non-compliant cryptographic implementations. Systems found to be in violation shall be isolated from the network or decommissioned until remediated.</p>

<p>This Cryptography Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Department at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in the threat landscape, or updates to cryptographic standards (e.g., NIST guidelines). Revisions must be approved by the CISO. All changes shall be communicated to relevant stakeholders.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Cryptography Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the use, management, and protection of cryptographic controls at [Company Name]. Its purpose is to ensure the confidentiality, integrity, authenticity, and non-repudiation of information assets in alignment with business objectives and regulatory obligations. The policy provides the authoritative framework for implementing cryptographic safeguards against unauthorized access, data breaches, and tampering.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, managed, or processed by [Company Name], including data at rest, in transit, and in use. It encompasses all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party service providers who develop, implement, manage, or interact with cryptographic systems or protected data. This includes, but is not limited to, applications, databases, network communications, cloud services, endpoints, and portable media.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Cryptography:</strong> The discipline that embodies the principles, means, and methods for transforming data to hide its information content, establish its authenticity, prevent its undetected modification, and prevent its repudiation.<br><strong>Cryptographic Key:</strong> A parameter used in conjunction with a cryptographic algorithm that determines its operation (e.g., transformation of plaintext to ciphertext).<br><strong>Key Management:</strong> The activities involving the handling of cryptographic keys and related metadata during their entire lifecycle, including generation, storage, distribution, rotation, archival, and destruction.<br><strong>TLS/SSL:</strong> Transport Layer Security / Secure Sockets Layer, cryptographic protocols designed to provide communications security over a computer network.<br><strong>FIPS 140-2/3:</strong> Federal Information Processing Standards Publication 140-2/3, a U.S. government standard that specifies security requirements for cryptographic modules.<br><strong>Strong Cryptography:</strong> Cryptography that is based on industry-tested and accepted algorithms, along with strong key lengths and proper implementation.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates the use of strong, approved cryptography to protect the confidentiality and integrity of its sensitive information. The implementation of cryptographic controls shall be risk-based, consistently applied, and managed through a formal lifecycle. The use of weak, deprecated, or non-compliant cryptographic algorithms, protocols, or key lengths is strictly prohibited.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1 Algorithm and Protocol Standards</h4><p>All cryptographic implementations shall use algorithms and protocols approved by [Company Name]''s Information Security Department. As a minimum baseline:<br>- Symmetric encryption shall use AES with a key length of 256 bits.<br>- Asymmetric encryption and digital signatures shall use RSA (3072-bit or higher) or Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) with curves of at least 256 bits (e.g., P-256).<br>- Cryptographic hash functions shall use SHA-256 or stronger (e.g., SHA-384, SHA-512).<br>- The use of deprecated algorithms (e.g., MD5, SHA-1, DES, 3DES, RC4) for any security purpose is forbidden.</p><h4>5.2 Transport Security</h4><p>All network communications transmitting sensitive or confidential data shall be encrypted in transit.<br>- External web applications and APIs must use TLS 1.2 or higher. TLS 1.0 and 1.1 are prohibited.<br>- TLS configurations must disable weak cipher suites (e.g., those using CBC mode, NULL, or anonymous authentication) and shall support perfect forward secrecy (PFS).<br>- Internal service-to-service communications within production environments must also be encrypted using mutually authenticated TLS (mTLS) or an equivalent approved protocol.</p><h4>5.3 Data at Rest Encryption</h4><p>Sensitive data stored on any medium must be encrypted.<br>- All databases containing sensitive information (e.g., PII, financial data, credentials) shall utilize application-layer encryption, transparent data encryption (TDE), or full-disk encryption.<br>- Encryption keys for data at rest shall not be stored on the same logical or physical system as the encrypted data they protect.<br>- Laptops, mobile devices, and removable media (e.g., USB drives) containing company data must employ full-disk encryption (e.g., BitLocker, FileVault) with a pre-boot authentication mechanism.</p><h4>5.4 Cryptographic Key Management</h4><p>A formal key management lifecycle policy shall be established and followed for all cryptographic keys.<br>- Keys shall be generated using approved, cryptographically secure random number generators.<br>- Secret and private keys shall be stored within a certified Hardware Security Module (HSM), cloud key management service (KMS), or a secure, dedicated key management server. Storage in configuration files, source code, or plaintext databases is strictly prohibited.<br>- Key distribution shall use secure channels. Symmetric keys shall never be transmitted in plaintext.<br>- Keys shall be rotated at least annually, or immediately upon suspicion of compromise. Key archival and secure destruction procedures must be documented and followed.</p><h4>5.5 Certificate Management</h4><p>All digital certificates used for authentication or encryption must be managed through a formal process.<br>- Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) certificates shall be issued by a trusted, managed Certificate Authority (CA). Self-signed certificates are prohibited in production environments except for specific, approved internal use cases.<br>- Certificate lifetimes shall not exceed 13 months (398 days).<br>- A process for monitoring, renewing, and revoking certificates must be implemented to prevent service disruption or use of expired certificates.</p><h4>5.6 Development and Implementation</h4><p>Cryptographic functions shall not be implemented using custom or \\\"home-grown\\\" algorithms. Only vetted, industry-standard cryptographic libraries (e.g., OpenSSL, Bouncy Castle, platform-native APIs) shall be used.<br>- All cryptographic implementations, including selection of algorithms, modes of operation (e.g., GCM, CBC), and initialization vectors (IVs), must be reviewed by the Information Security Department prior to deployment.<br>- Cryptographic modules used for protecting regulated data should be FIPS 140-2/3 validated where applicable.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>Cryptographic controls shall be implemented to ensure compliance with all applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to GDPR, CCPA, HIPAA, PCI DSS, and SOX. The policy aligns with ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A controls A.10 (Cryptography) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security and Confidentiality. The Information Security Department is responsible for interpreting the cryptographic requirements of such frameworks and mapping them to this policy''s controls.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the approval, enforcement, and overall effectiveness of this policy.<br><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Responsible for defining cryptographic standards, maintaining the approved algorithms list, conducting security reviews of implementations, and auditing compliance.<br><strong>IT Operations/Cloud Teams:</strong> Responsible for the secure configuration, management, and monitoring of cryptographic systems (e.g., HSMs, KMS, TLS terminators) in accordance with this policy.<br><strong>Development Teams:</strong> Responsible for integrating approved cryptographic libraries and following secure coding practices as defined in section 5.6.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to use cryptographic systems as mandated (e.g., encrypted VPNs, encrypted drives) and to report any suspected misuse or weakness in cryptographic controls.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any employee, contractor, or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal prosecution. The Information Security Department shall conduct regular audits and technical assessments (e.g., vulnerability scans, configuration reviews) to detect non-compliant cryptographic implementations. Systems found to be in violation shall be isolated from the network or decommissioned until remediated.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This Cryptography Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Department at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in the threat landscape, or updates to cryptographic standards (e.g., NIST guidelines). Revisions must be approved by the CISO. All changes shall be communicated to relevant stakeholders.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-19 06:15:56.703635', '"[\"Both\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-20 06:04:52.445', NULL),
	(56, '898107', 'Data Protection Policy', '<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>This policy establishes the mandatory framework for protecting the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of all data assets owned, processed, or controlled by [Company Name]. It defines the principles, controls, and responsibilities required to safeguard data against unauthorized access, disclosure, alteration, destruction, or loss, and to ensure compliance with applicable legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations including, but not limited to, the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), ISO 27001, and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria.</p>

<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>This policy applies universally to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties (collectively, "Personnel") who access, process, store, or transmit [Company Name] data. It encompasses all data, regardless of format (electronic, paper, etc.) or state (at-rest, in-transit, in-use), processed on all company-owned or managed systems, networks, applications, and facilities, including cloud services and personal devices used for business purposes (BYOD).</p>

<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p><strong>Personal Data:</strong> Any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person (''data subject''), as defined by the GDPR.<br><strong>Confidential Data:</strong> Non-public proprietary information, including trade secrets, financial data, and strategic plans.<br><strong>Sensitive Data:</strong> A subset of Personal Data requiring heightened protection (e.g., racial/ethnic origin, political opinions, religious beliefs, health data, biometric data).<br><strong>Data Processing:</strong> Any operation performed on data, including collection, recording, organization, storage, alteration, retrieval, consultation, use, disclosure, erasure, or destruction.<br><strong>Data Controller:</strong> The entity that determines the purposes and means of processing Personal Data.<br><strong>Data Processor:</strong> The entity that processes Personal Data on behalf of the Controller.<br><strong>Encryption at Rest:</strong> The cryptographic protection of stored data using a minimum of AES-256.<br><strong>Encryption in Transit:</strong> The cryptographic protection of data traversing a network using TLS 1.2 or higher.</p>

<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>[Company Name] mandates that all data shall be protected according to its classification level, sensitivity, and associated legal requirements. All Personnel are strictly prohibited from accessing, using, or disclosing data except as necessary to perform authorized job functions. The organization is committed to implementing a defense-in-depth strategy, adhering to the principles of data minimization, purpose limitation, and accountability, and maintaining a state of continuous compliance with all relevant data protection frameworks.</p>

<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><h2>5.1 Data Classification and Handling</h2><p>All data assets must be classified according to the corporate Data Classification Standard as Public, Internal, Confidential, or Restricted. Handling procedures, including labeling, storage, transmission, and disposal requirements, shall be strictly enforced based on the assigned classification. Restricted and Confidential data shall not be stored on portable media (e.g., USB drives) without explicit written authorization and mandatory full-disk encryption.</p><h2>5.2 Access Control and Authentication</h2><p>Access to data shall be granted on a strict need-to-know and least-privilege basis. All access requests must be formally approved by the data owner or their designated delegate. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for all personnel accessing systems containing Confidential or Restricted data, or when accessing corporate networks remotely. Privileged access accounts (e.g., system administrators) shall be subject to heightened monitoring, session recording, and just-in-time provisioning.</p><h2>5.3 Encryption Requirements</h2><p>All Restricted and Confidential data must be encrypted at rest using a minimum of AES-256. All data transmissions over public or untrusted networks (including email) must be protected using TLS 1.2 or higher. The use of deprecated protocols (e.g., SSLv3, TLS 1.0/1.1) or weak ciphers is strictly forbidden. Encryption keys shall be managed through a dedicated Key Management Service (KMS) and stored separately from the encrypted data.</p><h2>5.4 Data Retention and Secure Disposal</h2><p>Data shall not be retained beyond the period necessary for its defined business purpose or as required by law. A formal data retention schedule must be maintained and enforced. At the end of the retention period, data must be securely and irreversibly destroyed. For electronic media, this requires cryptographic erasure (NIST 800-88 Clear/Purge) or physical destruction. Paper documents containing Confidential or Restricted data must be shredded via cross-cut shredder or incinerated.</p><h2>5.5 Logging, Monitoring, and Incident Response</h2><p>All access to, and processing of, Confidential and Restricted data must be logged. Logs must include user identity, timestamp, action performed, and data identifier. Logs shall be centrally aggregated, protected from tampering, and retained for a minimum of 90 days to support incident investigation. A formal Security Incident Response Plan must be maintained. Any suspected or confirmed data breach must be reported to the CISO or Data Protection Officer (DPO) immediately, triggering the incident response process.</p><h2>5.6 Third-Party and Cloud Security</h2><p>Before engaging any third-party vendor (Data Processor) that will handle [Company Name] data, a security assessment must be completed. A Data Processing Agreement (DPA) that contractually binds the vendor to equivalent protection standards is mandatory. For cloud services, the Shared Responsibility Model must be explicitly understood, and configuration baselines (e.g., disabling public read/write access on storage buckets) shall be enforced and audited quarterly.</p>

<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>[Company Name] shall comply with all applicable data protection laws and regulations in jurisdictions where it operates, including but not limited to the EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), the California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA), and sector-specific requirements. The organization shall maintain documented evidence of compliance for audits against ISO 27001:2022 Annex A controls (e.g., A.5, A.6, A.8, A.9, A.12, A.13, A.14, A.17, A.18) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity, Privacy). Data Protection Impact Assessments (DPIAs) shall be conducted for high-risk processing activities as mandated by the GDPR.</p>

<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p><strong>Board of Directors/Executive Management:</strong> Ultimate accountability for data protection governance and resource allocation.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Responsible for the development, implementation, and oversight of this policy and the overall security program.<br><strong>Data Protection Officer (DPO):</strong> Required under GDPR; oversees compliance, acts as point of contact for supervisory authorities and data subjects.<br><strong>Data Owners (Business Function Heads):</strong> Classify data, authorize access, and define retention periods for data within their domain.<br><strong>Data Custodians (IT/Security Teams):</strong> Implement and maintain technical controls (access, encryption, backups) as directed by data owners.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Mandated to complete annual data protection training, adhere to this policy, and report any security incidents or policy violations immediately.</p>

<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>Any violation of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and potential legal or civil penalties. The Information Security Department is authorized to monitor systems and network traffic to ensure compliance. Deliberate circumvention of security controls, unauthorized data exfiltration, or failure to report a data breach constitutes gross misconduct and will be dealt with accordingly.</p>

<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>This policy shall be reviewed and approved by the CISO and Legal Department at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, business operations, or applicable regulations. All revisions must be formally version-controlled, communicated to all personnel, and archived. The effectiveness of controls outlined in this policy shall be validated through internal audits and penetration testing exercises.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"Purpose\",\"content\":\"<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>This policy establishes the mandatory framework for protecting the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of all data assets owned, processed, or controlled by [Company Name]. It defines the principles, controls, and responsibilities required to safeguard data against unauthorized access, disclosure, alteration, destruction, or loss, and to ensure compliance with applicable legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations including, but not limited to, the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), ISO 27001, and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Scope\",\"content\":\"<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>This policy applies universally to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties (collectively, \\\"Personnel\\\") who access, process, store, or transmit [Company Name] data. It encompasses all data, regardless of format (electronic, paper, etc.) or state (at-rest, in-transit, in-use), processed on all company-owned or managed systems, networks, applications, and facilities, including cloud services and personal devices used for business purposes (BYOD).</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Definitions\",\"content\":\"<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p><strong>Personal Data:</strong> Any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person (''data subject''), as defined by the GDPR.<br><strong>Confidential Data:</strong> Non-public proprietary information, including trade secrets, financial data, and strategic plans.<br><strong>Sensitive Data:</strong> A subset of Personal Data requiring heightened protection (e.g., racial/ethnic origin, political opinions, religious beliefs, health data, biometric data).<br><strong>Data Processing:</strong> Any operation performed on data, including collection, recording, organization, storage, alteration, retrieval, consultation, use, disclosure, erasure, or destruction.<br><strong>Data Controller:</strong> The entity that determines the purposes and means of processing Personal Data.<br><strong>Data Processor:</strong> The entity that processes Personal Data on behalf of the Controller.<br><strong>Encryption at Rest:</strong> The cryptographic protection of stored data using a minimum of AES-256.<br><strong>Encryption in Transit:</strong> The cryptographic protection of data traversing a network using TLS 1.2 or higher.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>[Company Name] mandates that all data shall be protected according to its classification level, sensitivity, and associated legal requirements. All Personnel are strictly prohibited from accessing, using, or disclosing data except as necessary to perform authorized job functions. The organization is committed to implementing a defense-in-depth strategy, adhering to the principles of data minimization, purpose limitation, and accountability, and maintaining a state of continuous compliance with all relevant data protection frameworks.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><h2>5.1 Data Classification and Handling</h2><p>All data assets must be classified according to the corporate Data Classification Standard as Public, Internal, Confidential, or Restricted. Handling procedures, including labeling, storage, transmission, and disposal requirements, shall be strictly enforced based on the assigned classification. Restricted and Confidential data shall not be stored on portable media (e.g., USB drives) without explicit written authorization and mandatory full-disk encryption.</p><h2>5.2 Access Control and Authentication</h2><p>Access to data shall be granted on a strict need-to-know and least-privilege basis. All access requests must be formally approved by the data owner or their designated delegate. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for all personnel accessing systems containing Confidential or Restricted data, or when accessing corporate networks remotely. Privileged access accounts (e.g., system administrators) shall be subject to heightened monitoring, session recording, and just-in-time provisioning.</p><h2>5.3 Encryption Requirements</h2><p>All Restricted and Confidential data must be encrypted at rest using a minimum of AES-256. All data transmissions over public or untrusted networks (including email) must be protected using TLS 1.2 or higher. The use of deprecated protocols (e.g., SSLv3, TLS 1.0/1.1) or weak ciphers is strictly forbidden. Encryption keys shall be managed through a dedicated Key Management Service (KMS) and stored separately from the encrypted data.</p><h2>5.4 Data Retention and Secure Disposal</h2><p>Data shall not be retained beyond the period necessary for its defined business purpose or as required by law. A formal data retention schedule must be maintained and enforced. At the end of the retention period, data must be securely and irreversibly destroyed. For electronic media, this requires cryptographic erasure (NIST 800-88 Clear/Purge) or physical destruction. Paper documents containing Confidential or Restricted data must be shredded via cross-cut shredder or incinerated.</p><h2>5.5 Logging, Monitoring, and Incident Response</h2><p>All access to, and processing of, Confidential and Restricted data must be logged. Logs must include user identity, timestamp, action performed, and data identifier. Logs shall be centrally aggregated, protected from tampering, and retained for a minimum of 90 days to support incident investigation. A formal Security Incident Response Plan must be maintained. Any suspected or confirmed data breach must be reported to the CISO or Data Protection Officer (DPO) immediately, triggering the incident response process.</p><h2>5.6 Third-Party and Cloud Security</h2><p>Before engaging any third-party vendor (Data Processor) that will handle [Company Name] data, a security assessment must be completed. A Data Processing Agreement (DPA) that contractually binds the vendor to equivalent protection standards is mandatory. For cloud services, the Shared Responsibility Model must be explicitly understood, and configuration baselines (e.g., disabling public read/write access on storage buckets) shall be enforced and audited quarterly.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>[Company Name] shall comply with all applicable data protection laws and regulations in jurisdictions where it operates, including but not limited to the EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), the California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA), and sector-specific requirements. The organization shall maintain documented evidence of compliance for audits against ISO 27001:2022 Annex A controls (e.g., A.5, A.6, A.8, A.9, A.12, A.13, A.14, A.17, A.18) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity, Privacy). Data Protection Impact Assessments (DPIAs) shall be conducted for high-risk processing activities as mandated by the GDPR.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p><strong>Board of Directors/Executive Management:</strong> Ultimate accountability for data protection governance and resource allocation.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Responsible for the development, implementation, and oversight of this policy and the overall security program.<br><strong>Data Protection Officer (DPO):</strong> Required under GDPR; oversees compliance, acts as point of contact for supervisory authorities and data subjects.<br><strong>Data Owners (Business Function Heads):</strong> Classify data, authorize access, and define retention periods for data within their domain.<br><strong>Data Custodians (IT/Security Teams):</strong> Implement and maintain technical controls (access, encryption, backups) as directed by data owners.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Mandated to complete annual data protection training, adhere to this policy, and report any security incidents or policy violations immediately.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>Any violation of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and potential legal or civil penalties. The Information Security Department is authorized to monitor systems and network traffic to ensure compliance. Deliberate circumvention of security controls, unauthorized data exfiltration, or failure to report a data breach constitutes gross misconduct and will be dealt with accordingly.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>This policy shall be reviewed and approved by the CISO and Legal Department at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, business operations, or applicable regulations. All revisions must be formally version-controlled, communicated to all personnel, and archived. The effectiveness of controls outlined in this policy shall be validated through internal audits and penetration testing exercises.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-22 09:39:17.910448', '"[\"ISO 27001\",\"GDPR\",\"SOC 2\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-20 06:05:33.279', NULL),
	(66, 'POL-INTEL-001', 'Intelligent Access Control Policy', '<p>The purpose of this Intelligent Access Control Policy is to establish a formal, risk-based framework for the provisioning, management, monitoring, and revocation of logical and physical access to [Company Name]''s information assets, systems, networks, and facilities. This policy mandates the principle of least privilege and ensures that access is granted based on verified identity, business need, and role, thereby protecting the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information in alignment with ISO 27001:2022 Annex A.5 (Information Security Policies) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security and Confidentiality.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all individuals, entities, and systems that interact with [Company Name]''s information assets, including but not limited to: all employees (full-time, part-time, temporary); contractors, consultants, and third-party service providers; all information systems, whether owned, operated, leased, or cloud-based; all data, regardless of classification or storage location; and all physical facilities owned or occupied by [Company Name].</p>

<p><strong>Access Control:</strong> The process of granting or denying specific requests to: 1) obtain and use information and related information processing services; and 2) enter specific physical facilities.<br><strong>Principle of Least Privilege:</strong> The security principle that users and systems shall be granted the minimum levels of access—or permissions—necessary to perform their authorized tasks.<br><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> An authentication method that requires the user to provide two or more verification factors to gain access to a resource.<br><strong>Privileged Access:</strong> Any access that provides the ability to make significant configuration changes, override security controls, access sensitive data, or administer systems (e.g., root, administrator, domain admin, service accounts).<br><strong>Role-Based Access Control (RBAC):</strong> A method of regulating access to computer or network resources based on the roles of individual users within an enterprise.<br><strong>Just-In-Time (JIT) Access:</strong> A privileged access management strategy where elevated permissions are granted for a specific, limited time period to perform a defined task, after which they are automatically revoked.</p>

<p>[Company Name] shall implement and maintain a comprehensive, intelligent access control system to protect its information assets from unauthorized access, modification, or disclosure. Access to all systems and data shall be strictly controlled, logged, and audited. The implementation of access controls shall be based on business requirements, security best practices, and a formal risk assessment. Violations of this policy are strictly prohibited and may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal prosecution.</p>

<h3>5.1 Identity and Authentication Management</h3><p>5.1.1 A unique user ID shall be assigned to each individual for accountability. Shared or generic accounts are strictly prohibited for human users, except where technically unavoidable for system functions, in which case they shall be tightly controlled and monitored.<br>5.1.2 All user access to [Company Name]''s internal networks, cloud environments, and business applications must be authenticated. The use of weak or default passwords is forbidden.<br>5.1.3 <strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) is mandatory</strong> for: all remote network access (VPN, RDP, SSH); all access to administrative consoles (cloud portals, infrastructure management); all access to systems containing sensitive or confidential data as defined by the Data Classification Policy; and for all privileged users, without exception.<br>5.1.4 Password policies shall enforce a minimum length of 14 characters, complexity, and a maximum age of 90 days. Password history shall prevent the reuse of the last 5 passwords.</p><h3>5.2 Authorization and Access Provisioning</h3><p>5.2.1 Access rights shall be granted based on the formal <strong>principle of least privilege</strong> and <strong>need-to-know</strong>.<br>5.2.2 A formal, documented user access review and approval process shall exist for all access requests. Approval must be obtained from the data/system owner or designated manager prior to provisioning.<br>5.2.3 Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) shall be implemented where feasible to standardize access permissions based on job function.<br>5.2.4 Segregation of Duties (SoD) controls shall be implemented to prevent conflict of interest and fraud (e.g., a user who can request a payment cannot also approve it).</p><h3>5.3 Privileged Access Management (PAM)</h3><p>5.3.1 Privileged accounts (e.g., local administrators, domain admins, root, service accounts) shall be inventoried, and their use shall be strictly controlled and monitored.<br>5.3.2 Privileged sessions shall, where possible, be brokered through a dedicated PAM solution. Direct login with privileged credentials to critical systems is forbidden.<br>5.3.3 Just-In-Time (JIT) access elevation shall be implemented for privileged tasks, with approvals logged and sessions recorded.<br>5.3.4 All activity performed under a privileged account must be logged in an immutable audit trail, capturing command history, files accessed, and timestamps.</p><h3>5.4 Physical and Environmental Access</h3><p>5.4.1 Physical access to data centers, server rooms, and network closets shall be controlled via electronic access systems (e.g., card readers, biometrics). Access logs from these systems shall be retained for a minimum of 90 days.<br>5.4.2 Access to secure areas shall be reviewed quarterly. Lost or stolen access cards must be reported immediately to the Security Team for deactivation.<br>5.4.3 Visitors to secure areas must be escorted at all times and their entry and exit logged.</p><h3>5.5 Access Review and Recertification</h3><p>5.5.1 Access rights for all users shall be formally reviewed and recertified by system/data owners at least semi-annually, or upon any role change.<br>5.5.2 Privileged access rights shall be reviewed and recertified quarterly.<br>5.5.3 Any access rights not explicitly re-approved during the recertification cycle shall be automatically revoked.</p><h3>5.6 Access Revocation and Termination</h3><p>5.6.1 Access rights for employees, contractors, and third parties shall be revoked immediately upon termination of employment or contract. This process shall be integrated with the Human Resources (HR) termination workflow.<br>5.6.2 For planned departures, access shall be revoked no later than the individual''s last day of work. For unplanned or involuntary terminations, access shall be revoked concurrently with the termination notification.</p><h3>5.7 Logging, Monitoring, and Auditing</h3><p>5.7.1 All authentication events (successful and failed), privilege escalations, and access to critical data must be logged. Logs must include user ID, timestamp, source IP address, and action performed.<br>5.7.2 Access logs shall be centrally aggregated, protected from tampering, and retained for a minimum of 365 days to support forensic investigation and compliance audits.<br>5.7.3 Automated monitoring shall be configured to alert the Security Operations Center (SOC) of anomalous access patterns, brute-force attacks, or policy violations.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data protection laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), industry standards (e.g., PCI DSS for payment data), and contractual commitments to clients. The Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) is responsible for mapping policy controls to specific requirements of ISO 27001:2022 (notably A.5.1, A.5.15, A.5.16, A.5.17, A.8.1-A.8.4) and the SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (CC1, CC5, CC6). Regular internal and external audits will be conducted to verify adherence to this policy and the aforementioned frameworks.</p>

<p><strong>Board of Directors / Executive Management:</strong> Provide overall governance and resource commitment for the access control program.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Owns this policy, oversees its implementation, and reports on its effectiveness.<br><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department / System Owners:</strong> Responsible for the technical implementation of access controls, provisioning/de-provisioning user accounts, and maintaining system-specific access procedures.<br><strong>Data Owners / Business Unit Managers:</strong> Responsible for authorizing access requests for their respective data and systems and performing periodic access recertifications.<br><strong>Human Resources (HR):</strong> Responsible for promptly notifying IT and Security of employee status changes (hire, transfer, termination).<br><strong>All Users:</strong> Responsible for safeguarding their authentication credentials, complying with access control procedures, and reporting any suspected unauthorized access immediately.</p>

<p>Any individual found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement. Unauthorized access, attempts to circumvent access controls, sharing of credentials, or failure to report known policy violations are considered serious misconduct. Legal action may be pursued in cases involving theft, fraud, or willful damage to [Company Name]''s information assets. The CISO, in conjunction with Human Resources and Legal counsel, is authorized to investigate suspected violations and determine appropriate sanctions.</p>

<p>This Intelligent Access Control Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in technology, or updates to legal and regulatory requirements. Proposed revisions shall be submitted to the Information Security Steering Committee for approval. Upon approval, the revised policy shall be communicated to all relevant personnel. The version control and revision history of this document shall be maintained by the Information Security Department.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>The purpose of this Intelligent Access Control Policy is to establish a formal, risk-based framework for the provisioning, management, monitoring, and revocation of logical and physical access to [Company Name]''s information assets, systems, networks, and facilities. This policy mandates the principle of least privilege and ensures that access is granted based on verified identity, business need, and role, thereby protecting the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information in alignment with ISO 27001:2022 Annex A.5 (Information Security Policies) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security and Confidentiality.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all individuals, entities, and systems that interact with [Company Name]''s information assets, including but not limited to: all employees (full-time, part-time, temporary); contractors, consultants, and third-party service providers; all information systems, whether owned, operated, leased, or cloud-based; all data, regardless of classification or storage location; and all physical facilities owned or occupied by [Company Name].</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Access Control:</strong> The process of granting or denying specific requests to: 1) obtain and use information and related information processing services; and 2) enter specific physical facilities.<br><strong>Principle of Least Privilege:</strong> The security principle that users and systems shall be granted the minimum levels of access—or permissions—necessary to perform their authorized tasks.<br><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> An authentication method that requires the user to provide two or more verification factors to gain access to a resource.<br><strong>Privileged Access:</strong> Any access that provides the ability to make significant configuration changes, override security controls, access sensitive data, or administer systems (e.g., root, administrator, domain admin, service accounts).<br><strong>Role-Based Access Control (RBAC):</strong> A method of regulating access to computer or network resources based on the roles of individual users within an enterprise.<br><strong>Just-In-Time (JIT) Access:</strong> A privileged access management strategy where elevated permissions are granted for a specific, limited time period to perform a defined task, after which they are automatically revoked.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] shall implement and maintain a comprehensive, intelligent access control system to protect its information assets from unauthorized access, modification, or disclosure. Access to all systems and data shall be strictly controlled, logged, and audited. The implementation of access controls shall be based on business requirements, security best practices, and a formal risk assessment. Violations of this policy are strictly prohibited and may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal prosecution.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h3>5.1 Identity and Authentication Management</h3><p>5.1.1 A unique user ID shall be assigned to each individual for accountability. Shared or generic accounts are strictly prohibited for human users, except where technically unavoidable for system functions, in which case they shall be tightly controlled and monitored.<br>5.1.2 All user access to [Company Name]''s internal networks, cloud environments, and business applications must be authenticated. The use of weak or default passwords is forbidden.<br>5.1.3 <strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) is mandatory</strong> for: all remote network access (VPN, RDP, SSH); all access to administrative consoles (cloud portals, infrastructure management); all access to systems containing sensitive or confidential data as defined by the Data Classification Policy; and for all privileged users, without exception.<br>5.1.4 Password policies shall enforce a minimum length of 14 characters, complexity, and a maximum age of 90 days. Password history shall prevent the reuse of the last 5 passwords.</p><h3>5.2 Authorization and Access Provisioning</h3><p>5.2.1 Access rights shall be granted based on the formal <strong>principle of least privilege</strong> and <strong>need-to-know</strong>.<br>5.2.2 A formal, documented user access review and approval process shall exist for all access requests. Approval must be obtained from the data/system owner or designated manager prior to provisioning.<br>5.2.3 Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) shall be implemented where feasible to standardize access permissions based on job function.<br>5.2.4 Segregation of Duties (SoD) controls shall be implemented to prevent conflict of interest and fraud (e.g., a user who can request a payment cannot also approve it).</p><h3>5.3 Privileged Access Management (PAM)</h3><p>5.3.1 Privileged accounts (e.g., local administrators, domain admins, root, service accounts) shall be inventoried, and their use shall be strictly controlled and monitored.<br>5.3.2 Privileged sessions shall, where possible, be brokered through a dedicated PAM solution. Direct login with privileged credentials to critical systems is forbidden.<br>5.3.3 Just-In-Time (JIT) access elevation shall be implemented for privileged tasks, with approvals logged and sessions recorded.<br>5.3.4 All activity performed under a privileged account must be logged in an immutable audit trail, capturing command history, files accessed, and timestamps.</p><h3>5.4 Physical and Environmental Access</h3><p>5.4.1 Physical access to data centers, server rooms, and network closets shall be controlled via electronic access systems (e.g., card readers, biometrics). Access logs from these systems shall be retained for a minimum of 90 days.<br>5.4.2 Access to secure areas shall be reviewed quarterly. Lost or stolen access cards must be reported immediately to the Security Team for deactivation.<br>5.4.3 Visitors to secure areas must be escorted at all times and their entry and exit logged.</p><h3>5.5 Access Review and Recertification</h3><p>5.5.1 Access rights for all users shall be formally reviewed and recertified by system/data owners at least semi-annually, or upon any role change.<br>5.5.2 Privileged access rights shall be reviewed and recertified quarterly.<br>5.5.3 Any access rights not explicitly re-approved during the recertification cycle shall be automatically revoked.</p><h3>5.6 Access Revocation and Termination</h3><p>5.6.1 Access rights for employees, contractors, and third parties shall be revoked immediately upon termination of employment or contract. This process shall be integrated with the Human Resources (HR) termination workflow.<br>5.6.2 For planned departures, access shall be revoked no later than the individual''s last day of work. For unplanned or involuntary terminations, access shall be revoked concurrently with the termination notification.</p><h3>5.7 Logging, Monitoring, and Auditing</h3><p>5.7.1 All authentication events (successful and failed), privilege escalations, and access to critical data must be logged. Logs must include user ID, timestamp, source IP address, and action performed.<br>5.7.2 Access logs shall be centrally aggregated, protected from tampering, and retained for a minimum of 365 days to support forensic investigation and compliance audits.<br>5.7.3 Automated monitoring shall be configured to alert the Security Operations Center (SOC) of anomalous access patterns, brute-force attacks, or policy violations.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data protection laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), industry standards (e.g., PCI DSS for payment data), and contractual commitments to clients. The Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) is responsible for mapping policy controls to specific requirements of ISO 27001:2022 (notably A.5.1, A.5.15, A.5.16, A.5.17, A.8.1-A.8.4) and the SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (CC1, CC5, CC6). Regular internal and external audits will be conducted to verify adherence to this policy and the aforementioned frameworks.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Board of Directors / Executive Management:</strong> Provide overall governance and resource commitment for the access control program.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Owns this policy, oversees its implementation, and reports on its effectiveness.<br><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department / System Owners:</strong> Responsible for the technical implementation of access controls, provisioning/de-provisioning user accounts, and maintaining system-specific access procedures.<br><strong>Data Owners / Business Unit Managers:</strong> Responsible for authorizing access requests for their respective data and systems and performing periodic access recertifications.<br><strong>Human Resources (HR):</strong> Responsible for promptly notifying IT and Security of employee status changes (hire, transfer, termination).<br><strong>All Users:</strong> Responsible for safeguarding their authentication credentials, complying with access control procedures, and reporting any suspected unauthorized access immediately.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any individual found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement. Unauthorized access, attempts to circumvent access controls, sharing of credentials, or failure to report known policy violations are considered serious misconduct. Legal action may be pursued in cases involving theft, fraud, or willful damage to [Company Name]''s information assets. The CISO, in conjunction with Human Resources and Legal counsel, is authorized to investigate suspected violations and determine appropriate sanctions.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This Intelligent Access Control Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in technology, or updates to legal and regulatory requirements. Proposed revisions shall be submitted to the Information Security Steering Committee for approval. Upon approval, the revised policy shall be communicated to all relevant personnel. The version control and revision history of this document shall be maintained by the Information Security Department.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2026-02-18 19:51:08.650427', '"[\"ISO 27001\",\"SOC 2\"]"', 1, true, NULL, '2026-02-20 06:06:20.893', '"[{\"id\":\"usesCloud\",\"question\":\"Does the organization use cloud infrastructure (AWS/Azure/GCP)?\",\"type\":\"boolean\",\"defaultValue\":true,\"category\":\"Infrastructure\"},{\"id\":\"mfaVendor\",\"question\":\"What is your primary MFA provider?\",\"type\":\"select\",\"options\":[\"Okta\",\"Microsoft Authenticator\",\"Duo\",\"Google Authenticator\"],\"defaultValue\":\"Okta\",\"category\":\"Security Tools\"}]"'),
	(32, '665128', 'Remote Access Policy', '<p>This Remote Access Policy establishes the mandatory security controls and governance framework for all remote access connections to [Company Name]''s internal networks, systems, and data. The purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate information assets by ensuring remote access is provisioned, monitored, and managed in a secure and compliant manner, in alignment with ISO 27001 and SOC 2 trust service criteria.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third-party agents (collectively, "Authorized Users") who require access to internal corporate resources from any external or non-corporately managed location. It encompasses all remote access methods, including but not limited to Virtual Private Networks (VPNs), remote desktop protocols (RDP, VNC), cloud-based application access, and secure shell (SSH). This policy applies to all corporate-owned and personally-owned devices used for such access.</p>

<p><strong>Remote Access:</strong> Any connection to [Company Name]''s internal network or systems initiated from a location outside the physical corporate perimeter.<br/><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> An authentication method that requires the user to provide two or more verification factors to gain access.<br/><strong>Virtual Private Network (VPN):</strong> A secured, encrypted tunnel for network traffic between a remote device and the corporate network.<br/><strong>Privileged Access:</strong> Access rights that allow for the configuration, management, or significant alteration of systems, applications, or data.<br/><strong>Endpoint Security Agent:</strong> Mandatory software installed on a device to enforce security policies (e.g., antivirus, host-based firewall, disk encryption).</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all remote access to corporate information resources shall be strictly controlled, centrally managed, and continuously monitored. Remote access is a privilege granted based on business necessity and adherence to the security requirements defined herein. Unauthorized remote access connections or the circumvention of defined security controls are strictly prohibited and constitute a violation of this policy.</p>

<h4>5.1 Authorization and Provisioning</h4><p>All requests for remote access must be formally approved by the employee''s department head and the Information Security team prior to provisioning. Access shall be granted on a least-privilege basis and must be reviewed quarterly.</p><h4>5.2 Authentication Controls</h4><p>All remote access sessions must be protected by [Company Name]-approved Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA). The use of password-only authentication for remote access is forbidden. MFA shall be required at initial connection and may be required again for access to highly sensitive systems.</p><h4>5.3 Connection Security</h4><p>All remote network access must be routed through the corporate-approved Virtual Private Network (VPN) gateway. The VPN must use strong encryption (e.g., AES-256-GCM) and a secure key exchange protocol (e.g., IKEv2). Split-tunneling is prohibited; all client traffic must be routed through the VPN tunnel while connected.</p><h4>5.4 Endpoint Security Requirements</h4><p>Any device used for remote access must have the corporate Endpoint Security Agent installed, enabled, and reporting as compliant. Mandatory controls include: a host-based firewall set to deny-by-default, up-to-date anti-malware software, full-disk encryption for data-at-rest, and a supported operating system with all critical security patches applied within 14 days of release.</p><h4>5.5 Session Management and Timeouts</h4><p>Remote access sessions shall be configured to automatically lock after 15 minutes of user inactivity and terminate after a maximum of 12 hours of continuous connectivity, requiring re-authentication. All session establishment and termination events must be logged.</p><h4>5.6 Prohibited Activities and Environments</h4><p>Accessing corporate resources from public or untrusted wireless networks (e.g., airports, cafes) without the VPN is strictly prohibited. The use of corporate credentials on non-corporate devices or for personal services is forbidden. Storing sensitive corporate data on local drives of remote devices is not permitted unless encrypted and explicitly authorized.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to satisfy specific controls from ISO 27001:2022 Annex A (notably A.5.15, A.5.16, A.8.22, A.8.23, A.8.24, A.8.25) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria relevant to Security and Confidentiality. All remote access logging must be maintained to support audit requirements for a minimum of 90 days, with key security events retained for one year. Remote access activities are subject to monitoring and review to ensure compliance with this policy and relevant data protection regulations.</p>

<p><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for the design, implementation, and monitoring of remote access security controls; conducting periodic reviews and audits.<br/><strong>IT Department / Network Team:</strong> Responsible for the provisioning, maintenance, and technical operation of the remote access infrastructure (e.g., VPN concentrators).<br/><strong>Authorized Users:</strong> Responsible for adhering to all policy requirements, securing their remote access devices, and reporting any suspected security incidents immediately.<br/><strong>Department Heads / Managers:</strong> Responsible for approving remote access requests based on business need and ensuring their staff are aware of and comply with this policy.</p>

<p>Any employee or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal action. The Information Security team is authorized to immediately disable the remote access of any user or device found to be non-compliant or posing a security risk to [Company Name].</p>

<p>This Remote Access Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security team at least annually, or following significant changes to the technical infrastructure or threat landscape. Revisions must be approved by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) or designated authority.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Remote Access Policy establishes the mandatory security controls and governance framework for all remote access connections to [Company Name]''s internal networks, systems, and data. The purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate information assets by ensuring remote access is provisioned, monitored, and managed in a secure and compliant manner, in alignment with ISO 27001 and SOC 2 trust service criteria.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third-party agents (collectively, \\\"Authorized Users\\\") who require access to internal corporate resources from any external or non-corporately managed location. It encompasses all remote access methods, including but not limited to Virtual Private Networks (VPNs), remote desktop protocols (RDP, VNC), cloud-based application access, and secure shell (SSH). This policy applies to all corporate-owned and personally-owned devices used for such access.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Remote Access:</strong> Any connection to [Company Name]''s internal network or systems initiated from a location outside the physical corporate perimeter.<br/><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> An authentication method that requires the user to provide two or more verification factors to gain access.<br/><strong>Virtual Private Network (VPN):</strong> A secured, encrypted tunnel for network traffic between a remote device and the corporate network.<br/><strong>Privileged Access:</strong> Access rights that allow for the configuration, management, or significant alteration of systems, applications, or data.<br/><strong>Endpoint Security Agent:</strong> Mandatory software installed on a device to enforce security policies (e.g., antivirus, host-based firewall, disk encryption).</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates that all remote access to corporate information resources shall be strictly controlled, centrally managed, and continuously monitored. Remote access is a privilege granted based on business necessity and adherence to the security requirements defined herein. Unauthorized remote access connections or the circumvention of defined security controls are strictly prohibited and constitute a violation of this policy.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1 Authorization and Provisioning</h4><p>All requests for remote access must be formally approved by the employee''s department head and the Information Security team prior to provisioning. Access shall be granted on a least-privilege basis and must be reviewed quarterly.</p><h4>5.2 Authentication Controls</h4><p>All remote access sessions must be protected by [Company Name]-approved Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA). The use of password-only authentication for remote access is forbidden. MFA shall be required at initial connection and may be required again for access to highly sensitive systems.</p><h4>5.3 Connection Security</h4><p>All remote network access must be routed through the corporate-approved Virtual Private Network (VPN) gateway. The VPN must use strong encryption (e.g., AES-256-GCM) and a secure key exchange protocol (e.g., IKEv2). Split-tunneling is prohibited; all client traffic must be routed through the VPN tunnel while connected.</p><h4>5.4 Endpoint Security Requirements</h4><p>Any device used for remote access must have the corporate Endpoint Security Agent installed, enabled, and reporting as compliant. Mandatory controls include: a host-based firewall set to deny-by-default, up-to-date anti-malware software, full-disk encryption for data-at-rest, and a supported operating system with all critical security patches applied within 14 days of release.</p><h4>5.5 Session Management and Timeouts</h4><p>Remote access sessions shall be configured to automatically lock after 15 minutes of user inactivity and terminate after a maximum of 12 hours of continuous connectivity, requiring re-authentication. All session establishment and termination events must be logged.</p><h4>5.6 Prohibited Activities and Environments</h4><p>Accessing corporate resources from public or untrusted wireless networks (e.g., airports, cafes) without the VPN is strictly prohibited. The use of corporate credentials on non-corporate devices or for personal services is forbidden. Storing sensitive corporate data on local drives of remote devices is not permitted unless encrypted and explicitly authorized.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy is designed to satisfy specific controls from ISO 27001:2022 Annex A (notably A.5.15, A.5.16, A.8.22, A.8.23, A.8.24, A.8.25) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria relevant to Security and Confidentiality. All remote access logging must be maintained to support audit requirements for a minimum of 90 days, with key security events retained for one year. Remote access activities are subject to monitoring and review to ensure compliance with this policy and relevant data protection regulations.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for the design, implementation, and monitoring of remote access security controls; conducting periodic reviews and audits.<br/><strong>IT Department / Network Team:</strong> Responsible for the provisioning, maintenance, and technical operation of the remote access infrastructure (e.g., VPN concentrators).<br/><strong>Authorized Users:</strong> Responsible for adhering to all policy requirements, securing their remote access devices, and reporting any suspected security incidents immediately.<br/><strong>Department Heads / Managers:</strong> Responsible for approving remote access requests based on business need and ensuring their staff are aware of and comply with this policy.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any employee or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal action. The Information Security team is authorized to immediately disable the remote access of any user or device found to be non-compliant or posing a security risk to [Company Name].</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This Remote Access Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security team at least annually, or following significant changes to the technical infrastructure or threat landscape. Revisions must be approved by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) or designated authority.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-20 21:17:22.618477', '"[\"ISO 27001\",\"SOC 2\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-20 06:06:51.802', NULL),
	(67, 'POL-SAMPLE-001', 'Master Intelligent Security Policy', '<p>The purpose of this Master Intelligent Security Policy is to establish the authoritative, risk-based framework for the governance, implementation, and continuous assurance of [Company Name]''s information security program. This policy mandates the integration of intelligent security controls, including but not limited to artificial intelligence (AI), machine learning (ML), and advanced analytics, to proactively protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information assets. It is designed to ensure compliance with the requirements of ISO/IEC 27001, SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria, and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF).</p>

<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, operated, or entrusted to [Company Name], irrespective of format or location. This includes, but is not limited to, all data, systems, networks, applications (including AI/ML models and supporting infrastructure), cloud services, and physical premises. The policy is binding on all personnel, including employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any third-party entities accessing or processing [Company Name] information assets.</p>

<p><strong>Intelligent Security System (ISS):</strong> Any integrated set of security controls that utilizes AI, ML, automation, or predictive analytics to detect, prevent, or respond to security threats.<br><strong>AI/ML Model:</strong> A software artifact that implements a decision-making process derived from data through algorithmic training.<br><strong>Model Artifact:</strong> The complete set of files required to reconstruct and operate an AI/ML model, including training data, code, parameters, and configuration.<br><strong>Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response (SOAR):</strong> A platform that integrates security tools and automates incident response workflows.<br><strong>Policy as Code (PaC):</strong> The management and deployment of security policies through machine-readable definition files, enabling automated enforcement and validation.</p>

<p>[Company Name] is committed to implementing a world-class, intelligent security posture. The organization shall architect its security controls to be proactive, adaptive, and data-driven. The deployment and operation of all Intelligent Security Systems are strictly governed by this policy to ensure they are effective, ethical, compliant, and aligned with business objectives. Any deviation from the controls specified herein is expressly forbidden without formal, documented exception approved by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO).</p>

<h4>5.1 Intelligent Security System Governance</h4><p>5.1.1 All proposed ISS deployments must undergo a formal security architecture review and risk assessment prior to procurement or development. Approval authority rests with the Information Security Department.<br>5.1.2 A centralized registry of all production ISS components, including AI/ML models, their intended purpose, data sources, and responsible owners, shall be maintained and reviewed quarterly.<br>5.1.3 The use of open-source or third-party AI/ML components within an ISS is forbidden unless a comprehensive software composition analysis and security review have been completed and documented.</p><h4>5.2 Data Security for Intelligent Systems</h4><p>5.2.1 All data used for training, testing, or operation of AI/ML models must be classified according to the Data Classification Policy. Use of Confidential or Restricted data requires explicit authorization.<br>5.2.2 Where technically feasible, synthetic data or differential privacy techniques shall be employed to minimize exposure of sensitive production data during model training.<br>5.2.3 All data in transit to and from ISS components must be encrypted using TLS 1.2 or higher. Data at rest within ISS storage must be encrypted using AES-256 or an approved equivalent.</p><h4>5.3 Identity and Access Management (IAM) for ISS</h4><p>5.3.1 Access to ISS administrative interfaces, model repositories, and training pipelines shall be strictly controlled via multi-factor authentication (MFA).<br>5.3.2 The principle of least privilege must be enforced. Service accounts for automated ISS tasks shall use short-lived, scoped credentials managed by a privileged access management (PAM) solution.<br>5.3.3 All access events, including model training initiation, configuration changes, and data queries by automated agents, must be logged to a centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system.</p><h4>5.4 Secure Development and Deployment of AI/ML Models</h4><p>5.4.1 AI/ML model development must follow a secure development lifecycle (SDLC) integrated with version control for all model artifacts.<br>5.4.2 All models must undergo adversarial robustness testing and bias/fairness evaluation before promotion to a production environment.<br>5.4.3 Deployment of models shall be conducted via automated pipelines that include static and dynamic security scanning of container images and deployment manifests.</p><h4>5.5 Monitoring, Logging, and Automated Response</h4><p>5.5.1 All ISS components shall generate audit logs detailing system health, performance metrics, security events, and all decision-making actions with high confidence scores.<br>5.5.2 Logs must be ingested into the corporate SIEM and retained for a minimum of 90 days for active analysis and one year for compliance purposes.<br>5.5.3 Security playbooks for common threat scenarios (e.g., model poisoning, data exfiltration via API) must be codified and deployed within the SOAR platform. Automated containment actions for high-fidelity alerts are mandated.</p><h4>5.6 Policy as Code and Continuous Compliance</h4><p>5.6.1 Security policies for cloud infrastructure hosting ISS (e.g., network segmentation, encryption settings) shall be defined, versioned, and enforced as code.<br>5.6.2 Continuous compliance scanning tools must be configured to validate the runtime state of ISS environments against Policy as Code definitions at least every 24 hours.<br>5.6.3 Any drift from the defined secure state shall generate a high-priority alert and trigger an automated remediation workflow or isolate the non-compliant asset.</p>

<p>The design and operation of Intelligent Security Systems shall adhere to all applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations. This includes, but is not limited to, data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), industry-specific mandates, and intellectual property rights. The Information Security Department, in conjunction with Legal and Compliance, is responsible for conducting periodic reviews to ensure ISS activities remain compliant. Any use of AI/ML for automated decision-making that has a legal or significant effect on individuals must include provisions for human review and explanation, as required by law.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for this policy''s approval, implementation, and effectiveness. Owns the intelligent security strategy.<br><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Responsible for policy execution, architecture reviews, threat modeling for ISS, monitoring, and incident response.<br><strong>Data Science & AI Engineering Teams:</strong> Responsible for implementing secure development practices, model testing, and maintaining the integrity of model artifacts.<br><strong>Cloud & Infrastructure Teams:</strong> Responsible for provisioning secure, compliant infrastructure for ISS as defined by Policy as Code.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to report any suspected security weaknesses or anomalous behavior related to intelligent systems immediately.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal prosecution. Third-party vendors in violation of policy requirements stipulated in their contracts may be subject to penalties, contract termination, and liability for damages. The Information Security Department is authorized to immediately disconnect or isolate any ISS component deemed to be operating in an insecure or non-compliant manner that poses an imminent risk to the organization.</p>

<p>This Master Intelligent Security Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the Information Security Steering Committee at least annually, or following any significant security incident, technological change, or update to relevant compliance frameworks. Revisions must be formally versioned, approved, and communicated to all stakeholders. The policy''s effectiveness shall be measured through key risk indicators (KRIs) and metrics reported to executive management.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>The purpose of this Master Intelligent Security Policy is to establish the authoritative, risk-based framework for the governance, implementation, and continuous assurance of [Company Name]''s information security program. This policy mandates the integration of intelligent security controls, including but not limited to artificial intelligence (AI), machine learning (ML), and advanced analytics, to proactively protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information assets. It is designed to ensure compliance with the requirements of ISO/IEC 27001, SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria, and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF).</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, operated, or entrusted to [Company Name], irrespective of format or location. This includes, but is not limited to, all data, systems, networks, applications (including AI/ML models and supporting infrastructure), cloud services, and physical premises. The policy is binding on all personnel, including employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any third-party entities accessing or processing [Company Name] information assets.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Intelligent Security System (ISS):</strong> Any integrated set of security controls that utilizes AI, ML, automation, or predictive analytics to detect, prevent, or respond to security threats.<br><strong>AI/ML Model:</strong> A software artifact that implements a decision-making process derived from data through algorithmic training.<br><strong>Model Artifact:</strong> The complete set of files required to reconstruct and operate an AI/ML model, including training data, code, parameters, and configuration.<br><strong>Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response (SOAR):</strong> A platform that integrates security tools and automates incident response workflows.<br><strong>Policy as Code (PaC):</strong> The management and deployment of security policies through machine-readable definition files, enabling automated enforcement and validation.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] is committed to implementing a world-class, intelligent security posture. The organization shall architect its security controls to be proactive, adaptive, and data-driven. The deployment and operation of all Intelligent Security Systems are strictly governed by this policy to ensure they are effective, ethical, compliant, and aligned with business objectives. Any deviation from the controls specified herein is expressly forbidden without formal, documented exception approved by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO).</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1 Intelligent Security System Governance</h4><p>5.1.1 All proposed ISS deployments must undergo a formal security architecture review and risk assessment prior to procurement or development. Approval authority rests with the Information Security Department.<br>5.1.2 A centralized registry of all production ISS components, including AI/ML models, their intended purpose, data sources, and responsible owners, shall be maintained and reviewed quarterly.<br>5.1.3 The use of open-source or third-party AI/ML components within an ISS is forbidden unless a comprehensive software composition analysis and security review have been completed and documented.</p><h4>5.2 Data Security for Intelligent Systems</h4><p>5.2.1 All data used for training, testing, or operation of AI/ML models must be classified according to the Data Classification Policy. Use of Confidential or Restricted data requires explicit authorization.<br>5.2.2 Where technically feasible, synthetic data or differential privacy techniques shall be employed to minimize exposure of sensitive production data during model training.<br>5.2.3 All data in transit to and from ISS components must be encrypted using TLS 1.2 or higher. Data at rest within ISS storage must be encrypted using AES-256 or an approved equivalent.</p><h4>5.3 Identity and Access Management (IAM) for ISS</h4><p>5.3.1 Access to ISS administrative interfaces, model repositories, and training pipelines shall be strictly controlled via multi-factor authentication (MFA).<br>5.3.2 The principle of least privilege must be enforced. Service accounts for automated ISS tasks shall use short-lived, scoped credentials managed by a privileged access management (PAM) solution.<br>5.3.3 All access events, including model training initiation, configuration changes, and data queries by automated agents, must be logged to a centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system.</p><h4>5.4 Secure Development and Deployment of AI/ML Models</h4><p>5.4.1 AI/ML model development must follow a secure development lifecycle (SDLC) integrated with version control for all model artifacts.<br>5.4.2 All models must undergo adversarial robustness testing and bias/fairness evaluation before promotion to a production environment.<br>5.4.3 Deployment of models shall be conducted via automated pipelines that include static and dynamic security scanning of container images and deployment manifests.</p><h4>5.5 Monitoring, Logging, and Automated Response</h4><p>5.5.1 All ISS components shall generate audit logs detailing system health, performance metrics, security events, and all decision-making actions with high confidence scores.<br>5.5.2 Logs must be ingested into the corporate SIEM and retained for a minimum of 90 days for active analysis and one year for compliance purposes.<br>5.5.3 Security playbooks for common threat scenarios (e.g., model poisoning, data exfiltration via API) must be codified and deployed within the SOAR platform. Automated containment actions for high-fidelity alerts are mandated.</p><h4>5.6 Policy as Code and Continuous Compliance</h4><p>5.6.1 Security policies for cloud infrastructure hosting ISS (e.g., network segmentation, encryption settings) shall be defined, versioned, and enforced as code.<br>5.6.2 Continuous compliance scanning tools must be configured to validate the runtime state of ISS environments against Policy as Code definitions at least every 24 hours.<br>5.6.3 Any drift from the defined secure state shall generate a high-priority alert and trigger an automated remediation workflow or isolate the non-compliant asset.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>The design and operation of Intelligent Security Systems shall adhere to all applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations. This includes, but is not limited to, data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), industry-specific mandates, and intellectual property rights. The Information Security Department, in conjunction with Legal and Compliance, is responsible for conducting periodic reviews to ensure ISS activities remain compliant. Any use of AI/ML for automated decision-making that has a legal or significant effect on individuals must include provisions for human review and explanation, as required by law.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for this policy''s approval, implementation, and effectiveness. Owns the intelligent security strategy.<br><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Responsible for policy execution, architecture reviews, threat modeling for ISS, monitoring, and incident response.<br><strong>Data Science & AI Engineering Teams:</strong> Responsible for implementing secure development practices, model testing, and maintaining the integrity of model artifacts.<br><strong>Cloud & Infrastructure Teams:</strong> Responsible for provisioning secure, compliant infrastructure for ISS as defined by Policy as Code.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to report any suspected security weaknesses or anomalous behavior related to intelligent systems immediately.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal prosecution. Third-party vendors in violation of policy requirements stipulated in their contracts may be subject to penalties, contract termination, and liability for damages. The Information Security Department is authorized to immediately disconnect or isolate any ISS component deemed to be operating in an insecure or non-compliant manner that poses an imminent risk to the organization.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This Master Intelligent Security Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the Information Security Steering Committee at least annually, or following any significant security incident, technological change, or update to relevant compliance frameworks. Revisions must be formally versioned, approved, and communicated to all stakeholders. The policy''s effectiveness shall be measured through key risk indicators (KRIs) and metrics reported to executive management.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2026-02-18 20:01:12.036173', '"[\"ISO 27001\",\"SOC 2\",\"NIST CSF\"]"', 1, true, NULL, '2026-02-20 06:07:32.862', '"[{\"id\":\"cloud_environment\",\"question\":\"Primary Cloud Infrastructure Provider?\",\"type\":\"select\",\"options\":[\"AWS\",\"Azure\",\"Google Cloud\",\"On-Premises\"],\"defaultValue\":\"AWS\"},{\"id\":\"biometrics_enabled\",\"question\":\"Is biometric authentication (FaceID/Fingerprint) enabled for mobile devices?\",\"type\":\"boolean\",\"defaultValue\":false},{\"id\":\"staff_count\",\"question\":\"Approximate total headcount (contractors included)?\",\"type\":\"number\",\"defaultValue\":25},{\"id\":\"data_privacy_officer\",\"question\":\"Designated Data Privacy Officer (DPO) Full Name?\",\"type\":\"text\",\"defaultValue\":\"John Doe\"}]"'),
	(6, '1', 'Information Security Policy', '<p>The purpose of this Information Security Policy is to establish the framework for protecting the information assets of [Company Name] from all threats, whether internal or external, deliberate or accidental. This policy mandates the implementation of a comprehensive Information Security Management System (ISMS) to ensure the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information in alignment with business objectives, regulatory requirements, and the standards set forth by ISO/IEC 27001 and SOC 2.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, managed, or processed by [Company Name], regardless of format or location. This includes, but is not limited to, electronic data, physical records, software, hardware, and network infrastructure. The policy is binding on all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties who access, use, or manage [Company Name]''s information assets or information processing facilities.</p>

<p><strong>Information Asset:</strong> Any data, document, or resource that has value to the organization.<br><strong>Confidentiality:</strong> The property that information is not made available or disclosed to unauthorized individuals, entities, or processes.<br><strong>Integrity:</strong> The property of safeguarding the accuracy and completeness of assets.<br><strong>Availability:</strong> The property of being accessible and usable upon demand by an authorized entity.<br><strong>ISMS:</strong> Information Security Management System, the systematic approach to managing sensitive company information.<br><strong>Risk Treatment:</strong> The process of selecting and implementing measures to modify risk.<br><strong>Residual Risk:</strong> The risk remaining after risk treatment.</p>

<p>[Company Name] is committed to protecting its information assets through a risk-based approach. Information security is the responsibility of every individual within the organization. All personnel shall adhere to the principles of least privilege, need-to-know, and defense-in-depth. Any activity that compromises the security of information assets is strictly prohibited and may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal prosecution.</p>

<h4>5.1 Access Control</h4><p>5.1.1. Access to information assets shall be granted based on the principle of least privilege and must be formally authorized.<br>5.1.2. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for all remote access and administrative accounts.<br>5.1.3. User access rights shall be reviewed and recertified at least quarterly. All dormant accounts (inactive for 90 days) shall be disabled or removed.<br>5.1.4. Privileged access sessions shall be logged, monitored, and subject to periodic audit.</p><h4>5.2 Cryptography</h4><p>5.2.1. All data classified as Confidential or higher must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 or an equivalent approved algorithm.<br>5.2.2. Data in transit over public or untrusted networks must be protected using TLS 1.2 or higher.<br>5.2.3. Cryptographic keys shall be managed through a dedicated key management system, with key generation, storage, rotation, and destruction procedures formally documented.</p><h4>5.3 Physical and Environmental Security</h4><p>5.3.1. Physical access to data centers, server rooms, and network closets shall be controlled via badge access and logged.<br>5.3.2. Environmental controls (temperature, humidity, fire suppression) must be maintained and monitored 24/7 for all critical infrastructure locations.</p><h4>5.4 Operations Security</h4><p>5.4.1. All systems must be hardened according to a defined baseline configuration before deployment.<br>5.4.2. A formal change management process shall govern all modifications to hardware, software, or network configurations.<br>5.4.3. Anti-malware software with automatic signature updates must be deployed on all endpoints and servers.</p><h4>5.5 Communications Security</h4><p>5.5.1. All wireless network infrastructure must be approved and configured by the Information Technology (IT) Department. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted networks for company business is strictly forbidden.<br>5.5.2. Network segmentation shall be implemented to isolate sensitive environments (e.g., production, development) from general corporate networks.</p><h4>5.6 System Acquisition, Development, and Maintenance</h4><p>5.6.1. Security requirements shall be defined and integrated into all phases of the system development lifecycle (SDLC).<br>5.6.2. All application code must undergo static and dynamic application security testing (SAST/DAST) prior to release.</p><h4>5.7 Supplier Relationships</h4><p>5.7.1. All third-party suppliers with access to [Company Name] information assets must be subject to a security assessment and bound by a contract containing explicit information security obligations.</p><h4>5.8 Information Security Incident Management</h4><p>5.8.1. A formal incident response plan shall be maintained and tested annually.<br>5.8.2. All suspected security incidents must be reported immediately to the Information Security team. Containment, eradication, and recovery actions shall be documented.</p><h4>5.9 Information Security Aspects of Business Continuity Management</h4><p>5.9.1. Business continuity and disaster recovery plans must include specific procedures for the restoration of information security controls.<br>5.9.2. Backups of critical data and systems shall be performed regularly, stored securely off-site, and tested for integrity at least semi-annually.</p><h4>5.10 Compliance</h4><p>5.10.1. Systems shall be configured to collect and retain security event logs for a minimum of 90 days to support monitoring and investigation.<br>5.10.2. Technical controls shall be implemented to prevent unauthorized data exfiltration and to ensure data processing complies with relevant privacy regulations.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to ensure [Company Name]''s compliance with relevant legal, statutory, regulatory, and contractual requirements related to information security and data protection, including but not limited to GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, HIPAA (if applicable), and the control frameworks of ISO/IEC 27001 and SOC 2 (Trust Services Criteria). The Legal and Compliance departments are responsible for identifying and communicating changes in the regulatory landscape that impact this policy.</p>

<p><strong>Board of Directors / Executive Management:</strong> Ultimate responsibility for information security governance and resource allocation.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Overall ownership of the ISMS, policy development, and security program oversight.<br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Implementation, monitoring, and operational management of security controls.<br><strong>IT Department:</strong> Secure configuration and maintenance of IT infrastructure in accordance with this policy.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for adhering to all security policies and procedures, and for reporting security incidents.</p>

<p>Violations of this Information Security Policy will be treated with the utmost seriousness. Individuals found to have violated this policy will be subject to disciplinary action commensurate with the severity of the breach, which may include verbal or written warnings, suspension of system access, termination of employment or contract, and pursuit of legal remedies. All enforcement actions shall be documented.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in business operations, or updates to relevant standards and regulations. All revisions must be approved by executive management. The most current version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel covered by its scope.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>The purpose of this Information Security Policy is to establish the framework for protecting the information assets of [Company Name] from all threats, whether internal or external, deliberate or accidental. This policy mandates the implementation of a comprehensive Information Security Management System (ISMS) to ensure the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information in alignment with business objectives, regulatory requirements, and the standards set forth by ISO/IEC 27001 and SOC 2.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, managed, or processed by [Company Name], regardless of format or location. This includes, but is not limited to, electronic data, physical records, software, hardware, and network infrastructure. The policy is binding on all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties who access, use, or manage [Company Name]''s information assets or information processing facilities.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Information Asset:</strong> Any data, document, or resource that has value to the organization.<br><strong>Confidentiality:</strong> The property that information is not made available or disclosed to unauthorized individuals, entities, or processes.<br><strong>Integrity:</strong> The property of safeguarding the accuracy and completeness of assets.<br><strong>Availability:</strong> The property of being accessible and usable upon demand by an authorized entity.<br><strong>ISMS:</strong> Information Security Management System, the systematic approach to managing sensitive company information.<br><strong>Risk Treatment:</strong> The process of selecting and implementing measures to modify risk.<br><strong>Residual Risk:</strong> The risk remaining after risk treatment.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] is committed to protecting its information assets through a risk-based approach. Information security is the responsibility of every individual within the organization. All personnel shall adhere to the principles of least privilege, need-to-know, and defense-in-depth. Any activity that compromises the security of information assets is strictly prohibited and may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal prosecution.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1 Access Control</h4><p>5.1.1. Access to information assets shall be granted based on the principle of least privilege and must be formally authorized.<br>5.1.2. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for all remote access and administrative accounts.<br>5.1.3. User access rights shall be reviewed and recertified at least quarterly. All dormant accounts (inactive for 90 days) shall be disabled or removed.<br>5.1.4. Privileged access sessions shall be logged, monitored, and subject to periodic audit.</p><h4>5.2 Cryptography</h4><p>5.2.1. All data classified as Confidential or higher must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 or an equivalent approved algorithm.<br>5.2.2. Data in transit over public or untrusted networks must be protected using TLS 1.2 or higher.<br>5.2.3. Cryptographic keys shall be managed through a dedicated key management system, with key generation, storage, rotation, and destruction procedures formally documented.</p><h4>5.3 Physical and Environmental Security</h4><p>5.3.1. Physical access to data centers, server rooms, and network closets shall be controlled via badge access and logged.<br>5.3.2. Environmental controls (temperature, humidity, fire suppression) must be maintained and monitored 24/7 for all critical infrastructure locations.</p><h4>5.4 Operations Security</h4><p>5.4.1. All systems must be hardened according to a defined baseline configuration before deployment.<br>5.4.2. A formal change management process shall govern all modifications to hardware, software, or network configurations.<br>5.4.3. Anti-malware software with automatic signature updates must be deployed on all endpoints and servers.</p><h4>5.5 Communications Security</h4><p>5.5.1. All wireless network infrastructure must be approved and configured by the Information Technology (IT) Department. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted networks for company business is strictly forbidden.<br>5.5.2. Network segmentation shall be implemented to isolate sensitive environments (e.g., production, development) from general corporate networks.</p><h4>5.6 System Acquisition, Development, and Maintenance</h4><p>5.6.1. Security requirements shall be defined and integrated into all phases of the system development lifecycle (SDLC).<br>5.6.2. All application code must undergo static and dynamic application security testing (SAST/DAST) prior to release.</p><h4>5.7 Supplier Relationships</h4><p>5.7.1. All third-party suppliers with access to [Company Name] information assets must be subject to a security assessment and bound by a contract containing explicit information security obligations.</p><h4>5.8 Information Security Incident Management</h4><p>5.8.1. A formal incident response plan shall be maintained and tested annually.<br>5.8.2. All suspected security incidents must be reported immediately to the Information Security team. Containment, eradication, and recovery actions shall be documented.</p><h4>5.9 Information Security Aspects of Business Continuity Management</h4><p>5.9.1. Business continuity and disaster recovery plans must include specific procedures for the restoration of information security controls.<br>5.9.2. Backups of critical data and systems shall be performed regularly, stored securely off-site, and tested for integrity at least semi-annually.</p><h4>5.10 Compliance</h4><p>5.10.1. Systems shall be configured to collect and retain security event logs for a minimum of 90 days to support monitoring and investigation.<br>5.10.2. Technical controls shall be implemented to prevent unauthorized data exfiltration and to ensure data processing complies with relevant privacy regulations.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy is designed to ensure [Company Name]''s compliance with relevant legal, statutory, regulatory, and contractual requirements related to information security and data protection, including but not limited to GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, HIPAA (if applicable), and the control frameworks of ISO/IEC 27001 and SOC 2 (Trust Services Criteria). The Legal and Compliance departments are responsible for identifying and communicating changes in the regulatory landscape that impact this policy.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Board of Directors / Executive Management:</strong> Ultimate responsibility for information security governance and resource allocation.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Overall ownership of the ISMS, policy development, and security program oversight.<br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Implementation, monitoring, and operational management of security controls.<br><strong>IT Department:</strong> Secure configuration and maintenance of IT infrastructure in accordance with this policy.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for adhering to all security policies and procedures, and for reporting security incidents.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Violations of this Information Security Policy will be treated with the utmost seriousness. Individuals found to have violated this policy will be subject to disciplinary action commensurate with the severity of the breach, which may include verbal or written warnings, suspension of system access, termination of employment or contract, and pursuit of legal remedies. All enforcement actions shall be documented.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in business operations, or updates to relevant standards and regulations. All revisions must be approved by executive management. The most current version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel covered by its scope.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-19 06:15:54.290528', '"[\"ISO 27001 / SOC 2\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-20 06:08:11.071', '"[{\"id\":\"q_1771452393824\",\"question\":\"New Question\",\"type\":\"boolean\",\"defaultValue\":false}]"'),
	(31, '507790', 'Network Security Policy', '<p>This Network Security Policy establishes the mandatory framework and technical controls to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of [Company Name]''s information assets and network infrastructure. It defines the principles, standards, and procedures required to safeguard network resources from unauthorized access, misuse, modification, or destruction, in alignment with business objectives and regulatory obligations.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third-party personnel (collectively "Personnel"). It encompasses all network infrastructure owned, operated, or leased by [Company Name], including but not limited to: data centers, cloud environments, corporate offices, remote access connections, wireless networks, and all connected devices (servers, workstations, network appliances, IoT devices). This policy extends to any system that processes, stores, or transmits [Company Name] data.</p>

<p><strong>DMZ (Demilitarized Zone):</strong> A physical or logical subnet that contains and isolates externally-facing services from the internal trusted network.<br/><strong>Least Privilege:</strong> The security principle that users and systems shall be granted the minimum levels of access necessary to perform their authorized functions.<br/><strong>Network Segmentation:</strong> The practice of dividing a network into smaller, isolated subnetworks to control traffic flow and contain security breaches.<br/><strong>Stateful Inspection Firewall:</strong> A firewall that monitors the state of active connections and makes filtering decisions based on the context of the traffic.<br/><strong>Zero Trust Architecture:</strong> A security model that mandates strict identity verification for every person and device attempting to access resources on a private network, regardless of location.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates a defense-in-depth approach to network security, integrating multiple layers of physical, technical, and administrative controls. All network access shall be governed by the principles of least privilege and need-to-know. The design, implementation, and operation of the corporate network must ensure the secure processing and transmission of information, protect against internal and external threats, and maintain compliance with all applicable legal, regulatory, and contractual requirements.</p>

<h4>5.1 Network Architecture and Segmentation</h4><p>[Company Name] shall implement a logically segmented network architecture. A minimum of three security zones (e.g., untrusted, DMZ, trusted internal) must be established and enforced by next-generation firewalls. All user workstations shall reside in a segment separate from server and infrastructure segments. Micro-segmentation must be applied within data center and cloud environments to restrict east-west traffic.</p><h4>5.2 Access Control and Authentication</h4><p>Access to all network resources shall be granted based on authenticated identity and role. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for all administrative access and remote network access (e.g., VPN). Network device configurations must enforce role-based access control (RBAC). All service accounts used for network device management must have complex, unique credentials that are rotated at least every 90 days.</p><h4>5.3 Perimeter and Internal Security Controls</h4><p>Stateful inspection or next-generation firewalls must be deployed at all network boundaries, including internet egress points and inter-zone boundaries. Intrusion Prevention Systems (IPS) shall be deployed in-line to monitor and block malicious traffic. All inbound and outbound traffic must be explicitly allowed by firewall rules; a default-deny rule shall be in place for all rule sets.</p><h4>5.4 Wireless Network Security</h4><p>All wireless infrastructure and access points must be approved and managed centrally by the Information Technology (IT) Department. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted networks for corporate data or access is strictly forbidden. Corporate wireless networks shall use WPA2-Enterprise or WPA3-Enterprise with 802.1X authentication. Guest wireless access must be isolated in a separate VLAN with no connectivity to the internal corporate network.</p><h4>5.5 Remote Access Security</h4><p>Remote access to the corporate network shall only be permitted through the approved corporate Virtual Private Network (VPN) solution. Split-tunneling is prohibited; all remote user traffic must be routed through the corporate VPN gateway. VPN sessions must timeout after a maximum of 12 hours of inactivity and require re-authentication.</p><h4>5.6 Vulnerability Management and Patching</h4><p>A formal inventory of all network devices (firewalls, routers, switches, wireless controllers) must be maintained and updated automatically. Critical and high-severity security patches for network infrastructure must be applied within 30 days of vendor release. Vulnerability scans of the internal and external network perimeter must be conducted at least quarterly, with findings remediated according to risk-based timelines.</p><h4>5.7 Logging, Monitoring, and Incident Response</h4><p>All network security devices (firewalls, IPS, VPN concentrators) must generate audit logs. Logs must record all allowed and denied traffic, configuration changes, and authentication events. These logs must be forwarded in real-time to a centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system and retained for a minimum of 90 days. The Security Operations Center (SOC) shall monitor these logs 24/7/365 for indicators of compromise.</p>

<p>Network security controls shall be designed and operated to meet the requirements of relevant frameworks including ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.8.20, A.8.21, A.8.22, A.8.23, A.8.24, A.8.25, A.8.26), SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Availability), and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (Protect, Detect). This policy also supports compliance with data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA) by enforcing controls for the secure transmission of personal data. Any deviation from this policy for legal or investigative purposes requires written authorization from the CISO and General Counsel.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimately responsible for the development, approval, and overall governance of this policy.<br/><strong>Network Engineering Team:</strong> Responsible for the secure design, implementation, and maintenance of the network infrastructure in accordance with this policy.<br/><strong>Security Operations Center (SOC):</strong> Responsible for the 24/7 monitoring of network security events, incident triage, and initial response.<br/><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for adhering to this policy, reporting suspected security incidents or policy violations, and using corporate network resources only for authorized purposes.</p>

<p>Compliance with this policy is mandatory. Violations may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and may lead to civil or criminal liability. The IT Department and SOC shall implement automated technical controls (e.g., NAC, firewall rules) to enforce policy requirements. Regular audits and automated compliance scans will be conducted to verify adherence. Any exception to this policy requires a formal, documented risk acceptance approved by the CISO.</p>

<p>This Network Security Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or following any significant security incident or major change in the network architecture or threat landscape. Revisions must be approved by the CISO and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. The policy version and revision history shall be maintained in a central document repository.</p>', '"[{\"title\":\"1. Purpose\",\"content\":\"<p>This Network Security Policy establishes the mandatory framework and technical controls to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of [Company Name]''s information assets and network infrastructure. It defines the principles, standards, and procedures required to safeguard network resources from unauthorized access, misuse, modification, or destruction, in alignment with business objectives and regulatory obligations.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"2. Scope\",\"content\":\"<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third-party personnel (collectively \\\"Personnel\\\"). It encompasses all network infrastructure owned, operated, or leased by [Company Name], including but not limited to: data centers, cloud environments, corporate offices, remote access connections, wireless networks, and all connected devices (servers, workstations, network appliances, IoT devices). This policy extends to any system that processes, stores, or transmits [Company Name] data.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"3. Definitions\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>DMZ (Demilitarized Zone):</strong> A physical or logical subnet that contains and isolates externally-facing services from the internal trusted network.<br/><strong>Least Privilege:</strong> The security principle that users and systems shall be granted the minimum levels of access necessary to perform their authorized functions.<br/><strong>Network Segmentation:</strong> The practice of dividing a network into smaller, isolated subnetworks to control traffic flow and contain security breaches.<br/><strong>Stateful Inspection Firewall:</strong> A firewall that monitors the state of active connections and makes filtering decisions based on the context of the traffic.<br/><strong>Zero Trust Architecture:</strong> A security model that mandates strict identity verification for every person and device attempting to access resources on a private network, regardless of location.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"4. Policy Statement\",\"content\":\"<p>[Company Name] mandates a defense-in-depth approach to network security, integrating multiple layers of physical, technical, and administrative controls. All network access shall be governed by the principles of least privilege and need-to-know. The design, implementation, and operation of the corporate network must ensure the secure processing and transmission of information, protect against internal and external threats, and maintain compliance with all applicable legal, regulatory, and contractual requirements.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"5. Policy Requirements\",\"content\":\"<h4>5.1 Network Architecture and Segmentation</h4><p>[Company Name] shall implement a logically segmented network architecture. A minimum of three security zones (e.g., untrusted, DMZ, trusted internal) must be established and enforced by next-generation firewalls. All user workstations shall reside in a segment separate from server and infrastructure segments. Micro-segmentation must be applied within data center and cloud environments to restrict east-west traffic.</p><h4>5.2 Access Control and Authentication</h4><p>Access to all network resources shall be granted based on authenticated identity and role. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for all administrative access and remote network access (e.g., VPN). Network device configurations must enforce role-based access control (RBAC). All service accounts used for network device management must have complex, unique credentials that are rotated at least every 90 days.</p><h4>5.3 Perimeter and Internal Security Controls</h4><p>Stateful inspection or next-generation firewalls must be deployed at all network boundaries, including internet egress points and inter-zone boundaries. Intrusion Prevention Systems (IPS) shall be deployed in-line to monitor and block malicious traffic. All inbound and outbound traffic must be explicitly allowed by firewall rules; a default-deny rule shall be in place for all rule sets.</p><h4>5.4 Wireless Network Security</h4><p>All wireless infrastructure and access points must be approved and managed centrally by the Information Technology (IT) Department. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted networks for corporate data or access is strictly forbidden. Corporate wireless networks shall use WPA2-Enterprise or WPA3-Enterprise with 802.1X authentication. Guest wireless access must be isolated in a separate VLAN with no connectivity to the internal corporate network.</p><h4>5.5 Remote Access Security</h4><p>Remote access to the corporate network shall only be permitted through the approved corporate Virtual Private Network (VPN) solution. Split-tunneling is prohibited; all remote user traffic must be routed through the corporate VPN gateway. VPN sessions must timeout after a maximum of 12 hours of inactivity and require re-authentication.</p><h4>5.6 Vulnerability Management and Patching</h4><p>A formal inventory of all network devices (firewalls, routers, switches, wireless controllers) must be maintained and updated automatically. Critical and high-severity security patches for network infrastructure must be applied within 30 days of vendor release. Vulnerability scans of the internal and external network perimeter must be conducted at least quarterly, with findings remediated according to risk-based timelines.</p><h4>5.7 Logging, Monitoring, and Incident Response</h4><p>All network security devices (firewalls, IPS, VPN concentrators) must generate audit logs. Logs must record all allowed and denied traffic, configuration changes, and authentication events. These logs must be forwarded in real-time to a centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system and retained for a minimum of 90 days. The Security Operations Center (SOC) shall monitor these logs 24/7/365 for indicators of compromise.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\",\"content\":\"<p>Network security controls shall be designed and operated to meet the requirements of relevant frameworks including ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.8.20, A.8.21, A.8.22, A.8.23, A.8.24, A.8.25, A.8.26), SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Availability), and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (Protect, Detect). This policy also supports compliance with data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA) by enforcing controls for the secure transmission of personal data. Any deviation from this policy for legal or investigative purposes requires written authorization from the CISO and General Counsel.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"7. Roles and Responsibilities\",\"content\":\"<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimately responsible for the development, approval, and overall governance of this policy.<br/><strong>Network Engineering Team:</strong> Responsible for the secure design, implementation, and maintenance of the network infrastructure in accordance with this policy.<br/><strong>Security Operations Center (SOC):</strong> Responsible for the 24/7 monitoring of network security events, incident triage, and initial response.<br/><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for adhering to this policy, reporting suspected security incidents or policy violations, and using corporate network resources only for authorized purposes.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"8. Enforcement\",\"content\":\"<p>Compliance with this policy is mandatory. Violations may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and may lead to civil or criminal liability. The IT Department and SOC shall implement automated technical controls (e.g., NAC, firewall rules) to enforce policy requirements. Regular audits and automated compliance scans will be conducted to verify adherence. Any exception to this policy requires a formal, documented risk acceptance approved by the CISO.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true},{\"title\":\"9. Review and Revision\",\"content\":\"<p>This Network Security Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or following any significant security incident or major change in the network architecture or threat landscape. Revisions must be approved by the CISO and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. The policy version and revision history shall be maintained in a central document repository.</p>\",\"defaultEnabled\":true}]"', '2025-12-20 21:17:22.508899', '"[\"ISO 27001\",\"SOC 2\",\"NIST CSF\"]"', NULL, true, NULL, '2026-02-20 06:08:47.174', '"[{\"id\":\"q_1771455702503_ji8s43ypg\",\"question\":\"Does your organization handle, process, or store any regulated data types subject to specific compliance frameworks (e.g., HIPAA, GDPR, CCPA, PCI-DSS)?\",\"type\":\"boolean\",\"options\":[],\"defaultValue\":false},{\"id\":\"q_1771455702503_ntfwtvguk\",\"question\":\"What is the primary method for remote employee access to internal network resources?\",\"type\":\"boolean\",\"options\":[\"VPN only\",\"Zero Trust Network Access (ZTNA)\",\"Virtual Desktop Infrastructure (VDI)\",\"Direct access with multi-factor authentication\",\"Other\"],\"defaultValue\":false},{\"id\":\"q_1771455702503_qtc3q2cte\",\"question\":\"Does your organization operate in a hybrid or multi-cloud environment requiring network segmentation between on-premises and cloud resources?\",\"type\":\"boolean\",\"options\":[],\"defaultValue\":false},{\"id\":\"q_1771455727570_r94clw8lt\",\"question\":\"Does your organization utilize Internet of Things (IoT) devices or operational technology (OT) networks that require integration with your corporate IT network?\",\"type\":\"boolean\",\"options\":[],\"defaultValue\":false},{\"id\":\"q_1771455727570_opg4upzx9\",\"question\":\"What is the primary method for securing wireless network access for employees and guests?\",\"type\":\"select\",\"options\":[\"WPA3-Enterprise with 802.1X\",\"WPA2/WPA3-Personal with PSK\",\"Captive portal with separate VLAN\",\"Certificate-based authentication\",\"Other\"],\"defaultValue\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"q_1771455727570_b1vngcr2t\",\"question\":\"Does your organization conduct regular penetration testing or vulnerability assessments of your network infrastructure?\",\"type\":\"boolean\",\"options\":[],\"defaultValue\":false},{\"id\":\"q_1771455752415_57wtsnyo1\",\"question\":\"Does your organization use network-connected industrial control systems (ICS) or supervisory control and data acquisition (SCADA) systems?\",\"type\":\"boolean\",\"options\":[],\"defaultValue\":false},{\"id\":\"q_1771455752415_5s18vopnc\",\"question\":\"What is the primary method for network traffic monitoring and intrusion detection?\",\"type\":\"select\",\"options\":[\"Commercial SIEM/SOAR platform\",\"Open-source tools (e.g., Wazuh, Suricata)\",\"Managed Security Service Provider (MSSP)\",\"Basic firewall logging only\",\"None\"],\"defaultValue\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"q_1771455752415_lc3rqxd91\",\"question\":\"Does your organization allow employees to connect personal devices (BYOD) to the corporate network?\",\"type\":\"boolean\",\"options\":[],\"defaultValue\":false}]"');


