First, implement a formal access control policy that defines authorization procedures for users, processes, and devices. Technologically, enforce this by configuring your systems to require unique user accounts with strong authentication (like multi-factor authentication), implementing role-based access controls (RBAC) to assign permissions based on job function, and using network access control (NAC) or device certificates to authorize only known devices. Ensure all service accounts (processes) are documented and have their access reviewed.

A common pitfall is focusing only on user accounts while neglecting service accounts and device-level access, which creates significant vulnerabilities. Another is granting excessive initial permissions or failing to conduct regular access reviews, leading to privilege creep. Avoid using shared accounts, as they break accountability.

To demonstrate compliance, gather this evidence: your documented access control policy, screenshots of Active Directory or system settings showing RBAC groups and assigned permissions, a list of all service accounts with their purposes, network diagrams or NAC configuration showing device authorization rules, and reports from quarterly access reviews showing the recertification of user and service account privileges.', 'control', 612, 4, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2026-01-06 23:49:09.622119', '2026-01-06 23:50:49.923'),
	(16, 3, 'control_implementation', 'completed', 'medium', 'Remediate Gap: 3.1.10 - 3.1.10 - Use session lock with pattern-hiding displays to prevent access and viewing of data after a period o...', '--- AI Remediation Plan ---
Implement a policy and configure all systems to automatically lock user sessions and hide displayed information after a maximum of 15 minutes of inactivity. For Windows systems, configure the screen saver with a password and set the "Screen saver timeout" and "Wait time for screen saver" in Group Policy or local settings. For macOS, set "Start screen saver after" and require a password immediately in Security & Privacy settings. Apply similar configurations to all managed workstations, servers with local logins, and mobile devices.

A common pitfall is applying settings only to end-user workstations while neglecting administrative consoles, virtual machine consoles, or kiosk devices. Ensure the policy covers all system types. Another pitfall is setting an excessively long timeout (e.g., 30+ minutes), which does not meet the intent of the control. Avoid disabling the lock feature for convenience.

To demonstrate compliance, collect and retain the following evidence: 1) The written policy defining the maximum 15-minute inactivity period and requirement for pattern-hiding. 2) Screenshots or configuration exports (e.g., `gpedit.msc` settings, macOS system preferences) showing the applied timeout and password-on-resume settings. 3) A system inventory list with a sampling method, confirming the settings are deployed uniformly across the environment. 4) Procedure documents for how these settings are managed and enforced.', 'control', 621, 4, NULL, NULL, '2026-01-15 00:00:00', '2026-01-07 22:54:44.413', false, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2026-01-06 23:53:16.580212', '2026-01-07 22:54:46.431'),
	(17, 3, 'control_implementation', 'pending', 'medium', 'Remediate Gap: A.5.3 - Segregation of duties', 'Implement control A.5.3.', 'control', 10, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, 36, '2026-01-12 09:51:40.741605', '2026-01-12 09:51:40.741605'),
	(18, 681, 'risk_review', 'pending', 'high', 'Annual Business Impact Analysis (BIA) Required', 'No completed Business Impact Analysis (BIA) project was found for the last 12 months. Regular BIAs are critical for ISO 22301 and SOC 2 compliance.', 'task', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-02-12 10:35:07.938', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, '"{\"source\":\"advisor_autopilot\",\"generatedAt\":\"2026-01-13T10:35:08.044Z\"}"', NULL, '2026-01-13 10:35:08.39244', '2026-01-13 10:35:08.39244'),
	(19, 731, 'control_implementation', 'pending', 'medium', 'AI RMF GOV 1.1 — Legal & Regulatory Requirements', 'AI System ID: 3
Framework: NIST AI RMF
